  (id, module, domain, skill, difficulty, passage, prompt, choices, answer, explanation, choice_explanations)
values
  ($q$adv-h62$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$√(x − 2) = x − 4<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["3","9","6","−6"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Squaring both sides gives x − 2 = x² − 8x + 16, so x² − 9x + 18 = 0, which factors as (x − 6)(x − 3) = 0. Squaring can introduce extraneous solutions, so check both: the right side x − 4 must be nonnegative. For x = 3, the right side is −1 < 0, so x = 3 is extraneous. Only x = 6 works, so the sum of all solutions is 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h63$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$3<sup>2x</sup> − 36(3<sup>x</sup>) + 243 = 0<br><br>What is the greatest solution to the given equation?$q$, $q$["36","3","5","27"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let u = 3<sup>x</sup>, so 3<sup>2x</sup> = u². The equation becomes u² − 36u + 243 = 0, which factors as (u − 9)(u − 27) = 0. So 3<sup>x</sup> = 9 or 3<sup>x</sup> = 27, giving x = 2 or x = 3. The greatest solution is 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h64$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + ax² + bx − 60<br><br>In the given polynomial function, a and b are constants. If −4 and −5 are zeros of p, what is the third zero of p?$q$, $q$["3","−3","−60","−9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a cubic x³ + ax² + bx + d, the product of the three zeros is −d. Here −d = 60, so (−4)(−5)r = 60, giving 20r = 60 and r = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h65$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$An object is launched upward from a platform. Its height h, in feet, t seconds after launch is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 64t + 32. For how many seconds is the object at a height of at least 60 feet?$q$, $q$["3.5","0.5","2","3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Set h(t) = 60: −16t² + 64t + 32 = 60, so −16t² + 64t − 28 = 0. Dividing by −16 gives t² − 4t + 1.75 = 0, so t = 0.5 or t = 3.5. The object is at or above 60 feet between these times, for 3.5 − 0.5 = 3 seconds.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h66$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$A population of bacteria increases by 125% every 2 years. Assuming the increase occurs at a constant percent rate per year, by what percent does it increase each year?$q$, $q$["62.5%","125%","50%","31%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the per-year growth factor is r, then r<sup>2</sup> = 1 + 1.25 = 2.25. Taking the square root gives r = 1.5, which is a 50% increase each year. Dividing 125% by 2 is a common error, because percent changes compound rather than add.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h67$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = ab<sup>x</sup>, where a and b are positive constants. If f(2) = 216 and f(5) = 1728, what is the value of f(4)?$q$, $q$["1224","864","432","1728"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Dividing, f(5)/f(2) = b<sup>3</sup> = 8, so b = 2. Then a = f(2)/b<sup>2</sup> = 54, so f(x) = 54(2)<sup>x</sup> and f(4) = 54(2)<sup>4</sup> = 864.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h68$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + 14x + c = 0<br><br>In the given equation, c is a constant. The equation has two real solutions, and the positive difference between them is 6. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["40","−40","49","46"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The solutions add to −14 (the opposite of the x-coefficient) and differ by 6. So they are (−14 + 6)/2 = −4 and (−14 − 6)/2 = −10. Their product is c = (−4)(−10) = 40.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h69$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + kx + 8 = 0<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant. For how many positive integer values of k does the equation have no real solutions?$q$, $q$["11","6","4","5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A quadratic has no real solutions when its discriminant is negative: k² − 4(1)(8) < 0, so k² < 32. The positive integers with k² < 32 are 1 through 5, so there are 5 values.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h70$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 3, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to √(x<sup>7</sup>) · ∜(x), where x > 0?$q$, $q$["x<sup>13/4</sup>","x<sup>7/8</sup>","x<sup>15/4</sup>","x<sup>4/3</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each radical as a rational exponent: √(x<sup>7</sup>) = x<sup>7/2</sup> and ∜(x) = x<sup>1/4</sup>. Multiplying powers with the same base adds exponents: 7/2 + 1/4 = 15/4, so the expression equals x<sup>15/4</sup>.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h71$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$(x² + kx − 21)/(x + 7) = x − 3<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant, and the equation is true for all x ≠ −7. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["10","4","−10","−21"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiplying both sides by x + 7 gives x² + kx − 21 = (x + 7)(x − 3) = x² + 4x − 21. Matching x-coefficients gives k = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h72$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is a quadratic function whose graph in the xy-plane has its vertex at x = 6. If f(0) = −37 and f(4) = −5, what is the value of f(7)?$q$, $q$["−2","−1","−3","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Write f(x) = a(x − 6)² + k. From f(0): a(36) + k = −37. From f(4): a(4) + k = −5. Subtracting gives 32a = −32, so a = −1 and k = −1. Then f(7) = −1(1)² − 1 = −2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h73$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 4, null, $q$p(x) = 3x³ + ax² + x + b<br><br>In the polynomial p, a and b are constants. When p(x) is divided by x − 2, the remainder is 31, and p(−1) = −11. What is the value of ab?$q$, $q$["−7","44","4","−44"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$By the remainder theorem, p(2) = 31: 24 + 4a + 2 + b = 31, so 4a + b = 5. Also p(−1) = −11: a + b = −7. Subtracting, 3a = 12, so a = 4 and b = −11. Thus ab = −44.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h74$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = x² − 2 and g(x) = 3x + k, where k is a positive constant. If f(g(3)) = 287, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−26","17","8","289"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$g(3) = 9 + k, so f(g(3)) = (9 + k)² − 2 = 287. Then (9 + k)² = 289, so 9 + k = 17 or 9 + k = −17. These give k = 8 or k = −26. Since k is positive, k = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h75$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 5x + 6. The function g is defined by g(x) = f(x + 5). What is the sum of the zeros of g?$q$, $q$["5","−5","15","0"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(x) = (x − 3)(x − 2), so the zeros of f are 3 and 2. g(x) = 0 when x + 5 equals a zero of f, so x = 3 − 5 = −2 or x = 2 − 5 = −3. The sum is −5.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h76$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$If 3<sup>x + 1</sup> − 3<sup>x − 1</sup> = 72, what is the value of 9<sup>x</sup>?$q$, $q$["729","3","27","243"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Factor out the smaller power, 3<sup>x − 1</sup>: 3<sup>x − 1</sup>(3<sup>2</sup> − 1) = 72, so 3<sup>x − 1</sup>(8) = 72 and 3<sup>x − 1</sup> = 9. Then x − 1 = 2, so x = 3, and 9<sup>x</sup> = 729.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h77$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 4, null, $q$x² + bx + c ≤ 0<br><br>In the given inequality, b and c are constants. The solution set of the inequality is −4 ≤ x ≤ 6. What is the value of b+c?$q$, $q$["22","−22","−23","−26"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The solution set is the interval between the zeros, so the zeros are −4 and 6, and the expression equals (x + 4)(x − 6) (the parabola opens upward, so it is ≤ 0 between its zeros). Expanding gives x² − 2x − 24, so b = −2 and c = −24, and b + c = −26.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h78$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The number of cells in a culture is halved every 9 hours. At time t = 0, there are 2400 cells. Which function gives the number of cells, N(t), t minutes after time t = 0?$q$, $q$["2400(1/2)<sup>t/9</sup>","2400(1/2)<sup>540t</sup>","2400(1/2)<sup>t/540</sup>","2400(1/2)<sup>9t</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The quantity is multiplied by 1/2 once every 9 hours. Since t is measured in minutes, 9 hours is 540 minutes, so the number of periods after t minutes is t/540. Therefore N(t) = 2400(1/2)<sup>t/540</sup>. Using t/9 would treat t as hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h79$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = 2x² − 2x − 6<br>y = −18x − 36<br><br>The graphs of the given equations in the xy-plane intersect at the points (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂). What is the value of y₁ + y₂?$q$, $q$["−8","72","36","−72"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Set the expressions equal: 2x² − 2x − 6 = −18x − 36, so 2x² + 16x + 30 = 0, which factors as 2(x + 5)(x + 3) = 0. So x₁ and x₂ are −5 and −3, and the corresponding y-values on the line are −18(−5) − 36 = 54 and −18(−3) − 36 = 18. The sum is 72.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h80$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (4x − 7)(−x − 5) − 5(x² − 1) is equivalent to ax² + bx + c, where a, b, and c are constants. What is the value of a + b + c?$q$, $q$["18","8","−18","19"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A quick route: a + b + c is the value of the expression at x = 1. That gives (−3)(−6) − 5(1 − 1) = 18 − 0 = 18. (Expanding fully gives −9x² − 13x + 40.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h81$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = −2x² + 20x + 4<br>y = 4x + k<br><br>In the given system of equations, k is a constant. If the system has exactly one real solution, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["4","−36","34","36"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Setting the expressions equal: −2x² + 20x + 4 = 4x + k, so −2x² + 16x + 4 − k = 0. Exactly one solution means the discriminant is 0: (16)² − 4(−2)(4 − k) = 0, which gives k = 36. The repeated solution is x = −(16)/(2·(−2)) = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h82$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$√(4x + 17) = x + 3<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−4","−2","2","6"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Squaring both sides gives 4x + 17 = x² + 6x + 9, so x² + 2x − 8 = 0, which factors as (x − 2)(x + 4) = 0. Squaring can introduce extraneous solutions, so check both: the right side x + 3 must be nonnegative. For x = −4, the right side is −1 < 0, so x = −4 is extraneous. Only x = 2 works, so the sum of all solutions is 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h83$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$2<sup>2x</sup> − 24(2<sup>x</sup>) + 128 = 0<br><br>What is the sum of the solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["24","7","12","16"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let u = 2<sup>x</sup>, so 2<sup>2x</sup> = u². The equation becomes u² − 24u + 128 = 0, which factors as (u − 8)(u − 16) = 0. So 2<sup>x</sup> = 8 or 2<sup>x</sup> = 16, giving x = 3 or x = 4. The sum is 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h84$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, null, $q$p(x) = x³  + bx + c<br><br>In the given polynomial function, b and c are constants. If −3 and 2 are zeros of p, what is the third zero of p?$q$, $q$["1","−1","3","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a cubic x³ + ax² + bx + c, the sum of the zeros is −a. Here −a = 0, so −3 + 2 + r = 0, giving r = 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h85$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$An object is launched upward from a platform. Its height h, in feet, t seconds after launch is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 80t + 94. For how many seconds is the object at a height of at least 130 feet?$q$, $q$["4.5","0.5","2.5","4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Set h(t) = 130: −16t² + 80t + 94 = 130, so −16t² + 80t − 36 = 0. Dividing by −16 gives t² − 5t + 2.25 = 0, so t = 0.5 or t = 4.5. The object is at or above 130 feet between these times, for 4.5 − 0.5 = 4 seconds.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h86$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The number of active users of an app increases by 44% every 2 years. Assuming the increase occurs at a constant percent rate per year, by what percent does it increase each year?$q$, $q$["22%","44%","20%","11%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the per-year growth factor is r, then r<sup>2</sup> = 1 + 0.44 = 1.44. Taking the square root gives r = 1.2, which is a 20% increase each year. Dividing 44% by 2 is a common error, because percent changes compound rather than add.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h87$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = ab<sup>x</sup>, where a and b are positive constants. If f(2) = 36 and f(4) = 16, what is the value of f(3)?$q$, $q$["26","24","36","16"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Dividing, f(4)/f(2) = b<sup>2</sup> = 4/9, so b = 2/3. Then a = f(2)/b<sup>2</sup> = 81, so f(x) = 81(2/3)<sup>x</sup> and f(3) = 81(2/3)<sup>3</sup> = 24.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h88$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² − 3x + c = 0<br><br>In the given equation, c is a constant. The equation has two real solutions, and the positive difference between them is 5. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["−4","1","4","−3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The solutions add to 3 (the opposite of the x-coefficient) and differ by 5. So they are (3 + 5)/2 = 4 and (3 − 5)/2 = −1. Their product is c = (4)(−1) = −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h89$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$4x² + kx + 6 = 0<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant. For how many positive integer values of k does the equation have no real solutions?$q$, $q$["19","10","8","9"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A quadratic has no real solutions when its discriminant is negative: k² − 4(4)(6) < 0, so k² < 96. The positive integers with k² < 96 are 1 through 9, so there are 9 values.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h90$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 4, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to <sup>5</sup>√(x<sup>2</sup>) · <sup>6</sup>√(x<sup>4</sup>), where x > 0?$q$, $q$["x<sup>−4/15</sup>","x<sup>4/15</sup>","x<sup>16/15</sup>","x<sup>6/11</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each radical as a rational exponent: <sup>5</sup>√(x<sup>2</sup>) = x<sup>2/5</sup> and <sup>6</sup>√(x<sup>4</sup>) = x<sup>2/3</sup>. Multiplying powers with the same base adds exponents: 2/5 + 2/3 = 16/15, so the expression equals x<sup>16/15</sup>.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h91$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (x² + 11x + 27)/(x + 4) is equivalent to x + 7 + R/(x + 4), where R is a constant and x ≠ −4. What is the value of R?$q$, $q$["27","−1","1","28"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiply both sides by (x + 4): x² + 11x + 27 = (x + 4)(x + 7) + R = x² + 11x + 28 + R. Matching constants: 27 = 28 + R, so R = −1. (Equivalently, by the remainder theorem, R equals the numerator evaluated at x = −4.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h92$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is a quadratic function whose graph in the xy-plane has its vertex at x = 5. If f(0) = −67 and f(3) = −4, what is the value of f(9)?$q$, $q$["−40","8","−43","40"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Write f(x) = a(x − 5)² + k. From f(0): a(25) + k = −67. From f(3): a(4) + k = −4. Subtracting gives 21a = −63, so a = −3 and k = 8. Then f(9) = −3(4)² + 8 = −40.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h93$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 4, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + ax² − x + b<br><br>In the polynomial p, a and b are constants. When p(x) is divided by x − 1, the remainder is −5, and p(2) = −8. What is the value of a + b?$q$, $q$["6","5","−3","−5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$By the remainder theorem, p(1) = −5: 1 + a − 1 + b = −5, so a + b = −5. Also p(2) = −8: 4a + b = −14. Subtracting, −3a = 9, so a = −3 and b = −2. Thus a + b = −5.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h94$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = x² + 6 and g(x) = 4x + k, where k is a positive constant. If f(g(2)) = 127, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−19","11","3","121"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$g(2) = 8 + k, so f(g(2)) = (8 + k)² + 6 = 127. Then (8 + k)² = 121, so 8 + k = 11 or 8 + k = −11. These give k = 3 or k = −19. Since k is positive, k = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h95$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 7x − 8. The function g is defined by g(x) = f(x − 3). What is the sum of the zeros of g?$q$, $q$["−7","−1","−13","1"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(x) = (x − 1)(x + 8), so the zeros of f are 1 and −8. g(x) = 0 when x − 3 equals a zero of f, so x = 1 + 3 = 4 or x = −8 + 3 = −5. The sum is −1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h96$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$If 3<sup>x + 2</sup> − 3<sup>x − 2</sup> = 240, what is the value of 3<sup>x + 1</sup>?$q$, $q$["81","3","27","55"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Factor out the smaller power, 3<sup>x − 2</sup>: 3<sup>x − 2</sup>(3<sup>4</sup> − 1) = 240, so 3<sup>x − 2</sup>(80) = 240 and 3<sup>x − 2</sup> = 3. Then x − 2 = 1, so x = 3, and 3<sup>x + 1</sup> = 81.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h97$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 4, null, $q$−2x² + bx + c ≥ 0<br><br>In the given inequality, b and c are constants. The solution set of the inequality is 3 ≤ x ≤ 11. What is the value of b+c?$q$, $q$["94","47","33","−38"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The solution set is the interval between the zeros, so the zeros are 3 and 11, and the expression equals −2(x − 3)(x − 11) (the parabola opens downward, so it is ≥ 0 between its zeros). Expanding gives −2x² + 28x − 66, so b = 28 and c = −66, and b + c = −38.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h99$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = 2x² + x + 3<br>y = −5x − 1<br><br>The graphs of the given equations in the xy-plane intersect at the points (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂). What is the value of x₁ + x₂?$q$, $q$["3","−3","1/2","−5/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Set the expressions equal: 2x² + x + 3 = −5x − 1, so 2x² + 6x + 4 = 0, which factors as 2(x + 2)(x + 1) = 0. So x₁ and x₂ are −2 and −1, with sum −3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h100$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (x + 4)(−4x + 9) − 3(x² + 7) is equivalent to ax² + bx + c, where a, b, and c are constants. What is the value of a + b + c?$q$, $q$["1","43","25","49"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A quick route: a + b + c is the value of the expression at x = 1. That gives (5)(5) − 3(1 + 7) = 25 − 24 = 1. (Expanding fully gives −7x² − 7x + 15.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h101$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = x² − 4x + 3<br>y = 6x + k<br><br>In the given system of equations, k is a constant. If the system has exactly one real solution, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["3","22","−21","−22"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Setting the expressions equal: x² − 4x + 3 = 6x + k, so x² − 10x + 3 − k = 0. Exactly one solution means the discriminant is 0: (−10)² − 4(1)(3 − k) = 0, which gives k = −22. The repeated solution is x = −(−10)/(2·1) = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h102$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$√(7x + 46) = x + 4<br><br>What is the solution to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−6","−1","5","−5"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Squaring both sides gives 7x + 46 = x² + 8x + 16, so x² + x − 30 = 0, which factors as (x − 5)(x + 6) = 0. Squaring can introduce extraneous solutions, so check both: the right side x + 4 must be nonnegative. For x = −6, the right side is −2 < 0, so x = −6 is extraneous. Only x = 5 works.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h103$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$2<sup>2x</sup> − 6(2<sup>x</sup>) + 8 = 0<br><br>What is the sum of the solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["6","3","2","4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let u = 2<sup>x</sup>, so 2<sup>2x</sup> = u². The equation becomes u² − 6u + 8 = 0, which factors as (u − 2)(u − 4) = 0. So 2<sup>x</sup> = 2 or 2<sup>x</sup> = 4, giving x = 1 or x = 2. The sum is 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h104$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + 3x² + bx + c<br><br>In the given polynomial function, b and c are constants. If −1 and 2 are zeros of p, what is the third zero of p?$q$, $q$["−4","4","−2","1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a cubic x³ + ax² + bx + c, the sum of the zeros is −a. Here −a = −3, so −1 + 2 + r = −3, giving r = −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h105$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$An object is launched upward from a platform. Its height h, in feet, t seconds after launch is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 64t + 52. For how many seconds is the object at a height of at least 80 feet?$q$, $q$["3.5","0.5","2","3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Set h(t) = 80: −16t² + 64t + 52 = 80, so −16t² + 64t − 28 = 0. Dividing by −16 gives t² − 4t + 1.75 = 0, so t = 0.5 or t = 3.5. The object is at or above 80 feet between these times, for 3.5 − 0.5 = 3 seconds.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h106$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The amount of a medication in a patient's bloodstream decreases by 51% every 2 decades. Assuming the decrease occurs at a constant percent rate per decade, by what percent does it decrease each decade?$q$, $q$["25.5%","51%","30%","13%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the per-decade growth factor is r, then r<sup>2</sup> = 1 − 0.51 = 0.49. Taking the square root gives r = 0.7, which is a 30% decrease each decade. Dividing 51% by 2 is a common error, because percent changes compound rather than add.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h107$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = ab<sup>x</sup>, where a and b are positive constants. If f(1) = 96 and f(3) = 384, what is the value of f(4)?$q$, $q$["528","768","384","1536"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Dividing, f(3)/f(1) = b<sup>2</sup> = 4, so b = 2. Then a = f(1)/b<sup>1</sup> = 48, so f(x) = 48(2)<sup>x</sup> and f(4) = 48(2)<sup>4</sup> = 768.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h108$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² − 14x + c = 0<br><br>In the given equation, c is a constant. The equation has two real solutions, and the positive difference between them is 4. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["45","48","49","−45"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The solutions add to 14 (the opposite of the x-coefficient) and differ by 4. So they are (14 + 4)/2 = 9 and (14 − 4)/2 = 5. Their product is c = (9)(5) = 45.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h109$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$3x² + kx + 2 = 0<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant. For how many positive integer values of k does the equation have no real solutions?$q$, $q$["9","5","3","4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A quadratic has no real solutions when its discriminant is negative: k² − 4(3)(2) < 0, so k² < 24. The positive integers with k² < 24 are 1 through 4, so there are 4 values.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h110$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 3, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to √(x) ÷ ∛(x<sup>2</sup>), where x > 0?$q$, $q$["x<sup>7/6</sup>","x<sup>1/3</sup>","x<sup>−1/6</sup>","x<sup>−1</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each radical as a rational exponent: √(x) = x<sup>1/2</sup> and ∛(x<sup>2</sup>) = x<sup>2/3</sup>. Dividing powers with the same base subtracts exponents: 1/2 − 2/3 = −1/6, so the expression equals x<sup>−1/6</sup>.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h111$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (x² − 2x − 26)/(x − 5) is equivalent to x + 3 + R/(x − 5), where R is a constant and x ≠ 5. What is the value of R?$q$, $q$["−26","−11","11","−15"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiply both sides by (x − 5): x² − 2x − 26 = (x − 5)(x + 3) + R = x² − 2x − 15 + R. Matching constants: −26 = −15 + R, so R = −11. (Equivalently, by the remainder theorem, R equals the numerator evaluated at x = 5.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h112$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is a quadratic function whose graph in the xy-plane has its vertex at x = −1. If f(0) = 6 and f(−2) = 6, what is the value of f(−6)?$q$, $q$["102","2","106","−102"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Write f(x) = a(x − (−1))² + k. From f(0): a(1) + k = 6. From f(−2): a(1) + k = 6. Subtracting gives 0a = 0, so a = 4 and k = 2. Then f(−6) = 4(−5)² + 2 = 102.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h113$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 4, null, $q$p(x) = 3x³ + ax² − 4x + b<br><br>In the polynomial p, a and b are constants. When p(x) is divided by x + 3, the remainder is −23, and p(−2) = 0. What is the value of ab?$q$, $q$["−2","48","6","−48"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$By the remainder theorem, p(−3) = −23: −81 + 9a + 12 + b = −23, so 9a + b = 46. Also p(−2) = 0: 4a + b = 16. Subtracting, 5a = 30, so a = 6 and b = −8. Thus ab = −48.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h114$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = x² − 2 and g(x) = 2x + k, where k is a positive constant. If f(g(−1)) = 23, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−3","5","7","25"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$g(−1) = −2 + k, so f(g(−1)) = (−2 + k)² − 2 = 23. Then (−2 + k)² = 25, so −2 + k = 5 or −2 + k = −5. These give k = 7 or k = −3. Since k is positive, k = 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h115$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 2x − 24. The function g is defined by g(x) = f(x + 1). What is the sum of the zeros of g?$q$, $q$["−2","−4","0","4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(x) = (x + 6)(x − 4), so the zeros of f are −6 and 4. g(x) = 0 when x + 1 equals a zero of f, so x = −6 − 1 = −7 or x = 4 − 1 = 3. The sum is −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h116$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$If 2<sup>x + 3</sup> − 2<sup>x − 2</sup> = 62, what is the value of 4<sup>x</sup>?$q$, $q$["64","3","8","32"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Factor out the smaller power, 2<sup>x − 2</sup>: 2<sup>x − 2</sup>(2<sup>5</sup> − 1) = 62, so 2<sup>x − 2</sup>(31) = 62 and 2<sup>x − 2</sup> = 2. Then x − 2 = 1, so x = 3, and 4<sup>x</sup> = 64.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h117$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 4, null, $q$−2x² + bx + c ≥ 0<br><br>In the given inequality, b and c are constants. The solution set of the inequality is −4 ≤ x ≤ 3. What is the value of b+c?$q$, $q$["−26","−13","−12","22"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The solution set is the interval between the zeros, so the zeros are −4 and 3, and the expression equals −2(x + 4)(x − 3) (the parabola opens downward, so it is ≥ 0 between its zeros). Expanding gives −2x² − 2x + 24, so b = −2 and c = 24, and b + c = 22.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h119$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = 2x² − x − 8<br>y = −3x + 52<br><br>The graphs of the given equations in the xy-plane intersect at the points (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂). What is the value of x₁ + x₂?$q$, $q$["1","−1","−1/2","−3/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Set the expressions equal: 2x² − x − 8 = −3x + 52, so 2x² + 2x − 60 = 0, which factors as 2(x − 5)(x + 6) = 0. So x₁ and x₂ are 5 and −6, with sum −1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h120$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (4x − 5)(4x + 9) − 3(x² − 7) is equivalent to ax² + bx + c, where a, b, and c are constants. What is the value of a + b + c?$q$, $q$["5","−37","−13","−31"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A quick route: a + b + c is the value of the expression at x = 1. That gives (−1)(13) − 3(1 − 7) = −13 + 18 = 5. (Expanding fully gives 13x² + 16x − 24.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h121$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = −x² + 8x + 5<br>y = 4x + k<br><br>In the given system of equations, k is a constant. If the system has exactly one real solution, what is the value of x in that solution?$q$, $q$["−2","4","3","2"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Setting the expressions equal: −x² + 8x + 5 = 4x + k, so −x² + 4x + 5 − k = 0. Exactly one solution means the discriminant is 0: (4)² − 4(−1)(5 − k) = 0, which gives k = 9. The repeated solution is x = −(4)/(2·(−1)) = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h122$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$√(5x + 46) = x + 2<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−6","1","7","−7"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Squaring both sides gives 5x + 46 = x² + 4x + 4, so x² − x − 42 = 0, which factors as (x − 7)(x + 6) = 0. Squaring can introduce extraneous solutions, so check both: the right side x + 2 must be nonnegative. For x = −6, the right side is −4 < 0, so x = −6 is extraneous. Only x = 7 works, so the sum of all solutions is 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h123$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$2<sup>2x</sup> − 6(2<sup>x</sup>) + 8 = 0<br><br>What is the product of the solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["6","2","3","4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let u = 2<sup>x</sup>, so 2<sup>2x</sup> = u². The equation becomes u² − 6u + 8 = 0, which factors as (u − 2)(u − 4) = 0. So 2<sup>x</sup> = 2 or 2<sup>x</sup> = 4, giving x = 1 or x = 2. The product is 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h124$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + x² + bx + c<br><br>In the given polynomial function, b and c are constants. If −4 and −3 are zeros of p, what is the third zero of p?$q$, $q$["6","−6","8","−7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a cubic x³ + ax² + bx + c, the sum of the zeros is −a. Here −a = −1, so −4 + (−3) + r = −1, giving r = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h125$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$An object is launched upward from a platform. Its height h, in feet, t seconds after launch is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 72t + 18. For how many seconds is the object at a height of at least 90 feet?$q$, $q$["3","2.25","0.75","1.5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Set h(t) = 90: −16t² + 72t + 18 = 90, so −16t² + 72t − 72 = 0. Dividing by −16 gives t² − 4.5t + 4.5 = 0, so t = 1.5 or t = 3. The object is at or above 90 feet between these times, for 3 − 1.5 = 1.5 seconds.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h126$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The number of active users of an app increases by 69% every 2 months. Assuming the increase occurs at a constant percent rate per month, by what percent does it increase each month?$q$, $q$["34.5%","69%","30%","17%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the per-month growth factor is r, then r<sup>2</sup> = 1 + 0.69 = 1.69. Taking the square root gives r = 1.3, which is a 30% increase each month. Dividing 69% by 2 is a common error, because percent changes compound rather than add.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h127$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = ab<sup>x</sup>, where a and b are positive constants. If f(2) = 192 and f(4) = 768, what is the value of f(5)?$q$, $q$["1056","1536","768","3072"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Dividing, f(4)/f(2) = b<sup>2</sup> = 4, so b = 2. Then a = f(2)/b<sup>2</sup> = 48, so f(x) = 48(2)<sup>x</sup> and f(5) = 48(2)<sup>5</sup> = 1536.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h128$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + 2x + c = 0<br><br>In the given equation, c is a constant. The equation has two real solutions, and the positive difference between them is 6. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["−8","8","1","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The solutions add to −2 (the opposite of the x-coefficient) and differ by 6. So they are (−2 + 6)/2 = 2 and (−2 − 6)/2 = −4. Their product is c = (2)(−4) = −8.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h129$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$3x² + kx + 12 = 0<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant. For how many positive integer values of k does the equation have no real solutions?$q$, $q$["23","12","10","11"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A quadratic has no real solutions when its discriminant is negative: k² − 4(3)(12) < 0, so k² < 144. The positive integers with k² < 144 are 1 through 11, so there are 11 values.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h130$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 4, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to <sup>5</sup>√(x<sup>2</sup>) ÷ <sup>6</sup>√(x<sup>3</sup>), where x > 0?$q$, $q$["x<sup>9/10</sup>","x<sup>1/5</sup>","x<sup>−1/10</sup>","x<sup>−1</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each radical as a rational exponent: <sup>5</sup>√(x<sup>2</sup>) = x<sup>2/5</sup> and <sup>6</sup>√(x<sup>3</sup>) = x<sup>1/2</sup>. Dividing powers with the same base subtracts exponents: 2/5 − 1/2 = −1/10, so the expression equals x<sup>−1/10</sup>.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h131$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (x²  − 18)/(x + 3) is equivalent to x − 3 + R/(x + 3), where R is a constant and x ≠ −3. What is the value of R?$q$, $q$["−18","−9","9","−27"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiply both sides by (x + 3): x² − 18 = (x + 3)(x − 3) + R = x² − 9 + R. Matching constants: −18 = −9 + R, so R = −9. (Equivalently, by the remainder theorem, R equals the numerator evaluated at x = −3.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h132$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is a quadratic function whose graph in the xy-plane has its vertex at x = 1. If f(0) = 5 and f(−2) = 29, what is the value of f(4)?$q$, $q$["29","2","32","−29"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Write f(x) = a(x − 1)² + k. From f(0): a(1) + k = 5. From f(−2): a(9) + k = 29. Subtracting gives −8a = −24, so a = 3 and k = 2. Then f(4) = 3(3)² + 2 = 29.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h133$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 4, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + ax² + 5x + b<br><br>In the polynomial p, a and b are constants. When p(x) is divided by x + 1, the remainder is 3, and p(2) = 33. What is the value of a + b?$q$, $q$["14","−9","2","9"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$By the remainder theorem, p(−1) = 3: −1 + a − 5 + b = 3, so a + b = 9. Also p(2) = 33: 4a + b = 15. Subtracting, −3a = −6, so a = 2 and b = 7. Thus a + b = 9.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h134$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = x² + 8 and g(x) = 3x + k, where k is a positive constant. If f(g(3)) = 204, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−23","14","5","196"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$g(3) = 9 + k, so f(g(3)) = (9 + k)² + 8 = 204. Then (9 + k)² = 196, so 9 + k = 14 or 9 + k = −14. These give k = 5 or k = −23. Since k is positive, k = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h135$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 5x − 6. The function g is defined by g(x) = f(x + 3). What is the sum of the zeros of g?$q$, $q$["5","−1","11","1"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(x) = (x − 6)(x + 1), so the zeros of f are 6 and −1. g(x) = 0 when x + 3 equals a zero of f, so x = 6 − 3 = 3 or x = −1 − 3 = −4. The sum is −1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h136$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$If 2<sup>x + 2</sup> − 2<sup>x − 1</sup> = 14, what is the value of 4<sup>x</sup>?$q$, $q$["16","2","4","8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Factor out the smaller power, 2<sup>x − 1</sup>: 2<sup>x − 1</sup>(2<sup>3</sup> − 1) = 14, so 2<sup>x − 1</sup>(7) = 14 and 2<sup>x − 1</sup> = 2. Then x − 1 = 1, so x = 2, and 4<sup>x</sup> = 16.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h137$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 4, null, $q$−2x² + bx + c ≥ 0<br><br>In the given inequality, b and c are constants. The solution set of the inequality is 3 ≤ x ≤ 9. What is the value of b+c?$q$, $q$["78","39","27","−30"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The solution set is the interval between the zeros, so the zeros are 3 and 9, and the expression equals −2(x − 3)(x − 9) (the parabola opens downward, so it is ≥ 0 between its zeros). Expanding gives −2x² + 24x − 54, so b = 24 and c = −54, and b + c = −30.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h139$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = x² − 6x + 1<br>y = x − 5<br><br>The graphs of the given equations in the xy-plane intersect at the points (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂). What is the value of x₁ + x₂?$q$, $q$["−7","7","−6","1"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Set the expressions equal: x² − 6x + 1 = x − 5, so x² − 7x + 6 = 0, which factors as (x − 1)(x − 6) = 0. So x₁ and x₂ are 1 and 6, with sum 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h140$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (−x − 4)(−4x + 4) − 2(x² − 4) is equivalent to ax² + bx + c, where a, b, and c are constants. What is the value of a + b + c?$q$, $q$["6","−10","0","−6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A quick route: a + b + c is the value of the expression at x = 1. That gives (−5)(0) − 2(1 − 4) = 0 + 6 = 6. (Expanding fully gives 2x² + 12x − 8.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h141$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = 2x² + 11x + 6<br>y = −5x + k<br><br>In the given system of equations, k is a constant. If the system has exactly one real solution, what is the value of x in that solution?$q$, $q$["4","−8","−3","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Setting the expressions equal: 2x² + 11x + 6 = −5x + k, so 2x² + 16x + 6 − k = 0. Exactly one solution means the discriminant is 0: (16)² − 4(2)(6 − k) = 0, which gives k = −26. The repeated solution is x = −(16)/(2·2) = −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h142$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$√(3x + 22) = x + 6<br><br>What is the solution to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−7","−9","−2","2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Squaring both sides gives 3x + 22 = x² + 12x + 36, so x² + 9x + 14 = 0, which factors as (x + 2)(x + 7) = 0. Squaring can introduce extraneous solutions, so check both: the right side x + 6 must be nonnegative. For x = −7, the right side is −1 < 0, so x = −7 is extraneous. Only x = −2 works.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h143$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$2<sup>2x</sup> − 24(2<sup>x</sup>) + 128 = 0<br><br>What is the product of the solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["24","12","7","16"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let u = 2<sup>x</sup>, so 2<sup>2x</sup> = u². The equation becomes u² − 24u + 128 = 0, which factors as (u − 8)(u − 16) = 0. So 2<sup>x</sup> = 8 or 2<sup>x</sup> = 16, giving x = 3 or x = 4. The product is 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h144$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + ax² + bx + 100<br><br>In the given polynomial function, a and b are constants. If 5 and 4 are zeros of p, what is the third zero of p?$q$, $q$["−5","5","100","9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a cubic x³ + ax² + bx + d, the product of the three zeros is −d. Here −d = −100, so (5)(4)r = −100, giving 20r = −100 and r = −5.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h145$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$An object is launched upward from a platform. Its height h, in feet, t seconds after launch is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 64t + 122. For how many seconds is the object at a height of at least 150 feet?$q$, $q$["3.5","0.5","2","3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Set h(t) = 150: −16t² + 64t + 122 = 150, so −16t² + 64t − 28 = 0. Dividing by −16 gives t² − 4t + 1.75 = 0, so t = 0.5 or t = 3.5. The object is at or above 150 feet between these times, for 3.5 − 0.5 = 3 seconds.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h146$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$A population of bacteria increases by 33.1% every 3 years. Assuming the increase occurs at a constant percent rate per year, by what percent does it increase each year?$q$, $q$["11.03%","33.1%","10%","6%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the per-year growth factor is r, then r<sup>3</sup> = 1 + 0.331 = 1.331. Taking the cube root gives r = 1.1, which is a 10% increase each year. Dividing 33.1% by 3 is a common error, because percent changes compound rather than add.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h147$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = ab<sup>x</sup>, where a and b are positive constants. If f(2) = 192 and f(5) = 12288, what is the value of f(4)?$q$, $q$["8256","3072","768","12288"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Dividing, f(5)/f(2) = b<sup>3</sup> = 64, so b = 4. Then a = f(2)/b<sup>2</sup> = 12, so f(x) = 12(4)<sup>x</sup> and f(4) = 12(4)<sup>4</sup> = 3072.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h148$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² − 13x + c = 0<br><br>In the given equation, c is a constant. The equation has two real solutions, and the positive difference between them is 3. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["40","−40","43","41"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The solutions add to 13 (the opposite of the x-coefficient) and differ by 3. So they are (13 + 3)/2 = 8 and (13 − 3)/2 = 5. Their product is c = (8)(5) = 40.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h149$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$3x² + kx + 3 = 0<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant. For how many positive integer values of k does the equation have no real solutions?$q$, $q$["11","6","4","5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A quadratic has no real solutions when its discriminant is negative: k² − 4(3)(3) < 0, so k² < 36. The positive integers with k² < 36 are 1 through 5, so there are 5 values.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h150$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 4, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to ∜(x) · <sup>6</sup>√(x<sup>4</sup>), where x > 0?$q$, $q$["x<sup>−5/12</sup>","x<sup>1/6</sup>","x<sup>11/12</sup>","x<sup>1/2</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each radical as a rational exponent: ∜(x) = x<sup>1/4</sup> and <sup>6</sup>√(x<sup>4</sup>) = x<sup>2/3</sup>. Multiplying powers with the same base adds exponents: 1/4 + 2/3 = 11/12, so the expression equals x<sup>11/12</sup>.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h151$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (x² + 4x − 21)/(x + 6) is equivalent to x − 2 + R/(x + 6), where R is a constant and x ≠ −6. What is the value of R?$q$, $q$["−21","−9","9","−12"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiply both sides by (x + 6): x² + 4x − 21 = (x + 6)(x − 2) + R = x² + 4x − 12 + R. Matching constants: −21 = −12 + R, so R = −9. (Equivalently, by the remainder theorem, R equals the numerator evaluated at x = −6.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h152$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is a quadratic function whose graph in the xy-plane has its vertex at x = 3. If f(0) = 35 and f(7) = 56, what is the value of f(1)?$q$, $q$["20","8","23","−20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Write f(x) = a(x − 3)² + k. From f(0): a(9) + k = 35. From f(7): a(16) + k = 56. Subtracting gives −7a = −21, so a = 3 and k = 8. Then f(1) = 3(−2)² + 8 = 20.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h153$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 4, null, $q$p(x) = 3x³ + ax² + 5x + b<br><br>In the polynomial p, a and b are constants. When p(x) is divided by x − 1, the remainder is 13, and p(−3) = −123. What is the value of a + b?$q$, $q$["−36","−5","−4","5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$By the remainder theorem, p(1) = 13: 3 + a + 5 + b = 13, so a + b = 5. Also p(−3) = −123: 9a + b = −27. Subtracting, −8a = 32, so a = −4 and b = 9. Thus a + b = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h154$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = x² + 8 and g(x) = 2x + k, where k is a positive constant. If f(g(−1)) = 44, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−4","6","8","36"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$g(−1) = −2 + k, so f(g(−1)) = (−2 + k)² + 8 = 44. Then (−2 + k)² = 36, so −2 + k = 6 or −2 + k = −6. These give k = 8 or k = −4. Since k is positive, k = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h155$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 14x + 48. The function g is defined by g(x) = f(x + 6). What is the sum of the zeros of g?$q$, $q$["14","2","26","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(x) = (x − 6)(x − 8), so the zeros of f are 6 and 8. g(x) = 0 when x + 6 equals a zero of f, so x = 6 − 6 = 0 or x = 8 − 6 = 2. The sum is 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h156$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$If 3<sup>x + 1</sup> − 3<sup>x − 1</sup> = 72, what is the value of 3<sup>x + 1</sup>?$q$, $q$["81","3","27","55"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Factor out the smaller power, 3<sup>x − 1</sup>: 3<sup>x − 1</sup>(3<sup>2</sup> − 1) = 72, so 3<sup>x − 1</sup>(8) = 72 and 3<sup>x − 1</sup> = 9. Then x − 1 = 2, so x = 3, and 3<sup>x + 1</sup> = 81.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h157$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 4, null, $q$x² + bx + c ≤ 0<br><br>In the given inequality, b and c are constants. The solution set of the inequality is −1 ≤ x ≤ 5. What is the value of b+c?$q$, $q$["1","−1","−4","−9"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The solution set is the interval between the zeros, so the zeros are −1 and 5, and the expression equals (x + 1)(x − 5) (the parabola opens upward, so it is ≤ 0 between its zeros). Expanding gives x² − 4x − 5, so b = −4 and c = −5, and b + c = −9.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h159$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = −x² + 3x − 6<br>y = 4x − 26<br><br>The graphs of the given equations in the xy-plane intersect at the points (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂). What is the value of x₁x₂?$q$, $q$["20","−20","6","26"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Set the expressions equal: −x² + 3x − 6 = 4x − 26, so −x² − x + 20 = 0, which factors as −(x − 4)(x + 5) = 0. So x₁ and x₂ are 4 and −5, with product −20.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h160$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (−2x − 9)(−3x + 8) − 3(x² − 7) is equivalent to ax² + bx + c, where a, b, and c are constants. What is the value of a + b + c?$q$, $q$["−37","−79","−55","−73"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A quick route: a + b + c is the value of the expression at x = 1. That gives (−11)(5) − 3(1 − 7) = −55 + 18 = −37. (Expanding fully gives 3x² + 11x − 51.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h161$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = −2x² + 12x + 1<br>y = 4x + k<br><br>In the given system of equations, k is a constant. If the system has exactly one real solution, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["1","−9","7","9"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Setting the expressions equal: −2x² + 12x + 1 = 4x + k, so −2x² + 8x + 1 − k = 0. Exactly one solution means the discriminant is 0: (8)² − 4(−2)(1 − k) = 0, which gives k = 9. The repeated solution is x = −(8)/(2·(−2)) = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h162$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$√(3x + 19) = x − 3<br><br>What is the solution to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−1","9","10","−10"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Squaring both sides gives 3x + 19 = x² − 6x + 9, so x² − 9x − 10 = 0, which factors as (x − 10)(x + 1) = 0. Squaring can introduce extraneous solutions, so check both: the right side x − 3 must be nonnegative. For x = −1, the right side is −4 < 0, so x = −1 is extraneous. Only x = 10 works.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h163$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$3<sup>2x</sup> − 84(3<sup>x</sup>) + 243 = 0<br><br>What is the product of the solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["84","4","5","81"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let u = 3<sup>x</sup>, so 3<sup>2x</sup> = u². The equation becomes u² − 84u + 243 = 0, which factors as (u − 3)(u − 81) = 0. So 3<sup>x</sup> = 3 or 3<sup>x</sup> = 81, giving x = 1 or x = 4. The product is 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h164$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + ax² + bx − 40<br><br>In the given polynomial function, a and b are constants. If 2 and −4 are zeros of p, what is the third zero of p?$q$, $q$["−5","5","−40","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a cubic x³ + ax² + bx + d, the product of the three zeros is −d. Here −d = 40, so (2)(−4)r = 40, giving −8r = 40 and r = −5.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h165$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$An object is launched upward from a platform. Its height h, in feet, t seconds after launch is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 56t + 86. For how many seconds is the object at a height of at least 110 feet?$q$, $q$["3","0.5","1.75","2.5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Set h(t) = 110: −16t² + 56t + 86 = 110, so −16t² + 56t − 24 = 0. Dividing by −16 gives t² − 3.5t + 1.5 = 0, so t = 0.5 or t = 3. The object is at or above 110 feet between these times, for 3 − 0.5 = 2.5 seconds.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h166$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The value of an investment increases by 44% every 2 years. Assuming the increase occurs at a constant percent rate per year, by what percent does it increase each year?$q$, $q$["22%","44%","20%","11%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the per-year growth factor is r, then r<sup>2</sup> = 1 + 0.44 = 1.44. Taking the square root gives r = 1.2, which is a 20% increase each year. Dividing 44% by 2 is a common error, because percent changes compound rather than add.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h167$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = ab<sup>x</sup>, where a and b are positive constants. If f(2) = 256 and f(4) = 4096, what is the value of f(3)?$q$, $q$["2176","1024","256","4096"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Dividing, f(4)/f(2) = b<sup>2</sup> = 16, so b = 4. Then a = f(2)/b<sup>2</sup> = 16, so f(x) = 16(4)<sup>x</sup> and f(3) = 16(4)<sup>3</sup> = 1024.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h168$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + 13x + c = 0<br><br>In the given equation, c is a constant. The equation has two real solutions, and the positive difference between them is 5. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["36","41","−36","37"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The solutions add to −13 (the opposite of the x-coefficient) and differ by 5. So they are (−13 + 5)/2 = −4 and (−13 − 5)/2 = −9. Their product is c = (−4)(−9) = 36.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h169$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$4x² + kx + 9 = 0<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant. For how many positive integer values of k does the equation have no real solutions?$q$, $q$["23","12","10","11"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A quadratic has no real solutions when its discriminant is negative: k² − 4(4)(9) < 0, so k² < 144. The positive integers with k² < 144 are 1 through 11, so there are 11 values.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h170$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 4, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to <sup>5</sup>√(x<sup>4</sup>) ÷ ∜(x<sup>2</sup>), where x > 0?$q$, $q$["x<sup>13/10</sup>","x<sup>2/5</sup>","x<sup>3/10</sup>","x<sup>2</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each radical as a rational exponent: <sup>5</sup>√(x<sup>4</sup>) = x<sup>4/5</sup> and ∜(x<sup>2</sup>) = x<sup>1/2</sup>. Dividing powers with the same base subtracts exponents: 4/5 − 1/2 = 3/10, so the expression equals x<sup>3/10</sup>.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h171$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$(x² + kx − 2)/(x + 1) = x − 2<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant, and the equation is true for all x ≠ −1. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["3","−1","−3","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiplying both sides by x + 1 gives x² + kx − 2 = (x + 1)(x − 2) = x² − x − 2. Matching x-coefficients gives k = −1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h172$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is a quadratic function whose graph in the xy-plane has its vertex at x = 4. If f(0) = −30 and f(1) = −16, what is the value of f(3)?$q$, $q$["0","2","−2","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Write f(x) = a(x − 4)² + k. From f(0): a(16) + k = −30. From f(1): a(9) + k = −16. Subtracting gives 7a = −14, so a = −2 and k = 2. Then f(3) = −2(−1)² + 2 = 0.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h173$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 4, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + ax² + 2x + b<br><br>In the polynomial p, a and b are constants. When p(x) is divided by x − 2, the remainder is 33, and p(−1) = 6. What is the value of ab?$q$, $q$["9","−20","4","20"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$By the remainder theorem, p(2) = 33: 8 + 4a + 4 + b = 33, so 4a + b = 21. Also p(−1) = 6: a + b = 9. Subtracting, 3a = 12, so a = 4 and b = 5. Thus ab = 20.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h174$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = x² + 9 and g(x) = 3x + k, where k is a positive constant. If f(g(3)) = 265, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−25","16","7","256"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$g(3) = 9 + k, so f(g(3)) = (9 + k)² + 9 = 265. Then (9 + k)² = 256, so 9 + k = 16 or 9 + k = −16. These give k = 7 or k = −25. Since k is positive, k = 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h175$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 11x + 24. The function g is defined by g(x) = f(x − 3). What is the sum of the zeros of g?$q$, $q$["11","17","5","−17"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(x) = (x − 3)(x − 8), so the zeros of f are 3 and 8. g(x) = 0 when x − 3 equals a zero of f, so x = 3 + 3 = 6 or x = 8 + 3 = 11. The sum is 17.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h176$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$If 2<sup>x + 1</sup> − 2<sup>x − 1</sup> = 12, what is the value of 2<sup>x + 1</sup>?$q$, $q$["16","3","8","17"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Factor out the smaller power, 2<sup>x − 1</sup>: 2<sup>x − 1</sup>(2<sup>2</sup> − 1) = 12, so 2<sup>x − 1</sup>(3) = 12 and 2<sup>x − 1</sup> = 4. Then x − 1 = 2, so x = 3, and 2<sup>x + 1</sup> = 16.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h177$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 4, null, $q$3x² + bx + c ≤ 0<br><br>In the given inequality, b and c are constants. The solution set of the inequality is −7 ≤ x ≤ −2. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["−42","14","45","42"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The solution set is the interval between the zeros, so the zeros are −7 and −2, and the expression equals 3(x + 7)(x + 2) (the parabola opens upward, so it is ≤ 0 between its zeros). Expanding gives 3x² + 27x + 42, so b = 27 and c = 42.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h179$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = x² − 2x + 10<br>y = 5x − 2<br><br>The graphs of the given equations in the xy-plane intersect at the points (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂). What is the value of y₁ + y₂?$q$, $q$["7","31","5","−31"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Set the expressions equal: x² − 2x + 10 = 5x − 2, so x² − 7x + 12 = 0, which factors as (x − 4)(x − 3) = 0. So x₁ and x₂ are 4 and 3, and the corresponding y-values on the line are 5(4) − 2 = 18 and 5(3) − 2 = 13. The sum is 31.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h180$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (4x − 9)(−2x + 2) − 4(x² + 4) is equivalent to ax² + bx + c, where a, b, and c are constants. What is the value of a + b + c?$q$, $q$["−20","12","0","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A quick route: a + b + c is the value of the expression at x = 1. That gives (−5)(0) − 4(1 + 4) = 0 − 20 = −20. (Expanding fully gives −12x² + 26x − 34.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h181$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = 2x² − 8x − 1<br>y = 4x + k<br><br>In the given system of equations, k is a constant. If the system has exactly one real solution, what is the value of x in that solution?$q$, $q$["−3","6","4","3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Setting the expressions equal: 2x² − 8x − 1 = 4x + k, so 2x² − 12x − 1 − k = 0. Exactly one solution means the discriminant is 0: (−12)² − 4(2)(−1 − k) = 0, which gives k = −19. The repeated solution is x = −(−12)/(2·2) = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h182$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$√(6x + 28) = x + 2<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−4","2","6","−6"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Squaring both sides gives 6x + 28 = x² + 4x + 4, so x² − 2x − 24 = 0, which factors as (x − 6)(x + 4) = 0. Squaring can introduce extraneous solutions, so check both: the right side x + 2 must be nonnegative. For x = −4, the right side is −2 < 0, so x = −4 is extraneous. Only x = 6 works, so the sum of all solutions is 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h183$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$5<sup>2x</sup> − 6(5<sup>x</sup>) + 5 = 0<br><br>What is the sum of the solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["6","1","0","5"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let u = 5<sup>x</sup>, so 5<sup>2x</sup> = u². The equation becomes u² − 6u + 5 = 0, which factors as (u − 1)(u − 5) = 0. So 5<sup>x</sup> = 1 or 5<sup>x</sup> = 5, giving x = 0 or x = 1. The sum is 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h184$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, null, $q$p(x) = x³ − 2x² + bx + c<br><br>In the given polynomial function, b and c are constants. If −3 and 4 are zeros of p, what is the third zero of p?$q$, $q$["1","−1","3","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a cubic x³ + ax² + bx + c, the sum of the zeros is −a. Here −a = 2, so −3 + 4 + r = 2, giving r = 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h185$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$An object is launched upward from a platform. Its height h, in feet, t seconds after launch is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 48t + 50. For how many seconds is the object at a height of at least 70 feet?$q$, $q$["2.5","0.5","1.5","2"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Set h(t) = 70: −16t² + 48t + 50 = 70, so −16t² + 48t − 20 = 0. Dividing by −16 gives t² − 3t + 1.25 = 0, so t = 0.5 or t = 2.5. The object is at or above 70 feet between these times, for 2.5 − 0.5 = 2 seconds.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h186$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The amount of a medication in a patient's bloodstream decreases by 48.8% every 3 months. Assuming the decrease occurs at a constant percent rate per month, by what percent does it decrease each month?$q$, $q$["16.27%","48.8%","20%","8%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the per-month growth factor is r, then r<sup>3</sup> = 1 − 0.488 = 0.512. Taking the cube root gives r = 0.8, which is a 20% decrease each month. Dividing 48.8% by 3 is a common error, because percent changes compound rather than add.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h187$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = ab<sup>x</sup>, where a and b are positive constants. If f(3) = 3456 and f(5) = 55296, what is the value of f(4)?$q$, $q$["29376","13824","3456","55296"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Dividing, f(5)/f(3) = b<sup>2</sup> = 16, so b = 4. Then a = f(3)/b<sup>3</sup> = 54, so f(x) = 54(4)<sup>x</sup> and f(4) = 54(4)<sup>4</sup> = 13824.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h188$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² − 13x + c = 0<br><br>In the given equation, c is a constant. The equation has two real solutions, and the positive difference between them is 1. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["42","43","−42","41"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The solutions add to 13 (the opposite of the x-coefficient) and differ by 1. So they are (13 + 1)/2 = 7 and (13 − 1)/2 = 6. Their product is c = (7)(6) = 42.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h189$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$2x² + kx + 1 = 0<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant. For how many positive integer values of k does the equation have no real solutions?$q$, $q$["5","3","1","2"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A quadratic has no real solutions when its discriminant is negative: k² − 4(2)(1) < 0, so k² < 8. The positive integers with k² < 8 are 1 through 2, so there are 2 values.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h190$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 3, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to √(x<sup>7</sup>) · ∛(x<sup>5</sup>), where x > 0?$q$, $q$["x<sup>11/6</sup>","x<sup>35/6</sup>","x<sup>31/6</sup>","x<sup>12/5</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each radical as a rational exponent: √(x<sup>7</sup>) = x<sup>7/2</sup> and ∛(x<sup>5</sup>) = x<sup>5/3</sup>. Multiplying powers with the same base adds exponents: 7/2 + 5/3 = 31/6, so the expression equals x<sup>31/6</sup>.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h191$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (x² − 2x − 37)/(x − 8) is equivalent to x + 6 + R/(x − 8), where R is a constant and x ≠ 8. What is the value of R?$q$, $q$["−37","11","−11","−48"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiply both sides by (x − 8): x² − 2x − 37 = (x − 8)(x + 6) + R = x² − 2x − 48 + R. Matching constants: −37 = −48 + R, so R = 11. (Equivalently, by the remainder theorem, R equals the numerator evaluated at x = 8.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h192$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is a quadratic function whose graph in the xy-plane has its vertex at x = 1. If f(0) = 20 and f(0) = 20, what is the value of f(−3)?$q$, $q$["65","17","68","−65"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Write f(x) = a(x − 1)² + k. From f(0): a(1) + k = 20. From f(0): a(1) + k = 20. Subtracting gives 0a = 0, so a = 3 and k = 17. Then f(−3) = 3(−4)² + 17 = 65.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h193$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 4, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + ax² − 6x + b<br><br>In the polynomial p, a and b are constants. When p(x) is divided by x − 2, the remainder is −26, and p(−3) = −61. What is the value of ab?$q$, $q$["−4","12","−6","−12"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$By the remainder theorem, p(2) = −26: 8 + 4a − 12 + b = −26, so 4a + b = −22. Also p(−3) = −61: 9a + b = −52. Subtracting, −5a = 30, so a = −6 and b = 2. Thus ab = −12.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h194$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = x² + 9 and g(x) = 2x + k, where k is a positive constant. If f(g(3)) = 153, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−18","12","6","144"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$g(3) = 6 + k, so f(g(3)) = (6 + k)² + 9 = 153. Then (6 + k)² = 144, so 6 + k = 12 or 6 + k = −12. These give k = 6 or k = −18. Since k is positive, k = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h195$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 9x + 14. The function g is defined by g(x) = f(x − 1). What is the product of the zeros of g?$q$, $q$["14","24","6","11"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(x) = (x − 2)(x − 7), so the zeros of f are 2 and 7. g(x) = 0 when x − 1 equals a zero of f, so x = 2 + 1 = 3 or x = 7 + 1 = 8. The product is 24.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h196$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$If 2<sup>x + 2</sup> − 2<sup>x − 1</sup> = 28, what is the value of 2<sup>x + 1</sup>?$q$, $q$["16","3","8","17"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Factor out the smaller power, 2<sup>x − 1</sup>: 2<sup>x − 1</sup>(2<sup>3</sup> − 1) = 28, so 2<sup>x − 1</sup>(7) = 28 and 2<sup>x − 1</sup> = 4. Then x − 1 = 2, so x = 3, and 2<sup>x + 1</sup> = 16.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h197$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 4, null, $q$−2x² + bx + c ≥ 0<br><br>In the given inequality, b and c are constants. The solution set of the inequality is 3 ≤ x ≤ 10. What is the value of b?$q$, $q$["−26","13","−13","26"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The solution set is the interval between the zeros, so the zeros are 3 and 10, and the expression equals −2(x − 3)(x − 10) (the parabola opens downward, so it is ≥ 0 between its zeros). Expanding gives −2x² + 26x − 60, so b = 26 and c = −60.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h198$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The number of cells in a culture is halved every 6 hours. At time t = 0, there are 960 cells. Which function gives the number of cells, N(t), t minutes after time t = 0?$q$, $q$["960(1/2)<sup>t/6</sup>","960(1/2)<sup>360t</sup>","960(1/2)<sup>t/360</sup>","960(1/2)<sup>6t</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The quantity is multiplied by 1/2 once every 6 hours. Since t is measured in minutes, 6 hours is 360 minutes, so the number of periods after t minutes is t/360. Therefore N(t) = 960(1/2)<sup>t/360</sup>. Using t/6 would treat t as hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h199$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = x² + 4x + 4<br>y = 11x − 8<br><br>The graphs of the given equations in the xy-plane intersect at the points (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂). What is the value of y₁ + y₂?$q$, $q$["7","61","11","−61"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Set the expressions equal: x² + 4x + 4 = 11x − 8, so x² − 7x + 12 = 0, which factors as (x − 4)(x − 3) = 0. So x₁ and x₂ are 4 and 3, and the corresponding y-values on the line are 11(4) − 8 = 36 and 11(3) − 8 = 25. The sum is 61.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h200$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (x − 1)(−2x + 9) − 2(x² − 5) is equivalent to ax² + bx + c, where a, b, and c are constants. What is the value of a + b + c?$q$, $q$["8","−12","0","−8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A quick route: a + b + c is the value of the expression at x = 1. That gives (0)(7) − 2(1 − 5) = 0 + 8 = 8. (Expanding fully gives −4x² + 11x + 1.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h201$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = 3x² − 27x + 3<br>y = −3x + k<br><br>In the given system of equations, k is a constant. If the system has exactly one real solution, what is the value of x in that solution?$q$, $q$["−4","8","5","4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Setting the expressions equal: 3x² − 27x + 3 = −3x + k, so 3x² − 24x + 3 − k = 0. Exactly one solution means the discriminant is 0: (−24)² − 4(3)(3 − k) = 0, which gives k = −45. The repeated solution is x = −(−24)/(2·3) = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h202$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$√(2x + 36) = x − 6<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["0","−14","14","15"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Squaring both sides gives 2x + 36 = x² − 12x + 36, so x² − 14x = 0, which factors as (x − 14)x = 0. Squaring can introduce extraneous solutions, so check both: the right side x − 6 must be nonnegative. For x = 0, the right side is −6 < 0, so x = 0 is extraneous. Only x = 14 works, so the sum of all solutions is 14.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h203$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$3<sup>2x</sup> − 4(3<sup>x</sup>) + 3 = 0<br><br>What is the sum of the solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["4","1","0","3"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let u = 3<sup>x</sup>, so 3<sup>2x</sup> = u². The equation becomes u² − 4u + 3 = 0, which factors as (u − 1)(u − 3) = 0. So 3<sup>x</sup> = 1 or 3<sup>x</sup> = 3, giving x = 0 or x = 1. The sum is 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h204$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + x² + bx + c<br><br>In the given polynomial function, b and c are constants. If 5 and −2 are zeros of p, what is the third zero of p?$q$, $q$["−4","4","−2","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a cubic x³ + ax² + bx + c, the sum of the zeros is −a. Here −a = −1, so 5 + (−2) + r = −1, giving r = −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h205$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$An object is launched upward from a platform. Its height h, in feet, t seconds after launch is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 96t + 22. For how many seconds is the object at a height of at least 130 feet?$q$, $q$["4.5","1.5","4","3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Set h(t) = 130: −16t² + 96t + 22 = 130, so −16t² + 96t − 108 = 0. Dividing by −16 gives t² − 6t + 6.75 = 0, so t = 1.5 or t = 4.5. The object is at or above 130 feet between these times, for 4.5 − 1.5 = 3 seconds.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h206$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The mass of a radioactive sample decreases by 48.8% every 3 months. Assuming the decrease occurs at a constant percent rate per month, by what percent does it decrease each month?$q$, $q$["16.27%","48.8%","20%","8%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the per-month growth factor is r, then r<sup>3</sup> = 1 − 0.488 = 0.512. Taking the cube root gives r = 0.8, which is a 20% decrease each month. Dividing 48.8% by 3 is a common error, because percent changes compound rather than add.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h207$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = ab<sup>x</sup>, where a and b are positive constants. If f(3) = 2048 and f(6) = 131072, what is the value of f(4)?$q$, $q$["45056","8192","2048","32768"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Dividing, f(6)/f(3) = b<sup>3</sup> = 64, so b = 4. Then a = f(3)/b<sup>3</sup> = 32, so f(x) = 32(4)<sup>x</sup> and f(4) = 32(4)<sup>4</sup> = 8192.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h208$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + 4x + c = 0<br><br>In the given equation, c is a constant. The equation has two real solutions, and the positive difference between them is 6. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["−5","5","4","1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The solutions add to −4 (the opposite of the x-coefficient) and differ by 6. So they are (−4 + 6)/2 = 1 and (−4 − 6)/2 = −5. Their product is c = (1)(−5) = −5.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h209$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$2x² + kx + 2 = 0<br><br>In the given equation, k is an integer constant. For how many values of k does the equation have no real solutions?$q$, $q$["3","9","6","7"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$No real solutions means the discriminant is negative: k² − 4(2)(2) < 0, so k² < 16. The integers satisfying this are −3 through 3, including 0, which is 7 values.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h210$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 4, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to <sup>5</sup>√(x<sup>4</sup>) ÷ √(x), where x > 0?$q$, $q$["x<sup>13/10</sup>","x<sup>2/5</sup>","x<sup>3/10</sup>","x<sup>1</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each radical as a rational exponent: <sup>5</sup>√(x<sup>4</sup>) = x<sup>4/5</sup> and √(x) = x<sup>1/2</sup>. Dividing powers with the same base subtracts exponents: 4/5 − 1/2 = 3/10, so the expression equals x<sup>3/10</sup>.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h211$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (x² − 13x + 52)/(x − 5) is equivalent to x − 8 + R/(x − 5), where R is a constant and x ≠ 5. What is the value of R?$q$, $q$["52","12","−12","40"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiply both sides by (x − 5): x² − 13x + 52 = (x − 5)(x − 8) + R = x² − 13x + 40 + R. Matching constants: 52 = 40 + R, so R = 12. (Equivalently, by the remainder theorem, R equals the numerator evaluated at x = 5.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h212$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is a quadratic function whose graph in the xy-plane has its vertex at x = 5. If f(0) = 18 and f(8) = 2, what is the value of f(6)?$q$, $q$["−6","−7","−5","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Write f(x) = a(x − 5)² + k. From f(0): a(25) + k = 18. From f(8): a(9) + k = 2. Subtracting gives 16a = 16, so a = 1 and k = −7. Then f(6) = 1(1)² − 7 = −6.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h213$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 4, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + ax² − x + b<br><br>In the polynomial p, a and b are constants. When p(x) is divided by x + 1, the remainder is −7, and p(3) = 25. What is the value of ab?$q$, $q$["−7","8","1","−8"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$By the remainder theorem, p(−1) = −7: −1 + a + 1 + b = −7, so a + b = −7. Also p(3) = 25: 9a + b = 1. Subtracting, −8a = −8, so a = 1 and b = −8. Thus ab = −8.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h214$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = x² + 6 and g(x) = 3x + k, where k is a positive constant. If f(g(3)) = 150, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−21","12","3","144"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$g(3) = 9 + k, so f(g(3)) = (9 + k)² + 6 = 150. Then (9 + k)² = 144, so 9 + k = 12 or 9 + k = −12. These give k = 3 or k = −21. Since k is positive, k = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h215$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 49. The function g is defined by g(x) = f(x + 3). What is the product of the zeros of g?$q$, $q$["−49","−40","−6","−37"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(x) = (x − 7)(x + 7), so the zeros of f are 7 and −7. g(x) = 0 when x + 3 equals a zero of f, so x = 7 − 3 = 4 or x = −7 − 3 = −10. The product is −40.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h216$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$If 3<sup>x + 2</sup> − 3<sup>x − 2</sup> = 2160, what is the value of 9<sup>x</sup>?$q$, $q$["59049","5","243","19683"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Factor out the smaller power, 3<sup>x − 2</sup>: 3<sup>x − 2</sup>(3<sup>4</sup> − 1) = 2160, so 3<sup>x − 2</sup>(80) = 2160 and 3<sup>x − 2</sup> = 27. Then x − 2 = 3, so x = 5, and 9<sup>x</sup> = 59049.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h217$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 4, null, $q$3x² + bx + c ≤ 0<br><br>In the given inequality, b and c are constants. The solution set of the inequality is −6 ≤ x ≤ 4. What is the value of b+c?$q$, $q$["78","−26","−24","−66"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The solution set is the interval between the zeros, so the zeros are −6 and 4, and the expression equals 3(x + 6)(x − 4) (the parabola opens upward, so it is ≤ 0 between its zeros). Expanding gives 3x² + 6x − 72, so b = 6 and c = −72, and b + c = −66.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h219$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = 2x² − 6x + 3<br>y = −10x + 9<br><br>The graphs of the given equations in the xy-plane intersect at the points (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂). What is the value of x₁ + x₂?$q$, $q$["2","−2","−3","−5"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Set the expressions equal: 2x² − 6x + 3 = −10x + 9, so 2x² + 4x − 6 = 0, which factors as 2(x − 1)(x + 3) = 0. So x₁ and x₂ are 1 and −3, with sum −2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h220$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (4x − 7)(−x + 1) − 5(x² + 2) is equivalent to ax² + bx + c, where a, b, and c are constants. What is the value of a + b + c?$q$, $q$["−15","5","0","15"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A quick route: a + b + c is the value of the expression at x = 1. That gives (−3)(0) − 5(1 + 2) = 0 − 15 = −15. (Expanding fully gives −9x² + 11x − 17.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h221$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = −2x² − 9x − 3<br>y = −5x + k<br><br>In the given system of equations, k is a constant. If the system has exactly one real solution, what is the value of x in that solution?$q$, $q$["1","−2","0","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Setting the expressions equal: −2x² − 9x − 3 = −5x + k, so −2x² − 4x − 3 − k = 0. Exactly one solution means the discriminant is 0: (−4)² − 4(−2)(−3 − k) = 0, which gives k = −1. The repeated solution is x = −(−4)/(2·(−2)) = −1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h222$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$√(5x + 4) = x − 4<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["1","13","12","−12"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Squaring both sides gives 5x + 4 = x² − 8x + 16, so x² − 13x + 12 = 0, which factors as (x − 12)(x − 1) = 0. Squaring can introduce extraneous solutions, so check both: the right side x − 4 must be nonnegative. For x = 1, the right side is −3 < 0, so x = 1 is extraneous. Only x = 12 works, so the sum of all solutions is 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h223$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$2<sup>2x</sup> − 24(2<sup>x</sup>) + 128 = 0<br><br>What is the greatest solution to the given equation?$q$, $q$["24","4","7","16"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let u = 2<sup>x</sup>, so 2<sup>2x</sup> = u². The equation becomes u² − 24u + 128 = 0, which factors as (u − 8)(u − 16) = 0. So 2<sup>x</sup> = 8 or 2<sup>x</sup> = 16, giving x = 3 or x = 4. The greatest solution is 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h224$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, null, $q$p(x) = x³ − x² + bx + c<br><br>In the given polynomial function, b and c are constants. If 1 and −5 are zeros of p, what is the third zero of p?$q$, $q$["5","−5","7","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a cubic x³ + ax² + bx + c, the sum of the zeros is −a. Here −a = 1, so 1 + (−5) + r = 1, giving r = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h225$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$An object is launched upward from a platform. Its height h, in feet, t seconds after launch is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 80t + 24. For how many seconds is the object at a height of at least 120 feet?$q$, $q$["3","2","2.5","1"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Set h(t) = 120: −16t² + 80t + 24 = 120, so −16t² + 80t − 96 = 0. Dividing by −16 gives t² − 5t + 6 = 0, so t = 2 or t = 3. The object is at or above 120 feet between these times, for 3 − 2 = 1 seconds.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h226$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$A population of bacteria increases by 56.25% every 2 hours. Assuming the increase occurs at a constant percent rate per hour, by what percent does it increase each hour?$q$, $q$["28.13%","56.25%","25%","14%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the per-hour growth factor is r, then r<sup>2</sup> = 1 + 0.5625 = 1.5625. Taking the square root gives r = 1.25, which is a 25% increase each hour. Dividing 56.25% by 2 is a common error, because percent changes compound rather than add.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h227$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = ab<sup>x</sup>, where a and b are positive constants. If f(3) = 384 and f(5) = 1536, what is the value of f(4)?$q$, $q$["960","768","384","1536"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Dividing, f(5)/f(3) = b<sup>2</sup> = 4, so b = 2. Then a = f(3)/b<sup>3</sup> = 48, so f(x) = 48(2)<sup>x</sup> and f(4) = 48(2)<sup>4</sup> = 768.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h228$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² − 14x + c = 0<br><br>In the given equation, c is a constant. The equation has two real solutions, and the positive difference between them is 6. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["40","−40","49","46"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The solutions add to 14 (the opposite of the x-coefficient) and differ by 6. So they are (14 + 6)/2 = 10 and (14 − 6)/2 = 4. Their product is c = (10)(4) = 40.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h229$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + kx + 1 = 0<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant. For how many positive integer values of k does the equation have no real solutions?$q$, $q$["3","2","0","1"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A quadratic has no real solutions when its discriminant is negative: k² − 4(1)(1) < 0, so k² < 4. The positive integers with k² < 4 are 1 through 1, so there are 1 values.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h230$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 3, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to ∜(x<sup>3</sup>) · √(x<sup>3</sup>), where x > 0?$q$, $q$["x<sup>−3/4</sup>","x<sup>9/8</sup>","x<sup>9/4</sup>","x<sup>1</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each radical as a rational exponent: ∜(x<sup>3</sup>) = x<sup>3/4</sup> and √(x<sup>3</sup>) = x<sup>3/2</sup>. Multiplying powers with the same base adds exponents: 3/4 + 3/2 = 9/4, so the expression equals x<sup>9/4</sup>.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h231$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$(x² + kx + 45)/(x − 9) = x − 5<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant, and the equation is true for all x ≠ 9. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−4","−14","4","45"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiplying both sides by x − 9 gives x² + kx + 45 = (x − 9)(x − 5) = x² − 14x + 45. Matching x-coefficients gives k = −14.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h232$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is a quadratic function whose graph in the xy-plane has its vertex at x = −1. If f(0) = 0 and f(−2) = 0, what is the value of f(−6)?$q$, $q$["−48","2","−50","48"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Write f(x) = a(x − (−1))² + k. From f(0): a(1) + k = 0. From f(−2): a(1) + k = 0. Subtracting gives 0a = 0, so a = −2 and k = 2. Then f(−6) = −2(−5)² + 2 = −48.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h233$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 4, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + ax² + 2x + b<br><br>In the polynomial p, a and b are constants. When p(x) is divided by x − 3, the remainder is 79, and p(1) = 1. What is the value of a + b?$q$, $q$["−48","2","6","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$By the remainder theorem, p(3) = 79: 27 + 9a + 6 + b = 79, so 9a + b = 46. Also p(1) = 1: a + b = −2. Subtracting, 8a = 48, so a = 6 and b = −8. Thus a + b = −2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h234$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = x² + 6 and g(x) = 4x + k, where k is a positive constant. If f(g(1)) = 31, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−9","5","1","25"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$g(1) = 4 + k, so f(g(1)) = (4 + k)² + 6 = 31. Then (4 + k)² = 25, so 4 + k = 5 or 4 + k = −5. These give k = 1 or k = −9. Since k is positive, k = 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h235$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 3x − 28. The function g is defined by g(x) = f(x + 5). What is the sum of the zeros of g?$q$, $q$["−3","−13","7","13"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(x) = (x − 4)(x + 7), so the zeros of f are 4 and −7. g(x) = 0 when x + 5 equals a zero of f, so x = 4 − 5 = −1 or x = −7 − 5 = −12. The sum is −13.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h236$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$If 2<sup>x + 1</sup> − 2<sup>x − 1</sup> = 12, what is the value of 4<sup>x</sup>?$q$, $q$["64","3","8","32"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Factor out the smaller power, 2<sup>x − 1</sup>: 2<sup>x − 1</sup>(2<sup>2</sup> − 1) = 12, so 2<sup>x − 1</sup>(3) = 12 and 2<sup>x − 1</sup> = 4. Then x − 1 = 2, so x = 3, and 4<sup>x</sup> = 64.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h237$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 4, null, $q$−x² + bx + c ≥ 0<br><br>In the given inequality, b and c are constants. The solution set of the inequality is −2 ≤ x ≤ 7. What is the value of b+c?$q$, $q$["−9","−14","18","19"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The solution set is the interval between the zeros, so the zeros are −2 and 7, and the expression equals −1(x + 2)(x − 7) (the parabola opens downward, so it is ≥ 0 between its zeros). Expanding gives −x² + 5x + 14, so b = 5 and c = 14, and b + c = 19.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h239$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = 2x² + 5x − 9<br>y = 7x + 51<br><br>The graphs of the given equations in the xy-plane intersect at the points (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂). What is the value of x₁x₂?$q$, $q$["30","−30","−9/2","51/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Set the expressions equal: 2x² + 5x − 9 = 7x + 51, so 2x² − 2x − 60 = 0, which factors as 2(x − 6)(x + 5) = 0. So x₁ and x₂ are 6 and −5, with product −30.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h240$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (4x − 6)(3x − 8) − 4(x² − 7) is equivalent to ax² + bx + c, where a, b, and c are constants. What is the value of a + b + c?$q$, $q$["34","−22","10","−14"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A quick route: a + b + c is the value of the expression at x = 1. That gives (−2)(−5) − 4(1 − 7) = 10 + 24 = 34. (Expanding fully gives 8x² − 50x + 76.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h241$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = −2x² + 2x + 8<br>y = −2x + k<br><br>In the given system of equations, k is a constant. If the system has exactly one real solution, what is the value of x in that solution?$q$, $q$["−1","2","10","1"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Setting the expressions equal: −2x² + 2x + 8 = −2x + k, so −2x² + 4x + 8 − k = 0. Exactly one solution means the discriminant is 0: (4)² − 4(−2)(8 − k) = 0, which gives k = 10. The repeated solution is x = −(4)/(2·(−2)) = 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h242$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$√(4x + 29) = x + 2<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−5","0","5","10"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Squaring both sides gives 4x + 29 = x² + 4x + 4, so x² − 25 = 0, which factors as (x − 5)(x + 5) = 0. Squaring can introduce extraneous solutions, so check both: the right side x + 2 must be nonnegative. For x = −5, the right side is −3 < 0, so x = −5 is extraneous. Only x = 5 works, so the sum of all solutions is 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h243$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$5<sup>2x</sup> − 26(5<sup>x</sup>) + 25 = 0<br><br>What is the product of the solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["26","0","2","25"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let u = 5<sup>x</sup>, so 5<sup>2x</sup> = u². The equation becomes u² − 26u + 25 = 0, which factors as (u − 1)(u − 25) = 0. So 5<sup>x</sup> = 1 or 5<sup>x</sup> = 25, giving x = 0 or x = 2. The product is 0.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h244$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + ax² + bx + 15<br><br>In the given polynomial function, a and b are constants. If −1 and 5 are zeros of p, what is the third zero of p?$q$, $q$["3","−3","15","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a cubic x³ + ax² + bx + d, the product of the three zeros is −d. Here −d = −15, so (−1)(5)r = −15, giving −5r = −15 and r = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h245$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$An object is launched upward from a platform. Its height h, in feet, t seconds after launch is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 64t + 12. For how many seconds is the object at a height of at least 40 feet?$q$, $q$["3.5","0.5","2","3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Set h(t) = 40: −16t² + 64t + 12 = 40, so −16t² + 64t − 28 = 0. Dividing by −16 gives t² − 4t + 1.75 = 0, so t = 0.5 or t = 3.5. The object is at or above 40 feet between these times, for 3.5 − 0.5 = 3 seconds.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h246$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The number of active users of an app increases by 33.1% every 3 years. Assuming the increase occurs at a constant percent rate per year, by what percent does it increase each year?$q$, $q$["11.03%","33.1%","10%","6%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the per-year growth factor is r, then r<sup>3</sup> = 1 + 0.331 = 1.331. Taking the cube root gives r = 1.1, which is a 10% increase each year. Dividing 33.1% by 3 is a common error, because percent changes compound rather than add.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h247$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = ab<sup>x</sup>, where a and b are positive constants. If f(2) = 20 and f(5) = 160, what is the value of f(7)?$q$, $q$["760/3","640","320","1280"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Dividing, f(5)/f(2) = b<sup>3</sup> = 8, so b = 2. Then a = f(2)/b<sup>2</sup> = 5, so f(x) = 5(2)<sup>x</sup> and f(7) = 5(2)<sup>7</sup> = 640.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h248$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² − 11x + c = 0<br><br>In the given equation, c is a constant. The equation has two real solutions, and the positive difference between them is 3. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["28","−28","31","29"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The solutions add to 11 (the opposite of the x-coefficient) and differ by 3. So they are (11 + 3)/2 = 7 and (11 − 3)/2 = 4. Their product is c = (7)(4) = 28.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h249$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$2x² + kx + 3 = 0<br><br>In the given equation, k is an integer constant. For how many values of k does the equation have no real solutions?$q$, $q$["4","11","8","9"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$No real solutions means the discriminant is negative: k² − 4(2)(3) < 0, so k² < 24. The integers satisfying this are −4 through 4, including 0, which is 9 values.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h250$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 4, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to ∜(x<sup>7</sup>) · ∛(x<sup>5</sup>), where x > 0?$q$, $q$["x<sup>1/12</sup>","x<sup>35/12</sup>","x<sup>41/12</sup>","x<sup>12/7</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each radical as a rational exponent: ∜(x<sup>7</sup>) = x<sup>7/4</sup> and ∛(x<sup>5</sup>) = x<sup>5/3</sup>. Multiplying powers with the same base adds exponents: 7/4 + 5/3 = 41/12, so the expression equals x<sup>41/12</sup>.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h251$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$(x² + kx − 18)/(x − 3) = x + 6<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant, and the equation is true for all x ≠ 3. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−9","3","9","−18"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiplying both sides by x − 3 gives x² + kx − 18 = (x − 3)(x + 6) = x² + 3x − 18. Matching x-coefficients gives k = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h252$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is a quadratic function whose graph in the xy-plane has its vertex at x = 6. If f(0) = −137 and f(9) = −29, what is the value of f(4)?$q$, $q$["−9","7","−13","9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Write f(x) = a(x − 6)² + k. From f(0): a(36) + k = −137. From f(9): a(9) + k = −29. Subtracting gives 27a = −108, so a = −4 and k = 7. Then f(4) = −4(−2)² + 7 = −9.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h253$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 4, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + ax² − 5x + b<br><br>In the polynomial p, a and b are constants. When p(x) is divided by x + 2, the remainder is 26, and p(−3) = 37. What is the value of a + b?$q$, $q$["20","−9","5","9"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$By the remainder theorem, p(−2) = 26: −8 + 4a + 10 + b = 26, so 4a + b = 24. Also p(−3) = 37: 9a + b = 49. Subtracting, −5a = −25, so a = 5 and b = 4. Thus a + b = 9.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h254$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = x² − 3 and g(x) = 4x + k, where k is a positive constant. If f(g(1)) = 141, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−16","12","8","144"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$g(1) = 4 + k, so f(g(1)) = (4 + k)² − 3 = 141. Then (4 + k)² = 144, so 4 + k = 12 or 4 + k = −12. These give k = 8 or k = −16. Since k is positive, k = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h255$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 3x + 2. The function g is defined by g(x) = f(x + 4). What is the product of the zeros of g?$q$, $q$["2","30","6","−11"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(x) = (x + 2)(x + 1), so the zeros of f are −2 and −1. g(x) = 0 when x + 4 equals a zero of f, so x = −2 − 4 = −6 or x = −1 − 4 = −5. The product is 30.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h256$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$If 3<sup>x + 2</sup> − 3<sup>x − 1</sup> = 78, what is the value of 3<sup>x + 1</sup>?$q$, $q$["27","2","9","19"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Factor out the smaller power, 3<sup>x − 1</sup>: 3<sup>x − 1</sup>(3<sup>3</sup> − 1) = 78, so 3<sup>x − 1</sup>(26) = 78 and 3<sup>x − 1</sup> = 3. Then x − 1 = 1, so x = 2, and 3<sup>x + 1</sup> = 27.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h257$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 4, null, $q$−2x² + bx + c ≥ 0<br><br>In the given inequality, b and c are constants. The solution set of the inequality is 1 ≤ x ≤ 6. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["12","6","−14","−12"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The solution set is the interval between the zeros, so the zeros are 1 and 6, and the expression equals −2(x − 1)(x − 6) (the parabola opens downward, so it is ≥ 0 between its zeros). Expanding gives −2x² + 14x − 12, so b = 14 and c = −12.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h259$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = 2x² − 5x + 5<br>y = −17x − 11<br><br>The graphs of the given equations in the xy-plane intersect at the points (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂). What is the value of x₁ + x₂?$q$, $q$["6","−6","−5/2","−17/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Set the expressions equal: 2x² − 5x + 5 = −17x − 11, so 2x² + 12x + 16 = 0, which factors as 2(x + 4)(x + 2) = 0. So x₁ and x₂ are −4 and −2, with sum −6.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h260$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (4x + 3)(−x + 4) − 2(x² − 9) is equivalent to ax² + bx + c, where a, b, and c are constants. What is the value of a + b + c?$q$, $q$["37","1","21","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A quick route: a + b + c is the value of the expression at x = 1. That gives (7)(3) − 2(1 − 9) = 21 + 16 = 37. (Expanding fully gives −6x² + 13x + 30.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h261$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = 2x² + x + 1<br>y = 5x + k<br><br>In the given system of equations, k is a constant. If the system has exactly one real solution, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["1","4","0","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Setting the expressions equal: 2x² + x + 1 = 5x + k, so 2x² − 4x + 1 − k = 0. Exactly one solution means the discriminant is 0: (−4)² − 4(2)(1 − k) = 0, which gives k = −1. The repeated solution is x = −(−4)/(2·2) = 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h262$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$√(x + 23) = x + 3<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−7","−5","2","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Squaring both sides gives x + 23 = x² + 6x + 9, so x² + 5x − 14 = 0, which factors as (x − 2)(x + 7) = 0. Squaring can introduce extraneous solutions, so check both: the right side x + 3 must be nonnegative. For x = −7, the right side is −4 < 0, so x = −7 is extraneous. Only x = 2 works, so the sum of all solutions is 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h263$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$5<sup>2x</sup> − 26(5<sup>x</sup>) + 25 = 0<br><br>What is the sum of the solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["26","2","0","25"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let u = 5<sup>x</sup>, so 5<sup>2x</sup> = u². The equation becomes u² − 26u + 25 = 0, which factors as (u − 1)(u − 25) = 0. So 5<sup>x</sup> = 1 or 5<sup>x</sup> = 25, giving x = 0 or x = 2. The sum is 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h264$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + 4x² + bx + c<br><br>In the given polynomial function, b and c are constants. If 4 and −2 are zeros of p, what is the third zero of p?$q$, $q$["−6","6","−4","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a cubic x³ + ax² + bx + c, the sum of the zeros is −a. Here −a = −4, so 4 + (−2) + r = −4, giving r = −6.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h265$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$An object is launched upward from a platform. Its height h, in feet, t seconds after launch is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 72t + 74. For how many seconds is the object at a height of at least 130 feet?$q$, $q$["3.5","1","2.25","2.5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Set h(t) = 130: −16t² + 72t + 74 = 130, so −16t² + 72t − 56 = 0. Dividing by −16 gives t² − 4.5t + 3.5 = 0, so t = 1 or t = 3.5. The object is at or above 130 feet between these times, for 3.5 − 1 = 2.5 seconds.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h266$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The number of active users of an app increases by 72.8% every 3 hours. Assuming the increase occurs at a constant percent rate per hour, by what percent does it increase each hour?$q$, $q$["24.27%","72.8%","20%","12%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the per-hour growth factor is r, then r<sup>3</sup> = 1 + 0.728 = 1.728. Taking the cube root gives r = 1.2, which is a 20% increase each hour. Dividing 72.8% by 3 is a common error, because percent changes compound rather than add.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h267$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = ab<sup>x</sup>, where a and b are positive constants. If f(1) = 24 and f(3) = 96, what is the value of f(4)?$q$, $q$["132","192","96","384"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Dividing, f(3)/f(1) = b<sup>2</sup> = 4, so b = 2. Then a = f(1)/b<sup>1</sup> = 12, so f(x) = 12(2)<sup>x</sup> and f(4) = 12(2)<sup>4</sup> = 192.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h268$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² − 14x + c = 0<br><br>In the given equation, c is a constant. The equation has two real solutions, and the positive difference between them is 8. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["33","47","49","41"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The solutions add to 14 (the opposite of the x-coefficient) and differ by 8. So they are (14 + 8)/2 = 11 and (14 − 8)/2 = 3. Their product is c = (11)(3) = 33.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h269$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + kx + 6 = 0<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant. For how many positive integer values of k does the equation have no real solutions?$q$, $q$["9","5","3","4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A quadratic has no real solutions when its discriminant is negative: k² − 4(1)(6) < 0, so k² < 24. The positive integers with k² < 24 are 1 through 4, so there are 4 values.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h270$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 4, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to ∜(x<sup>5</sup>) · <sup>6</sup>√(x<sup>5</sup>), where x > 0?$q$, $q$["x<sup>5/12</sup>","x<sup>25/24</sup>","x<sup>25/12</sup>","x<sup>1</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each radical as a rational exponent: ∜(x<sup>5</sup>) = x<sup>5/4</sup> and <sup>6</sup>√(x<sup>5</sup>) = x<sup>5/6</sup>. Multiplying powers with the same base adds exponents: 5/4 + 5/6 = 25/12, so the expression equals x<sup>25/12</sup>.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h271$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$(x² + kx + 35)/(x + 7) = x + 5<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant, and the equation is true for all x ≠ −7. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["2","12","−2","35"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiplying both sides by x + 7 gives x² + kx + 35 = (x + 7)(x + 5) = x² + 12x + 35. Matching x-coefficients gives k = 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h272$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is a quadratic function whose graph in the xy-plane has its vertex at x = −1. If f(0) = 19 and f(2) = 27, what is the value of f(−5)?$q$, $q$["34","18","35","−34"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Write f(x) = a(x − (−1))² + k. From f(0): a(1) + k = 19. From f(2): a(9) + k = 27. Subtracting gives −8a = −8, so a = 1 and k = 18. Then f(−5) = 1(−4)² + 18 = 34.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h273$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 4, null, $q$p(x) = 2x³ + ax² + 5x + b<br><br>In the polynomial p, a and b are constants. When p(x) is divided by x + 2, the remainder is −60, and p(1) = −9. What is the value of ab?$q$, $q$["−16","−60","−6","60"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$By the remainder theorem, p(−2) = −60: −16 + 4a − 10 + b = −60, so 4a + b = −34. Also p(1) = −9: a + b = −16. Subtracting, 3a = −18, so a = −6 and b = −10. Thus ab = 60.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h274$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = x² − 8 and g(x) = 4x + k, where k is a positive constant. If f(g(3)) = 161, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−25","13","1","169"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$g(3) = 12 + k, so f(g(3)) = (12 + k)² − 8 = 161. Then (12 + k)² = 169, so 12 + k = 13 or 12 + k = −13. These give k = 1 or k = −25. Since k is positive, k = 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h275$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 7x + 10. The function g is defined by g(x) = f(x + 1). What is the product of the zeros of g?$q$, $q$["10","4","18","5"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(x) = (x − 2)(x − 5), so the zeros of f are 2 and 5. g(x) = 0 when x + 1 equals a zero of f, so x = 2 − 1 = 1 or x = 5 − 1 = 4. The product is 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h276$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$If 3<sup>x + 2</sup> − 3<sup>x − 1</sup> = 234, what is the value of 9<sup>x</sup>?$q$, $q$["729","3","27","243"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Factor out the smaller power, 3<sup>x − 1</sup>: 3<sup>x − 1</sup>(3<sup>3</sup> − 1) = 234, so 3<sup>x − 1</sup>(26) = 234 and 3<sup>x − 1</sup> = 9. Then x − 1 = 2, so x = 3, and 9<sup>x</sup> = 729.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h277$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 4, null, $q$2x² + bx + c ≤ 0<br><br>In the given inequality, b and c are constants. The solution set of the inequality is 3 ≤ x ≤ 13. What is the value of b+c?$q$, $q$["−110","55","39","46"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The solution set is the interval between the zeros, so the zeros are 3 and 13, and the expression equals 2(x − 3)(x − 13) (the parabola opens upward, so it is ≤ 0 between its zeros). Expanding gives 2x² − 32x + 78, so b = −32 and c = 78, and b + c = 46.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h279$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = −x² − x + 3<br>y = 6x + 13<br><br>The graphs of the given equations in the xy-plane intersect at the points (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂). What is the value of x₁x₂?$q$, $q$["−10","10","−3","−13"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Set the expressions equal: −x² − x + 3 = 6x + 13, so −x² − 7x − 10 = 0, which factors as −(x + 5)(x + 2) = 0. So x₁ and x₂ are −5 and −2, with product 10.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h280$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (5x + 9)(4x − 7) − 3(x² + 7) is equivalent to ax² + bx + c, where a, b, and c are constants. What is the value of a + b + c?$q$, $q$["−66","−24","−42","−18"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A quick route: a + b + c is the value of the expression at x = 1. That gives (14)(−3) − 3(1 + 7) = −42 − 24 = −66. (Expanding fully gives 17x² + x − 84.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h281$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = x² + 14x + 8<br>y = 6x + k<br><br>In the given system of equations, k is a constant. If the system has exactly one real solution, what is the value of x in that solution?$q$, $q$["4","−8","−3","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Setting the expressions equal: x² + 14x + 8 = 6x + k, so x² + 8x + 8 − k = 0. Exactly one solution means the discriminant is 0: (8)² − 4(1)(8 − k) = 0, which gives k = −8. The repeated solution is x = −(8)/(2·1) = −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h282$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$√(x + 4) = x + 2<br><br>What is the solution to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−3","3","0","1"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Squaring both sides gives x + 4 = x² + 4x + 4, so x² + 3x = 0, which factors as x(x + 3) = 0. Squaring can introduce extraneous solutions, so check both: the right side x + 2 must be nonnegative. For x = −3, the right side is −1 < 0, so x = −3 is extraneous. Only x = 0 works.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h283$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has x-intercepts at (1, 0) and (7, 0) and a y-intercept at (0, 14). What is the minimum value of f?$q$, $q$["14","−9","−18","4"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$From the x-intercepts, f(x) = a(x − 1)(x − 7) for some constant a. Since f(0) = 14, a(−1)(−7) = 14, so a = 2. The vertex lies halfway between the intercepts, at x = 4, so the minimum value is f(4) = 2(3)(−3) = −18. Assuming a = 1 gives −9.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h284$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = 5x + 4 and f(g(x)) = 10x² + 5x + 39. What is the value of g(−2)?$q$, $q$["69","13","349","73/5"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(g(−2)) = 10(−2)² + 5(−2) + 39 = 69. Since f(g(−2)) = 5·g(−2) + 4, 5·g(−2) = 65, so g(−2) = 13. The value 69 is f(g(−2)), not g(−2).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h285$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = a(x + 4)² − 9<br>y = 63<br><br>In the given system of equations, a is a positive constant. The graphs of the equations in the xy-plane intersect at two points that are 12 units apart. What is the value of a?$q$, $q$["1/2","2","12","7/4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The two intersection points are symmetric about the axis x = −4, so each is 12/2 = 6 units from it. At x = 2, a(6)² − 9 = 63, so 36a = 72 and a = 2. Using the full distance 12 instead of half of it gives 1/2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h286$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The power used by a heating element is directly proportional to the square of the current through it. If the current is increased by 30%, which of the following describes the change in the power?$q$, $q$["It increases by 30%.","It increases by 69%.","It increases by 60%.","It decreases by 40.83%."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The power is multiplied by (1.3)² = 1.69, which corresponds to an increase of 69%. A change of 30% treats the relationship as linear, ignoring the square.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h287$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = 3x + 3 and f(g(x)) = 3x² + 12x + 18. What is the value of g(−2)?$q$, $q$["21","3","6","1"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$f(g(−2)) = 3(−2)² + 12(−2) + 18 = 6. Since f(g(−2)) = 3·g(−2) + 3, 3·g(−2) = 3, so g(−2) = 1. The value 6 is f(g(−2)), not g(−2).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h288$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$(1/25)<sup>x − 2</sup> = 125<sup>x − 2</sup> · (1/5)<br><br>What value of x satisfies the given equation?$q$, $q$["11/5","3","2","3/5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rewrite each side as a power of 5: 1/25 = 5<sup>−2</sup>, 125 = 5<sup>3</sup>, and (1/5) = 5<sup>−1</sup>. The equation becomes 5<sup>−2(x − 2)</sup> = 5<sup>3(x − 2) + (−1)</sup>, so −2x + 4 = 3x − 7. Then 5x = 11 and x = 11/5.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h289$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = −2x − 5 and f(g(x)) = −2x² + 8x − 13. What is the value of g(−2)?$q$, $q$["21","−37","16","69"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$f(g(−2)) = −2(−2)² + 8(−2) − 13 = −37. Since f(g(−2)) = -2·g(−2) − 5, -2·g(−2) = −32, so g(−2) = 16. The value −37 is f(g(−2)), not g(−2).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h290$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = a(x − 2)² − 8<br>y = 24<br><br>In the given system of equations, a is a positive constant. The graphs of the equations in the xy-plane intersect at two points that are 8 units apart. What is the value of a?$q$, $q$["1/2","8","3/2","2"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The two intersection points are symmetric about the axis x = 2, so each is 8/2 = 4 units from it. At x = 6, a(4)² − 8 = 24, so 16a = 32 and a = 2. Using the full distance 8 instead of half of it gives 1/2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h291$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 12x + 37. The function g is defined by g(x) = −2f(x + 2) + 6. What is the maximum value of g(x)?$q$, $q$["−68","4","8","−14"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Completing the square, f(x) = (x + 6)² + 1, so f has a minimum value of 1. Multiplying by −2 turns that minimum into a maximum of −2(1) = −2, and adding 6 gives 4. The horizontal shift changes where the maximum occurs, not its value. Using the constant term 37 instead of the minimum value gives −68.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h292$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function Q(t) = 300(0.36)<sup>t/2</sup> models the amount Q of a substance, where t is the number of years since the start of the study. By what percent does Q decrease each year?$q$, $q$["64%","32%","82%","40%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Q is multiplied by 0.36 every 2 years. The one-year factor b satisfies b<sup>2</sup> = 0.36, so b = 0.6, which is a 40% decrease each year. The value 64% is the change over 2 years, and dividing it by 2 ignores compounding.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h293$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function Q(t) = 120(0.81)<sup>t/2</sup> models a quantity Q, where t is the number of days since the start of the study. By what percent does Q decrease each day?$q$, $q$["59.5%","10%","9.5%","19%"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Q is multiplied by 0.81 every 2 days. The one-day factor b satisfies b<sup>2</sup> = 0.81, so b = 0.9, which is a 10% decrease each day. The value 19% is the change over 2 days, and dividing it by 2 ignores compounding.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h294$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has x-intercepts at (−9, 0) and (−7, 0) and a y-intercept at (0, 42). What is the minimum value of f?$q$, $q$["−8","−1","−2/3","42"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$From the x-intercepts, f(x) = a(x + 9)(x + 7) for some constant a. Since f(0) = 42, a(9)(7) = 42, so a = 2/3. The vertex lies halfway between the intercepts, at x = −8, so the minimum value is f(−8) = (2/3)(1)(−1) = −2/3. Assuming a = 1 gives −1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h295$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 6x + 11. The function g is defined by g(x) = −4f(x + 1) − 1. What is the maximum value of g(x)?$q$, $q$["−45","−9","−4","7"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Completing the square, f(x) = (x − 3)² + 2, so f has a minimum value of 2. Multiplying by −4 turns that minimum into a maximum of −4(2) = −8, and adding −1 gives −9. The horizontal shift changes where the maximum occurs, not its value. Using the constant term 11 instead of the minimum value gives −45.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h296$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = 3x² − 12x − 15. In the xy-plane, the graph of y = f(x) + k, where k is a constant, has exactly one x-intercept. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["27","108","9","−27"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(x) = 3(x + 1)(x − 5), so its vertex is at x = 2, and its minimum value is 3(3)(−3) = −27. The graph of y = f(x) + k touches the x-axis exactly once when the minimum is shifted up to 0, so k = 27.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h297$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function Q(t) = 300(1.5625)<sup>t/2</sup> models the value Q of an account, where t is the number of days since the start of the study. By what percent does Q increase each day?$q$, $q$["25%","28.12%","56.25%","78.12%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Q is multiplied by 1.5625 every 2 days. The one-day factor b satisfies b<sup>2</sup> = 1.5625, so b = 1.25, which is a 25% increase each day. The value 56.25% is the change over 2 days, and dividing it by 2 ignores compounding.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h298$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = −2x − 9 and f(g(x)) = −4x² − 23. What is the value of g(−1)?$q$, $q$["45","9","−27","18"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(g(−1)) = −4(−1)² − 23 = −27. Since f(g(−1)) = -2·g(−1) − 9, -2·g(−1) = −18, so g(−1) = 9. The value −27 is f(g(−1)), not g(−1).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h299$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = 3x² − 42x + 135. In the xy-plane, the graph of y = f(x) + k, where k is a constant, has exactly one x-intercept. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−12","48","12","6"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$f(x) = 3(x − 5)(x − 9), so its vertex is at x = 7, and its minimum value is 3(2)(−2) = −12. The graph of y = f(x) + k touches the x-axis exactly once when the minimum is shifted up to 0, so k = 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h300$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has x-intercepts at (−9, 0) and (−5, 0) and a y-intercept at (0, 45). What is the minimum value of f?$q$, $q$["4","−4","−7","45"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$From the x-intercepts, f(x) = a(x + 9)(x + 5) for some constant a. Since f(0) = 45, a(9)(5) = 45, so a = 1. The vertex lies halfway between the intercepts, at x = −7, so the minimum value is f(−7) = 1(2)(−2) = −4. Assuming a = 1 gives −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h301$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + y² = 104<br>xy = 20<br><br>If x &gt; y &gt; 0, what is the value of x − y?$q$, $q$["√84","12","64","8"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$(x − y)² = x² − 2xy + y² = 104 − 2(20) = 64. Since x &gt; y, x − y is positive, so x − y = √64 = 8. The value 64 is (x − y)², not x − y, and 12 is x + y = √(104 + 2·20).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h302$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f(h) = 500(1.3)<sup>h</sup> gives the number of subscribers to a newsletter h half-years after it was first measured. Which function g gives the same quantity t years after it was first measured?$q$, $q$["g(t) = 500(1.3)<sup>t/2</sup>","g(t) = 500(2.6)<sup>t</sup>","g(t) = 500(1.69)<sup>2t</sup>","g(t) = 500(1.69)<sup>t</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Each year contains 2 half-years, so h = 2t. Then f = 500(1.3)<sup>2t</sup> = 500((1.3)²)<sup>t</sup> = 500(1.69)<sup>t</sup>. Doubling the base to 2.6 is not the same as squaring it, and the exponent t/2 uses the conversion in the wrong direction.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h303$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = a(x + 2)² − 12<br>y = 132<br><br>In the given system of equations, a is a positive constant. The graphs of the equations in the xy-plane intersect at two points that are 12 units apart. What is the value of a?$q$, $q$["4","1","24","11/3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The two intersection points are symmetric about the axis x = −2, so each is 12/2 = 6 units from it. At x = 4, a(6)² − 12 = 132, so 36a = 144 and a = 4. Using the full distance 12 instead of half of it gives 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h304$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$x/(x − 4) − 13/(x + 2) = 24/((x − 4)(x + 2))<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["11","7","4","−7"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiplying both sides by (x − 4)(x + 2) gives x(x + 2) − 13(x − 4) = 24, which simplifies to x² − 11x + 28 = 0, or (x − 4)(x − 7) = 0. The value x = 4 makes the denominator x − 4 equal 0, so it is extraneous. The only solution is 7, so the sum of all solutions is 7. Including the extraneous value gives 11.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h305$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = a(x + 1)² − 6<br>y = 42<br><br>In the given system of equations, a is a positive constant. The graphs of the equations in the xy-plane intersect at two points that are 8 units apart. What is the value of a?$q$, $q$["3","3/4","21/8","12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The two intersection points are symmetric about the axis x = −1, so each is 8/2 = 4 units from it. At x = 3, a(4)² − 6 = 42, so 16a = 48 and a = 3. Using the full distance 8 instead of half of it gives 3/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h306$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 6x − 16. In the xy-plane, the graph of y = f(x) + k, where k is a constant, has exactly one x-intercept. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["25","100","−25","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(x) = (x + 8)(x − 2), so its vertex is at x = −3, and its minimum value is (5)(−5) = −25. The graph of y = f(x) + k touches the x-axis exactly once when the minimum is shifted up to 0, so k = 25.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h307$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + y² = 85<br>xy = 42<br><br>If x &gt; y &gt; 0, what is the value of x − y?$q$, $q$["1","13","√43","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$(x − y)² = x² − 2xy + y² = 85 − 2(42) = 1. Since x &gt; y, x − y is positive, so x − y = √1 = 1. The value 1 is (x − y)², not x − y, and 13 is x + y = √(85 + 2·42).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h308$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f(h) = 400(0.9)<sup>h</sup> gives the value, in dollars, of a collectible h half-years after it was first measured. Which function g gives the same quantity t years after it was first measured?$q$, $q$["g(t) = 400(0.81)<sup>t</sup>","g(t) = 400(1.8)<sup>t</sup>","g(t) = 400(0.9)<sup>t/2</sup>","g(t) = 400(0.81)<sup>2t</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Each year contains 2 half-years, so h = 2t. Then f = 400(0.9)<sup>2t</sup> = 400((0.9)²)<sup>t</sup> = 400(0.81)<sup>t</sup>. Doubling the base to 1.8 is not the same as squaring it, and the exponent t/2 uses the conversion in the wrong direction.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h309$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 10x + 32. The function g is defined by g(x) = −3f(x − 4) − 7. What is the maximum value of g(x)?$q$, $q$["−28","14","0","−103"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Completing the square, f(x) = (x − 5)² + 7, so f has a minimum value of 7. Multiplying by −3 turns that minimum into a maximum of −3(7) = −21, and adding −7 gives −28. The horizontal shift changes where the maximum occurs, not its value. Using the constant term 32 instead of the minimum value gives −103.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h310$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$x/(x + 6) + 20/(x − 6) = 72/((x + 6)(x − 6))<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−8","−14","8","−6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying both sides by (x + 6)(x − 6) gives x(x − 6) + 20(x + 6) = 72, which simplifies to x² + 14x + 48 = 0, or (x + 6)(x + 8) = 0. The value x = −6 makes the denominator x + 6 equal 0, so it is extraneous. The only solution is −8, so the sum of all solutions is −8. Including the extraneous value gives −14.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h311$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f(t) = 2000(0.64)<sup>t</sup> gives the population of a town t years after it was first measured. Which function g gives the same quantity h half-years after it was first measured?$q$, $q$["g(h) = 2000(0.8)<sup>h/2</sup>","g(h) = 2000(0.8)<sup>h</sup>","g(h) = 2000(0.64)<sup>2h</sup>","g(h) = 2000(0.32)<sup>h</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$There are 2 half-years in a year, so t = h/2. Then f = 2000(0.64)<sup>h/2</sup> = 2000((0.64)<sup>1/2</sup>)<sup>h</sup> = 2000(0.8)<sup>h</sup>, because 0.8² = 0.64. Writing (0.64)<sup>2h</sup> uses t = 2h, which reverses the conversion, and halving 0.64 does not halve the growth rate.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h312$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$x/(x − 5) − 11/(x − 1) = 20/((x − 5)(x − 1))<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["7","5","−7","12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying both sides by (x − 5)(x − 1) gives x(x − 1) − 11(x − 5) = 20, which simplifies to x² − 12x + 35 = 0, or (x − 5)(x − 7) = 0. The value x = 5 makes the denominator x − 5 equal 0, so it is extraneous. The only solution is 7, so the sum of all solutions is 7. Including the extraneous value gives 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h313$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 12x + 40. The function g is defined by g(x) = −3f(x + 4) − 3. What is the maximum value of g(x)?$q$, $q$["−15","9","−123","−3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Completing the square, f(x) = (x + 6)² + 4, so f has a minimum value of 4. Multiplying by −3 turns that minimum into a maximum of −3(4) = −12, and adding −3 gives −15. The horizontal shift changes where the maximum occurs, not its value. Using the constant term 40 instead of the minimum value gives −123.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h314$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + y² = 82<br>xy = 9<br><br>If x &gt; y &gt; 0, what is the value of x − y?$q$, $q$["8","√73","10","64"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$(x − y)² = x² − 2xy + y² = 82 − 2(9) = 64. Since x &gt; y, x − y is positive, so x − y = √64 = 8. The value 64 is (x − y)², not x − y, and 10 is x + y = √(82 + 2·9).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h315$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The loudness of a sound, measured as intensity, is inversely proportional to the square of the distance from its source. If the distance from the source is decreased by 20%, which of the following describes the change in the intensity?$q$, $q$["It increases by 25%.","It decreases by 36%.","It increases by 44%.","It increases by 56.25%."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The intensity is multiplied by 1/(0.8)² = 1.5625, which corresponds to an increase of 56.25%. A change of 20% treats the relationship as linear, ignoring the square.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h316$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has its vertex at (−1, 1) and passes through the point (−2, −1). What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["3","−3","−1","1"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$In vertex form, f(x) = a(x + 1)² + 1. Substituting (−2, −1): −1 = a(−1)² + 1, so a = −2. Then f(0) = −2(1)² + 1 = −1. The vertex's y-coordinate 1 is the y-intercept only when the vertex is on the y-axis.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h317$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$(1/9)<sup>x − 1</sup> = 81<sup>x − 2</sup> · 3<br><br>What value of x satisfies the given equation?$q$, $q$["5/2","5/3","5/6","3/2"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Rewrite each side as a power of 3: 1/9 = 3<sup>−2</sup>, 81 = 3<sup>4</sup>. The equation becomes 3<sup>−2(x − 1)</sup> = 3<sup>4(x − 2) + 1</sup>, so −2x + 2 = 4x − 7. Then 6x = 9 and x = 3/2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h318$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 2x − 35. In the xy-plane, the graph of y = f(x) + k, where k is a constant, has exactly one x-intercept. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−36","144","6","36"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$f(x) = (x + 7)(x − 5), so its vertex is at x = −1, and its minimum value is (6)(−6) = −36. The graph of y = f(x) + k touches the x-axis exactly once when the minimum is shifted up to 0, so k = 36.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h319$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f(t) = 200(0.36)<sup>t</sup> gives the value, in dollars, of a collectible t years after it was first measured. Which function g gives the same quantity h half-years after it was first measured?$q$, $q$["g(h) = 200(0.6)<sup>h</sup>","g(h) = 200(0.36)<sup>2h</sup>","g(h) = 200(0.6)<sup>h/2</sup>","g(h) = 200(0.18)<sup>h</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 2 half-years in a year, so t = h/2. Then f = 200(0.36)<sup>h/2</sup> = 200((0.36)<sup>1/2</sup>)<sup>h</sup> = 200(0.6)<sup>h</sup>, because 0.6² = 0.36. Writing (0.36)<sup>2h</sup> uses t = 2h, which reverses the conversion, and halving 0.36 does not halve the growth rate.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h320$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + y² = 45<br>xy = 18<br><br>If x &gt; y &gt; 0, what is the value of x − y?$q$, $q$["4","3","√27","9"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$(x − y)² = x² − 2xy + y² = 45 − 2(18) = 9. Since x &gt; y, x − y is positive, so x − y = √9 = 3. The value 9 is (x − y)², not x − y, and 9 is x + y = √(45 + 2·18).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h321$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = 3x + 2 and f(g(x)) = 6x² + 3x − 1. What is the value of g(−1)?$q$, $q$["0","2","8","4/3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(g(−1)) = 6(−1)² + 3(−1) − 1 = 2. Since f(g(−1)) = 3·g(−1) + 2, 3·g(−1) = 0, so g(−1) = 0. The value 2 is f(g(−1)), not g(−1).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h322$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + y² = 113<br>xy = 56<br><br>If x &gt; y &gt; 0, what is the value of x − y?$q$, $q$["2","15","1","√57"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$(x − y)² = x² − 2xy + y² = 113 − 2(56) = 1. Since x &gt; y, x − y is positive, so x − y = √1 = 1. The value 1 is (x − y)², not x − y, and 15 is x + y = √(113 + 2·56).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h323$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 6x. In the xy-plane, the graph of y = f(x) + k, where k is a constant, has exactly one x-intercept. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["3","36","9","−9"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$f(x) = (x + 6)(x), so its vertex is at x = −3, and its minimum value is (3)(−3) = −9. The graph of y = f(x) + k touches the x-axis exactly once when the minimum is shifted up to 0, so k = 9.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h324$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f(h) = 2000(0.7)<sup>h</sup> gives the number of subscribers to a newsletter h half-years after it was first measured. Which function g gives the same quantity t years after it was first measured?$q$, $q$["g(t) = 2000(0.49)<sup>t</sup>","g(t) = 2000(1.4)<sup>t</sup>","g(t) = 2000(0.49)<sup>2t</sup>","g(t) = 2000(0.7)<sup>t/2</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Each year contains 2 half-years, so h = 2t. Then f = 2000(0.7)<sup>2t</sup> = 2000((0.7)²)<sup>t</sup> = 2000(0.49)<sup>t</sup>. Doubling the base to 1.4 is not the same as squaring it, and the exponent t/2 uses the conversion in the wrong direction.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h325$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$(1/2)<sup>x − 1</sup> = 4<sup>x + 5</sup> · (1/2)<br><br>What value of x satisfies the given equation?$q$, $q$["−10/3","−10","−8/3","−3"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each side as a power of 2: 1/2 = 2<sup>−1</sup>, 4 = 2<sup>2</sup>, and (1/2) = 2<sup>−1</sup>. The equation becomes 2<sup>−1(x − 1)</sup> = 2<sup>2(x + 5) + (−1)</sup>, so −x + 1 = 2x + 9. Then 3x = −8 and x = −8/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h326$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function Q(t) = 5000(1.69)<sup>t/2</sup> models the value Q of an account, where t is the number of years since the start of the study. By what percent does Q increase each year?$q$, $q$["84.5%","30%","69%","34.5%"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Q is multiplied by 1.69 every 2 years. The one-year factor b satisfies b<sup>2</sup> = 1.69, so b = 1.3, which is a 30% increase each year. The value 69% is the change over 2 years, and dividing it by 2 ignores compounding.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h327$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The intensity of light from a small bulb is inversely proportional to the square of the distance from the bulb. If the distance from the bulb is decreased by 20%, which of the following describes the change in the intensity?$q$, $q$["It increases by 44%.","It increases by 25%.","It increases by 56.25%.","It decreases by 36%."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The intensity is multiplied by 1/(0.8)² = 1.5625, which corresponds to an increase of 56.25%. A change of 20% treats the relationship as linear, ignoring the square.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h328$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has x-intercepts at (−3, 0) and (3, 0) and a y-intercept at (0, −18). What is the minimum value of f?$q$, $q$["−18","0","18","−9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$From the x-intercepts, f(x) = a(x + 3)(x − 3) for some constant a. Since f(0) = −18, a(3)(−3) = −18, so a = 2. The vertex lies halfway between the intercepts, at x = 0, so the minimum value is f(0) = 2(3)(−3) = −18. Assuming a = 1 gives −9.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h329$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has its vertex at (−3, 4) and passes through the point (−1, −8). What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["31","−23","4","13"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In vertex form, f(x) = a(x + 3)² + 4. Substituting (−1, −8): −8 = a(2)² + 4, so a = −3. Then f(0) = −3(3)² + 4 = −23. The vertex's y-coordinate 4 is the y-intercept only when the vertex is on the y-axis.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h330$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The gravitational force between two objects is inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers. If the distance between their centers is increased by 150%, which of the following describes the change in the gravitational force?$q$, $q$["It decreases by 84%.","It decreases by 60%.","It decreases by 75%.","It increases by 525%."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The gravitational force is multiplied by 1/(2.5)² = 0.16, which corresponds to a decrease of 84%. A change of 150% treats the relationship as linear, ignoring the square.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h331$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has x-intercepts at (5, 0) and (7, 0) and a y-intercept at (0, 70). What is the minimum value of f?$q$, $q$["−1","−2","70","6"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$From the x-intercepts, f(x) = a(x − 5)(x − 7) for some constant a. Since f(0) = 70, a(−5)(−7) = 70, so a = 2. The vertex lies halfway between the intercepts, at x = 6, so the minimum value is f(6) = 2(1)(−1) = −2. Assuming a = 1 gives −1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h332$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$(1/9)<sup>x − 1</sup> = 27<sup>x − 4</sup> · 3<br><br>What value of x satisfies the given equation?$q$, $q$["9/5","14/5","13/5","9"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each side as a power of 3: 1/9 = 3<sup>−2</sup>, 27 = 3<sup>3</sup>. The equation becomes 3<sup>−2(x − 1)</sup> = 3<sup>3(x − 4) + 1</sup>, so −2x + 2 = 3x − 11. Then 5x = 13 and x = 13/5.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h333$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$(1/9)<sup>x + 3</sup> = 81<sup>x + 4</sup> · 3<br><br>What value of x satisfies the given equation?$q$, $q$["−23/6","−11/6","−11/3","−11/2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rewrite each side as a power of 3: 1/9 = 3<sup>−2</sup>, 81 = 3<sup>4</sup>. The equation becomes 3<sup>−2(x + 3)</sup> = 3<sup>4(x + 4) + 1</sup>, so −2x − 6 = 4x + 17. Then 6x = −23 and x = −23/6.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h334$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$2x² − x − 7 = 0<br><br>The solutions to the given equation are r and s. What is the value of r² + s²?$q$, $q$["−27/4","1/4","29/4","57/4"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sum of the solutions is r + s = 1/2 and the product is rs = −7/2. Since r² + s² = (r + s)² − 2rs, r² + s² = (1/2)² − 2(−7/2) = 1/4 + 7 = 29/4. The value 1/4 is (r + s)², which still includes the 2rs term.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h335$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = 2x − 5 and f(g(x)) = 4x² − 2x + 11. What is the value of g(3)?$q$, $q$["18","41","23","77"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$f(g(3)) = 43² − 23 + 11 = 41. Since f(g(3)) = 2·g(3) − 5, 2·g(3) = 46, so g(3) = 23. The value 41 is f(g(3)), not g(3).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h336$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$(1/9)<sup>x</sup> = 3<sup>x − 3</sup> · (1/9)<br><br>What value of x satisfies the given equation?$q$, $q$["1","−5/3","−5","5/3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Rewrite each side as a power of 3: 1/9 = 3<sup>−2</sup>, and (1/9) = 3<sup>−2</sup>. The equation becomes 3<sup>−2(x)</sup> = 3<sup>1(x − 3) + (−2)</sup>, so −2x = x − 5. Then 3x = 5 and x = 5/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h337$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has x-intercepts at (−2, 0) and (8, 0) and a y-intercept at (0, −16). What is the minimum value of f?$q$, $q$["−25","3","−16","25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$From the x-intercepts, f(x) = a(x + 2)(x − 8) for some constant a. Since f(0) = −16, a(2)(−8) = −16, so a = 1. The vertex lies halfway between the intercepts, at x = 3, so the minimum value is f(3) = 1(5)(−5) = −25. Assuming a = 1 gives −25.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h338$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + y² = 212<br>xy = 56<br><br>If x &gt; y &gt; 0, what is the value of x − y?$q$, $q$["100","√156","10","18"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$(x − y)² = x² − 2xy + y² = 212 − 2(56) = 100. Since x &gt; y, x − y is positive, so x − y = √100 = 10. The value 100 is (x − y)², not x − y, and 18 is x + y = √(212 + 2·56).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h339$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = 4x + 5 and f(g(x)) = 12x² + 29. What is the value of g(−3)?$q$, $q$["71/2","33","137","553"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(g(−3)) = 12(−3)² + 29 = 137. Since f(g(−3)) = 4·g(−3) + 5, 4·g(−3) = 132, so g(−3) = 33. The value 137 is f(g(−3)), not g(−3).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h340$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$x/(x − 5) − 11/(x + 2) = 35/((x − 5)(x + 2))<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["5","4","−4","9"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiplying both sides by (x − 5)(x + 2) gives x(x + 2) − 11(x − 5) = 35, which simplifies to x² − 9x + 20 = 0, or (x − 5)(x − 4) = 0. The value x = 5 makes the denominator x − 5 equal 0, so it is extraneous. The only solution is 4, so the sum of all solutions is 4. Including the extraneous value gives 9.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h341$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 4x − 32. In the xy-plane, the graph of y = f(x) + k, where k is a constant, has exactly one x-intercept. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["36","6","144","−36"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(x) = (x + 8)(x − 4), so its vertex is at x = −2, and its minimum value is (6)(−6) = −36. The graph of y = f(x) + k touches the x-axis exactly once when the minimum is shifted up to 0, so k = 36.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h342$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$x/(x − 3) + 4/(x − 5) = −6/((x − 3)(x − 5))<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["3","2","1","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Multiplying both sides by (x − 3)(x − 5) gives x(x − 5) + 4(x − 3) = −6, which simplifies to x² − x − 6 = 0, or (x − 3)(x + 2) = 0. The value x = 3 makes the denominator x − 3 equal 0, so it is extraneous. The only solution is −2, so the sum of all solutions is −2. Including the extraneous value gives 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h343$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has x-intercepts at (−8, 0) and (6, 0) and a y-intercept at (0, −48). What is the minimum value of f?$q$, $q$["−1","49","−49","−48"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$From the x-intercepts, f(x) = a(x + 8)(x − 6) for some constant a. Since f(0) = −48, a(8)(−6) = −48, so a = 1. The vertex lies halfway between the intercepts, at x = −1, so the minimum value is f(−1) = 1(7)(−7) = −49. Assuming a = 1 gives −49.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h344$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$x/(x − 4) − 14/(x + 4) = 32/((x − 4)(x + 4))<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["10","4","6","−6"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Multiplying both sides by (x − 4)(x + 4) gives x(x + 4) − 14(x − 4) = 32, which simplifies to x² − 10x + 24 = 0, or (x − 4)(x − 6) = 0. The value x = 4 makes the denominator x − 4 equal 0, so it is extraneous. The only solution is 6, so the sum of all solutions is 6. Including the extraneous value gives 10.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h345$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$(1/5)<sup>x + 1</sup> = 125<sup>x</sup> · (1/25)<br><br>What value of x satisfies the given equation?$q$, $q$["1/4","−1/4","3/2","3/4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rewrite each side as a power of 5: 1/5 = 5<sup>−1</sup>, 125 = 5<sup>3</sup>, and (1/25) = 5<sup>−2</sup>. The equation becomes 5<sup>−1(x + 1)</sup> = 5<sup>3(x) + (−2)</sup>, so −x − 1 = 3x − 2. Then 4x = 1 and x = 1/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h346$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has its vertex at (4, −6) and passes through the point (2, 6). What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["6","−54","−6","42"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$In vertex form, f(x) = a(x − 4)² + (−6). Substituting (2, 6): 6 = a(−2)² + (−6), so a = 3. Then f(0) = 3(−4)² + (−6) = 42. The vertex's y-coordinate −6 is the y-intercept only when the vertex is on the y-axis.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h347$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has its vertex at (−5, −3) and passes through the point (−2, 3). What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["−3","41/3","−59/3","−19/3"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In vertex form, f(x) = a(x + 5)² + (−3). Substituting (−2, 3): 3 = a(3)² + (−3), so a = 2/3. Then f(0) = (2/3)(5)² + (−3) = 41/3. The vertex's y-coordinate −3 is the y-intercept only when the vertex is on the y-axis.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h348$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + y² = 232<br>xy = 84<br><br>If x &gt; y &gt; 0, what is the value of x − y?$q$, $q$["8","20","√148","64"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$(x − y)² = x² − 2xy + y² = 232 − 2(84) = 64. Since x &gt; y, x − y is positive, so x − y = √64 = 8. The value 64 is (x − y)², not x − y, and 20 is x + y = √(232 + 2·84).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h349$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 14x + 45. In the xy-plane, the graph of y = f(x) + k, where k is a constant, has exactly one x-intercept. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["4","16","−4","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(x) = (x − 5)(x − 9), so its vertex is at x = 7, and its minimum value is (2)(−2) = −4. The graph of y = f(x) + k touches the x-axis exactly once when the minimum is shifted up to 0, so k = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h350$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The kinetic energy of a moving cart is directly proportional to the square of its speed. If the cart's speed is decreased by 30%, which of the following describes the change in its kinetic energy?$q$, $q$["It increases by 104.08%.","It decreases by 51%.","It decreases by 60%.","It decreases by 30%."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Its kinetic energy is multiplied by (0.7)² = 0.49, which corresponds to a decrease of 51%. A change of 30% treats the relationship as linear, ignoring the square.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h351$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 12x + 37. The function g is defined by g(x) = −4f(x + 4) + 2. What is the maximum value of g(x)?$q$, $q$["−2","6","−12","−146"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Completing the square, f(x) = (x + 6)² + 1, so f has a minimum value of 1. Multiplying by −4 turns that minimum into a maximum of −4(1) = −4, and adding 2 gives −2. The horizontal shift changes where the maximum occurs, not its value. Using the constant term 37 instead of the minimum value gives −146.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h352$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function Q(t) = 2400(0.343)<sup>t/3</sup> models the number Q of bacteria in a sample, where t is the number of months since the start of the study. By what percent does Q decrease each month?$q$, $q$["65.7%","88.57%","30%","21.9%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Q is multiplied by 0.343 every 3 months. The one-month factor b satisfies b<sup>3</sup> = 0.343, so b = 0.7, which is a 30% decrease each month. The value 65.7% is the change over 3 months, and dividing it by 3 ignores compounding.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h353$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The intensity of light from a small bulb is inversely proportional to the square of the distance from the bulb. If the distance from the bulb is increased by 150%, which of the following describes the change in the intensity?$q$, $q$["It decreases by 75%.","It decreases by 60%.","It increases by 525%.","It decreases by 84%."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The intensity is multiplied by 1/(2.5)² = 0.16, which corresponds to a decrease of 84%. A change of 150% treats the relationship as linear, ignoring the square.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h354$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 6x + 13. The function g is defined by g(x) = −2f(x − 4) + 4. What is the maximum value of g(x)?$q$, $q$["−16","−4","−22","12"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Completing the square, f(x) = (x + 3)² + 4, so f has a minimum value of 4. Multiplying by −2 turns that minimum into a maximum of −2(4) = −8, and adding 4 gives −4. The horizontal shift changes where the maximum occurs, not its value. Using the constant term 13 instead of the minimum value gives −22.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h355$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has x-intercepts at (2, 0) and (4, 0) and a y-intercept at (0, 8). What is the minimum value of f?$q$, $q$["3","−1","8","1"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$From the x-intercepts, f(x) = a(x − 2)(x − 4) for some constant a. Since f(0) = 8, a(−2)(−4) = 8, so a = 1. The vertex lies halfway between the intercepts, at x = 3, so the minimum value is f(3) = 1(1)(−1) = −1. Assuming a = 1 gives −1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h356$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = a(x − 3)² + 5<br>y = 55<br><br>In the given system of equations, a is a positive constant. The graphs of the equations in the xy-plane intersect at two points that are 10 units apart. What is the value of a?$q$, $q$["1/2","10","2","11/5"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The two intersection points are symmetric about the axis x = 3, so each is 10/2 = 5 units from it. At x = 8, a(5)² + 5 = 55, so 25a = 50 and a = 2. Using the full distance 10 instead of half of it gives 1/2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h357$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + y² = 37<br>xy = 6<br><br>If x &gt; y &gt; 0, what is the value of x − y?$q$, $q$["5","7","√31","25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$(x − y)² = x² − 2xy + y² = 37 − 2(6) = 25. Since x &gt; y, x − y is positive, so x − y = √25 = 5. The value 25 is (x − y)², not x − y, and 7 is x + y = √(37 + 2·6).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h358$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f(t) = 2000(1.1025)<sup>t</sup> gives the value, in dollars, of a collectible t years after it was first measured. Which function g gives the same quantity h half-years after it was first measured?$q$, $q$["g(h) = 2000(1.05)<sup>h</sup>","g(h) = 2000(0.55125)<sup>h</sup>","g(h) = 2000(1.1025)<sup>2h</sup>","g(h) = 2000(1.05)<sup>h/2</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 2 half-years in a year, so t = h/2. Then f = 2000(1.1025)<sup>h/2</sup> = 2000((1.1025)<sup>1/2</sup>)<sup>h</sup> = 2000(1.05)<sup>h</sup>, because 1.05² = 1.1025. Writing (1.1025)<sup>2h</sup> uses t = 2h, which reverses the conversion, and halving 1.1025 does not halve the growth rate.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h359$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$x/(x + 6) + 3/(x − 1) = 42/((x + 6)(x − 1))<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−4","−6","4","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Multiplying both sides by (x + 6)(x − 1) gives x(x − 1) + 3(x + 6) = 42, which simplifies to x² + 2x − 24 = 0, or (x + 6)(x − 4) = 0. The value x = −6 makes the denominator x + 6 equal 0, so it is extraneous. The only solution is 4, so the sum of all solutions is 4. Including the extraneous value gives −2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h360$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The area of a square tile is proportional to the square of its side length. If the side length is increased by 30%, which of the following describes the change in the area?$q$, $q$["It increases by 69%.","It increases by 60%.","It decreases by 40.83%.","It increases by 30%."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The area is multiplied by (1.3)² = 1.69, which corresponds to an increase of 69%. A change of 30% treats the relationship as linear, ignoring the square.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h361$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = ax² − 5<br>y = 95<br><br>In the given system of equations, a is a positive constant. The graphs of the equations in the xy-plane intersect at two points that are 10 units apart. What is the value of a?$q$, $q$["1","19/5","4","20"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The two intersection points are symmetric about the axis x = 0, so each is 10/2 = 5 units from it. At x = 5, a(5)² − 5 = 95, so 25a = 100 and a = 4. Using the full distance 10 instead of half of it gives 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h362$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has its vertex at (1, −3) and passes through the point (4, −12). What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["−2","−3","2","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$In vertex form, f(x) = a(x − 1)² + (−3). Substituting (4, −12): −12 = a(3)² + (−3), so a = −1. Then f(0) = −1(−1)² + (−3) = −4. The vertex's y-coordinate −3 is the y-intercept only when the vertex is on the y-axis.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h363$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 10x + 9. In the xy-plane, the graph of y = f(x) + k, where k is a constant, has exactly one x-intercept. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["16","64","−16","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(x) = (x − 1)(x − 9), so its vertex is at x = 5, and its minimum value is (4)(−4) = −16. The graph of y = f(x) + k touches the x-axis exactly once when the minimum is shifted up to 0, so k = 16.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h364$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = 2x² + 20x + 18. In the xy-plane, the graph of y = f(x) + k, where k is a constant, has exactly one x-intercept. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["8","32","128","−32"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(x) = 2(x + 9)(x + 1), so its vertex is at x = −5, and its minimum value is 2(4)(−4) = −32. The graph of y = f(x) + k touches the x-axis exactly once when the minimum is shifted up to 0, so k = 32.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h365$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = −3x + 6 and f(g(x)) = −6x² − 3x + 15. What is the value of g(3)?$q$, $q$["−48","150","18","14"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$f(g(3)) = −63² − 33 + 15 = −48. Since f(g(3)) = -3·g(3) + 6, -3·g(3) = −54, so g(3) = 18. The value −48 is f(g(3)), not g(3).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h366$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² − 8x − 4 = 0<br><br>The solutions to the given equation are r and s. What is the value of r² + s²?$q$, $q$["56","64","68","72"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sum of the solutions is r + s = 8 and the product is rs = −4. Since r² + s² = (r + s)² − 2rs, r² + s² = (8)² − 2(−4) = 64 + 8 = 72. The value 64 is (r + s)², which still includes the 2rs term.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h367$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 6x + 6. The function g is defined by g(x) = −4f(x − 3) + 2. What is the maximum value of g(x)?$q$, $q$["14","4","−10","−22"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Completing the square, f(x) = (x − 3)² − 3, so f has a minimum value of −3. Multiplying by −4 turns that minimum into a maximum of −4((−3)) = 12, and adding 2 gives 14. The horizontal shift changes where the maximum occurs, not its value. Using the constant term 6 instead of the minimum value gives −22.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h368$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 4x + 1. The function g is defined by g(x) = −3f(x − 1) − 3. What is the maximum value of g(x)?$q$, $q$["−12","6","−6","18"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Completing the square, f(x) = (x + 2)² − 3, so f has a minimum value of −3. Multiplying by −3 turns that minimum into a maximum of −3((−3)) = 9, and adding −3 gives 6. The horizontal shift changes where the maximum occurs, not its value. Using the constant term 1 instead of the minimum value gives −6.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h369$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has its vertex at (−1, 4) and passes through the point (−3, 6). What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["4","7/2","−7/2","9/2"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$In vertex form, f(x) = a(x + 1)² + 4. Substituting (−3, 6): 6 = a(−2)² + 4, so a = 1/2. Then f(0) = (1/2)(1)² + 4 = 9/2. The vertex's y-coordinate 4 is the y-intercept only when the vertex is on the y-axis.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h370$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = a(x − 2)² + 3<br>y = 8<br><br>In the given system of equations, a is a positive constant. The graphs of the equations in the xy-plane intersect at two points that are 4 units apart. What is the value of a?$q$, $q$["2","11/4","5/4","5/2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The two intersection points are symmetric about the axis x = 2, so each is 4/2 = 2 units from it. At x = 4, a(2)² + 3 = 8, so 4a = 5 and a = 5/4. Using the full distance 4 instead of half of it gives 5/16.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h371$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has x-intercepts at (3, 0) and (7, 0) and a y-intercept at (0, 63). What is the minimum value of f?$q$, $q$["5","63","−12","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$From the x-intercepts, f(x) = a(x − 3)(x − 7) for some constant a. Since f(0) = 63, a(−3)(−7) = 63, so a = 3. The vertex lies halfway between the intercepts, at x = 5, so the minimum value is f(5) = 3(2)(−2) = −12. Assuming a = 1 gives −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h372$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$(1/3)<sup>x + 1</sup> = 9<sup>x + 5</sup> · 3<br><br>What value of x satisfies the given equation?$q$, $q$["−10","−11/3","−10/3","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Rewrite each side as a power of 3: 1/3 = 3<sup>−1</sup>, 9 = 3<sup>2</sup>. The equation becomes 3<sup>−1(x + 1)</sup> = 3<sup>2(x + 5) + 1</sup>, so −x − 1 = 2x + 11. Then 3x = −12 and x = −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h373$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f(t) = 1200(1.21)<sup>t</sup> gives the number of members of a club t years after it was first measured. Which function g gives the same quantity h half-years after it was first measured?$q$, $q$["g(h) = 1200(1.1)<sup>h</sup>","g(h) = 1200(1.21)<sup>2h</sup>","g(h) = 1200(1.1)<sup>h/2</sup>","g(h) = 1200(0.605)<sup>h</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 2 half-years in a year, so t = h/2. Then f = 1200(1.21)<sup>h/2</sup> = 1200((1.21)<sup>1/2</sup>)<sup>h</sup> = 1200(1.1)<sup>h</sup>, because 1.1² = 1.21. Writing (1.21)<sup>2h</sup> uses t = 2h, which reverses the conversion, and halving 1.21 does not halve the growth rate.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h374$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = 5x − 1 and f(g(x)) = 15x² + 5x − 31. What is the value of g(3)?$q$, $q$["594","24","118/5","119"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(g(3)) = 153² + 53 − 31 = 119. Since f(g(3)) = 5·g(3) − 1, 5·g(3) = 120, so g(3) = 24. The value 119 is f(g(3)), not g(3).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h375$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = −2x + 4 and f(g(x)) = −2x² − 4x + 14. What is the value of g(−2)?$q$, $q$["−5","14","−9","−24"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(g(−2)) = −2(−2)² − 4(−2) + 14 = 14. Since f(g(−2)) = -2·g(−2) + 4, -2·g(−2) = 10, so g(−2) = −5. The value 14 is f(g(−2)), not g(−2).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h376$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function Q(t) = 1500(0.49)<sup>t/2</sup> models a quantity Q, where t is the number of months since the start of the study. By what percent does Q decrease each month?$q$, $q$["25.5%","30%","51%","75.5%"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Q is multiplied by 0.49 every 2 months. The one-month factor b satisfies b<sup>2</sup> = 0.49, so b = 0.7, which is a 30% decrease each month. The value 51% is the change over 2 months, and dividing it by 2 ignores compounding.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h377$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f(t) = 200(1.96)<sup>t</sup> gives the number of subscribers to a newsletter t years after it was first measured. Which function g gives the same quantity h half-years after it was first measured?$q$, $q$["g(h) = 200(1.96)<sup>2h</sup>","g(h) = 200(0.98)<sup>h</sup>","g(h) = 200(1.4)<sup>h/2</sup>","g(h) = 200(1.4)<sup>h</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are 2 half-years in a year, so t = h/2. Then f = 200(1.96)<sup>h/2</sup> = 200((1.96)<sup>1/2</sup>)<sup>h</sup> = 200(1.4)<sup>h</sup>, because 1.4² = 1.96. Writing (1.96)<sup>2h</sup> uses t = 2h, which reverses the conversion, and halving 1.96 does not halve the growth rate.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h378$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has its vertex at (−2, −2) and passes through the point (−3, −3). What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["2","−6","−2","0"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In vertex form, f(x) = a(x + 2)² + (−2). Substituting (−3, −3): −3 = a(−1)² + (−2), so a = −1. Then f(0) = −1(2)² + (−2) = −6. The vertex's y-coordinate −2 is the y-intercept only when the vertex is on the y-axis.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h379$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f(t) = 500(0.36)<sup>t</sup> gives the number of subscribers to a newsletter t years after it was first measured. Which function g gives the same quantity h half-years after it was first measured?$q$, $q$["g(h) = 500(0.18)<sup>h</sup>","g(h) = 500(0.6)<sup>h/2</sup>","g(h) = 500(0.6)<sup>h</sup>","g(h) = 500(0.36)<sup>2h</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$There are 2 half-years in a year, so t = h/2. Then f = 500(0.36)<sup>h/2</sup> = 500((0.36)<sup>1/2</sup>)<sup>h</sup> = 500(0.6)<sup>h</sup>, because 0.6² = 0.36. Writing (0.36)<sup>2h</sup> uses t = 2h, which reverses the conversion, and halving 0.36 does not halve the growth rate.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h380$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = −2x − 9 and f(g(x)) = 2x² + 8x − 7. What is the value of g(3)?$q$, $q$["−13","−22","35","−79"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(g(3)) = 23² + 83 − 7 = 35. Since f(g(3)) = -2·g(3) − 9, -2·g(3) = 44, so g(3) = −22. The value 35 is f(g(3)), not g(3).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h381$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$2x² + 11x + 8 = 0<br><br>The solutions to the given equation are r and s. What is the value of r² + s²?$q$, $q$["153/4","89/4","121/4","57/4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sum of the solutions is r + s = −11/2 and the product is rs = 4. Since r² + s² = (r + s)² − 2rs, r² + s² = (−11/2)² − 2(4) = 121/4 − 8 = 89/4. The value 121/4 is (r + s)², which still includes the 2rs term.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h382$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = 2x² − 16x − 18. In the xy-plane, the graph of y = f(x) + k, where k is a constant, has exactly one x-intercept. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["50","200","−50","10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(x) = 2(x + 1)(x − 9), so its vertex is at x = 4, and its minimum value is 2(5)(−5) = −50. The graph of y = f(x) + k touches the x-axis exactly once when the minimum is shifted up to 0, so k = 50.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h383$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 4x. In the xy-plane, the graph of y = f(x) + k, where k is a constant, has exactly one x-intercept. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["2","−4","16","4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$f(x) = (x + 4)(x), so its vertex is at x = −2, and its minimum value is (2)(−2) = −4. The graph of y = f(x) + k touches the x-axis exactly once when the minimum is shifted up to 0, so k = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h384$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has x-intercepts at (−8, 0) and (−4, 0) and a y-intercept at (0, 32). What is the minimum value of f?$q$, $q$["−6","−4","32","4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$From the x-intercepts, f(x) = a(x + 8)(x + 4) for some constant a. Since f(0) = 32, a(8)(4) = 32, so a = 1. The vertex lies halfway between the intercepts, at x = −6, so the minimum value is f(−6) = 1(2)(−2) = −4. Assuming a = 1 gives −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h385$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function Q(t) = 5000(0.81)<sup>t/2</sup> models a quantity Q, where t is the number of years since the start of the study. By what percent does Q decrease each year?$q$, $q$["10%","59.5%","19%","9.5%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Q is multiplied by 0.81 every 2 years. The one-year factor b satisfies b<sup>2</sup> = 0.81, so b = 0.9, which is a 10% decrease each year. The value 19% is the change over 2 years, and dividing it by 2 ignores compounding.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h386$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The gravitational force between two objects is inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers. If the distance between their centers is decreased by 60%, which of the following describes the change in the gravitational force?$q$, $q$["It decreases by 84%.","It increases by 150%.","It increases by 525%.","It increases by 156%."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The gravitational force is multiplied by 1/(0.4)² = 6.25, which corresponds to an increase of 525%. A change of 60% treats the relationship as linear, ignoring the square.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h387$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$3x² + 11x + 7 = 0<br><br>The solutions to the given equation are r and s. What is the value of r² + s²?$q$, $q$["121/9","163/9","79/9","−5/9"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sum of the solutions is r + s = −11/3 and the product is rs = 7/3. Since r² + s² = (r + s)² − 2rs, r² + s² = (−11/3)² − 2(7/3) = 121/9 − 14/3 = 79/9. The value 121/9 is (r + s)², which still includes the 2rs term.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h388$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function Q(t) = 120(1.69)<sup>t/2</sup> models the value Q of an account, where t is the number of years since the start of the study. By what percent does Q increase each year?$q$, $q$["34.5%","84.5%","30%","69%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Q is multiplied by 1.69 every 2 years. The one-year factor b satisfies b<sup>2</sup> = 1.69, so b = 1.3, which is a 30% increase each year. The value 69% is the change over 2 years, and dividing it by 2 ignores compounding.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h389$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$2x² − 11x − 11 = 0<br><br>The solutions to the given equation are r and s. What is the value of r² + s²?$q$, $q$["165/4","77/4","209/4","121/4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sum of the solutions is r + s = 11/2 and the product is rs = −11/2. Since r² + s² = (r + s)² − 2rs, r² + s² = (11/2)² − 2(−11/2) = 121/4 + 11 = 165/4. The value 121/4 is (r + s)², which still includes the 2rs term.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h390$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = 2x² − 8. In the xy-plane, the graph of y = f(x) + k, where k is a constant, has exactly one x-intercept. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−8","4","8","32"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$f(x) = 2(x + 2)(x − 2), so its vertex is at x = 0, and its minimum value is 2(2)(−2) = −8. The graph of y = f(x) + k touches the x-axis exactly once when the minimum is shifted up to 0, so k = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h391$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has x-intercepts at (−3, 0) and (5, 0) and a y-intercept at (0, −10). What is the minimum value of f?$q$, $q$["1","−10","−16","−32/3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$From the x-intercepts, f(x) = a(x + 3)(x − 5) for some constant a. Since f(0) = −10, a(3)(−5) = −10, so a = 2/3. The vertex lies halfway between the intercepts, at x = 1, so the minimum value is f(1) = (2/3)(4)(−4) = −32/3. Assuming a = 1 gives −16.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h392$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has x-intercepts at (3, 0) and (5, 0) and a y-intercept at (0, 30). What is the minimum value of f?$q$, $q$["4","−2","−1","30"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$From the x-intercepts, f(x) = a(x − 3)(x − 5) for some constant a. Since f(0) = 30, a(−3)(−5) = 30, so a = 2. The vertex lies halfway between the intercepts, at x = 4, so the minimum value is f(4) = 2(1)(−1) = −2. Assuming a = 1 gives −1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h393$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = ax² − 9<br>y = 9<br><br>In the given system of equations, a is a positive constant. The graphs of the equations in the xy-plane intersect at two points that are 6 units apart. What is the value of a?$q$, $q$["1/2","6","1","2"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The two intersection points are symmetric about the axis x = 0, so each is 6/2 = 3 units from it. At x = 3, a(3)² − 9 = 9, so 9a = 18 and a = 2. Using the full distance 6 instead of half of it gives 1/2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h394$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = a(x − 5)² − 2<br>y = 10<br><br>In the given system of equations, a is a positive constant. The graphs of the equations in the xy-plane intersect at two points that are 4 units apart. What is the value of a?$q$, $q$["3","3/4","5/2","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The two intersection points are symmetric about the axis x = 5, so each is 4/2 = 2 units from it. At x = 7, a(2)² − 2 = 10, so 4a = 12 and a = 3. Using the full distance 4 instead of half of it gives 3/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h395$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$(1/81)<sup>x</sup> = 9<sup>x</sup> · 9<br><br>What value of x satisfies the given equation?$q$, $q$["1","−1/3","0","1/3"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Rewrite each side as a power of 3: 1/81 = 3<sup>−4</sup>, 9 = 3<sup>2</sup>, and 9 = 3<sup>2</sup>. The equation becomes 3<sup>−4(x)</sup> = 3<sup>2(x) + 2</sup>, so −4x = 2x + 2. Then 6x = −2 and x = −1/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h396$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$2x² − 12x + 5 = 0<br><br>The solutions to the given equation are r and s. What is the value of r² + s²?$q$, $q$["26","31","36","41"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sum of the solutions is r + s = 6 and the product is rs = 5/2. Since r² + s² = (r + s)² − 2rs, r² + s² = (6)² − 2(5/2) = 36 − 5 = 31. The value 36 is (r + s)², which still includes the 2rs term.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h397$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = a(x + 1)² − 8<br>y = −4<br><br>In the given system of equations, a is a positive constant. The graphs of the equations in the xy-plane intersect at two points that are 4 units apart. What is the value of a?$q$, $q$["−1","1","1/4","2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The two intersection points are symmetric about the axis x = −1, so each is 4/2 = 2 units from it. At x = 1, a(2)² − 8 = −4, so 4a = 4 and a = 1. Using the full distance 4 instead of half of it gives 1/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h398$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The loudness of a sound, measured as intensity, is inversely proportional to the square of the distance from its source. If the distance from the source is increased by 25%, which of the following describes the change in the intensity?$q$, $q$["It decreases by 36%.","It increases by 56.25%.","It decreases by 20%.","It decreases by 43.75%."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The intensity is multiplied by 1/(1.25)² = 0.64, which corresponds to a decrease of 36%. A change of 25% treats the relationship as linear, ignoring the square.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h399$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$x/(x − 7) − 11/(x − 1) = 42/((x − 7)(x − 1))<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["5","7","12","−5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying both sides by (x − 7)(x − 1) gives x(x − 1) − 11(x − 7) = 42, which simplifies to x² − 12x + 35 = 0, or (x − 7)(x − 5) = 0. The value x = 7 makes the denominator x − 7 equal 0, so it is extraneous. The only solution is 5, so the sum of all solutions is 5. Including the extraneous value gives 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h400$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The area of a square tile is proportional to the square of its side length. If the side length is decreased by 10%, which of the following describes the change in the area?$q$, $q$["It increases by 23.46%.","It decreases by 10%.","It decreases by 20%.","It decreases by 19%."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The area is multiplied by (0.9)² = 0.81, which corresponds to a decrease of 19%. A change of 10% treats the relationship as linear, ignoring the square.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h401$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$2x² + 6x − 9 = 0<br><br>The solutions to the given equation are r and s. What is the value of r² + s²?$q$, $q$["0","9","18","27"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sum of the solutions is r + s = −3 and the product is rs = −9/2. Since r² + s² = (r + s)² − 2rs, r² + s² = (−3)² − 2(−9/2) = 9 + 9 = 18. The value 9 is (r + s)², which still includes the 2rs term.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h402$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$(1/16)<sup>x − 1</sup> = 4<sup>x − 3</sup> · (1/4)<br><br>What value of x satisfies the given equation?$q$, $q$["5/3","2/3","2","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each side as a power of 2: 1/16 = 2<sup>−4</sup>, 4 = 2<sup>2</sup>, and (1/4) = 2<sup>−2</sup>. The equation becomes 2<sup>−4(x − 1)</sup> = 2<sup>2(x − 3) + (−2)</sup>, so −4x + 4 = 2x − 8. Then 6x = 12 and x = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h403$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$x/(x + 1) + 2/(x − 3) = 4/((x + 1)(x − 3))<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["2","1","−2","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying both sides by (x + 1)(x − 3) gives x(x − 3) + 2(x + 1) = 4, which simplifies to x² − x − 2 = 0, or (x + 1)(x − 2) = 0. The value x = −1 makes the denominator x + 1 equal 0, so it is extraneous. The only solution is 2, so the sum of all solutions is 2. Including the extraneous value gives 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h404$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 6x − 7. In the xy-plane, the graph of y = f(x) + k, where k is a constant, has exactly one x-intercept. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["16","64","−16","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(x) = (x + 7)(x − 1), so its vertex is at x = −3, and its minimum value is (4)(−4) = −16. The graph of y = f(x) + k touches the x-axis exactly once when the minimum is shifted up to 0, so k = 16.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h405$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 8x + 12. In the xy-plane, the graph of y = f(x) + k, where k is a constant, has exactly one x-intercept. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−4","4","2","16"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(x) = (x + 6)(x + 2), so its vertex is at x = −4, and its minimum value is (2)(−2) = −4. The graph of y = f(x) + k touches the x-axis exactly once when the minimum is shifted up to 0, so k = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h406$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = a(x − 1)² − 12<br>y = 60<br><br>In the given system of equations, a is a positive constant. The graphs of the equations in the xy-plane intersect at two points that are 12 units apart. What is the value of a?$q$, $q$["5/3","12","2","1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The two intersection points are symmetric about the axis x = 1, so each is 12/2 = 6 units from it. At x = 7, a(6)² − 12 = 60, so 36a = 72 and a = 2. Using the full distance 12 instead of half of it gives 1/2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h407$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function Q(t) = 450(0.5625)<sup>t/2</sup> models the number Q of bacteria in a sample, where t is the number of months since the start of the study. By what percent does Q decrease each month?$q$, $q$["25%","71.88%","43.75%","21.88%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Q is multiplied by 0.5625 every 2 months. The one-month factor b satisfies b<sup>2</sup> = 0.5625, so b = 0.75, which is a 25% decrease each month. The value 43.75% is the change over 2 months, and dividing it by 2 ignores compounding.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h408$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = 4x + 3 and f(g(x)) = 8x² + 8x − 1. What is the value of g(3)?$q$, $q$["23","49/2","95","383"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(g(3)) = 83² + 83 − 1 = 95. Since f(g(3)) = 4·g(3) + 3, 4·g(3) = 92, so g(3) = 23. The value 95 is f(g(3)), not g(3).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h409$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 10x + 19. The function g is defined by g(x) = −4f(x − 1) − 1. What is the maximum value of g(x)?$q$, $q$["−25","23","28","−77"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Completing the square, f(x) = (x + 5)² − 6, so f has a minimum value of −6. Multiplying by −4 turns that minimum into a maximum of −4((−6)) = 24, and adding −1 gives 23. The horizontal shift changes where the maximum occurs, not its value. Using the constant term 19 instead of the minimum value gives −77.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h410$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function Q(t) = 2400(1.96)<sup>t/2</sup> models a quantity Q, where t is the number of weeks since the start of the study. By what percent does Q increase each week?$q$, $q$["98%","40%","96%","48%"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Q is multiplied by 1.96 every 2 weeks. The one-week factor b satisfies b<sup>2</sup> = 1.96, so b = 1.4, which is a 40% increase each week. The value 96% is the change over 2 weeks, and dividing it by 2 ignores compounding.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h411$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + 10x − 9 = 0<br><br>The solutions to the given equation are r and s. What is the value of r² + s²?$q$, $q$["100","118","82","109"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sum of the solutions is r + s = −10 and the product is rs = −9. Since r² + s² = (r + s)² − 2rs, r² + s² = (−10)² − 2(−9) = 100 + 18 = 118. The value 100 is (r + s)², which still includes the 2rs term.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h412$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + y² = 340<br>xy = 168<br><br>If x &gt; y &gt; 0, what is the value of x − y?$q$, $q$["√172","4","26","2"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$(x − y)² = x² − 2xy + y² = 340 − 2(168) = 4. Since x &gt; y, x − y is positive, so x − y = √4 = 2. The value 4 is (x − y)², not x − y, and 26 is x + y = √(340 + 2·168).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h413$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$(1/125)<sup>x − 4</sup> = 5<sup>x − 2</sup> · 5<br><br>What value of x satisfies the given equation?$q$, $q$["11/2","13/4","−11/4","7/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Rewrite each side as a power of 5: 1/125 = 5<sup>−3</sup>. The equation becomes 5<sup>−3(x − 4)</sup> = 5<sup>1(x − 2) + 1</sup>, so −3x + 12 = x − 1. Then 4x = 13 and x = 13/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h414$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 6x + 16. The function g is defined by g(x) = −2f(x − 3) + 4. What is the maximum value of g(x)?$q$, $q$["−28","18","−22","−10"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Completing the square, f(x) = (x − 3)² + 7, so f has a minimum value of 7. Multiplying by −2 turns that minimum into a maximum of −2(7) = −14, and adding 4 gives −10. The horizontal shift changes where the maximum occurs, not its value. Using the constant term 16 instead of the minimum value gives −28.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h415$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 6x + 17. The function g is defined by g(x) = −2f(x + 5) − 7. What is the maximum value of g(x)?$q$, $q$["−41","9","−2","−23"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Completing the square, f(x) = (x + 3)² + 8, so f has a minimum value of 8. Multiplying by −2 turns that minimum into a maximum of −2(8) = −16, and adding −7 gives −23. The horizontal shift changes where the maximum occurs, not its value. Using the constant term 17 instead of the minimum value gives −41.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h416$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f(h) = 500(1.4)<sup>h</sup> gives the value, in dollars, of a collectible h half-years after it was first measured. Which function g gives the same quantity t years after it was first measured?$q$, $q$["g(t) = 500(1.4)<sup>t/2</sup>","g(t) = 500(2.8)<sup>t</sup>","g(t) = 500(1.96)<sup>2t</sup>","g(t) = 500(1.96)<sup>t</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Each year contains 2 half-years, so h = 2t. Then f = 500(1.4)<sup>2t</sup> = 500((1.4)²)<sup>t</sup> = 500(1.96)<sup>t</sup>. Doubling the base to 2.8 is not the same as squaring it, and the exponent t/2 uses the conversion in the wrong direction.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h417$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has its vertex at (−1, 5) and passes through the point (2, −4). What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["−6","5","6","4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$In vertex form, f(x) = a(x + 1)² + 5. Substituting (2, −4): −4 = a(3)² + 5, so a = −1. Then f(0) = −1(1)² + 5 = 4. The vertex's y-coordinate 5 is the y-intercept only when the vertex is on the y-axis.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h418$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 8x + 7. The function g is defined by g(x) = −3f(x + 3) − 2. What is the maximum value of g(x)?$q$, $q$["33","−23","25","−29"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Completing the square, f(x) = (x − 4)² − 9, so f has a minimum value of −9. Multiplying by −3 turns that minimum into a maximum of −3((−9)) = 27, and adding −2 gives 25. The horizontal shift changes where the maximum occurs, not its value. Using the constant term 7 instead of the minimum value gives −23.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h419$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function Q(t) = 120(1.44)<sup>t/2</sup> models a quantity Q, where t is the number of days since the start of the study. By what percent does Q increase each day?$q$, $q$["44%","22%","72%","20%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Q is multiplied by 1.44 every 2 days. The one-day factor b satisfies b<sup>2</sup> = 1.44, so b = 1.2, which is a 20% increase each day. The value 44% is the change over 2 days, and dividing it by 2 ignores compounding.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h420$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + y² = 148<br>xy = 24<br><br>If x &gt; y &gt; 0, what is the value of x − y?$q$, $q$["100","√124","10","14"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$(x − y)² = x² − 2xy + y² = 148 − 2(24) = 100. Since x &gt; y, x − y is positive, so x − y = √100 = 10. The value 100 is (x − y)², not x − y, and 14 is x + y = √(148 + 2·24).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h421$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$x/(x − 3) − 8/(x − 1) = 6/((x − 3)(x − 1))<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−6","6","3","9"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiplying both sides by (x − 3)(x − 1) gives x(x − 1) − 8(x − 3) = 6, which simplifies to x² − 9x + 18 = 0, or (x − 3)(x − 6) = 0. The value x = 3 makes the denominator x − 3 equal 0, so it is extraneous. The only solution is 6, so the sum of all solutions is 6. Including the extraneous value gives 9.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h422$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$x/(x + 2) − 2/(x + 5) = −6/((x + 2)(x + 5))<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−1","−3","1","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying both sides by (x + 2)(x + 5) gives x(x + 5) − 2(x + 2) = −6, which simplifies to x² + 3x + 2 = 0, or (x + 2)(x + 1) = 0. The value x = −2 makes the denominator x + 2 equal 0, so it is extraneous. The only solution is −1, so the sum of all solutions is −1. Including the extraneous value gives −3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h423$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + y² = 250<br>xy = 117<br><br>If x &gt; y &gt; 0, what is the value of x − y?$q$, $q$["4","22","√133","16"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$(x − y)² = x² − 2xy + y² = 250 − 2(117) = 16. Since x &gt; y, x − y is positive, so x − y = √16 = 4. The value 16 is (x − y)², not x − y, and 22 is x + y = √(250 + 2·117).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h424$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 12x + 43. The function g is defined by g(x) = −4f(x + 4) + 7. What is the maximum value of g(x)?$q$, $q$["35","−21","−56","−165"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Completing the square, f(x) = (x + 6)² + 7, so f has a minimum value of 7. Multiplying by −4 turns that minimum into a maximum of −4(7) = −28, and adding 7 gives −21. The horizontal shift changes where the maximum occurs, not its value. Using the constant term 43 instead of the minimum value gives −165.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h425$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The power used by a heating element is directly proportional to the square of the current through it. If the current is increased by 50%, which of the following describes the change in the power?$q$, $q$["It increases by 100%.","It increases by 50%.","It increases by 125%.","It decreases by 55.56%."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The power is multiplied by (1.5)² = 2.25, which corresponds to an increase of 125%. A change of 50% treats the relationship as linear, ignoring the square.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h426$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f(t) = 200(1.44)<sup>t</sup> gives the population of a town t years after it was first measured. Which function g gives the same quantity h half-years after it was first measured?$q$, $q$["g(h) = 200(1.2)<sup>h/2</sup>","g(h) = 200(1.44)<sup>2h</sup>","g(h) = 200(1.2)<sup>h</sup>","g(h) = 200(0.72)<sup>h</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$There are 2 half-years in a year, so t = h/2. Then f = 200(1.44)<sup>h/2</sup> = 200((1.44)<sup>1/2</sup>)<sup>h</sup> = 200(1.2)<sup>h</sup>, because 1.2² = 1.44. Writing (1.44)<sup>2h</sup> uses t = 2h, which reverses the conversion, and halving 1.44 does not halve the growth rate.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h427$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² + 12x + 38. The function g is defined by g(x) = −2f(x + 4) − 8. What is the maximum value of g(x)?$q$, $q$["−12","12","−4","−84"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Completing the square, f(x) = (x + 6)² + 2, so f has a minimum value of 2. Multiplying by −2 turns that minimum into a maximum of −2(2) = −4, and adding −8 gives −12. The horizontal shift changes where the maximum occurs, not its value. Using the constant term 38 instead of the minimum value gives −84.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h428$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$(1/3)<sup>x − 5</sup> = 9<sup>x − 4</sup> · 9<br><br>What value of x satisfies the given equation?$q$, $q$["13/3","1","11/3","1/3"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each side as a power of 3: 1/3 = 3<sup>−1</sup>, 9 = 3<sup>2</sup>, and 9 = 3<sup>2</sup>. The equation becomes 3<sup>−1(x − 5)</sup> = 3<sup>2(x − 4) + 2</sup>, so −x + 5 = 2x − 6. Then 3x = 11 and x = 11/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h429$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has its vertex at (4, −1) and passes through the point (1, −10). What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["15","−1","−5","−17"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$In vertex form, f(x) = a(x − 4)² + (−1). Substituting (1, −10): −10 = a(−3)² + (−1), so a = −1. Then f(0) = −1(−4)² + (−1) = −17. The vertex's y-coordinate −1 is the y-intercept only when the vertex is on the y-axis.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h430$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$x/(x − 6) − 1/(x − 3) = 18/((x − 6)(x − 3))<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["2","6","4","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Multiplying both sides by (x − 6)(x − 3) gives x(x − 3) − 1(x − 6) = 18, which simplifies to x² − 4x − 12 = 0, or (x − 6)(x + 2) = 0. The value x = 6 makes the denominator x − 6 equal 0, so it is extraneous. The only solution is −2, so the sum of all solutions is −2. Including the extraneous value gives 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h431$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f(t) = 500(1.69)<sup>t</sup> gives the number of members of a club t years after it was first measured. Which function g gives the same quantity h half-years after it was first measured?$q$, $q$["g(h) = 500(1.3)<sup>h/2</sup>","g(h) = 500(1.69)<sup>2h</sup>","g(h) = 500(1.3)<sup>h</sup>","g(h) = 500(0.845)<sup>h</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$There are 2 half-years in a year, so t = h/2. Then f = 500(1.69)<sup>h/2</sup> = 500((1.69)<sup>1/2</sup>)<sup>h</sup> = 500(1.3)<sup>h</sup>, because 1.3² = 1.69. Writing (1.69)<sup>2h</sup> uses t = 2h, which reverses the conversion, and halving 1.69 does not halve the growth rate.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h432$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has its vertex at (−4, 9) and passes through the point (−1, 27). What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["1","−23","9","41"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$In vertex form, f(x) = a(x + 4)² + 9. Substituting (−1, 27): 27 = a(3)² + 9, so a = 2. Then f(0) = 2(4)² + 9 = 41. The vertex's y-coordinate 9 is the y-intercept only when the vertex is on the y-axis.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h433$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = a(x + 4)² + 2<br>y = 4<br><br>In the given system of equations, a is a positive constant. The graphs of the equations in the xy-plane intersect at two points that are 2 units apart. What is the value of a?$q$, $q$["6","2","1/2","4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The two intersection points are symmetric about the axis x = −4, so each is 2/2 = 1 units from it. At x = −3, a(1)² + 2 = 4, so 1a = 2 and a = 2. Using the full distance 2 instead of half of it gives 1/2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h434$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = a(x + 5)² + 3<br>y = 8<br><br>In the given system of equations, a is a positive constant. The graphs of the equations in the xy-plane intersect at two points that are 4 units apart. What is the value of a?$q$, $q$["11/4","2","5/2","5/4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The two intersection points are symmetric about the axis x = −5, so each is 4/2 = 2 units from it. At x = −3, a(2)² + 3 = 8, so 4a = 5 and a = 5/4. Using the full distance 4 instead of half of it gives 5/16.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h435$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The power used by a heating element is directly proportional to the square of the current through it. If the current is decreased by 20%, which of the following describes the change in the power?$q$, $q$["It decreases by 20%.","It increases by 56.25%.","It decreases by 36%.","It decreases by 40%."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The power is multiplied by (0.8)² = 0.64, which corresponds to a decrease of 36%. A change of 20% treats the relationship as linear, ignoring the square.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h436$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function Q(t) = 120(2.25)<sup>t/2</sup> models a quantity Q, where t is the number of days since the start of the study. By what percent does Q increase each day?$q$, $q$["125%","50%","112.5%","62.5%"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Q is multiplied by 2.25 every 2 days. The one-day factor b satisfies b<sup>2</sup> = 2.25, so b = 1.5, which is a 50% increase each day. The value 125% is the change over 2 days, and dividing it by 2 ignores compounding.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h437$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has x-intercepts at (−3, 0) and (9, 0) and a y-intercept at (0, −27). What is the minimum value of f?$q$, $q$["3","−27","−36","36"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$From the x-intercepts, f(x) = a(x + 3)(x − 9) for some constant a. Since f(0) = −27, a(3)(−9) = −27, so a = 1. The vertex lies halfway between the intercepts, at x = 3, so the minimum value is f(3) = 1(6)(−6) = −36. Assuming a = 1 gives −36.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h438$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = a(x + 1)² − 2<br>y = 10<br><br>In the given system of equations, a is a positive constant. The graphs of the equations in the xy-plane intersect at two points that are 4 units apart. What is the value of a?$q$, $q$["5/2","3","6","3/4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The two intersection points are symmetric about the axis x = −1, so each is 4/2 = 2 units from it. At x = 1, a(2)² − 2 = 10, so 4a = 12 and a = 3. Using the full distance 4 instead of half of it gives 3/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h439$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$4x² + 6x − 3 = 0<br><br>The solutions to the given equation are r and s. What is the value of r² + s²?$q$, $q$["33/4","15/4","3/4","9/4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sum of the solutions is r + s = −3/2 and the product is rs = −3/4. Since r² + s² = (r + s)² − 2rs, r² + s² = (−3/2)² − 2(−3/4) = 9/4 + 3/2 = 15/4. The value 9/4 is (r + s)², which still includes the 2rs term.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h440$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = 2x + 9 and f(g(x)) = 6x² + 4x − 3. What is the value of g(3)?$q$, $q$["63","135","27","36"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$f(g(3)) = 63² + 43 − 3 = 63. Since f(g(3)) = 2·g(3) + 9, 2·g(3) = 54, so g(3) = 27. The value 63 is f(g(3)), not g(3).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h441$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has x-intercepts at (−5, 0) and (3, 0) and a y-intercept at (0, −30). What is the minimum value of f?$q$, $q$["−30","−16","−32","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$From the x-intercepts, f(x) = a(x + 5)(x − 3) for some constant a. Since f(0) = −30, a(5)(−3) = −30, so a = 2. The vertex lies halfway between the intercepts, at x = −1, so the minimum value is f(−1) = 2(4)(−4) = −32. Assuming a = 1 gives −16.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h442$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has its vertex at (−2, −4) and passes through the point (1, 5). What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["−6","0","−4","−8"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In vertex form, f(x) = a(x + 2)² + (−4). Substituting (1, 5): 5 = a(3)² + (−4), so a = 1. Then f(0) = 1(2)² + (−4) = 0. The vertex's y-coordinate −4 is the y-intercept only when the vertex is on the y-axis.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h443$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$3x² + 10x − 12 = 0<br><br>The solutions to the given equation are r and s. What is the value of r² + s²?$q$, $q$["28/9","100/9","316/9","172/9"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sum of the solutions is r + s = −10/3 and the product is rs = −4. Since r² + s² = (r + s)² − 2rs, r² + s² = (−10/3)² − 2(−4) = 100/9 + 8 = 172/9. The value 100/9 is (r + s)², which still includes the 2rs term.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h444$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$x/(x + 1) + 7/(x − 4) = 5/((x + 1)(x − 4))<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−2","2","−1","−3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying both sides by (x + 1)(x − 4) gives x(x − 4) + 7(x + 1) = 5, which simplifies to x² + 3x + 2 = 0, or (x + 1)(x + 2) = 0. The value x = −1 makes the denominator x + 1 equal 0, so it is extraneous. The only solution is −2, so the sum of all solutions is −2. Including the extraneous value gives −3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h445$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = 3x² − 12x − 36. In the xy-plane, the graph of y = f(x) + k, where k is a constant, has exactly one x-intercept. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["48","12","192","−48"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(x) = 3(x + 2)(x − 6), so its vertex is at x = 2, and its minimum value is 3(4)(−4) = −48. The graph of y = f(x) + k touches the x-axis exactly once when the minimum is shifted up to 0, so k = 48.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h446$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has its vertex at (5, 8) and passes through the point (3, −4). What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["−7","8","83","−67"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$In vertex form, f(x) = a(x − 5)² + 8. Substituting (3, −4): −4 = a(−2)² + 8, so a = −3. Then f(0) = −3(−5)² + 8 = −67. The vertex's y-coordinate 8 is the y-intercept only when the vertex is on the y-axis.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h447$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function Q(t) = 2400(0.5625)<sup>t/2</sup> models a quantity Q, where t is the number of weeks since the start of the study. By what percent does Q decrease each week?$q$, $q$["71.88%","21.88%","25%","43.75%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Q is multiplied by 0.5625 every 2 weeks. The one-week factor b satisfies b<sup>2</sup> = 0.5625, so b = 0.75, which is a 25% decrease each week. The value 43.75% is the change over 2 weeks, and dividing it by 2 ignores compounding.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h448$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has its vertex at (2, 6) and passes through the point (1, 7). What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["10","6","2","8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In vertex form, f(x) = a(x − 2)² + 6. Substituting (1, 7): 7 = a(−1)² + 6, so a = 1. Then f(0) = 1(−2)² + 6 = 10. The vertex's y-coordinate 6 is the y-intercept only when the vertex is on the y-axis.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h449$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The kinetic energy of a moving cart is directly proportional to the square of its speed. If the cart's speed is increased by 30%, which of the following describes the change in its kinetic energy?$q$, $q$["It increases by 69%.","It decreases by 40.83%.","It increases by 30%.","It increases by 60%."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Its kinetic energy is multiplied by (1.3)² = 1.69, which corresponds to an increase of 69%. A change of 30% treats the relationship as linear, ignoring the square.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h450$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² − 12x + 6 = 0<br><br>The solutions to the given equation are r and s. What is the value of r² + s²?$q$, $q$["156","144","132","138"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sum of the solutions is r + s = 12 and the product is rs = 6. Since r² + s² = (r + s)² − 2rs, r² + s² = (12)² − 2(6) = 144 − 12 = 132. The value 144 is (r + s)², which still includes the 2rs term.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h451$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has its vertex at (−1, 6) and passes through the point (−4, −12). What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["−8","8","6","4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$In vertex form, f(x) = a(x + 1)² + 6. Substituting (−4, −12): −12 = a(−3)² + 6, so a = −2. Then f(0) = −2(1)² + 6 = 4. The vertex's y-coordinate 6 is the y-intercept only when the vertex is on the y-axis.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h452$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 10x + 18. The function g is defined by g(x) = −4f(x − 3) + 5. What is the maximum value of g(x)?$q$, $q$["8","−23","−67","33"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Completing the square, f(x) = (x − 5)² − 7, so f has a minimum value of −7. Multiplying by −4 turns that minimum into a maximum of −4((−7)) = 28, and adding 5 gives 33. The horizontal shift changes where the maximum occurs, not its value. Using the constant term 18 instead of the minimum value gives −67.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h453$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The area of a square tile is proportional to the square of its side length. If the side length is increased by 20%, which of the following describes the change in the area?$q$, $q$["It increases by 40%.","It decreases by 30.56%.","It increases by 20%.","It increases by 44%."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The area is multiplied by (1.2)² = 1.44, which corresponds to an increase of 44%. A change of 20% treats the relationship as linear, ignoring the square.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h454$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$(1/81)<sup>x + 3</sup> = 27<sup>x − 1</sup> · (1/9)<br><br>What value of x satisfies the given equation?$q$, $q$["−1","−17","−9/7","17/7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rewrite each side as a power of 3: 1/81 = 3<sup>−4</sup>, 27 = 3<sup>3</sup>, and (1/9) = 3<sup>−2</sup>. The equation becomes 3<sup>−4(x + 3)</sup> = 3<sup>3(x − 1) + (−2)</sup>, so −4x − 12 = 3x − 5. Then 7x = −7 and x = −1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h455$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$x/(x − 3) − 2/(x + 4) = 21/((x − 3)(x + 4))<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["5","−2","−5","3"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Multiplying both sides by (x − 3)(x + 4) gives x(x + 4) − 2(x − 3) = 21, which simplifies to x² + 2x − 15 = 0, or (x − 3)(x + 5) = 0. The value x = 3 makes the denominator x − 3 equal 0, so it is extraneous. The only solution is −5, so the sum of all solutions is −5. Including the extraneous value gives −2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h456$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = 3x + 4 and f(g(x)) = 9x² + 9x − 5. What is the value of g(−1)?$q$, $q$["−1/3","−11","−5","−3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$f(g(−1)) = 9(−1)² + 9(−1) − 5 = −5. Since f(g(−1)) = 3·g(−1) + 4, 3·g(−1) = −9, so g(−1) = −3. The value −5 is f(g(−1)), not g(−1).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h457$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$(1/25)<sup>x − 2</sup> = 125<sup>x − 1</sup> · 25<br><br>What value of x satisfies the given equation?$q$, $q$["1","−3/5","−3","7/5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rewrite each side as a power of 5: 1/25 = 5<sup>−2</sup>, 125 = 5<sup>3</sup>, and 25 = 5<sup>2</sup>. The equation becomes 5<sup>−2(x − 2)</sup> = 5<sup>3(x − 1) + 2</sup>, so −2x + 4 = 3x − 1. Then 5x = 5 and x = 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h458$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The graph of the quadratic function f in the xy-plane has its vertex at (−5, −6) and passes through the point (−6, −7). What is the y-coordinate of the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["19","−6","−31","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$In vertex form, f(x) = a(x + 5)² + (−6). Substituting (−6, −7): −7 = a(−1)² + (−6), so a = −1. Then f(0) = −1(5)² + (−6) = −31. The vertex's y-coordinate −6 is the y-intercept only when the vertex is on the y-axis.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h459$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$(1/5)<sup>x + 1</sup> = 125<sup>x − 4</sup> · (1/5)<br><br>What value of x satisfies the given equation?$q$, $q$["7/2","11/4","7","3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Rewrite each side as a power of 5: 1/5 = 5<sup>−1</sup>, 125 = 5<sup>3</sup>, and (1/5) = 5<sup>−1</sup>. The equation becomes 5<sup>−1(x + 1)</sup> = 5<sup>3(x − 4) + (−1)</sup>, so −x − 1 = 3x − 13. Then 4x = 12 and x = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h460$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$2x² − 8x − 3 = 0<br><br>The solutions to the given equation are r and s. What is the value of r² + s²?$q$, $q$["19","13","16","22"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sum of the solutions is r + s = 4 and the product is rs = −3/2. Since r² + s² = (r + s)² − 2rs, r² + s² = (4)² − 2(−3/2) = 16 + 3 = 19. The value 16 is (r + s)², which still includes the 2rs term.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h461$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = a(x − 1)² − 9<br>y = 7<br><br>In the given system of equations, a is a positive constant. The graphs of the equations in the xy-plane intersect at two points that are 8 units apart. What is the value of a?$q$, $q$["−1/8","1/4","1","4"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The two intersection points are symmetric about the axis x = 1, so each is 8/2 = 4 units from it. At x = 5, a(4)² − 9 = 7, so 16a = 16 and a = 1. Using the full distance 8 instead of half of it gives 1/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h462$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f(t) = 250(1.21)<sup>t</sup> gives the population of a town t years after it was first measured. Which function g gives the same quantity h half-years after it was first measured?$q$, $q$["g(h) = 250(0.605)<sup>h</sup>","g(h) = 250(1.1)<sup>h/2</sup>","g(h) = 250(1.21)<sup>2h</sup>","g(h) = 250(1.1)<sup>h</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are 2 half-years in a year, so t = h/2. Then f = 250(1.21)<sup>h/2</sup> = 250((1.21)<sup>1/2</sup>)<sup>h</sup> = 250(1.1)<sup>h</sup>, because 1.1² = 1.21. Writing (1.21)<sup>2h</sup> uses t = 2h, which reverses the conversion, and halving 1.21 does not halve the growth rate.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h463$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function Q(t) = 300(0.49)<sup>t/2</sup> models the amount Q of a substance, where t is the number of days since the start of the study. By what percent does Q decrease each day?$q$, $q$["25.5%","51%","30%","75.5%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Q is multiplied by 0.49 every 2 days. The one-day factor b satisfies b<sup>2</sup> = 0.49, so b = 0.7, which is a 30% decrease each day. The value 51% is the change over 2 days, and dividing it by 2 ignores compounding.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h464$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f(t) = 1500(0.64)<sup>t</sup> gives the number of subscribers to a newsletter t years after it was first measured. Which function g gives the same quantity h half-years after it was first measured?$q$, $q$["g(h) = 1500(0.8)<sup>h/2</sup>","g(h) = 1500(0.32)<sup>h</sup>","g(h) = 1500(0.64)<sup>2h</sup>","g(h) = 1500(0.8)<sup>h</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are 2 half-years in a year, so t = h/2. Then f = 1500(0.64)<sup>h/2</sup> = 1500((0.64)<sup>1/2</sup>)<sup>h</sup> = 1500(0.8)<sup>h</sup>, because 0.8² = 0.64. Writing (0.64)<sup>2h</sup> uses t = 2h, which reverses the conversion, and halving 0.64 does not halve the growth rate.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h465$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$3x² + x − 9 = 0<br><br>The solutions to the given equation are r and s. What is the value of r² + s²?$q$, $q$["−53/9","163/9","55/9","1/9"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sum of the solutions is r + s = −1/3 and the product is rs = −3. Since r² + s² = (r + s)² − 2rs, r² + s² = (−1/3)² − 2(−3) = 1/9 + 6 = 55/9. The value 1/9 is (r + s)², which still includes the 2rs term.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h466$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The power used by a heating element is directly proportional to the square of the current through it. If the current is increased by 10%, which of the following describes the change in the power?$q$, $q$["It increases by 20%.","It decreases by 17.36%.","It increases by 10%.","It increases by 21%."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The power is multiplied by (1.1)² = 1.21, which corresponds to an increase of 21%. A change of 10% treats the relationship as linear, ignoring the square.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h467$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function Q(t) = 600(2.25)<sup>t/2</sup> models the value Q of an account, where t is the number of weeks since the start of the study. By what percent does Q increase each week?$q$, $q$["125%","112.5%","62.5%","50%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Q is multiplied by 2.25 every 2 weeks. The one-week factor b satisfies b<sup>2</sup> = 2.25, so b = 1.5, which is a 50% increase each week. The value 125% is the change over 2 weeks, and dividing it by 2 ignores compounding.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h468$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + y² = 80<br>xy = 32<br><br>If x &gt; y &gt; 0, what is the value of x − y?$q$, $q$["4","12","16","√48"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$(x − y)² = x² − 2xy + y² = 80 − 2(32) = 16. Since x &gt; y, x − y is positive, so x − y = √16 = 4. The value 16 is (x − y)², not x − y, and 12 is x + y = √(80 + 2·32).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h469$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$3x² + 9x − 7 = 0<br><br>The solutions to the given equation are r and s. What is the value of r² + s²?$q$, $q$["9","41/3","13/3","23"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sum of the solutions is r + s = −3 and the product is rs = −7/3. Since r² + s² = (r + s)² − 2rs, r² + s² = (−3)² − 2(−7/3) = 9 + 14/3 = 41/3. The value 9 is (r + s)², which still includes the 2rs term.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h470$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = 5x + 6 and f(g(x)) = 10x² − 20x + 16. What is the value of g(−3)?$q$, $q$["32","172/5","166","836"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(g(−3)) = 10(−3)² − 20(−3) + 16 = 166. Since f(g(−3)) = 5·g(−3) + 6, 5·g(−3) = 160, so g(−3) = 32. The value 166 is f(g(−3)), not g(−3).$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-1$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A car travels 270 miles using 9 gallons of gas. What is the car's fuel efficiency, in miles per gallon?$q$, $q$["30","27","9","270"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$270 miles ÷ 9 gallons = 30 miles per gallon.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-2$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$In a class of 32 students, the ratio of boys to girls is 3:5. How many girls are in the class?$q$, $q$["20","12","15","17"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ratio has 8 total parts. Girls = (5/8) · 32 = 20.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-3$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A stock rose in value from $40 to $52. What was the percent increase?$q$, $q$["30%","12%","23%","33%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Percent increase = (52 − 40)/40 × 100 = 12/40 × 100 = 30%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-4$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$What is 20% of 60% of 500?$q$, $q$["60","100","120","50"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$60% of 500 = 300. 20% of 300 = 60.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-5$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A data set is 3, 5, 5, 7, 9, 50. Which measure of center is most affected by the value 50?$q$, $q$["The mean","The median","The mode","Neither the mean nor the median"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The mean is sensitive to extreme values (outliers), while the median, based on position, changes little when one extreme value is present.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-6$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$Which of the following data sets has the greatest standard deviation?$q$, $q$["{1, 10, 10, 19}","{10, 10, 10, 10}","{5, 10, 10, 15}","{9, 10, 10, 11}"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Standard deviation measures spread from the mean. {1, 10, 10, 19} has the widest spread of values around its mean, giving it the greatest standard deviation.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-7$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot of study hours versus test score has a line of best fit given by y = 5x + 60, where x is hours studied and y is the test score. What does the slope of this line represent?$q$, $q$["Each additional hour of study is associated with an increase of 5 points on the test.","A student who studies 0 hours is predicted to score 5 points.","Each additional point on the test requires 5 more hours of study.","The maximum possible test score is 5."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a linear model y = mx + b, the slope m represents the change in y per unit increase in x — here, 5 additional points per additional hour studied.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-8$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A bag contains 4 red, 3 blue, and 5 green marbles. What is the probability of randomly drawing a blue marble?$q$, $q$["1/4","1/3","3/5","1/12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 12 total marbles, and 3 are blue, so the probability is 3/12 = 1/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-9$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 90 people included 40 from Group A and 50 from Group B. 30% of Group A and 80% of Group B answered "yes" to a question. If a person who answered "yes" is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is from Group A?$q$, $q$["10/13","2/15","3/10","3/13"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Group A had 30% of 40 = 12 "yes" responses, and Group B had 80% of 50 = 40 "yes" responses, for a total of 52 "yes" responses. The probability the person is from Group A is 12/52 = 3/13.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-10$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A poll of 400 randomly selected voters found that 220 favor a proposal. Based on this sample, what is the best estimate of the percentage of all voters who favor the proposal?$q$, $q$["55%","220%","400%","45%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$220/400 = 0.55 = 55%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-11$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 1, null, $q$Which change would most directly decrease the margin of error of a survey estimate?$q$, $q$["Increasing the sample size","Decreasing the sample size","Asking a more biased question","Surveying fewer people from the population"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Margin of error generally decreases as sample size increases, because larger samples produce more precise estimates of the population.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-12$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A student scored 80 on a test worth 30% of the final grade and 90 on a test worth 70% of the final grade. What is the student's weighted average?$q$, $q$["87","85","88","86"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$0.30(80) + 0.70(90) = 24 + 63 = 87.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-13$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A printer prints 45 pages in 3 minutes at a constant rate. How many pages will it print in 20 minutes?$q$, $q$["300","45","225","60"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rate = 45/3 = 15 pages per minute. In 20 minutes: 15 × 20 = 300 pages.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-14$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A recipe that serves 4 people requires 250 grams of flour. How many grams of flour are needed to serve 10 people at the same ratio?$q$, $q$["625","600","1000","400"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$250 × (10/4) = 250 × 2.5 = 625 grams.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-15$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A study concludes that 'most people exercise regularly' based on a survey of visitors to a gym. What is the main flaw in this conclusion?$q$, $q$["The sample is not representative of the general population.","The sample size is too large to be useful.","Gym visitors cannot be surveyed accurately.","Exercise habits cannot be measured through surveys."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Surveying only gym visitors overrepresents people who already exercise, so the sample cannot be generalized to the population as a whole.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-16$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$The probability that a certain machine produces a defective part is 5/10 on any given run, independent of other runs. What is the probability that at least one of the next 3 parts is defective?$q$, $q$["1/2","7/8","1/8","3/2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The probability a single part is NOT defective is 1/2. For 3 independent runs, P(no defects) = (1/2)<sup>3</sup> = 125/1000. So P(at least one defective) = 1 − 125/1000 = 7/8.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-17$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$After a 20% discount, a shirt costs $32. What was the shirt's original price?$q$, $q$["$40","$38.40","$44","$36"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If the original price is p, then 0.80p = 32 → p = 40.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-18$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$In a scatterplot with a line of best fit, a data point lies far above the line. What does this indicate about that data point?$q$, $q$["Its actual value is greater than the value predicted by the line.","Its actual value is less than the value predicted by the line.","It lies exactly on the line of best fit.","It cannot be used to evaluate the model."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A point above the line of best fit has an actual y-value greater than the model's predicted value at that x.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-19$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$An alloy is 40% copper by mass. How many kilograms of copper are in 25 kilograms of the alloy?$q$, $q$["10","15","4","40"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$0.40 × 25 = 10 kilograms.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-20$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A fair coin is flipped 3 times. What is the probability of getting at least one heads?$q$, $q$["7/8","1/8","3/8","1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(at least one heads) = 1 − P(no heads) = 1 − (1/2)³ = 1 − 1/8 = 7/8.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-21$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A data set has a mean of 70 and a standard deviation of 5. What value is 2 standard deviations above the mean?$q$, $q$["80","75","72","85"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$70 + 2(5) = 70 + 10 = 80.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-22$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$In a survey, 60 of 150 respondents preferred Option A. What percent of respondents preferred a different option?$q$, $q$["60%","40%","90%","30%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$150 − 60 = 90 respondents preferred another option. 90/150 = 0.60 = 60%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-23$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A distribution of household incomes is strongly right-skewed, with a small number of very high incomes. Which is most likely true of the mean and median?$q$, $q$["The mean is greater than the median.","The median is greater than the mean.","The mean and median are equal.","The relationship cannot be determined."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a right-skewed distribution, a few very high values pull the mean upward more than they affect the median, so the mean exceeds the median.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-24$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A car travels at 90 kilometers per hour. What is this speed in meters per second?$q$, $q$["25","15","30","90"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$90 km/h = 90,000 m / 3,600 s = 25 m/s.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-25$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A company wants to estimate the average commute time for all of its employees. Which sampling method would produce the most reliable estimate?$q$, $q$["Randomly selecting 200 employees from the full employee roster","Asking only employees who arrive earliest each day","Surveying employees who volunteer to respond","Asking only employees in the company's headquarters office"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A random sample drawn from the entire employee roster is representative of the whole population, unlike the biased methods in the other choices.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-26$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The line of best fit for a data set is y = −6x + 89. For a data point where x = 7, the actual y-value observed is 41. What is the residual (actual minus predicted) for this point?$q$, $q$["47","−6","41","6"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The predicted value is y = −6(7) + 89 = 47. The residual is the actual value minus the predicted value: 41 − 47 = −6.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-27$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 180 people included 120 from Group A and 60 from Group B. 75% of Group A and 60% of Group B answered "yes" to a question (the rest answered "no"). If a person who answered "no" is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is from Group B?$q$, $q$["4/9","2/5","5/9","2/15"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Group A had 120 − 90 = 30 "no" responses, and Group B had 60 − 36 = 24 "no" responses, for a total of 54 "no" responses. The probability the person is from Group B is 24/54 = 4/9.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-28$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The mean of 6 numbers is 49. When one more number is added to the set, the new mean of all 7 numbers is 50.429. What is the value of the number that was added?$q$, $q$["49","353","59","64"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The original sum is 6 × 49 = 294. The new sum is 7 × 50.429 = 353. The added number is 353 − 294 = 59.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-29$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A car travels at a constant speed of 20 miles per hour. Using 1 mile = 5,280 feet, what is the car's speed in feet per second, rounded to the nearest tenth?$q$, $q$["20","58.6","29","29.3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Convert: 20 miles/hour × 5,280 feet/mile ÷ 3,600 seconds/hour = 29.3 feet per second.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-30$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A map has a scale of 1 inch to 15 miles. If two cities are 4.5 inches apart on the map, how many miles apart are they in reality?$q$, $q$["67.5","60","75","54"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$4.5 inches × 15 miles per inch = 67.5 miles.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-31$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of a random sample estimates that 37% of a population supports a proposal, with a margin of error of 5 percentage points. Based on this survey, is it plausible that exactly 42% of the population supports the proposal?$q$, $q$["No, because the margin of error only applies to the mean","Yes, because it falls within the margin of error","Yes, because the sample size is large enough","Cannot be determined without more information"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The survey's plausible range is 37% ± 5, which is from 32% to 42%. Since 42% falls within this range, the claim is plausible based on this survey.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-32$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A store increases the price of an item by 20% and then applies a 20% discount to the new price. Compared to the original price, the final price is:$q$, $q$["4% less than the original price","The same as the original price","4% more than the original price","20% less than the original price"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying by 1.20 then 0.80 gives an overall factor of 0.96, which is 4% less than the original price.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-33$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A line of best fit models a data set with a slope of 7 units per hour. According to this model, by how much does the predicted value change as the number of hours increases from 28 to 38?$q$, $q$["−70","77","70","7"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The predicted value changes by the slope times the change in x: 7 × (38 − 28) = 7 × 10 = 70.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-34$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A school has a ratio of teachers to students of 2:35. If the school has 70 teachers, how many students does it have?$q$, $q$["1225","1190","1260","700"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ratio 2:35 scales by a factor of 70/2 = 35. So students = 35 × 35 = 1225.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-35$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A hose fills a 60-gallon tank in 15 minutes at a constant rate. How many gallons does it fill in 4 minutes?$q$, $q$["16","15","4","240"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rate is 60/15 = 4 gallons per minute. In 4 minutes: 4 × 4 = 16 gallons.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-36$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A laptop originally priced at $800 is on sale for 25% off. What is the sale price?$q$, $q$["$600","$700","$575","$625"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$25% off means paying 75% of the price: 0.75 × 800 = $600.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-37$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$The data set 12, 15, 15, 18, 20 has a mean of 16. What is the sum of the deviations of each value from the mean?$q$, $q$["0","16","5","80"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sum of deviations from the mean is always 0, since the mean is the balancing point of the data: (−4) + (−1) + (−1) + 2 + 4 = 0.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-38$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A scatterplot of weekly hours worked (x) versus weekly pay (y, in dollars) has a line of best fit given by y = 15x + 20. According to this model, what is the predicted weekly pay for someone who works 30 hours?$q$, $q$["$470","$450","$500","$420"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 30 into y = 15x + 20: y = 15(30) + 20 = 450 + 20 = 470, so the predicted weekly pay is $470.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-39$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A standard six-sided die is rolled once. What is the probability of rolling a number greater than 4?$q$, $q$["1/3","1/2","2/3","1/6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rolling a 5 or 6 satisfies the condition, giving 2 favorable outcomes out of 6: 2/6 = 1/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-40$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A group of 50 people includes 30 who own a car. Of the car owners, 18 also own a bike. What is the probability that a randomly selected car owner also owns a bike?$q$, $q$["3/5","18/50","3/10","5/3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(bike | car) = (car owners who also own a bike) / (total car owners) = 18/30 = 3/5.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-41$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 1, null, $q$A random sample of 250 shoppers found that 100 prefer online shopping over in-store shopping. Based on this sample, what percentage of all shoppers would be expected to prefer online shopping?$q$, $q$["40%","25%","60%","10%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$100/250 = 0.40 = 40%, so the sample suggests about 40% of all shoppers prefer online shopping.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-42$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 1, null, $q$A researcher wants to estimate the average height of adult men in a country. Which method would introduce the least bias?$q$, $q$["Randomly selecting adult men from across the entire country","Measuring only men on a professional basketball team","Measuring only men who visit a particular gym","Measuring only men in one small town"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A random sample drawn from the entire population avoids the bias introduced by selecting from a narrow or unrepresentative subgroup.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-43$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A paint mixture uses blue and yellow paint in a ratio of 5:3 to make green paint. If a batch uses 9 liters of yellow paint, how many total liters of green paint does the batch make?$q$, $q$["24","20","15","27"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Yellow is 3 parts and equals 9 liters, so each part is 3 liters. Blue is 5 parts = 15 liters. Total = 15 + 9 = 24 liters.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-44$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A cyclist travels 5 kilometers in 20 minutes at a constant speed. At this rate, how many kilometers will the cyclist travel in 1.5 hours?$q$, $q$["22.5","15","18.75","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rate is 5/20 = 0.25 kilometers per minute. In 90 minutes (1.5 hours): 0.25 × 90 = 22.5 kilometers.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-45$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A company's revenue increased by 15% in the first year and then decreased by 10% in the second year. What was the overall percent change in revenue over the two years?$q$, $q$["3.5% increase","5% increase","5% decrease","2.5% increase"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying by 1.15 and then 0.90 gives 1.15 × 0.90 = 1.035, an overall increase of 3.5%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-46$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$Two data sets have the same mean, but data set A has a standard deviation of 2 and data set B has a standard deviation of 8. Which statement is best supported?$q$, $q$["The values in data set B are more spread out from the mean than the values in data set A.","The values in data set A are more spread out from the mean than the values in data set B.","Data sets A and B have identical distributions.","Data set B has a smaller range than data set A."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A higher standard deviation indicates greater spread of values around the mean, so data set B's values are more spread out than data set A's.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-47$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot shows a strong negative linear association between the age of a car (x, in years) and its resale value (y, in dollars), modeled by y = 18000 − 1200x. Based on this model, what is the predicted resale value of a car that is 6 years old?$q$, $q$["$10800","$12000","$9600","$7200"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 6: y = 18000 − 1200(6) = 18000 − 7200 = $10800.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-48$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A spinner is divided into 8 equal sections numbered 1 through 8. What is the probability that a single spin lands on an even number or a number greater than 6?$q$, $q$["5/8","3/4","1/2","7/8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Even numbers: {2, 4, 6, 8}. Numbers greater than 6: {7, 8}. Combined without double-counting 8: {2, 4, 6, 7, 8}, which is 5 outcomes out of 8, so the probability is 5/8.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-49$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 300 customers found that 180 bought a beverage. Of those who bought a beverage, 45 also bought a snack. Of the 120 customers who did not buy a beverage, 30 bought a snack. What is the probability that a randomly selected customer who bought a snack also bought a beverage?$q$, $q$["3/5","45/180","45/300","30/75"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total snack buyers = 45 + 30 = 75. Of those, 45 also bought a beverage, so the probability is 45/75 = 3/5.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-50$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 500 residents found that 340 support building a new park, with a margin of error of 4 percentage points. Which of the following is the most reasonable conclusion about all residents?$q$, $q$["Between about 64% and 72% of all residents likely support the new park.","Exactly 68% of all residents support the new park.","At least 72% of all residents support the new park.","Fewer than 64% of all residents support the new park."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sample estimate is 340/500 = 68%. With a margin of error of 4 percentage points, the plausible range for the population is about 64% to 72%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-51$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A news article claims that 'eating chocolate causes people to live longer,' based on an observational study that found chocolate eaters had longer average lifespans than non-eaters. What is the main flaw in this claim?$q$, $q$["The observational study shows correlation, not that chocolate causes longer life, since other factors may explain the link.","Observational studies can never be used to detect any association between variables.","The lifespans of chocolate eaters and non-eaters cannot be compared at all.","The claim would only be flawed if the sample size were smaller than 100."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An observational study can show an association, but without random assignment it cannot establish that chocolate causes longer life; other factors (confounding variables) could explain the pattern.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-52$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$In a mixture, the ratio of sand to gravel to cement is 6:3:7. If the total mixture weighs 80 kilograms, how many kilograms of gravel are in the mixture?$q$, $q$["35","20","30","15"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The ratio parts total 6 + 3 + 7 = 16, so each part weighs 80/16 = 5 kilograms. Gravel makes up 3 parts, so it weighs 3 × 5 = 15 kilograms.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-53$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$Pump A fills a tank at a rate of 3 liters per hour. Pump B fills the same tank at a rate of 2 liters every 10 minutes. Working together, how many liters do the two pumps fill in 6 hours?$q$, $q$["18","84","90","105"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Pump B's rate is 2 liters per 10 minutes = 12 liters per hour. Together, the pumps fill 3 + 12 = 15 liters per hour. In 6 hours, they fill 15 × 6 = 90 liters.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-54$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A value starts at 100. It first increases by 50%, and then the new value decreases by 20%. What is the overall percent change from the original value, to the nearest tenth of a percent?$q$, $q$["25","−30","20","30"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$After the increase, the value is 100 × 1.5 = 150. After the decrease, it is 150 × 0.8 = 120. The overall change is (120 − 100)/100 × 100% = +20%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-55$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A class of 18 students has an average test score of 72, and another class of 17 students has an average test score of 61. What is the average test score of all the students combined, rounded to the nearest tenth?$q$, $q$["66.7","67.7","66.5","67"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The combined total is 18(72) + 17(61) = 2333. Dividing by the total number of students, 18 + 17 = 35, gives an average of 2333/35 ≈ 66.7.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-56$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The line of best fit for a data set is y = −3x + 158. For a data point where x = 35, the actual y-value observed is 68. What is the residual (actual minus predicted) for this point?$q$, $q$["68","15","53","−15"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The predicted value is y = −3(35) + 158 = 53. The residual is the actual value minus the predicted value: 68 − 53 = 15.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-57$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A bag contains 6 red marbles and 4 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn at random, one after another, without replacement. What is the probability that both marbles are red?$q$, $q$["1/3","3/5","25/81","9/25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(first red) = 6/10. Without replacement, P(second red | first red) = 5/9. So P(both red) = (6/10) × (5/9) = 1/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-58$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 170 people included 50 from Group A and 120 from Group B. 75% of Group A and 80% of Group B answered "yes" to a question. If a person who answered "yes" is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is from Group A?$q$, $q$["19/85","19/25","48/67","19/67"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Group A had 75% of 50 = 38 "yes" responses, and Group B had 80% of 120 = 96 "yes" responses, for a total of 134 "yes" responses. The probability the person is from Group A is 38/134 = 19/67.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-59$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample estimates that 56% of a population favors a policy, with a margin of error of 5 percentage points. What is the smallest percentage that is within the margin of error of this estimate?$q$, $q$["56","61","46","51"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The margin of error gives a range from 56% − 5% to 56% + 5%, that is, from 51% to 61%. The smallest percentage in this range is 51%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-60$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A backpack originally priced at $65 is on sale for 20% off. What is the sale price?$q$, $q$["$52","$45","$78","$13"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$20% of $65 is $13, so the sale price is $65 − $13 = $52.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-61$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A fruit basket contains apples and oranges in a ratio of 4:7. If the basket has 44 pieces of fruit in total, how many oranges are in the basket?$q$, $q$["28","16","22","33"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ratio has 11 total parts, so each part is 44/11 = 4. Oranges = 7 × 4 = 28.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-62$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A conveyor belt moves 180 boxes in 12 minutes at a constant rate. How many boxes does it move in 5 minutes?$q$, $q$["75","36","60","90"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rate is 180/12 = 15 boxes per minute. In 5 minutes: 15 × 5 = 75 boxes.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-63$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A basketball player scores 12, 15, 15, 18, and 20 points in five games. What is the mode of this data set?$q$, $q$["15","16","18","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The mode is the value that appears most often. 15 appears twice, more than any other value.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-64$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A drawer contains 6 black socks, 4 white socks, and 2 gray socks. What is the probability of randomly selecting a white sock?$q$, $q$["1/3","1/2","1/6","1/4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 12 total socks, and 4 are white, so the probability is 4/12 = 1/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-65$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A scatterplot of daily hours of sunlight (x) versus plant height after one month, in centimeters (y), has a line of best fit given by y = 2x + 5. According to this model, what is the predicted height of a plant that receives 10 hours of sunlight per day?$q$, $q$["25 cm","20 cm","15 cm","30 cm"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 10 into y = 2x + 5: y = 2(10) + 5 = 25 cm.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-66$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 1, null, $q$Of 40 people surveyed, 25 like coffee. Of those who like coffee, 10 also like tea. What is the probability that a randomly selected coffee drinker also likes tea?$q$, $q$["10/25","10/40","15/25","25/40"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(tea | coffee) = (coffee drinkers who also like tea) / (total coffee drinkers) = 10/25.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-67$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 1, null, $q$A librarian wants to estimate how many books, on average, all students at a school read per year. Which method would produce the least biased estimate?$q$, $q$["Randomly selecting students from the entire school enrollment list","Surveying only students who visit the library every day","Surveying only students on the honor roll","Surveying only members of the after-school book club"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A random sample drawn from the entire student enrollment avoids the bias introduced by selecting only from groups that read more than average.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-68$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 1, null, $q$A random sample of 150 students found that 45 prefer online classes over in-person classes. Based on this sample, what percentage of all students would be expected to prefer online classes?$q$, $q$["30%","45%","15%","35%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$45/150 = 0.30 = 30%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-69$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A phone plan costs $80 per month. The price increases by 10%. What is the new monthly cost?$q$, $q$["$88","$90","$72","$8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A 10% increase on $80 is 0.10 × 80 = $8, so the new cost is $80 + $8 = $88.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-70$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$The high temperatures, in degrees Fahrenheit, recorded over a week are 60, 65, 68, 70, 72, 75, and 90. What is the range of this data set?$q$, $q$["30","15","20","90"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The range is the greatest value minus the least value: 90 − 60 = 30.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-71$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A charity's yearly budget is split between programs and administration in a ratio of 9:1. If the total budget is $50,000, how much is spent on administration?$q$, $q$["$5,000","$45,000","$10,000","$9,000"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ratio has 10 total parts, so each part is $50,000/10 = $5,000. Administration gets 1 part, or $5,000.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-72$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A runner completes a 10-kilometer race in 50 minutes at a constant pace. At this pace, how many minutes would it take the runner to complete 15 kilometers?$q$, $q$["75","65","90","60"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pace is 50/10 = 5 minutes per kilometer. For 15 kilometers: 5 × 15 = 75 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-73$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A phone's battery charge drops from 80% to 60%. What is the percent decrease, relative to the original 80%?$q$, $q$["25%","20%","33%","15%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Percent decrease = (80 − 60)/80 × 100 = 20/80 × 100 = 25%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-74$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot shows the relationship between monthly advertising spending (x, in thousands of dollars) and monthly sales (y, in thousands of dollars) for a business, modeled by the line of best fit y = 3x + 12. According to this model, what are the predicted monthly sales when advertising spending is $8,000?$q$, $q$["$36,000","$24,000","$44,000","$20,000"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 8 into y = 3x + 12: y = 3(8) + 12 = 36, so predicted sales are $36,000.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-75$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A fair six-sided die is rolled twice. What is the probability that the sum of the two rolls is 7?$q$, $q$["1/6","1/12","1/9","1/36"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 36 equally likely outcomes. The sums of 7 come from (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), (6,1): 6 outcomes, so the probability is 6/36 = 1/6.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-76$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A set of 6 exam scores has a mean of 82. If the lowest score, 52, is removed, what is the mean of the remaining 5 scores?$q$, $q$["88","82","76","90"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The original sum is 6 × 82 = 492. Removing 52 leaves a sum of 440, and 440/5 = 88.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-77$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 250 grocery shoppers found that 150 bought produce. Of those who bought produce, 60 also bought a bakery item. Of the 100 shoppers who did not buy produce, 20 bought a bakery item. What is the probability that a randomly selected customer who bought a bakery item also bought produce?$q$, $q$["3/4","60/150","60/250","20/80"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total bakery-item buyers = 60 + 20 = 80. Of those, 60 also bought produce, so the probability is 60/80 = 3/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-78$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A news report claims that 'taking vitamin supplements prevents colds,' based on an observational study in which supplement-takers had fewer colds than non-takers. What is the main flaw in this claim?$q$, $q$["The observational study shows correlation, not that supplements cause fewer colds, since other factors may explain the link.","Observational studies can never reveal any association between variables.","Colds cannot be counted accurately in a study.","The claim would only be flawed if fewer than 100 people were studied."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An observational study can show an association, but without random assignment it cannot establish that supplements cause fewer colds; other factors (confounding variables) could explain the pattern.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-79$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 600 residents found that 210 favor building a new library, with a margin of error of 4 percentage points. Which is the most reasonable conclusion about all residents?$q$, $q$["Between about 31% and 39% of all residents likely favor the new library.","Exactly 35% of all residents favor the new library.","At least 39% of all residents favor the new library.","Fewer than 31% of all residents favor the new library."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sample estimate is 210/600 = 35%. With a margin of error of 4 percentage points, the plausible range for the population is about 31% to 39%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-80$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A trail mix is made from peanuts, raisins, and chocolate chips in a ratio of 3:2:1 by weight. If a batch contains 15 ounces of peanuts, how many total ounces of trail mix does the batch contain?$q$, $q$["30","25","20","36"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Peanuts are 3 parts and equal 15 ounces, so each part is 5 ounces. Raisins = 2 × 5 = 10 ounces, chocolate chips = 1 × 5 = 5 ounces. Total = 15 + 10 + 5 = 30 ounces.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-81$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$Pump A fills a pool at 200 liters per hour, and Pump B fills the same pool at 150 liters per hour. If both pumps run together at their constant rates, how many minutes will it take to fill a 1,750-liter pool?$q$, $q$["300","5","525","700"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Combined rate = 200 + 150 = 350 liters per hour. Time = 1,750/350 = 5 hours = 300 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-82$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A city's population grew by 25% over one decade and then declined by 12% over the next decade. What was the overall percent change in population over the two decades?$q$, $q$["10% increase","13% increase","3% decrease","10% decrease"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying by 1.25 and then 0.88 gives 1.25 × 0.88 = 1.10, an overall increase of 10%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-83$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot shows a linear association between daily high temperature (x, in °F) and daily ice cream sales (y, in dollars), modeled by y = 25x − 200. Based on this model, what is the predicted sales on a day with a high temperature of 90°F?$q$, $q$["$2050","$2250","$1800","$2000"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 90: y = 25(90) − 200 = 2250 − 200 = $2050.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-84$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A distribution of exam scores is strongly left-skewed, with a small number of very low scores. Which is most likely true of the mean and median?$q$, $q$["The median is greater than the mean.","The mean is greater than the median.","The mean and median are equal.","The relationship cannot be determined."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a left-skewed distribution, a few very low values pull the mean downward more than they affect the median, so the median exceeds the mean.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-85$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A box contains 10 tickets numbered 1 through 10. One ticket is drawn at random. What is the probability that its number is a multiple of 3 or a multiple of 4?$q$, $q$["1/2","3/10","1/5","7/10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiples of 3: {3, 6, 9}. Multiples of 4: {4, 8}. Combined with no overlap: {3, 4, 6, 8, 9}, which is 5 outcomes out of 10, so the probability is 5/10 = 1/2.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-86$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A car dealership survey of 400 customers found that 240 bought a sedan. Of those who bought a sedan, 96 also purchased an extended warranty. Of the 160 customers who did not buy a sedan, 40 purchased an extended warranty. What is the probability that a randomly selected customer who purchased an extended warranty also bought a sedan?$q$, $q$["12/17","96/240","96/400","40/136"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total extended-warranty buyers = 96 + 40 = 136. Of those, 96 also bought a sedan, so the probability is 96/136 = 12/17.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-87$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A fitness app claims that '90% of users lose weight,' based only on reviews voluntarily submitted by users after they reached their goals. What is the main flaw in this claim?$q$, $q$["The reviewers are a self-selected group of successful users, not a representative sample of all app users.","Weight loss cannot be verified through a mobile app.","The claim would only be flawed if fewer than 90 users submitted reviews.","Percentages cannot be used to describe survey results."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only users who achieved their goals and chose to leave a review are counted, so the sample overrepresents success and cannot be generalized to all users.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-88$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of a random sample estimates that 46% of a population supports a proposal, with a margin of error of 3 percentage points. Based on this survey, is it plausible that exactly 47% of the population supports the proposal?$q$, $q$["Yes, because the sample size is large enough","Cannot be determined without more information","No, because the margin of error only applies to the mean","Yes, because it falls within the margin of error"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The survey's plausible range is 46% ± 3, which is from 43% to 49%. Since 47% falls within this range, the claim is plausible based on this survey.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-89$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A recipe uses 4 cups of flour for every 2 cups of sugar. If a baker uses 10 cups of flour, how many cups of sugar should be used to keep the same ratio?$q$, $q$["10","2","7","5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The scale factor from the original recipe is 10/4 = 2.5. Scaling the sugar amount by the same factor: 2 × 2.5 = 5 cups.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-90$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A car travels at a constant speed of 80 miles per hour. Using 1 mile = 5,280 feet, what is the car's speed in feet per second, rounded to the nearest tenth?$q$, $q$["80","117","234.6","117.3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Convert: 80 miles/hour × 5,280 feet/mile ÷ 3,600 seconds/hour = 117.3 feet per second.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-91$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$After a 20% discount, the price of an item is $280. What was the original price, in dollars, before the discount?$q$, $q$["300","336","350","330"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, then P × (1 − 20/100) = 280, so P × 0.8 = 280, giving P = 280/0.8 = 350.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-92$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A line of best fit models a data set with a slope of −9 units per hour. According to this model, by how much does the predicted value change as the number of hours increases from 18 to 23?$q$, $q$["−9","−45","−54","45"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The predicted value changes by the slope times the change in x: −9 × (23 − 18) = −9 × 5 = −45.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-93$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The mean of 8 numbers is 39. When one more number is added to the set, the new mean of all 9 numbers is 42.222. What is the value of the number that was added?$q$, $q$["68","380","39","73"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The original sum is 8 × 39 = 312. The new sum is 9 × 42.222 = 380. The added number is 380 − 312 = 68.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-94$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$The probability that a certain machine produces a defective part is 5/10 on any given run, independent of other runs. What is the probability that at least one of the next 3 parts is defective?$q$, $q$["3/2","1/2","7/8","1/8"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The probability a single part is NOT defective is 1/2. For 3 independent runs, P(no defects) = (1/2)<sup>3</sup> = 125/1000. So P(at least one defective) = 1 − 125/1000 = 7/8.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-95$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 120 people included 70 from Group A and 50 from Group B. 60% of Group A and 75% of Group B answered "yes" to a question (the rest answered "no"). If a person who answered "no" is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is from Group B?$q$, $q$["6/25","1/10","3/10","7/10"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Group A had 70 − 42 = 28 "no" responses, and Group B had 50 − 38 = 12 "no" responses, for a total of 40 "no" responses. The probability the person is from Group B is 12/40 = 3/10.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-96$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$Researchers randomly selected 500 adults from a city's population and surveyed their sleep habits and job satisfaction. They found a correlation between more sleep and higher job satisfaction. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["The results cannot be generalized because the sample was not randomly assigned to treatment groups","The results can be generalized to the city's adult population, and more sleep causes higher job satisfaction","A causal relationship can be established because the sample size is large","The results can be generalized to the city's adult population, but a causal relationship between sleep and job satisfaction cannot be established"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Because the sample was randomly selected (not randomly assigned to a sleep treatment), the results generalize to the population sampled, but since this is an observational study, no causal claim can be made.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-97$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample estimates that 49% of a population favors a policy, with a margin of error of 5 percentage points. What is the smallest percentage that is within the margin of error of this estimate?$q$, $q$["44","49","54","39"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The margin of error gives a range from 49% − 5% to 49% + 5%, that is, from 44% to 54%. The smallest percentage in this range is 44%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-98$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 1, null, $q$Of 60 people surveyed, 36 like hiking. Of those who like hiking, 24 also like camping. What is the probability that a randomly selected hiker also likes camping?$q$, $q$["2/3","2/5","3/2","3/5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(camping | hiking) = (hikers who also like camping) / (total hikers) = 24/36 = 2/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-99$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A group of 45 students includes 27 who play video games. Of those who play video games, 9 also watch livestreamed esports tournaments. What is the probability that a randomly selected video game player also watches livestreamed esports tournaments?$q$, $q$["1/3","1/5","3/5","2/3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(watches tournaments | plays games) = 9/27 = 1/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-100$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 180 moviegoers found that 100 watched an action film. Of those who watched an action film, 40 also bought popcorn. Of the 80 moviegoers who did not watch an action film, 20 bought popcorn. What is the probability that a randomly selected moviegoer who bought popcorn also watched an action film?$q$, $q$["2/3","40/100","40/180","20/60"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total popcorn buyers = 40 + 20 = 60. Of those, 40 also watched an action film, so the probability is 40/60 = 2/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-101$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 220 gym members found that 140 use the weight room. Of those who use the weight room, 70 also take group classes. Of the 80 members who do not use the weight room, 10 take group classes. What is the probability that a randomly selected member who takes group classes also uses the weight room?$q$, $q$["7/8","1/2","7/22","8/7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total group-class takers = 70 + 10 = 80. Of those, 70 also use the weight room, so the probability is 70/80 = 7/8.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-102$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A workplace survey of 320 employees found that 200 work in sales. Of those who work in sales, 50 have a graduate degree. Of the 120 employees who do not work in sales, 30 have a graduate degree. What is the probability that a randomly selected employee with a graduate degree works in sales?$q$, $q$["5/8","1/4","5/32","3/8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total employees with a graduate degree = 50 + 30 = 80. Of those, 50 work in sales, so the probability is 50/80 = 5/8.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-103$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 160 people included 40 from Group A and 120 from Group B. 80% of Group A and 50% of Group B answered "yes" to a question. If a person who answered "yes" is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is from Group A?$q$, $q$["4/5","8/23","15/23","1/5"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Group A had 80% of 40 = 32 "yes" responses, and Group B had 50% of 120 = 60 "yes" responses, for a total of 92 "yes" responses. The probability the person is from Group A is 32/92 = 8/23.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-104$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 120 people included 50 from Group A and 70 from Group B. 90% of Group A and 20% of Group B answered "yes" to a question (the rest answered "no"). If a person who answered "no" is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is from Group B?$q$, $q$["7/15","56/61","5/61","4/5"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Group A had 50 − 45 = 5 "no" responses, and Group B had 70 − 14 = 56 "no" responses, for a total of 61 "no" responses. The probability the person is from Group B is 56/61 = 56/61.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-105$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 1, null, $q$A school newspaper concludes that 'most students prefer online textbooks,' based on a survey of students who use the school's laptop cart. What is the main flaw in this conclusion?$q$, $q$["The sample is not representative of the general student population.","The sample size is too large to be useful.","Laptop users cannot be surveyed accurately.","Textbook preference cannot be measured through surveys."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Surveying only students who use the laptop cart overrepresents students already inclined toward digital tools, so the sample cannot be generalized to all students.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-106$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 1, null, $q$A researcher wants to estimate the average number of hours of sleep per night among all adults in a city. Which method would introduce the least bias?$q$, $q$["Randomly selecting adults from across the entire city","Surveying only adults leaving an all-night diner at 2 a.m.","Surveying only adults who post about insomnia on social media","Surveying only night-shift workers at one hospital"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A random sample drawn from the entire city population avoids the bias introduced by selecting from a narrow, unrepresentative subgroup.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-107$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey finds that people who own a standing desk report less back pain, on average, than people who don't. A blog headline claims 'standing desks cure back pain.' What is the main flaw in this claim?$q$, $q$["The study shows only an association, not that standing desks cause reduced back pain, since other factors may explain the link.","Observational surveys can never detect any real differences between groups.","Back pain cannot be measured or compared between people.","The claim would only be flawed if fewer than 50 people were surveyed."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Without randomly assigning people to use standing desks or not, the study cannot rule out confounding variables, such as overall health habits, that could explain the association.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-108$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 1, null, $q$Which change would most directly increase the margin of error of a survey estimate?$q$, $q$["Decreasing the sample size","Increasing the sample size","Randomly selecting more participants","Reducing bias in the survey question"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Margin of error generally increases as sample size decreases, because smaller samples produce less precise estimates of the population.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-109$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A city wants to estimate the proportion of all residents who support a new recycling program. Which sampling method would produce the most reliable estimate?$q$, $q$["Randomly selecting 500 residents from the full list of registered addresses","Surveying people who attend a town hall meeting about the program","Surveying residents who call in to a radio show","Surveying only residents of one neighborhood"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A random sample drawn from the entire list of residents is representative of the whole population, unlike the biased methods in the other choices.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-110$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A researcher randomly assigned 200 volunteers who agreed to participate in a study to either a new exercise program or no program, and measured changes in resting heart rate. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["A causal relationship between the exercise program and heart rate change can be established for people like those in the study, but the results cannot necessarily be generalized to the broader population","The results can be generalized to the entire population, but no causal relationship can be established","A causal relationship can be established, and the results can be generalized to the entire population","Neither a causal relationship nor generalization is possible from this study"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Random assignment (not random selection from the population) supports a causal conclusion for the study's volunteer participants, but without random sampling from the broader population, the results cannot be generalized beyond people similar to those volunteers.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-111$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A polling organization surveys a random sample of registered voters and reports that 54% support a measure, with a margin of error of 3 percentage points. Which of the following statements is best supported by this poll?$q$, $q$["Exactly 54% of all registered voters support the measure","The poll proves that the measure will pass","More than half of all registered voters definitely support the measure","It is plausible that the true percentage of all registered voters who support the measure is between 51% and 57%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A margin of error defines a plausible range for the true population value: 54% ± 3 percentage points, or 51% to 57%. It does not establish an exact value or a certainty.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-112$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 1, null, $q$A random sample of 180 shoppers found that 72 prefer self-checkout over cashier checkout. Based on this sample, what percentage of all shoppers would be expected to prefer self-checkout?$q$, $q$["40%","72%","60%","20%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$72/180 = 0.40 = 40%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-113$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 1, null, $q$A random sample of 120 patients found that 30 experienced side effects from a medication. Based on this sample, what percentage of all patients taking the medication would be expected to experience side effects?$q$, $q$["25%","30%","75%","12%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$30/120 = 0.25 = 25%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-114$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 350 commuters found that 140 use public transit. Based on this sample, what is the best estimate of the number of commuters who use public transit in a city of 21,000 commuters?$q$, $q$["8,400","6,000","8,750","5,250"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sample proportion is 140/350 = 0.40. Applying this rate to the population: 0.40 × 21,000 = 8,400 commuters.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-115$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 450 adults found that 162 own an electric vehicle. Based on this sample, what percentage of all adults would be expected to own an electric vehicle?$q$, $q$["36%","42%","64%","18%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$162/450 = 0.36 = 36%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-116$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 700 households found that 245 compost food waste. Based on this sample, what is the best estimate of the number of households that compost food waste in a town of 10,000 households?$q$, $q$["3,500","2,450","4,500","3,150"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sample proportion is 245/700 = 0.35. Applying this rate to the population: 0.35 × 10,000 = 3,500 households.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-117$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of a random sample estimates that 66% of a population supports a proposal, with a margin of error of 3 percentage points. Based on this survey, is it plausible that exactly 70% of the population supports the proposal?$q$, $q$["Cannot be determined without more information","Yes, because the sample size is large enough","No, because the margin of error only applies to the mean","No, because it falls outside the margin of error"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The survey's plausible range is 66% ± 3, which is from 63% to 69%. Since 70% falls outside this range, the claim is not plausible based on this survey.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-118$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample estimates that 40% of a population favors a policy, with a margin of error of 2 percentage points. What is the smallest percentage that is within the margin of error of this estimate?$q$, $q$["36","42","38","40"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The margin of error gives a range from 40% − 2% to 40% + 2%, that is, from 38% to 42%. The smallest percentage in this range is 38%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-119$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A data set is 4, 6, 6, 8, 8, 8, 40. Which measure of center is most affected by the value 40?$q$, $q$["The mean","The median","The mode","Neither the mean nor the median"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The mean is sensitive to extreme values (outliers), while the median and mode, based on position or frequency, change little when one extreme value is present.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-120$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A data set has a mean of 60 and a standard deviation of 8. What value is 3 standard deviations above the mean?$q$, $q$["84","68","76","92"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$60 + 3(8) = 60 + 24 = 84.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-121$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A cyclist's daily distances, in miles, over five days are 10, 12, 12, 14, and 17. What is the mode of this data set?$q$, $q$["12","13","14","17"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The mode is the value that appears most often. 12 appears twice, more than any other value.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-122$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A set of 5 quiz scores has a mean of 78. If the highest score, 98, is removed, what is the mean of the remaining 4 scores?$q$, $q$["73","78","82","68"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The original sum is 5 × 78 = 390. Removing 98 leaves a sum of 292, and 292/4 = 73.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-123$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$Two data sets have the same median, but data set X has a range of 40 and data set Y has a range of 10. Which statement is best supported?$q$, $q$["The values in data set X are more spread out than the values in data set Y.","The values in data set Y are more spread out than the values in data set X.","Data sets X and Y have identical distributions.","Data set X has a smaller standard deviation than data set Y."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A larger range indicates that the maximum and minimum values are farther apart, suggesting data set X's values are more spread out than data set Y's.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-124$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A distribution of ages at a retirement community is strongly left-skewed, with a small number of much younger residents. Which is most likely true of the mean and median?$q$, $q$["The median is greater than the mean.","The mean is greater than the median.","The mean and median are equal.","The relationship cannot be determined."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a left-skewed distribution, a few unusually low values pull the mean downward more than they affect the median, so the median exceeds the mean.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-125$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A class of 12 students has an average test score of 75, and another class of 8 students has an average test score of 73. What is the average test score of all the students combined, rounded to the nearest tenth?$q$, $q$["74","75.2","74.2","73.2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The combined total is 12(75) + 8(73) = 1484. Dividing by the total number of students, 12 + 8 = 20, gives an average of 1484/20 ≈ 74.2.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-126$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A tablet originally priced at $420 is on sale for 15% off. What is the sale price?$q$, $q$["$357","$405","$63","$367"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$15% of $420 is $63, so the sale price is $420 − $63 = $357.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-127$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$In a survey, 45 of 180 respondents preferred brand A. What percent of respondents preferred a different brand?$q$, $q$["75%","25%","60%","45%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$180 − 45 = 135 respondents preferred another brand. 135/180 = 0.75 = 75%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-128$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A painting's value rose from $2,500 to $3,250. What was the percent increase?$q$, $q$["30%","23%","75%","33%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Percent increase = (3250 − 2500)/2500 × 100 = 750/2500 × 100 = 30%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-129$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$What is 30% of 40% of 900?$q$, $q$["108","120","90","135"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$40% of 900 = 360. 30% of 360 = 108.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-130$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$After a 15% discount, a jacket costs $85. What was the jacket's original price?$q$, $q$["$100","$97.75","$110","$92"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If the original price is p, then 0.85p = 85 → p = 100.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-131$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A town's population increased by 10% in one year and then decreased by 5% the next year. What was the overall percent change in population over the two years?$q$, $q$["4.5% increase","5% increase","5% decrease","15% increase"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying by 1.10 and then 0.95 gives 1.10 × 0.95 = 1.045, an overall increase of 4.5%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-132$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A value starts at 40. It first increases by 50%, and then the new value decreases by 10%. What is the overall percent change from the original value, to the nearest tenth of a percent?$q$, $q$["35","40","−40","34"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$After the increase, the value is 40 × 1.5 = 60. After the decrease, it is 60 × 0.9 = 54. The overall change is (54 − 40)/40 × 100% = +35%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-133$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A bag contains 6 red, 5 blue, and 9 green marbles. What is the probability of randomly drawing a red marble?$q$, $q$["3/10","1/4","9/20","1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 20 total marbles, and 6 are red, so the probability is 6/20 = 3/10.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-134$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A standard six-sided die is rolled once. What is the probability of rolling a number less than 3?$q$, $q$["1/3","1/2","2/3","1/6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rolling a 1 or 2 satisfies the condition, giving 2 favorable outcomes out of 6: 2/6 = 1/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-135$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A fair coin is flipped 4 times. What is the probability of getting at least one tails?$q$, $q$["15/16","1/16","1/4","7/8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(at least one tails) = 1 − P(no tails) = 1 − (1/2)⁴ = 1 − 1/16 = 15/16.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-136$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A spinner is divided into 10 equal sections numbered 1 through 10. What is the probability that a single spin lands on an odd number or a number less than 4?$q$, $q$["3/5","4/5","1/2","7/10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Odd numbers: {1, 3, 5, 7, 9}. Numbers less than 4: {1, 2, 3}. Combined without double-counting 1 and 3: {1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 9}, which is 6 outcomes out of 10, so the probability is 6/10 = 3/5.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-137$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A fair six-sided die is rolled twice. What is the probability that the sum of the two rolls is 9?$q$, $q$["1/9","1/6","1/12","1/36"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 36 equally likely outcomes. The sums of 9 come from (3,6), (4,5), (5,4), (6,3): 4 outcomes, so the probability is 4/36 = 1/9.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-138$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A bag contains 8 red marbles and 4 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn at random, one after another, without replacement. What is the probability that both marbles are red?$q$, $q$["49/121","4/9","2/3","14/33"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$P(first red) = 8/12. Without replacement, P(second red | first red) = 7/11. So P(both red) = (8/12) × (7/11) = 14/33.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-139$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$The probability that a certain machine produces a defective part is 2/4 on any given run, independent of other runs. What is the probability that at least one of the next 2 parts is defective?$q$, $q$["1/4","1","1/2","3/4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The probability a single part is NOT defective is 1/2. For 2 independent runs, P(no defects) = (1/2)<sup>2</sup> = 4/16. So P(at least one defective) = 1 − 4/16 = 3/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-140$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A car travels 240 miles using 8 gallons of gas. What is the car's fuel efficiency, in miles per gallon?$q$, $q$["30","24","8","240"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$240 miles ÷ 8 gallons = 30 miles per gallon.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-141$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A copy machine prints 60 pages in 4 minutes at a constant rate. How many pages will it print in 15 minutes?$q$, $q$["225","60","240","180"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rate = 60/4 = 15 pages per minute. In 15 minutes: 15 × 15 = 225 pages.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-142$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A hose fills a 72-gallon tank in 18 minutes at a constant rate. How many gallons does it fill in 5 minutes?$q$, $q$["20","18","4","90"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rate is 72/18 = 4 gallons per minute. In 5 minutes: 4 × 5 = 20 gallons.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-143$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A train travels at 108 kilometers per hour. What is this speed in meters per second?$q$, $q$["30","18","25","108"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$108 km/h = 108,000 m / 3,600 s = 30 m/s.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-144$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A runner completes an 8-kilometer race in 32 minutes at a constant pace. At this pace, how many minutes would it take the runner to complete 20 kilometers?$q$, $q$["80","64","100","72"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pace is 32/8 = 4 minutes per kilometer. For 20 kilometers: 4 × 20 = 80 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-145$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$Pump A fills a tank at 180 liters per hour, and Pump B fills the same tank at 120 liters per hour. If both pumps run together at their constant rates, how many minutes will it take to fill a 1,500-liter tank?$q$, $q$["300","5","200","450"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Combined rate = 180 + 120 = 300 liters per hour. Time = 1,500/300 = 5 hours = 300 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-146$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$Pump A fills a tank at a rate of 6 liters per hour. Pump B fills the same tank at a rate of 1 liters every 10 minutes. Working together, how many liters do the two pumps fill in 6 hours?$q$, $q$["72","84","36","66"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Pump B's rate is 1 liters per 10 minutes = 6 liters per hour. Together, the pumps fill 6 + 6 = 12 liters per hour. In 6 hours, they fill 12 × 6 = 72 liters.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-147$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$In a class of 28 students, the ratio of boys to girls is 3:4. How many boys are in the class?$q$, $q$["12","16","14","21"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ratio has 7 total parts. Boys = (3/7) × 28 = 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-148$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A recipe that serves 6 people requires 300 grams of rice. How many grams of rice are needed to serve 9 people at the same ratio?$q$, $q$["450","400","500","350"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$300 × (9/6) = 300 × 1.5 = 450 grams.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-149$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A map has a scale of 1 inch to 8 miles. If two towns are 6.5 inches apart on the map, how many miles apart are they in reality?$q$, $q$["52","48","56","40"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$6.5 inches × 8 miles per inch = 52 miles.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-150$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A school has a ratio of students to computers of 9:2. If the school has 50 computers, how many students does it have?$q$, $q$["225","100","450","112.5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ratio 9:2 scales by a factor of 50/2 = 25. So students = 9 × 25 = 225.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-151$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A cleaning solution mixes water and vinegar in a ratio of 5:3. If a batch uses 24 cups of vinegar, how many total cups of cleaning solution does the batch make?$q$, $q$["64","40","48","72"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Vinegar is 3 parts and equals 24 cups, so each part is 8 cups. Water = 5 × 8 = 40 cups. Total = 40 + 24 = 64 cups.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-152$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A charity's budget is split between research and outreach in a ratio of 7:3. If the total budget is $80,000, how much is spent on outreach?$q$, $q$["$24,000","$56,000","$30,000","$8,000"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ratio has 10 total parts, so each part is $80,000/10 = $8,000. Outreach gets 3 parts, or $24,000.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-153$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$In a mixture, the ratio of sand to gravel to cement is 7:2:6. If the total mixture weighs 30 kilograms, how many kilograms of gravel are in the mixture?$q$, $q$["6","4","12","14"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The ratio parts total 7 + 2 + 6 = 15, so each part weighs 30/15 = 2 kilograms. Gravel makes up 2 parts, so it weighs 2 × 2 = 4 kilograms.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-154$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A scatterplot of hours of sleep (x) versus reaction time in milliseconds (y) has a line of best fit given by y = −8x + 320. What does the slope of this line represent?$q$, $q$["Each additional hour of sleep is associated with a decrease of 8 milliseconds in reaction time.","A person who sleeps 0 hours is predicted to have a reaction time of 8 milliseconds.","Each additional millisecond of reaction time requires 8 more hours of sleep.","The maximum possible reaction time is 8 milliseconds."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a linear model y = mx + b, the slope m represents the change in y per unit increase in x — here, a decrease of 8 milliseconds per additional hour of sleep.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-155$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A scatterplot of years of experience (x) versus hourly wage in dollars (y) has a line of best fit given by y = 2x + 18. According to this model, what is the predicted hourly wage for someone with 6 years of experience?$q$, $q$["$30","$24","$36","$20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 6 into y = 2x + 18: y = 2(6) + 18 = 30, so the predicted hourly wage is $30.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-156$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$In a scatterplot with a line of best fit, a data point lies far below the line. What does this indicate about that data point?$q$, $q$["Its actual value is less than the value predicted by the line.","Its actual value is greater than the value predicted by the line.","It lies exactly on the line of best fit.","It cannot be used to evaluate the model."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A point below the line of best fit has an actual y-value less than the model's predicted value at that x.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-157$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot shows the relationship between the number of employees at a company (x) and its monthly utility cost in dollars (y), modeled by the line of best fit y = 25x + 400. According to this model, what are the predicted monthly utility costs for a company with 40 employees?$q$, $q$["$1,400","$1,000","$1,600","$900"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 40 into y = 25x + 400: y = 25(40) + 400 = 1000 + 400 = $1,400.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-158$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot shows a strong negative linear association between the number of years since a phone model was released (x) and its resale value in dollars (y), modeled by y = 600 − 45x. Based on this model, what is the predicted resale value of a phone model released 8 years ago?$q$, $q$["$240","$360","$120","$255"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 8: y = 600 − 45(8) = 600 − 360 = $240.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-159$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The line of best fit for a data set is y = −6x + 37. For a data point where x = 27, the actual y-value observed is −129. What is the residual (actual minus predicted) for this point?$q$, $q$["−4","−125","−129","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The predicted value is y = −6(27) + 37 = −125. The residual is the actual value minus the predicted value: −129 − −125 = −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-160$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A line of best fit models a data set with a slope of −3 units per hour. According to this model, by how much does the predicted value change as the number of hours increases from 19 to 23?$q$, $q$["12","−3","−12","−15"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The predicted value changes by the slope times the change in x: −3 × (23 − 19) = −3 × 4 = −12.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-161$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A set of commute times, in minutes, for 7 employees is 18, 22, 22, 25, 29, 31, 40. What is the median of this data set?$q$, $q$["25","22","29","26.4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$With the 7 values sorted, the median is the 4th value: 18, 22, 22, 25, 29, 31, 40, so the median is 25.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-162$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$The daily high temperatures, in degrees Celsius, over a week are 18, 20, 21, 23, 24, 26, 33. What is the range of this data set?$q$, $q$["15","8","33","18"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The range is the greatest value minus the least value: 33 − 18 = 15.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-163$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A student scored 72 on an assignment worth 40% of the final grade and 96 on an assignment worth 60% of the final grade. What is the student's weighted average?$q$, $q$["86.4","84","88","82.4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$0.40(72) + 0.60(96) = 28.8 + 57.6 = 86.4.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-164$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A set of 7 exam scores has a mean of 80. If the lowest score, 56, is removed, what is the mean of the remaining 6 scores?$q$, $q$["84","80","74","90"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The original sum is 7 × 80 = 560. Removing 56 leaves a sum of 504, and 504/6 = 84.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-165$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$Which of the following data sets has the greatest standard deviation?$q$, $q$["{2, 14, 14, 26}","{14, 14, 14, 14}","{8, 14, 14, 20}","{12, 14, 14, 16}"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Standard deviation measures spread from the mean. {2, 14, 14, 26} has the widest spread of values around its mean, giving it the greatest standard deviation.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-166$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A distribution of home sale prices in a neighborhood is strongly right-skewed, with a few very expensive homes. Which is most likely true of the mean and median sale price?$q$, $q$["The mean is greater than the median.","The median is greater than the mean.","The mean and median are equal.","The relationship cannot be determined."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a right-skewed distribution, a few very high values pull the mean upward more than they affect the median, so the mean exceeds the median.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-167$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The mean of 7 numbers is 81. When one more number is added to the set, the new mean of all 8 numbers is 78.375. What is the value of the number that was added?$q$, $q$["627","81","65","60"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The original sum is 7 × 81 = 567. The new sum is 8 × 78.375 = 627. The added number is 627 − 567 = 60.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-168$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A class of 20 students has an average test score of 84, and another class of 11 students has an average test score of 83. What is the average test score of all the students combined, rounded to the nearest tenth?$q$, $q$["84.6","84","83.6","83.5"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The combined total is 20(84) + 11(83) = 2593. Dividing by the total number of students, 20 + 11 = 31, gives an average of 2593/31 ≈ 83.6.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-169$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A painting's value rose from $150 to $192. What was the percent increase?$q$, $q$["28%","42%","12%","32%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Percent increase = (192 − 150)/150 × 100 = 42/150 × 100 = 28%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-170$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$What is 25% of 80% of 400?$q$, $q$["80","100","120","64"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$80% of 400 = 320. 25% of 320 = 80.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-171$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A jacket originally priced at $90 is on sale for 30% off. What is the sale price?$q$, $q$["$63","$60","$27","$67"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$30% of $90 is $27, so the sale price is $90 − $27 = $63.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-172$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$After a 25% discount, a bike costs $225. What was the bike's original price?$q$, $q$["$300","$281.25","$320","$270"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If the original price is p, then 0.75p = 225 → p = 300.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-173$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$In a survey, 84 of 240 respondents preferred Option B. What percent of respondents preferred a different option?$q$, $q$["65%","35%","84%","15%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$240 − 84 = 156 respondents preferred another option. 156/240 = 0.65 = 65%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-174$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A company's revenue increased by 30% in the first year and then decreased by 20% in the second year. What was the overall percent change in revenue over the two years?$q$, $q$["4% increase","10% increase","4% decrease","50% increase"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying by 1.30 and then 0.80 gives 1.30 × 0.80 = 1.04, an overall increase of 4%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-175$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$After a 25% discount, the price of an item is $180. What was the original price, in dollars, before the discount?$q$, $q$["205","240","215","225"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$If the original price is P, then P × (1 − 25/100) = 180, so P × 0.75 = 180, giving P = 180/0.75 = 240.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-176$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A value starts at 100. It first increases by 50%, and then the new value decreases by 30%. What is the overall percent change from the original value, to the nearest tenth of a percent?$q$, $q$["5","10","−20","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$After the increase, the value is 100 × 1.5 = 150. After the decrease, it is 150 × 0.7 = 105. The overall change is (105 − 100)/100 × 100% = +5%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-177$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$After a 50% discount, the price of an item is $420. What was the original price, in dollars, before the discount?$q$, $q$["630","790","840","470"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, then P × (1 − 50/100) = 420, so P × 0.5 = 420, giving P = 420/0.5 = 840.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-178$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A scatterplot of hours of exercise per week (x) versus resting heart rate in beats per minute (y) has a line of best fit given by y = −1.5x + 75. What does the slope of this line represent?$q$, $q$["Each additional hour of exercise per week is associated with a decrease of 1.5 beats per minute in resting heart rate.","A person who exercises 0 hours is predicted to have a resting heart rate of 1.5 bpm.","Each additional beat per minute requires 1.5 more hours of exercise.","The minimum possible resting heart rate is 1.5 bpm."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a linear model y = mx + b, the slope m represents the change in y per unit increase in x — here, a decrease of 1.5 bpm per additional hour of exercise.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-179$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A scatterplot of years of experience (x) versus annual salary in thousands of dollars (y) has a line of best fit given by y = 3x + 45. According to this model, what is the predicted annual salary for someone with 10 years of experience?$q$, $q$["$75,000","$70,000","$80,000","$60,000"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 10 into y = 3x + 45: y = 3(10) + 45 = 75, so the predicted salary is $75,000.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-180$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$In a scatterplot with a line of best fit, a particular data point lies exactly on the line. What is true about the residual for that point?$q$, $q$["The residual is 0, since the actual value equals the predicted value.","The residual is positive.","The residual is negative.","The residual cannot be determined."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The residual is the actual value minus the predicted value. A point exactly on the line has an actual value equal to its predicted value, so the residual is 0.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-181$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot shows the relationship between a car's speed in miles per hour (x) and its stopping distance in feet (y), modeled by the line of best fit y = 2x + 10. According to this model, what is the predicted stopping distance for a car traveling 45 miles per hour?$q$, $q$["100 feet","90 feet","110 feet","80 feet"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 45 into y = 2x + 10: y = 2(45) + 10 = 100 feet.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-182$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot shows a strong negative linear association between a laptop's age in years (x) and its battery life in hours (y), modeled by y = 10 − 0.5x. Based on this model, what is the predicted battery life of a laptop that is 6 years old?$q$, $q$["7 hours","8 hours","4 hours","6.5 hours"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 6: y = 10 − 0.5(6) = 10 − 3 = 7 hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-183$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The line of best fit for a data set is y = −9x + 15. For a data point where x = 30, the actual y-value observed is −259. What is the residual (actual minus predicted) for this point?$q$, $q$["−259","4","−4","−255"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The predicted value is y = −9(30) + 15 = −255. The residual is the actual value minus the predicted value: −259 − −255 = −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-184$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A line of best fit models a data set with a slope of −4 units per hour. According to this model, by how much does the predicted value change as the number of hours increases from 8 to 12?$q$, $q$["−4","−20","16","−16"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The predicted value changes by the slope times the change in x: −4 × (12 − 8) = −4 × 4 = −16.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-185$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$In a class of 36 students, the ratio of boys to girls is 5:4. How many boys are in the class?$q$, $q$["20","16","18","24"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ratio has 9 total parts. Boys = (5/9) × 36 = 20.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-186$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A recipe that serves 5 people requires 300 grams of pasta. How many grams of pasta are needed to serve 8 people at the same ratio?$q$, $q$["480","450","500","375"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$300 × (8/5) = 300 × 1.6 = 480 grams.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-187$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A map has a scale of 1 inch to 12 miles. If two cities are 7.5 inches apart on the map, how many miles apart are they in reality?$q$, $q$["90","84","96","75"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$7.5 inches × 12 miles per inch = 90 miles.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-188$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A fruit punch mixes cranberry juice and lemonade in a ratio of 4:3 by volume. If a batch uses 21 cups of lemonade, how many total cups of fruit punch does the batch make?$q$, $q$["49","42","28","56"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Lemonade is 3 parts and equals 21 cups, so each part is 7 cups. Cranberry juice = 4 × 7 = 28 cups. Total = 28 + 21 = 49 cups.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-189$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A company has a ratio of managers to employees of 1:12. If the company has 156 employees, how many managers does it have?$q$, $q$["13","12","18","156"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$156 ÷ 12 = 13 managers.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-190$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A recipe uses 4 cups of flour for every 4 cups of sugar. If a baker uses 14 cups of flour, how many cups of sugar should be used to keep the same ratio?$q$, $q$["15","14","4","18"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The scale factor from the original recipe is 14/4 = 3.5. Scaling the sugar amount by the same factor: 4 × 3.5 = 14 cups.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-191$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$In a mixture, the ratio of sand to gravel to cement is 2:4:2. If the total mixture weighs 24 kilograms, how many kilograms of gravel are in the mixture?$q$, $q$["15","6","12","11"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The ratio parts total 2 + 4 + 2 = 8, so each part weighs 24/8 = 3 kilograms. Gravel makes up 4 parts, so it weighs 4 × 3 = 12 kilograms.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-192$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A truck travels 360 miles using 15 gallons of gas. What is the truck's fuel efficiency, in miles per gallon?$q$, $q$["24","15","36","360"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$360 miles ÷ 15 gallons = 24 miles per gallon.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-193$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A printer prints 72 pages in 4 minutes at a constant rate. How many pages will it print in 10 minutes?$q$, $q$["180","72","144","200"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rate = 72/4 = 18 pages per minute. In 10 minutes: 18 × 10 = 180 pages.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-194$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A cyclist travels at 54 kilometers per hour. What is this speed in meters per second?$q$, $q$["15","10","20","54"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$54 km/h = 54,000 m / 3,600 s = 15 m/s.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-195$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$Hose A fills a pool at 150 liters per hour, and Hose B fills the same pool at 100 liters per hour. If both hoses run together at their constant rates, how many minutes will it take to fill a 1,000-liter pool?$q$, $q$["240","4","200","300"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Combined rate = 150 + 100 = 250 liters per hour. Time = 1,000/250 = 4 hours = 240 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-196$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A hiker completes a 12-kilometer trail in 180 minutes at a constant pace. At this pace, how many minutes would it take the hiker to complete 18 kilometers?$q$, $q$["270","240","300","225"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pace is 180/12 = 15 minutes per kilometer. For 18 kilometers: 15 × 18 = 270 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-197$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A car travels at a constant speed of 70 miles per hour. Using 1 mile = 5,280 feet, what is the car's speed in feet per second, rounded to the nearest tenth?$q$, $q$["103","102.7","205.4","70"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Convert: 70 miles/hour × 5,280 feet/mile ÷ 3,600 seconds/hour = 102.7 feet per second.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-198$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$Pump A fills a tank at a rate of 2 liters per hour. Pump B fills the same tank at a rate of 2 liters every 10 minutes. Working together, how many liters do the two pumps fill in 2 hours?$q$, $q$["42","26","4","28"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Pump B's rate is 2 liters per 10 minutes = 12 liters per hour. Together, the pumps fill 2 + 12 = 14 liters per hour. In 2 hours, they fill 14 × 2 = 28 liters.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-199$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A bag contains 5 red, 4 blue, and 6 green marbles. What is the probability of randomly drawing a green marble?$q$, $q$["2/5","1/3","4/15","2/3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 15 total marbles, and 6 are green, so the probability is 6/15 = 2/5.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-200$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A standard six-sided die is rolled once. What is the probability of rolling an even number greater than 2?$q$, $q$["1/3","1/2","2/3","1/6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rolling a 4 or 6 satisfies the condition, giving 2 favorable outcomes out of 6: 2/6 = 1/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-201$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A fair coin is flipped 5 times. What is the probability of getting at least one heads?$q$, $q$["31/32","1/32","5/32","1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(at least one heads) = 1 − P(no heads) = 1 − (1/2)⁵ = 1 − 1/32 = 31/32.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-202$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A spinner is divided into 12 equal sections numbered 1 through 12. What is the probability that a single spin lands on a multiple of 3 or a multiple of 4?$q$, $q$["1/2","7/12","5/12","2/3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiples of 3: {3, 6, 9, 12}. Multiples of 4: {4, 8, 12}. Combined without double-counting 12: {3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 12}, which is 6 outcomes out of 12, so the probability is 6/12 = 1/2.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-203$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A fair six-sided die is rolled twice. What is the probability that the sum of the two rolls is 8?$q$, $q$["5/36","1/6","1/9","1/12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 36 equally likely outcomes. The sums of 8 come from (2,6), (3,5), (4,4), (5,3), (6,2): 5 outcomes, so the probability is 5/36.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-204$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A bag contains 3 red marbles and 7 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn at random, one after another, without replacement. What is the probability that both marbles are red?$q$, $q$["1/15","9/100","3/10","4/81"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(first red) = 3/10. Without replacement, P(second red | first red) = 2/9. So P(both red) = (3/10) × (2/9) = 1/15.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-205$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$The probability that a certain machine produces a defective part is 5/10 on any given run, independent of other runs. What is the probability that at least one of the next 2 parts is defective?$q$, $q$["1/2","3/4","1","1/4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The probability a single part is NOT defective is 1/2. For 2 independent runs, P(no defects) = (1/2)<sup>2</sup> = 25/100. So P(at least one defective) = 1 − 25/100 = 3/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-206$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 1, null, $q$A random sample of 320 students found that 80 prefer studying in groups over studying alone. Based on this sample, what percentage of all students would be expected to prefer studying in groups?$q$, $q$["25%","80%","20%","75%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$80/320 = 0.25 = 25%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-207$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 1, null, $q$A random sample of 90 commuters found that 54 use a bicycle to commute. Based on this sample, what percentage of all commuters would be expected to use a bicycle?$q$, $q$["60%","54%","40%","45%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$54/90 = 0.60 = 60%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-208$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 400 households found that 100 have a home security system. Based on this sample, what is the best estimate of the number of households with a home security system in a town of 16,000 households?$q$, $q$["4,000","2,500","4,500","1,600"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sample proportion is 100/400 = 0.25. Applying this rate to the population: 0.25 × 16,000 = 4,000 households.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-209$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 250 travelers found that 175 prefer window seats over aisle seats on flights. Based on this sample, what percentage of all travelers would be expected to prefer window seats?$q$, $q$["70%","75%","30%","65%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$175/250 = 0.70 = 70%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-210$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 1,000 adults found that 420 support a proposed city ordinance, with a margin of error of 3 percentage points. Which is the most reasonable conclusion about all adults?$q$, $q$["Between about 39% and 45% of all adults likely support the ordinance.","Exactly 42% of all adults support the ordinance.","At least 45% of all adults support the ordinance.","Fewer than 39% of all adults support the ordinance."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sample estimate is 420/1,000 = 42%. With a margin of error of 3 percentage points, the plausible range for the population is about 39% to 45%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-211$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of a random sample estimates that 66% of a population supports a proposal, with a margin of error of 2.5 percentage points. Based on this survey, is it plausible that exactly 70% of the population supports the proposal?$q$, $q$["No, because the margin of error only applies to the mean","Yes, because the sample size is large enough","Cannot be determined without more information","No, because it falls outside the margin of error"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The survey's plausible range is 66% ± 2.5, which is from 63.5% to 68.5%. Since 70% falls outside this range, the claim is not plausible based on this survey.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-212$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 1, null, $q$A magazine concludes that 'most adults own a smartwatch,' based on a survey of visitors to an electronics store. What is the main flaw in this conclusion?$q$, $q$["The sample is not representative of the general population.","The sample size is too large to be useful.","Electronics store visitors cannot be surveyed accurately.","Smartwatch ownership cannot be measured through surveys."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Surveying only electronics store visitors overrepresents people already inclined toward gadgets, so the sample cannot be generalized to all adults.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-213$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 1, null, $q$A researcher wants to estimate the average number of hours of television watched per week by all adults in a region. Which method would introduce the least bias?$q$, $q$["Randomly selecting adults from across the entire region","Surveying only adults at a cable TV trade show","Surveying only adults who post about TV shows on social media","Surveying only members of a television fan club"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A random sample drawn from the entire region avoids the bias introduced by selecting from a narrow, unrepresentative subgroup.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-214$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$Which change would most directly decrease the margin of error of a survey estimate, holding the sampling method constant?$q$, $q$["Increasing the sample size","Decreasing the sample size","Making the survey question more leading","Surveying fewer people from the population"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Margin of error generally decreases as sample size increases, because larger samples produce more precise estimates of the population.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-215$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A study finds that people who drink more green tea tend to have lower blood pressure, on average, than those who drink less. A headline claims 'green tea lowers blood pressure.' What is the main flaw in this claim?$q$, $q$["The study shows only an association, not that green tea causes lower blood pressure, since other factors may explain the link.","Observational studies can never detect any association between variables.","Blood pressure cannot be measured or compared between people.","The claim would only be flawed if fewer than 50 people were studied."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Without randomly assigning people to drink green tea or not, the study cannot rule out confounding variables, such as overall diet and health habits, that could explain the association.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-216$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A university wants to estimate the proportion of all students who are satisfied with campus dining. Which sampling method would produce the most reliable estimate?$q$, $q$["Randomly selecting 300 students from the full student enrollment list","Surveying students eating lunch in the dining hall at noon","Surveying students who respond to a posting on a dining hall bulletin board","Surveying only students living in one dormitory"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A random sample drawn from the entire enrollment list is representative of the whole student population, unlike the biased methods in the other choices.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-217$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A study observes that students who take an SAT prep course tend to have higher test scores than students who do not, but students chose for themselves whether to take the course. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["The results cannot be generalized or associated in any way","Taking the course causes higher test scores","A causal relationship can be concluded because the sample size was large","There is an association between taking the course and higher scores, but a causal relationship cannot be concluded because students were not randomly assigned to take the course"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Without random assignment to the course, other factors (such as motivation or prior ability) could explain the association, so only a correlation — not causation — can be concluded.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-218$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 1, null, $q$Of 72 people surveyed, 45 like swimming. Of those who like swimming, 27 also like running. What is the probability that a randomly selected swimmer also likes running?$q$, $q$["3/5","27/72","2/5","45/72"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(running | swimming) = (swimmers who also like running) / (total swimmers) = 27/45 = 3/5.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-219$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A group of 56 students includes 42 who play an instrument. Of those who play an instrument, 14 also sing in a choir. What is the probability that a randomly selected instrument player also sings in a choir?$q$, $q$["1/3","1/4","2/3","3/4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(choir | instrument) = 14/42 = 1/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-220$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 260 diners found that 150 ordered dessert. Of those who ordered dessert, 60 also ordered coffee. Of the 110 diners who did not order dessert, 20 ordered coffee. What is the probability that a randomly selected diner who ordered coffee also ordered dessert?$q$, $q$["3/4","60/150","60/260","20/80"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total coffee orderers = 60 + 20 = 80. Of those, 60 also ordered dessert, so the probability is 60/80 = 3/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-221$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 210 patients found that 126 received physical therapy. Of those who received physical therapy, 84 reported reduced pain within a month. Of the 84 patients who did not receive physical therapy, 21 reported reduced pain within a month. What is the probability that a randomly selected patient who reported reduced pain also received physical therapy?$q$, $q$["4/5","84/126","84/210","21/105"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total patients who reported reduced pain = 84 + 21 = 105. Of those, 84 also received physical therapy, so the probability is 84/105 = 4/5.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-222$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 180 travelers found that 108 checked a bag. Of those who checked a bag, 27 also paid for priority boarding. Of the 72 travelers who did not check a bag, 9 paid for priority boarding. What is the probability that a randomly selected traveler who paid for priority boarding also checked a bag?$q$, $q$["3/4","27/108","9/72","27/180"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total priority-boarding travelers = 27 + 9 = 36. Of those, 27 also checked a bag, so the probability is 27/36 = 3/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-223$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 160 people included 50 from Group A and 110 from Group B. 20% of Group A and 20% of Group B answered "yes" to a question. If a person who answered "yes" is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is from Group A?$q$, $q$["11/16","5/16","1/16","1/5"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Group A had 20% of 50 = 10 "yes" responses, and Group B had 20% of 110 = 22 "yes" responses, for a total of 32 "yes" responses. The probability the person is from Group A is 10/32 = 5/16.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-224$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A swimmer's lap times, in seconds, across six practice sets are 38.2, 39.5, 39.5, 40.1, 41.0, 42.6. What is the mode of this data set?$q$, $q$["39.5","40.1","39.8","42.6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The mode is the value that appears most often. 39.5 appears twice, more than any other value.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-225$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$The weekly attendance at a farmers market over seven weeks was 210, 225, 230, 238, 245, 250, and 290 people. What is the range of this data set?$q$, $q$["80","40","290","65"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The range is the greatest value minus the least value: 290 − 210 = 80.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-226$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A server's nightly tips, in dollars, this week are 40, 44, 44, 48, 52, 212. Which measure of center is most affected by the value 212?$q$, $q$["The mean","The median","The mode","Neither the mean nor the median"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The mean is sensitive to extreme values (outliers), while the median and mode, based on position or frequency, change little when one extreme value is present.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-227$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$In a cooking class, a student's final grade is based on a written exam worth 30% and a practical cooking exam worth 70%. If the student scored 70 on the written exam and 90 on the practical exam, what is the student's weighted average?$q$, $q$["84","80","76","88"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$0.30(70) + 0.70(90) = 21 + 63 = 84.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-228$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A set of 6 delivery times, in minutes, has a mean of 25. If the longest time, 60 minutes, is removed, what is the mean of the remaining 5 times?$q$, $q$["18","25","15","22"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The original sum is 6 × 25 = 150. Removing 60 leaves a sum of 90, and 90/5 = 18.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-229$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A distribution of hourly wages at a large retail company is strongly right-skewed, because a small number of executives earn far more than typical hourly workers. Which is most likely true of the mean and median wage?$q$, $q$["The mean is greater than the median.","The median is greater than the mean.","The mean and median are equal.","The relationship cannot be determined."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a right-skewed distribution, a few very high values pull the mean upward more than they affect the median, so the mean exceeds the median.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-230$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The mean of 13 numbers is 36. When one more number is added to the set, the new mean of all 14 numbers is 39.786. What is the value of the number that was added?$q$, $q$["94","557","89","36"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The original sum is 13 × 36 = 468. The new sum is 14 × 39.786 = 557. The added number is 557 − 468 = 89.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-231$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A class of 19 students has an average test score of 61, and another class of 17 students has an average test score of 76. What is the average test score of all the students combined, rounded to the nearest tenth?$q$, $q$["68.1","68","69.1","68.5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The combined total is 19(61) + 17(76) = 2451. Dividing by the total number of students, 19 + 17 = 36, gives an average of 2451/36 ≈ 68.1.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-232$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A tablet's price rose from $250 to $310. What was the percent increase?$q$, $q$["24%","19%","60%","20%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Percent increase = (310 − 250)/250 × 100 = 60/250 × 100 = 24%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-233$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$What is 15% of 40% of 600?$q$, $q$["36","90","240","54"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$40% of 600 = 240. 15% of 240 = 36.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-234$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A messenger bag originally priced at $120 is on sale for 35% off. What is the sale price?$q$, $q$["$78","$85","$42","$88"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$35% of $120 is $42, so the sale price is $120 − $42 = $78.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-235$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$After a 30% discount, a pair of headphones costs $63. What was the original price?$q$, $q$["$90","$81.90","$84","$93"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If the original price is p, then 0.70p = 63 → p = 90.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-236$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$In a survey, 72 of 240 respondents preferred a new logo design. What percent of respondents preferred a different design?$q$, $q$["70%","30%","72%","28%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$240 − 72 = 168 respondents preferred a different design. 168/240 = 0.70 = 70%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-237$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A nonprofit's donations increased by 20% in one year and then decreased by 15% the next year. What was the overall percent change in donations over the two years?$q$, $q$["2% increase","5% increase","2% decrease","3% decrease"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying by 1.20 and then 0.85 gives 1.20 × 0.85 = 1.02, an overall increase of 2%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-238$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A whale shark's recorded length decreased from 12 meters to 9 meters after a measurement correction. What is the percent decrease?$q$, $q$["25%","33%","20%","30%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Percent decrease = (12 − 9)/12 × 100 = 3/12 × 100 = 25%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-239$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A value starts at 80. It first increases by 30%, and then the new value decreases by 25%. What is the overall percent change from the original value, to the nearest tenth of a percent?$q$, $q$["−5","−2.5","2.5","5"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$After the increase, the value is 80 × 1.3 = 104. After the decrease, it is 104 × 0.75 = 78. The overall change is (78 − 80)/80 × 100% = −2.5%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-240$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$After a 50% discount, the price of an item is $330. What was the original price, in dollars, before the discount?$q$, $q$["610","660","380","495"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$If the original price is P, then P × (1 − 50/100) = 330, so P × 0.5 = 330, giving P = 330/0.5 = 660.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-241$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A scatterplot of weekly hours spent on homework (x) versus a weekly stress-level score (y, on a 0–100 scale) has a line of best fit given by y = 3x + 10. What does the slope of this line represent?$q$, $q$["Each additional hour of homework is associated with an increase of 3 points on the stress-level score.","A student who does 0 hours of homework is predicted to have a stress score of 3.","Each additional point on the stress score requires 3 more hours of homework.","The maximum possible stress score is 3."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In a linear model y = mx + b, the slope m represents the change in y per unit increase in x — here, 3 additional stress points per additional hour of homework.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-242$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A scatterplot of a plant's age in weeks (x) versus its height in centimeters (y) has a line of best fit given by y = 4x + 6. According to this model, what is the predicted height of a plant that is 9 weeks old?$q$, $q$["42 cm","36 cm","46 cm","40 cm"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 9 into y = 4x + 6: y = 4(9) + 6 = 42 cm.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-243$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot shows the relationship between a vehicle's weight in thousands of pounds (x) and its fuel economy in miles per gallon (y), modeled by the line of best fit y = 42 − 5x. According to this model, what is the predicted fuel economy for a vehicle weighing 4,000 pounds?$q$, $q$["22 mpg","20 mpg","24 mpg","18 mpg"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 4: y = 42 − 5(4) = 42 − 20 = 22 mpg.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-244$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A line of best fit is given by y = 7x − 10. If the actual y-value for x = 8 is 50, what is the residual?$q$, $q$["4","−4","46","50"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The predicted value is 7(8) − 10 = 46. The residual is actual minus predicted: 50 − 46 = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-245$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot shows a strong negative linear association between a smartphone's age in months (x) and its remaining battery capacity, as a percent (y), modeled by y = 100 − 0.8x. Based on this model, what is the predicted remaining battery capacity for a phone that is 30 months old?$q$, $q$["76%","80%","72%","84%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 30: y = 100 − 0.8(30) = 100 − 24 = 76%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-246$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The line of best fit for a data set is y = 8x + 103. For a data point where x = 21, the actual y-value observed is 261. What is the residual (actual minus predicted) for this point?$q$, $q$["271","10","−10","261"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The predicted value is y = 8(21) + 103 = 271. The residual is the actual value minus the predicted value: 261 − 271 = −10.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-247$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A line of best fit models a data set with a slope of −3 units per hour. According to this model, by how much does the predicted value change as the number of hours increases from 11 to 17?$q$, $q$["−21","18","−18","−3"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The predicted value changes by the slope times the change in x: −3 × (17 − 11) = −3 × 6 = −18.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-248$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$In a choir of 42 members, the ratio of sopranos to altos is 4:3. How many sopranos are in the choir?$q$, $q$["24","18","21","28"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ratio has 7 total parts. Sopranos = (4/7) × 42 = 24.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-249$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A recipe that serves 8 people requires 480 grams of rice. How many grams of rice are needed to serve 5 people at the same ratio?$q$, $q$["300","288","360","400"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$480 × (5/8) = 300 grams.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-250$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A map has a scale of 1 inch to 20 miles. If two landmarks are 3.25 inches apart on the map, how many miles apart are they in reality?$q$, $q$["65","60","70","50"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3.25 inches × 20 miles per inch = 65 miles.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-251$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A hardware store has a ratio of hammers to screwdrivers in stock of 7:5. If the store has 175 hammers, how many screwdrivers does it have?$q$, $q$["125","175","140","100"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ratio 7:5 scales by a factor of 175/7 = 25. So screwdrivers = 5 × 25 = 125.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-252$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A granola mix combines oats and dried fruit in a ratio of 7:2 by weight. If a batch uses 14 ounces of dried fruit, how many total ounces of granola mix does the batch make?$q$, $q$["63","49","56","70"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Dried fruit is 2 parts and equals 14 ounces, so each part is 7 ounces. Oats = 7 × 7 = 49 ounces. Total = 49 + 14 = 63 ounces.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-253$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A recipe uses 6 cups of flour for every 2 cups of sugar. If a baker uses 9 cups of flour, how many cups of sugar should be used to keep the same ratio?$q$, $q$["2","3","9","5"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The scale factor from the original recipe is 9/6 = 1.5. Scaling the sugar amount by the same factor: 2 × 1.5 = 3 cups.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-254$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$In a mixture, the ratio of sand to gravel to cement is 2:3:5. If the total mixture weighs 90 kilograms, how many kilograms of gravel are in the mixture?$q$, $q$["27","18","36","45"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ratio parts total 2 + 3 + 5 = 10, so each part weighs 90/10 = 9 kilograms. Gravel makes up 3 parts, so it weighs 3 × 9 = 27 kilograms.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-255$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A van travels 325 miles using 13 gallons of gas. What is the van's fuel efficiency, in miles per gallon?$q$, $q$["25","13","32.5","325"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$325 miles ÷ 13 gallons = 25 miles per gallon.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-256$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A 3D printer produces 36 small parts in 6 minutes at a constant rate. How many parts will it print in 25 minutes?$q$, $q$["150","36","180","125"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rate = 36/6 = 6 parts per minute. In 25 minutes: 6 × 25 = 150 parts.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-257$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A faucet fills an 84-liter basin in 12 minutes at a constant rate. How many liters does it fill in 7 minutes?$q$, $q$["49","42","56","63"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rate is 84/12 = 7 liters per minute. In 7 minutes: 7 × 7 = 49 liters.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-258$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A ferry travels at 45 kilometers per hour. What is this speed in meters per second?$q$, $q$["12.5","13.5","11.25","15"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$45 km/h = 45,000 m / 3,600 s = 12.5 m/s.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-259$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$Drain A empties a tank at 90 liters per hour, and Drain B empties the same tank at 60 liters per hour. If both drains run together at their constant rates, how many minutes will it take to empty a 450-liter tank?$q$, $q$["180","3","270","150"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Combined rate = 90 + 60 = 150 liters per hour. Time = 450/150 = 3 hours = 180 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-260$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A car travels at a constant speed of 70 miles per hour. Using 1 mile = 5,280 feet, what is the car's speed in feet per second, rounded to the nearest tenth?$q$, $q$["103","102.7","70","205.4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Convert: 70 miles/hour × 5,280 feet/mile ÷ 3,600 seconds/hour = 102.7 feet per second.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-261$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$Pump A fills a tank at a rate of 2 liters per hour. Pump B fills the same tank at a rate of 1 liters every 10 minutes. Working together, how many liters do the two pumps fill in 4 hours?$q$, $q$["28","40","32","8"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Pump B's rate is 1 liters per 10 minutes = 6 liters per hour. Together, the pumps fill 2 + 6 = 8 liters per hour. In 4 hours, they fill 8 × 4 = 32 liters.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-262$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A bag contains 7 red, 6 blue, and 7 green marbles. What is the probability of randomly drawing a green marble?$q$, $q$["7/20","3/10","1/4","7/13"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 20 total marbles, and 7 are green, so the probability is 7/20.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-263$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A standard six-sided die is rolled once. What is the probability of rolling a prime number?$q$, $q$["1/2","1/3","2/3","1/6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The prime numbers on the die are 2, 3, and 5, giving 3 favorable outcomes out of 6: 3/6 = 1/2.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-264$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A fair coin is flipped 6 times. What is the probability of getting at least one tails?$q$, $q$["63/64","1/64","6/64","1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(at least one tails) = 1 − P(no tails) = 1 − (1/2)⁶ = 1 − 1/64 = 63/64.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-265$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A spinner is divided into 15 equal sections numbered 1 through 15. What is the probability that a single spin lands on a multiple of 5 or a multiple of 6?$q$, $q$["1/3","2/5","7/15","1/5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiples of 5: {5, 10, 15}. Multiples of 6: {6, 12}. These sets do not overlap, giving 5 outcomes out of 15, so the probability is 5/15 = 1/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-266$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A fair six-sided die is rolled twice. What is the probability that the sum of the two rolls is 10?$q$, $q$["1/12","1/9","1/6","5/36"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 36 equally likely outcomes. The sums of 10 come from (4,6), (5,5), (6,4): 3 outcomes, so the probability is 3/36 = 1/12.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-267$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A bag contains 7 red marbles and 6 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn at random, one after another, without replacement. What is the probability that both marbles are red?$q$, $q$["7/26","1/4","7/13","49/169"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(first red) = 7/13. Without replacement, P(second red | first red) = 6/12. So P(both red) = (7/13) × (6/12) = 7/26.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-268$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$The probability that a certain machine produces a defective part is 1/5 on any given run, independent of other runs. What is the probability that at least one of the next 2 parts is defective?$q$, $q$["9/25","2/5","1/5","16/25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The probability a single part is NOT defective is 4/5. For 2 independent runs, P(no defects) = (4/5)<sup>2</sup> = 16/25. So P(at least one defective) = 1 − 16/25 = 9/25.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-269$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 1, null, $q$A random sample of 240 diners found that 60 ordered a vegetarian entrée. Based on this sample, what percentage of all diners would be expected to order a vegetarian entrée?$q$, $q$["25%","60%","40%","15%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$60/240 = 0.25 = 25%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-270$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 1, null, $q$A random sample of 80 patients found that 24 reported mild drowsiness after taking a new allergy medication. Based on this sample, what percentage of all patients taking the medication would be expected to report mild drowsiness?$q$, $q$["30%","24%","70%","20%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$24/80 = 0.30 = 30%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-271$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 320 commuters found that 96 carpool to work. Based on this sample, what is the best estimate of the number of commuters who carpool in a city of 24,000 commuters?$q$, $q$["7,200","6,400","7,680","5,760"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sample proportion is 96/320 = 0.30. Applying this rate to the population: 0.30 × 24,000 = 7,200 commuters.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-272$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 540 adults found that 189 subscribe to a streaming music service. Based on this sample, what percentage of all adults would be expected to subscribe to the service?$q$, $q$["35%","37%","65%","28%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$189/540 = 0.35 = 35%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-273$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 800 residents found that 296 favor extending a bus route, with a margin of error of 3 percentage points. Which is the most reasonable conclusion about all residents?$q$, $q$["Between about 34% and 40% of all residents likely favor extending the route.","Exactly 37% of all residents favor extending the route.","At least 40% of all residents favor extending the route.","Fewer than 34% of all residents favor extending the route."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sample estimate is 296/800 = 37%. With a margin of error of 3 percentage points, the plausible range for the population is about 34% to 40%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-274$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample estimates that 57% of a population favors a policy, with a margin of error of 3 percentage points. What is the smallest percentage that is within the margin of error of this estimate?$q$, $q$["57","54","60","51"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The margin of error gives a range from 57% − 3% to 57% + 3%, that is, from 54% to 60%. The smallest percentage in this range is 54%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-275$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 1, null, $q$A podcast host concludes that 'most people enjoy true crime stories,' based on a survey of the podcast's own listeners. What is the main flaw in this conclusion?$q$, $q$["The sample is not representative of the general population.","The sample size is too large to be useful.","Podcast listeners cannot be surveyed accurately.","Enjoyment of stories cannot be measured through surveys."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Surveying only the podcast's own listeners overrepresents people already interested in true crime, so the sample cannot be generalized to all people.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-276$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 1, null, $q$A researcher wants to estimate the average number of books read per year by all adults in a country. Which method would introduce the least bias?$q$, $q$["Randomly selecting adults from across the entire country","Surveying only adults waiting in line at a bookstore","Surveying only members of an online book club","Surveying only adults attending a literary festival"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A random sample drawn from the entire country avoids the bias introduced by selecting from a narrow, unrepresentative subgroup.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-277$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A study finds that people who use a particular meal-delivery service tend to report eating more vegetables, on average, than people who don't use it. An advertisement claims 'this meal-delivery service makes people eat more vegetables.' What is the main flaw in this claim?$q$, $q$["The study shows only an association, not that the service causes more vegetable consumption, since other factors may explain the link.","Observational studies can never detect any association between variables.","Vegetable consumption cannot be measured or compared between people.","The claim would only be flawed if fewer than 50 people were studied."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Without randomly assigning people to use the service or not, the study cannot rule out confounding variables, such as overall health consciousness, that could explain the association.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-278$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$Which change would most directly increase the margin of error of a survey estimate, holding the sampling method constant?$q$, $q$["Decreasing the sample size","Increasing the sample size","Randomly selecting more participants","Reducing bias in the survey question"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Margin of error generally increases as sample size decreases, because smaller samples produce less precise estimates of the population.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-279$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A hospital wants to estimate the proportion of all patients who are satisfied with their care. Which sampling method would produce the most reliable estimate?$q$, $q$["Randomly selecting 400 patients from the full list of discharged patients","Surveying patients who are still in the waiting room","Surveying patients who call in to complain","Surveying only patients in the maternity ward"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A random sample drawn from the full list of discharged patients is representative of the whole patient population, unlike the biased methods in the other choices.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-280$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A news article states that a study found people who drink coffee daily live longer, and concludes that coffee causes longevity. The study was observational, comparing self-reported coffee habits of 10,000 randomly sampled adults. Which of the following best describes a flaw in the article's conclusion?$q$, $q$["Random sampling means the results cannot be generalized to the broader population","The study did not randomly assign participants to drink coffee or not, so the observed association cannot establish that coffee causes longer life","The sample size of 10,000 is too small to detect any association","Self-reported data always produces results that are the opposite of the truth"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Causal conclusions require random assignment to treatment and control groups. Since this was an observational study with no random assignment, confounding variables (such as overall lifestyle) could explain the association instead of coffee itself.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-281$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 1, null, $q$Of 54 people surveyed, 36 like board games. Of those who like board games, 12 also like jigsaw puzzles. What is the probability that a randomly selected board-game fan also likes jigsaw puzzles?$q$, $q$["1/3","1/4","2/3","3/4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(puzzles | board games) = (fans who also like puzzles) / (total board-game fans) = 12/36 = 1/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-282$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 240 concertgoers found that 150 attended the opening act. Of those who attended the opening act, 90 also bought a t-shirt. Of the 90 concertgoers who did not attend the opening act, 10 bought a t-shirt. What is the probability that a randomly selected concertgoer who bought a t-shirt also attended the opening act?$q$, $q$["9/10","90/150","90/240","10/40"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total t-shirt buyers = 90 + 10 = 100. Of those, 90 also attended the opening act, so the probability is 90/100 = 9/10.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-283$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 300 pet owners found that 180 own a dog. Of those who own a dog, 72 also own a cat. Of the 120 pet owners who do not own a dog, 24 own a cat. What is the probability that a randomly selected pet owner who owns a cat also owns a dog?$q$, $q$["3/4","72/180","72/300","24/96"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total cat owners = 72 + 24 = 96. Of those, 72 also own a dog, so the probability is 72/96 = 3/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-284$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 400 theater patrons found that 240 saw the matinee showing. Of those who saw the matinee, 60 also purchased a program booklet. Of the 160 patrons who saw the evening showing, 20 purchased a program booklet. What is the probability that a randomly selected patron who purchased a program booklet also saw the matinee showing?$q$, $q$["3/4","60/240","60/400","20/80"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total booklet buyers = 60 + 20 = 80. Of those, 60 also saw the matinee, so the probability is 60/80 = 3/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-285$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 130 people included 50 from Group A and 80 from Group B. 40% of Group A and 80% of Group B answered "yes" to a question (the rest answered "no"). If a person who answered "no" is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is from Group B?$q$, $q$["8/23","8/65","1/5","15/23"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Group A had 50 − 20 = 30 "no" responses, and Group B had 80 − 64 = 16 "no" responses, for a total of 46 "no" responses. The probability the person is from Group B is 16/46 = 8/23.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-286$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 160 people included 100 from Group A and 60 from Group B. 50% of Group A and 10% of Group B answered "yes" to a question. If a person who answered "yes" is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is from Group A?$q$, $q$["3/28","1/2","25/28","5/16"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Group A had 50% of 100 = 50 "yes" responses, and Group B had 10% of 60 = 6 "yes" responses, for a total of 56 "yes" responses. The probability the person is from Group A is 50/56 = 25/28.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-287$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A jacket priced at $120 is marked down by 15%. What is the sale price?$q$, $q$["$102","$105","$18","$138"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$15% of $120 is $18, so the sale price is $120 − $18 = $102.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-288$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A savings account balance grows from $250 to $275. What is the percent increase?$q$, $q$["10%","25%","9%","11%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$(275 − 250)/250 × 100 = 25/250 × 100 = 10%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-289$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A city's recycling rate was 40% last year and rose to 46% this year. By how many percentage points did the recycling rate increase?$q$, $q$["6 percentage points","15 percentage points","46 percentage points","40 percentage points"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Percentage points measure the raw difference: 46% − 40% = 6 percentage points. This is not the same as the percent change, which is 6/40 = 15%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-290$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$After a 10% increase followed by a 10% decrease, a quantity's final value compared to its original value is:$q$, $q$["1% less than the original value","The same as the original value","1% more than the original value","20% less than the original value"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying by 1.10 and then 0.90 gives 1.10 × 0.90 = 0.99, which is 1% less than the original value.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-291$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A value starts at 70. It first increases by 25%, and then the new value decreases by 20%. What is the overall percent change from the original value, to the nearest tenth of a percent?$q$, $q$["0","−1","5","−5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$After the increase, the value is 70 × 1.25 = 87.5. After the decrease, it is 87.5 × 0.8 = 70. The overall change is (70 − 70)/70 × 100% = 0%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-292$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A set of 7 quiz scores, sorted in order, is 70, 75, 75, 80, 85, 90, 95. What is the median of this data set?$q$, $q$["80","75","85","82.5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$With 7 values sorted in order, the median is the 4th value: 80.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-293$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A data set has a median of 50 and a mean of 65, due to a few unusually high values. If those high values were removed, which measure of center would most likely change the most?$q$, $q$["The mean","The median","The mode","Neither the mean nor the median"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The mean is pulled toward extreme values, so removing the unusually high values would affect the mean far more than the median, which depends mainly on position.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-294$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$Data set A and data set B each have 10 values. Data set A has an interquartile range of 8, and data set B has an interquartile range of 20. Which statement is best supported?$q$, $q$["The middle 50% of values in data set B is more spread out than in data set A.","Data set A has a greater range than data set B.","Data sets A and B have the same median.","Data set B has fewer outliers than data set A."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interquartile range measures the spread of the middle 50% of the data; a larger IQR (20 vs. 8) means data set B's middle half is more spread out.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-295$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A set of 8 values has a mean of 25. One value, 60, is removed, and the mean of the remaining 7 values is calculated. What is the new mean?$q$, $q$["20","22","23","18"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The original sum is 8 × 25 = 200. Removing 60 leaves a sum of 200 − 60 = 140, and 140/7 = 20.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-296$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The mean of 14 numbers is 75. When one more number is added to the set, the new mean of all 15 numbers is 73. What is the value of the number that was added?$q$, $q$["1095","45","50","75"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The original sum is 14 × 75 = 1050. The new sum is 15 × 73 = 1095. The added number is 1095 − 1050 = 45.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-297$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A scatterplot of hours of sleep (x) versus reaction time in milliseconds (y) has a line of best fit y = −15x + 400. What does the slope indicate about the relationship between sleep and reaction time?$q$, $q$["Each additional hour of sleep is associated with a decrease of 15 milliseconds in reaction time.","Each additional hour of sleep is associated with an increase of 15 milliseconds in reaction time.","A person who sleeps 0 hours has a reaction time of 15 milliseconds.","Reaction time causes changes in hours of sleep."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The negative slope, −15, means reaction time decreases by 15 milliseconds for each additional hour of sleep.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-298$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A line of best fit for a scatterplot is y = 6x − 10. What is the predicted value of y when x = 8?$q$, $q$["38","48","28","44"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 8: y = 6(8) − 10 = 48 − 10 = 38.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-299$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot shows a weak positive linear association between two variables, with many points scattered far from the line of best fit. Which statement is best supported?$q$, $q$["The line of best fit is not a strong predictor of individual values of y from x.","The correlation coefficient is likely close to 1.","The two variables must be unrelated.","The line of best fit cannot be calculated for this data."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A weak association with points scattered far from the line indicates the linear model explains little of the variation, so it predicts individual values poorly, even though a positive trend exists.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-300$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A line of best fit is given by y = 7x + 15. For a data point at x = 10, the actual y-value is 90. What is the residual for this point?$q$, $q$["5","−5","85","90"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The predicted value is 7(10) + 15 = 85. The residual is actual minus predicted: 90 − 85 = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-301$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A researcher fits a line to a scatterplot of advertising spending versus sales and finds a correlation coefficient of r = 0.92. What does this value most directly indicate?$q$, $q$["There is a strong positive linear association between advertising spending and sales.","Advertising spending causes 92% of all sales.","There is a strong negative linear association between advertising spending and sales.","92% of the data points lie exactly on the line of best fit."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A correlation coefficient close to 1 indicates a strong positive linear association, but it does not establish causation or measure the percentage of points on the line.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-302$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The line of best fit for a data set is y = 5x + 87. For a data point where x = 18, the actual y-value observed is 165. What is the residual (actual minus predicted) for this point?$q$, $q$["177","12","−12","165"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The predicted value is y = 5(18) + 87 = 177. The residual is the actual value minus the predicted value: 165 − 177 = −12.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-303$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A garden has roses and tulips in a ratio of 2:9. If there are 18 roses, how many tulips are there?$q$, $q$["81","36","63","99"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Roses are 2 parts and equal 18, so each part is 9. Tulips = 9 × 9 = 81.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-304$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A scale model of a building uses a ratio of 1 inch to 25 feet. If the model is 8 inches tall, how tall is the actual building, in feet?$q$, $q$["200","180","220","160"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$8 inches × 25 feet per inch = 200 feet.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-305$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A solution is made by mixing acid and water in a ratio of 2:7. How many liters of water must be added to 4 liters of acid to maintain this ratio?$q$, $q$["14","8","18","10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Acid is 2 parts and equals 4 liters, so each part is 2 liters. Water = 7 × 2 = 14 liters.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-306$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$In a bag of mixed nuts, the ratio of almonds to cashews to walnuts is 4:5:3. If there are 45 cashews, how many total nuts are in the bag?$q$, $q$["108","99","117","90"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Cashews are 5 parts and equal 45, so each part is 9. Total parts = 4 + 5 + 3 = 12, so total nuts = 12 × 9 = 108.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-307$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A factory's defect rate is 3 defective items for every 250 produced. At this rate, how many defective items would be expected in a batch of 5,000 items?$q$, $q$["60","50","75","40"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The rate is 3 defective items per 250 produced. Since 5,000 is 20 times 250, the expected number of defects is 3 × 20 = 60.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-308$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A recipe uses 6 cups of flour for every 2 cups of sugar. If a baker uses 15 cups of flour, how many cups of sugar should be used to keep the same ratio?$q$, $q$["15","2","7","5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The scale factor from the original recipe is 15/6 = 2.5. Scaling the sugar amount by the same factor: 2 × 2.5 = 5 cups.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-309$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A train travels 180 miles in 3 hours at a constant speed. How far will it travel in 5 hours at the same speed?$q$, $q$["300","240","360","180"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rate = 180/3 = 60 miles per hour. In 5 hours: 60 × 5 = 300 miles.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-310$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A copier prints 24 pages per minute at a constant rate. How many minutes will it take to print 360 pages?$q$, $q$["15","12","20","24"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Time = 360 pages ÷ 24 pages per minute = 15 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-311$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A chemical reaction consumes a reactant at a constant rate of 2.5 grams per minute. How many grams are consumed in 1 hour and 12 minutes?$q$, $q$["180","150","165","175"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$1 hour 12 minutes = 72 minutes. At 2.5 grams per minute: 2.5 × 72 = 180 grams.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-312$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A car's fuel efficiency is 32 miles per gallon. If gasoline costs $3.60 per gallon, what is the approximate cost of fuel for a 400-mile trip?$q$, $q$["$45","$40","$50","$36"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The trip requires 400/32 = 12.5 gallons. At $3.60 per gallon, the cost is 12.5 × 3.60 = $45.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-313$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A car travels at a constant speed of 20 miles per hour. Using 1 mile = 5,280 feet, what is the car's speed in feet per second, rounded to the nearest tenth?$q$, $q$["58.6","29","29.3","20"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Convert: 20 miles/hour × 5,280 feet/mile ÷ 3,600 seconds/hour = 29.3 feet per second.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-314$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$Pump A fills a tank at a rate of 5 liters per hour. Pump B fills the same tank at a rate of 5 liters every 10 minutes. Working together, how many liters do the two pumps fill in 2 hours?$q$, $q$["105","70","10","68"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Pump B's rate is 5 liters per 10 minutes = 30 liters per hour. Together, the pumps fill 5 + 30 = 35 liters per hour. In 2 hours, they fill 35 × 2 = 70 liters.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-315$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A standard deck of 52 cards contains 13 hearts. What is the probability of randomly drawing a heart from a full, well-shuffled deck?$q$, $q$["1/4","1/13","1/2","4/13"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 13 hearts out of 52 cards, so the probability is 13/52 = 1/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-316$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A spinner has 10 equal sections numbered 1 through 10. What is the probability that a single spin lands on a number that is odd or less than 4?$q$, $q$["3/5","4/5","1/2","7/10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Odd numbers: {1, 3, 5, 7, 9}. Numbers less than 4: {1, 2, 3}. Combined without double-counting 1 and 3: {1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 9}, which is 6 outcomes out of 10, so the probability is 6/10 = 3/5.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-317$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A committee of 2 people is chosen at random from a group of 4 men and 3 women. What is the probability that both people chosen are women?$q$, $q$["1/7","3/7","2/7","1/21"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are C(7,2) = 21 ways to choose 2 people from 7, and C(3,2) = 3 ways to choose 2 women from 3. The probability is 3/21 = 1/7.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-318$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A fair coin is flipped 4 times. What is the probability of getting exactly 2 heads?$q$, $q$["3/8","1/4","1/2","1/16"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are C(4,2) = 6 ways to arrange exactly 2 heads among 4 flips, out of 2⁴ = 16 total outcomes, so the probability is 6/16 = 3/8.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-319$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A bag contains 3 red marbles and 5 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn at random, one after another, without replacement. What is the probability that both marbles are red?$q$, $q$["3/8","3/28","9/64","4/49"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$P(first red) = 3/8. Without replacement, P(second red | first red) = 2/7. So P(both red) = (3/8) × (2/7) = 3/28.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-320$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 1, null, $q$A random sample of 120 commuters found that 72 use public transportation. Based on this sample, what percentage of all commuters would be expected to use public transportation?$q$, $q$["60%","72%","48%","40%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$72/120 = 0.60 = 60%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-321$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 1, null, $q$A random sample of 80 patients at a clinic found that 20 reported side effects from a medication. Based on this sample, what is the best estimate of the number of patients who would report side effects out of a new group of 500 patients?$q$, $q$["125","100","150","80"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sample rate is 20/80 = 25%. Applying this rate to 500 patients gives 0.25 × 500 = 125.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-322$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 1, null, $q$A random sample of 60 trees in a forest found that 18 were a particular species. Based on this sample, what percentage of all trees in the forest would be expected to be that species?$q$, $q$["30%","18%","60%","42%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$18/60 = 0.30 = 30%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-323$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 350 adults found that 161 get at least 7 hours of sleep per night, with a margin of error of 4 percentage points. Which is the most reasonable conclusion about all adults?$q$, $q$["Between about 42% and 50% of all adults likely get at least 7 hours of sleep per night.","Exactly 46% of all adults get at least 7 hours of sleep per night.","At least 50% of all adults get at least 7 hours of sleep per night.","Fewer than 42% of all adults get at least 7 hours of sleep per night."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sample estimate is 161/350 = 46%. With a margin of error of 4 percentage points, the plausible range for the population is about 42% to 50%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-324$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A pollster increases a survey's sample size from 300 to 1,200 voters while keeping the sampling method the same. What is the most likely effect on the margin of error?$q$, $q$["The margin of error will decrease.","The margin of error will increase.","The margin of error will stay exactly the same.","The margin of error cannot be affected by sample size."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Increasing the sample size, with the same sampling method, generally decreases the margin of error because larger samples yield more precise estimates.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-325$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$Two independent random samples of voters are taken from the same city, each using the same sampling method. Sample 1 has 200 voters, and Sample 2 has 800 voters. Which sample's result is likely to give a more precise estimate of the true population percentage?$q$, $q$["Sample 2, because larger random samples generally produce more precise estimates.","Sample 1, because smaller samples are always more precise.","Both samples are equally precise, regardless of size.","Precision cannot depend on sample size."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Larger random samples tend to produce estimates with smaller margins of error, so Sample 2's result is likely more precise than Sample 1's.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-326$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 900 adults found that 180 plan to travel internationally next year, with a margin of error of 2 percentage points. Which statement is most consistent with this result?$q$, $q$["It is plausible that between 18% and 22% of all adults plan to travel internationally next year.","Exactly 20% of all adults plan to travel internationally next year.","The true population percentage must be exactly 18% or 22%.","Fewer than 18% of all adults plan to travel internationally next year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sample percentage is 180/900 = 20%. With a margin of error of 2 percentage points, the plausible range for the population is 18% to 22%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-327$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A quality-control team randomly samples 250 items from a large production run and finds 15 defective items. Based on this sample, about how many defective items would be expected in a production run of 10,000 items?$q$, $q$["600","150","400","750"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The defect rate in the sample is 15/250 = 6%. Applying this rate to 10,000 items gives 0.06 × 10,000 = 600.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-328$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of a random sample estimates that 34% of a population supports a proposal, with a margin of error of 5 percentage points. Based on this survey, is it plausible that exactly 32% of the population supports the proposal?$q$, $q$["Yes, because it falls within the margin of error","Cannot be determined without more information","Yes, because the sample size is large enough","No, because the margin of error only applies to the mean"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The survey's plausible range is 34% ± 5, which is from 29% to 39%. Since 32% falls within this range, the claim is plausible based on this survey.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-329$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample estimates that 67% of a population favors a policy, with a margin of error of 2.5 percentage points. What is the smallest percentage that is within the margin of error of this estimate?$q$, $q$["67","64.5","69.5","62"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The margin of error gives a range from 67% − 2.5% to 67% + 2.5%, that is, from 64.5% to 69.5%. The smallest percentage in this range is 64.5%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-330$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 1, null, $q$A talk show host claims 'most people prefer tea over coffee,' based on a call-in poll of listeners who phoned the show to vote. What is the main flaw in this claim?$q$, $q$["The listeners who chose to call in are not a representative sample of all people.","Call-in polls always produce the exact opposite of the true result.","Tea and coffee preferences cannot be measured by any survey.","The claim would only be flawed if fewer than 1,000 people called in."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A self-selected group of callers is not representative of the general population, so the result cannot be generalized.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-331$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 1, null, $q$A principal wants to estimate the average amount of homework time for all students at a school. Which method would produce the most representative estimate?$q$, $q$["Randomly selecting students from the full enrollment list","Surveying only students in advanced placement classes","Surveying only students who stay after school for tutoring","Surveying only students on the student council"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A random sample from the full enrollment list represents the whole student body, unlike samples drawn from a narrower, atypical subgroup.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-332$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 1, null, $q$A survey asks, 'Don't you agree that the excellent new park design is a good use of city funds?' What is the main problem with this survey question?$q$, $q$["The wording is leading and likely to bias respondents toward agreeing.","The question is too short to be understood.","The question cannot be answered by anyone.","The question only applies to people who dislike parks."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using loaded language like 'excellent' and phrasing the question to invite agreement biases responses, rather than neutrally measuring opinion.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-333$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A study randomly assigns participants to either take a new supplement or a placebo, without participants or researchers knowing who received which. The supplement group shows significantly better results. What design feature most supports a causal conclusion here?$q$, $q$["Random assignment combined with blinding reduces the influence of confounding variables and bias.","The use of a supplement instead of a drug guarantees causation.","Observational comparisons always support causal claims.","The sample size alone determines whether the result is causal."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Randomly assigning participants and blinding both groups controls for confounding variables and placebo effects, which strengthens the case that the supplement caused the improvement.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-334$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A city official claims that a new traffic law reduced accidents, citing that accidents dropped the month after the law passed. What additional information would most help evaluate this claim?$q$, $q$["Whether accident rates were already trending downward before the law, and data over a longer time period","The exact wording of the new law","The number of police officers employed by the city","The total population of the city"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Without knowing whether accidents were already decreasing or data over a longer period, a one-month drop could reflect a pre-existing trend or random variation rather than the law's effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-335$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A sample of 50 people is selected by choosing every 10th person who enters a particular grocery store on a single Tuesday morning. Which statement best describes a limitation of this sample for estimating the shopping habits of all adults in the city?$q$, $q$["The sample may not represent adults who shop at different times, stores, or days.","The sample is a simple random sample of all city adults.","The sample size of 50 is too large to be useful.","Systematic sampling can never be used in surveys."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Sampling only from one store on one morning excludes people who shop elsewhere or at other times, so the sample may not represent all adults in the city.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-336$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A researcher compares the test scores of students who chose to take an optional review course with those who did not, finding the review-course students scored higher on average. Why might this NOT prove the course caused higher scores?$q$, $q$["Students who chose to take the course may differ in motivation or ability from those who didn't, confounding the comparison.","Test scores can never be compared between two groups.","The review course must have caused the score difference, since the groups differ.","Observational studies always produce causal conclusions."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because students self-selected into the course, motivated or higher-achieving students may have been more likely to enroll, so other factors (confounding variables) could explain the score difference rather than the course itself.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-337$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A university wants to know whether a new study app improves grades for its students. It recruits 300 student volunteers and randomly assigns half to use the app and half not to, then compares end-of-semester GPAs. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion the university can draw?$q$, $q$["The app causes a GPA change for all students at the university","Among students similar to the volunteers, using the app can be said to cause a change in GPA, but the result may not generalize to all students at the university","No conclusion about cause can be drawn because volunteers are never representative","The results generalize to all students, but no causal claim is possible"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Random assignment allows a causal conclusion about the volunteer group, but because the volunteers were not randomly selected from the full student population, the finding cannot automatically be generalized to all students.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-338$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$Researchers randomly selected 500 adults from a city's population and surveyed their sleep habits and job satisfaction. They found a correlation between more sleep and higher job satisfaction. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["The results can be generalized to the city's adult population, but a causal relationship between sleep and job satisfaction cannot be established","The results cannot be generalized because the sample was not randomly assigned to treatment groups","A causal relationship can be established because the sample size is large","The results can be generalized to the city's adult population, and more sleep causes higher job satisfaction"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the sample was randomly selected (not randomly assigned to a sleep treatment), the results generalize to the population sampled, but since this is an observational study, no causal claim can be made.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-339$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A researcher randomly assigned 200 volunteers who agreed to participate in a study to either a new exercise program or no program, and measured changes in resting heart rate. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["A causal relationship between the exercise program and heart rate change can be established for people like those in the study, but the results cannot necessarily be generalized to the broader population","A causal relationship can be established, and the results can be generalized to the entire population","Neither a causal relationship nor generalization is possible from this study","The results can be generalized to the entire population, but no causal relationship can be established"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Random assignment (not random selection from the population) supports a causal conclusion for the study's volunteer participants, but without random sampling from the broader population, the results cannot be generalized beyond people similar to those volunteers.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-340$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 1, null, $q$Of 48 people surveyed, 30 like hiking. Of those who like hiking, 18 also like camping. What is the probability that a randomly selected hiking fan also likes camping?$q$, $q$["3/5","18/48","2/5","30/48"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(camping | hiking) = (hiking fans who also like camping) / (total hiking fans) = 18/30 = 3/5.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-341$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 1, null, $q$Of 60 students surveyed, 36 play video games. Of those who play video games, 27 also watch esports streams. What is the probability that a randomly selected video-game player also watches esports streams?$q$, $q$["3/4","27/60","1/4","36/60"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(watches esports | plays video games) = (video-game players who also watch esports) / (total video-game players) = 27/36 = 3/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-342$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 1, null, $q$Of 90 shoppers surveyed, 54 bought groceries online last month. Of those who bought groceries online, 36 also used a discount code. What is the probability that a randomly selected online grocery shopper also used a discount code?$q$, $q$["2/3","36/90","1/3","54/90"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(used discount code | shopped online) = (online shoppers who also used a discount code) / (total online shoppers) = 36/54 = 2/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-343$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 320 gym members found that 200 use the weight room. Of those who use the weight room, 80 also take group classes. Of the 120 members who do not use the weight room, 60 take group classes. What is the probability that a randomly selected member who takes group classes also uses the weight room?$q$, $q$["4/7","80/200","80/320","60/140"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total group-class takers = 80 + 60 = 140. Of those, 80 also use the weight room, so the probability is 80/140 = 4/7.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-344$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 500 moviegoers found that 300 saw an action film. Of those who saw an action film, 90 also bought a large popcorn. Of the 200 moviegoers who saw a different genre, 50 bought a large popcorn. What is the probability that a randomly selected moviegoer who bought a large popcorn also saw an action film?$q$, $q$["9/14","90/300","90/500","50/140"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total large-popcorn buyers = 90 + 50 = 140. Of those, 90 also saw an action film, so the probability is 90/140 = 9/14.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-345$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 270 students found that 180 take a science elective. Of those who take a science elective, 120 also take an art elective. Of the 90 students who do not take a science elective, 30 take an art elective. What is the probability that a randomly selected student who takes an art elective also takes a science elective?$q$, $q$["4/5","120/180","120/270","30/150"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total art-elective students = 120 + 30 = 150. Of those, 120 also take a science elective, so the probability is 120/150 = 4/5.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-346$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 210 office workers found that 140 work hybrid schedules. Of those who work hybrid, 35 commute by bike. Of the 70 workers who do not work hybrid, 21 commute by bike. What is the probability that a randomly selected worker who commutes by bike also works a hybrid schedule?$q$, $q$["5/8","35/140","35/210","21/56"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total bike commuters = 35 + 21 = 56. Of those, 35 also work a hybrid schedule, so the probability is 35/56 = 5/8.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-347$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 200 people included 100 from Group A and 100 from Group B. 50% of Group A and 30% of Group B answered "yes" to a question (the rest answered "no"). If a person who answered "no" is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is from Group B?$q$, $q$["7/10","7/20","5/12","7/12"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Group A had 100 − 50 = 50 "no" responses, and Group B had 100 − 30 = 70 "no" responses, for a total of 120 "no" responses. The probability the person is from Group B is 70/120 = 7/12.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-348$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 150 people included 60 from Group A and 90 from Group B. 10% of Group A and 40% of Group B answered "yes" to a question. If a person who answered "yes" is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is from Group A?$q$, $q$["6/7","1/7","1/10","1/25"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Group A had 10% of 60 = 6 "yes" responses, and Group B had 40% of 90 = 36 "yes" responses, for a total of 42 "yes" responses. The probability the person is from Group A is 6/42 = 1/7.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-349$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 140 people included 50 from Group A and 90 from Group B. 60% of Group A and 40% of Group B answered "yes" to a question (the rest answered "no"). If a person who answered "no" is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is from Group B?$q$, $q$["27/37","10/37","27/70","3/5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Group A had 50 − 30 = 20 "no" responses, and Group B had 90 − 36 = 54 "no" responses, for a total of 74 "no" responses. The probability the person is from Group B is 54/74 = 27/37.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-350$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A school's chess club has a ratio of returning members to new members of 5:3. If there are 18 new members, how many returning members are there?$q$, $q$["30","18","10.8","48"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Returning members = new members × (5/3) = 18 × 5/3 = 30.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-351$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$On a map, 2 centimeters represents 15 kilometers of actual distance. If two cities are 6 centimeters apart on the map, what is the actual distance between them, in kilometers?$q$, $q$["45","90","21","0.8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Actual distance = 6 × (15/2) = 45 kilometers. (Multiplying 6 by 15 without dividing by 2 gives the incorrect 90; inverting the scale gives 0.8.)$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-352$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A fruit punch recipe combines orange juice, pineapple juice, and sparkling water in the ratio 3:2:5. If a batch uses 10 liters of pineapple juice, how many total liters of punch are in the batch?$q$, $q$["50","25","40","35"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Pineapple juice is 2 parts and equals 10 liters, so each part is 5 liters. Orange juice = 3 × 5 = 15 L, sparkling water = 5 × 5 = 25 L. Total = 15 + 10 + 25 = 50 liters.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-353$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$At a bakery, the ratio of chocolate chip cookies to oatmeal cookies on a tray is 7:4. After a batch of oatmeal cookies is added, the ratio becomes 7:6, and there are still 28 chocolate chip cookies on the tray. How many oatmeal cookies were added?$q$, $q$["8","24","16","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Chocolate chip count stays at 28, which is 7 parts, so each part = 4. Originally oatmeal = 4 parts = 16; after the addition, oatmeal = 6 parts = 24. Cookies added = 24 − 16 = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-354$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A scale drawing of a park uses a scale where 3 inches represents 12 feet. If a path in the drawing is 7 inches long, how long is the actual path, in feet?$q$, $q$["28","84","21","16"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Scale factor = 12 ÷ 3 = 4 feet per inch. Actual length = 7 × 4 = 28 feet.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-355$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$In a mixture, the ratio of sand to gravel to cement is 4:4:6. If the total mixture weighs 112 kilograms, how many kilograms of gravel are in the mixture?$q$, $q$["48","32","40","31"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The ratio parts total 4 + 4 + 6 = 14, so each part weighs 112/14 = 8 kilograms. Gravel makes up 4 parts, so it weighs 4 × 8 = 32 kilograms.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-356$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$A recipe uses 2 cups of flour for every 4 cups of sugar. If a baker uses 7 cups of flour, how many cups of sugar should be used to keep the same ratio?$q$, $q$["4","7","14","18"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The scale factor from the original recipe is 7/2 = 3.5. Scaling the sugar amount by the same factor: 4 × 3.5 = 14 cups.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-357$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A canning plant fills 540 jars in 9 hours at a constant rate. How many jars does it fill in 5 hours?$q$, $q$["300","270","60","2700"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rate = 540/9 = 60 jars per hour. In 5 hours: 60 × 5 = 300 jars.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-358$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$Water flows from a tank at a constant rate of 15 liters per minute. How many liters flow out in 2 hours?$q$, $q$["1800","900","30","3600"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$2 hours = 120 minutes. 15 L/min × 120 min = 1,800 liters.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-359$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A delivery drone travels at a constant speed of 44 feet per second. What is its speed in miles per hour? (1 mile = 5,280 feet, 1 hour = 3,600 seconds)$q$, $q$["30","44","88","15"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$44 ft/s × 3,600 s/hr = 158,400 ft/hr. Converting to miles: 158,400 ÷ 5,280 = 30 miles per hour.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-360$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A cyclist bikes 18 miles in 72 minutes at a constant pace. At this same pace, how many minutes would it take to bike 30 miles?$q$, $q$["120","90","108","150"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Pace = 72 min ÷ 18 miles = 4 min/mile. Time for 30 miles = 4 × 30 = 120 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-361$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A tank holds 1,800 gallons when full. A pump fills it at a constant rate, completing the job in 6 hours. After the pump has run for 2 hours, how many more gallons are needed to fill the tank?$q$, $q$["1200","600","1500","300"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rate = 1800/6 = 300 gallons per hour. After 2 hours, 300 × 2 = 600 gallons have been added. Remaining = 1800 − 600 = 1,200 gallons.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-362$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A car travels at a constant speed of 50 miles per hour. Using 1 mile = 5,280 feet, what is the car's speed in feet per second, rounded to the nearest tenth?$q$, $q$["50","73","146.6","73.3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Convert: 50 miles/hour × 5,280 feet/mile ÷ 3,600 seconds/hour = 73.3 feet per second.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-363$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$Pump A fills a tank at a rate of 2 liters per hour. Pump B fills the same tank at a rate of 3 liters every 10 minutes. Working together, how many liters do the two pumps fill in 4 hours?$q$, $q$["76","8","80","100"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Pump B's rate is 3 liters per 10 minutes = 18 liters per hour. Together, the pumps fill 2 + 18 = 20 liters per hour. In 4 hours, they fill 20 × 4 = 80 liters.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-364$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$An $80 jacket is marked down by 25%. What is the sale price?$q$, $q$["$60","$55","$20","$65"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Discount = 25% of 80 = 20. Sale price = 80 − 20 = $60.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-365$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$A survey of 250 people found that 40% own a pet. How many people in the survey own a pet?$q$, $q$["100","150","60","40"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$40% of 250 = 0.40 × 250 = 100.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-366$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A price increased from $120 to $150, and then decreased by 20% from the new price. What is the final price?$q$, $q$["$120","$150","$96","$130"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$After the increase, the price is $150. A 20% decrease from $150 is 0.20×150 = $30, giving a final price of 150 − 30 = $120. (Applying the 20% decrease to the original $120 instead of the new $150 gives the incorrect $96.)$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-367$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A town's population grew by 8% in one year to reach 27,000. What was the population before the increase?$q$, $q$["25,000","24,840","26,000","29,160"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let P be the original population. P × 1.08 = 27,000, so P = 27,000/1.08 = 25,000. (Subtracting 8% of 27,000 instead of dividing gives the incorrect 24,840.)$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-368$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A retailer buys a lamp for $40 and sells it for $58. What is the markup percentage, based on the retailer's cost?$q$, $q$["45%","31%","18%","69%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Markup = (58 − 40)/40 = 18/40 = 0.45 = 45%. Using the sale price as the base (18/58 ≈ 31%) is a common error.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-369$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$After a 50% discount, the price of an item is $100. What was the original price, in dollars, before the discount?$q$, $q$["201","150","200","202"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, then P × (1 − 50/100) = 100, so P × 0.5 = 100, giving P = 100/0.5 = 200.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-370$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A value starts at 50. It first increases by 10%, and then the new value decreases by 40%. What is the overall percent change from the original value, to the nearest tenth of a percent?$q$, $q$["30","−29","−34","−30"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$After the increase, the value is 50 × 1.1 = 55. After the decrease, it is 55 × 0.6 = 33. The overall change is (33 − 50)/50 × 100% = −34%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-371$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A data set has values 4, 8, 8, 10, 15. What is the mode of this data set?$q$, $q$["8","10","9","15"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$8 appears twice, more often than any other value, so it is the mode. (The mean is 45/5=9.)$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-372$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$The weekly sales (in units) for a store over 5 weeks are 20, 22, 25, 22, 26. What is the median number of units sold?$q$, $q$["22","23","25","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Sorted: 20, 22, 22, 25, 26. The middle value is 22. (The mean is 115/5=23.)$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-373$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A data set of 7 exam scores has a mean of 82. If one score of 68 is removed, what is the mean of the remaining 6 scores?$q$, $q$["≈84.3","82","78","86"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total of 7 scores = 82×7 = 574. Removing 68 leaves 574−68=506 over 6 scores: 506/6 ≈ 84.3.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-374$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$Two sections of a course have the same mean score of 78 on a test, but Section A's scores range from 70 to 86 while Section B's scores range from 40 to 116. Which best describes the difference between the sections?$q$, $q$["Section B's scores are more spread out (greater variability) than Section A's, even though both have the same mean.","Section B has a higher average score than Section A.","Section A's scores are more spread out than Section B's.","The two sections have identical score distributions."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Equal means don't imply equal spread. Section B's wider range (40 to 116) indicates greater variability (standard deviation) than Section A's narrower range (70 to 86).$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-375$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A set of 10 numbers has a mean of 15. A new number, 15, is added to the set. What is the mean of the new set of 11 numbers?$q$, $q$["15","15.5","16","14.5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding a value equal to the existing mean does not change the mean: the new total is 150+15=165, divided by 11 = 15.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-376$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A class of 18 students has an average test score of 60, and another class of 7 students has an average test score of 72. What is the average test score of all the students combined, rounded to the nearest tenth?$q$, $q$["66","63","64.4","63.4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The combined total is 18(60) + 7(72) = 1584. Dividing by the total number of students, 18 + 7 = 25, gives an average of 1584/25 ≈ 63.4.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-377$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The mean of 12 numbers is 43. When one more number is added to the set, the new mean of all 13 numbers is 44.538. What is the value of the number that was added?$q$, $q$["63","579","43","68"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The original sum is 12 × 43 = 516. The new sum is 13 × 44.538 = 579. The added number is 579 − 516 = 63.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-378$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A scatterplot shows a strong negative linear relationship between the age of a car and its resale value. Which best describes this relationship?$q$, $q$["As a car's age increases, its resale value tends to decrease.","As a car's age increases, its resale value tends to increase.","A car's age and resale value are unrelated.","Older cars always have lower resale value than every newer car."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A strong negative relationship means the two variables move in opposite directions: as age increases, resale value tends to decrease.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-379$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A line of best fit for a scatterplot of hours worked (x) versus pay earned in dollars (y) is y = 18x + 5. According to this model, what is the predicted pay for working 0 hours?$q$, $q$["$5","$18","$0","$23"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The y-intercept of the line of best fit, 5, is the predicted value of y when x = 0.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-380$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot of advertising spending (x, in thousands of dollars) versus monthly sales (y, in thousands of dollars) has a line of best fit y = 2.5x + 30. Based on this model, what is the predicted increase in monthly sales for each additional $1,000 spent on advertising?$q$, $q$["$2,500","$30,000","$250","$2,500,000"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The slope, 2.5, means that for each additional $1,000 (one unit of x) spent on advertising, predicted monthly sales increase by 2.5 thousand dollars, or $2,500.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-381$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot plots the number of employees (x) at a company against annual revenue (y) in millions of dollars. The data shows a strong positive correlation. A news report claims, 'Hiring more employees causes a company's revenue to increase.' What is the main issue with this claim?$q$, $q$["Correlation alone does not establish causation; larger revenue could allow more hiring, or another factor could drive both.","The correlation coefficient must be negative for the claim to be questioned.","A strong positive correlation always proves a causal relationship.","Scatterplots cannot be used to study company data."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A strong correlation shows the two variables tend to move together, but it does not show that one causes the other — reverse causation or a confounding variable (such as company size) could explain the pattern.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-382$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot of weekly screen time (x, in hours) versus weekly outdoor physical activity (y, in hours) has a correlation coefficient of r = −0.82. Which statement correctly interprets this value?$q$, $q$["There is a strong negative linear relationship: more screen time is associated with less outdoor physical activity.","There is a strong positive linear relationship between screen time and outdoor activity.","There is a weak relationship between screen time and outdoor activity.","Screen time has no effect on outdoor activity."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$r = −0.82 is close to −1, indicating a strong negative linear association: as screen time increases, outdoor physical activity tends to decrease.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-383$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A line of best fit models a data set with a slope of 3 units per hour. According to this model, by how much does the predicted value change as the number of hours increases from 9 to 19?$q$, $q$["3","−30","30","33"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The predicted value changes by the slope times the change in x: 3 × (19 − 9) = 3 × 10 = 30.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-384$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The line of best fit for a data set is y = 7x + 100. For a data point where x = 32, the actual y-value observed is 323. What is the residual (actual minus predicted) for this point?$q$, $q$["1","−1","324","323"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The predicted value is y = 7(32) + 100 = 324. The residual is the actual value minus the predicted value: 323 − 324 = −1.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-385$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A bag contains 5 red marbles, 3 blue marbles, and 2 green marbles. If one marble is drawn at random, what is the probability that it is blue?$q$, $q$["3/10","1/3","2/10","5/10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(blue) = number of blue marbles / total marbles = 3/10.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-386$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A spinner is divided into 9 equal sections numbered 1 through 9. What is the probability that a single spin lands on a multiple of 3?$q$, $q$["1/3","4/9","1/9","2/9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The multiples of 3 from 1-9 are 3, 6, and 9 — 3 out of 9 equally likely outcomes, so the probability is 3/9 = 1/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-387$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A standard six-sided die is rolled twice. What is the probability that the sum of the two rolls is 9?$q$, $q$["1/9","1/6","1/12","4/6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 36 equally likely outcomes. The pairs summing to 9 are (3,6), (4,5), (5,4), (6,3) — 4 outcomes. Probability = 4/36 = 1/9.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-388$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A jar has 12 chips numbered 1 through 12. Two chips are drawn at random without replacement. What is the probability that both chips show even numbers?$q$, $q$["5/22","1/4","1/2","25/144"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(first even) = 6/12. Without replacement, P(second even | first even) = 5/11. P(both even) = (6/12)(5/11) = 30/132 = 5/22. (Treating the draws as independent with replacement would incorrectly give (6/12)² = 1/4.)$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-389$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$At a school fair, a game gives a prize to 1 in 20 players, independently each time. If a student plays the game twice, what is the probability that the student wins at least once?$q$, $q$["39/400","1/10","19/20","361/400"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$P(no win in one play) = 19/20. P(no win in two plays) = (19/20)² = 361/400. P(at least one win) = 1 − 361/400 = 39/400.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-390$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$The probability that a certain machine produces a defective part is 2/4 on any given run, independent of other runs. What is the probability that at least one of the next 3 parts is defective?$q$, $q$["7/8","1/2","1/8","3/2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The probability a single part is NOT defective is 1/2. For 3 independent runs, P(no defects) = (1/2)<sup>3</sup> = 8/64. So P(at least one defective) = 1 − 8/64 = 7/8.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-391$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A survey of 90 students found that 60 take a foreign language class and, of those, 24 also take a music class. What is the probability that a randomly selected student who takes a foreign language class also takes a music class?$q$, $q$["2/5","24/90","1/3","60/90"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Given that the student takes a foreign language class (60 students), 24 of them also take a music class, so the probability is 24/60 = 2/5.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-392$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 300 adults found that 180 own a smart speaker. Of those who own a smart speaker, 108 also own a smart thermostat. Of the 120 adults who do not own a smart speaker, 24 own a smart thermostat. What is the probability that a randomly selected adult who owns a smart thermostat also owns a smart speaker?$q$, $q$["9/11","108/180","108/300","24/132"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total thermostat owners = 108 + 24 = 132. Of those, 108 also own a smart speaker, so the probability is 108/132 = 9/11.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-393$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A diagnostic screening program tests 400 patients for a condition. 100 patients test positive. Of those who test positive, 90 actually have the condition. Of the 300 who test negative, 15 actually have the condition. What is the probability that a randomly selected patient who has the condition tested positive?$q$, $q$["6/7","90/100","90/400","15/105"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total patients with the condition = 90 + 15 = 105. Of those, 90 tested positive, so the probability is 90/105 = 6/7.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-394$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 500 library patrons found that 300 borrow e-books. Of those who borrow e-books, 210 also borrow audiobooks. Of the 200 patrons who do not borrow e-books, 40 borrow audiobooks. What is the probability that a randomly selected patron who borrows audiobooks also borrows e-books?$q$, $q$["21/25","210/300","210/500","40/250"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total audiobook borrowers = 210 + 40 = 250. Of those, 210 also borrow e-books, so the probability is 210/250 = 21/25.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-395$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A streaming service's data shows 360 subscribers, of whom 240 watch on a smart TV. Of those who watch on a smart TV, 180 also watch on a mobile device. Of the 120 subscribers who do not watch on a smart TV, 12 also watch on a mobile device. What is the probability that a randomly selected subscriber who watches on a mobile device also watches on a smart TV?$q$, $q$["15/16","180/240","180/360","12/192"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total mobile-device viewers = 180 + 12 = 192. Of those, 180 also watch on a smart TV, so the probability is 180/192 = 15/16.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-396$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 190 people included 120 from Group A and 70 from Group B. 90% of Group A and 25% of Group B answered "yes" to a question. If a person who answered "yes" is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is from Group A?$q$, $q$["54/95","1/7","9/10","6/7"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Group A had 90% of 120 = 108 "yes" responses, and Group B had 25% of 70 = 18 "yes" responses, for a total of 126 "yes" responses. The probability the person is from Group A is 108/126 = 6/7.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-397$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of 140 people included 90 from Group A and 50 from Group B. 60% of Group A and 90% of Group B answered "yes" to a question (the rest answered "no"). If a person who answered "no" is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is from Group B?$q$, $q$["1/10","5/41","1/28","36/41"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Group A had 90 − 54 = 36 "no" responses, and Group B had 50 − 45 = 5 "no" responses, for a total of 41 "no" responses. The probability the person is from Group B is 5/41 = 5/41.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-398$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 1, null, $q$A random sample of 160 podcast listeners found that 96 subscribe to a premium ad-free plan. Based on this sample, what is the best estimate of the percentage of all podcast listeners who subscribe to a premium plan?$q$, $q$["60%","96%","160%","40%"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$96/160 = 0.60 = 60%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-399$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 1, null, $q$A random sample of 80 households in a city found an average monthly electricity bill of $142. Based on this sample, which is the best estimate of the average monthly electricity bill for all households in the city?$q$, $q$["$142","$80","$142 times 80","It cannot be estimated without surveying every household"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sample mean, $142, is the best available point estimate for the population mean when the sample is random.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-400$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 200 students at a university found that 70% plan to attend graduate school. If the university has 8,000 students, what is the best estimate of the number of students who plan to attend graduate school?$q$, $q$["5,600","140","8,000","4,000"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$70% of 8,000 = 0.70 × 8,000 = 5,600.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-401$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 60 packages shipped by a warehouse had a mean weight of 4.2 pounds. Based on this sample, what is the best estimate of the total weight, in pounds, of all 9,000 packages shipped by the warehouse that month?$q$, $q$["37,800","4.2","252","2,143"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Estimated total weight = sample mean × population count = 4.2 × 9,000 = 37,800 pounds.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-402$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$Two random samples are taken from the same large population: Sample A has 50 people, and Sample B has 500 people. Both samples are used to estimate the same population proportion. Which statement is most accurate?$q$, $q$["Sample B's estimate is likely more precise (smaller margin of error) than Sample A's, because larger random samples tend to produce more reliable estimates.","Sample A's estimate is automatically more accurate because it is a smaller, more manageable sample.","Both samples will produce exactly the same estimate, since both are random.","Sample size does not affect the precision of an estimate, only the sampling method does."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$All else equal, larger random samples tend to yield estimates with smaller margins of error, making them more precise.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-403$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A quality inspector randomly samples 60 bolts from a large shipment and finds that 3 are defective. Based on this sample, what is the best estimate of the number of defective bolts in a shipment of 3,000 bolts?$q$, $q$["150","180","3","60"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Sample defect rate = 3/60 = 0.05 = 5%. Estimated defective bolts in 3,000 = 0.05 × 3,000 = 150.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-404$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey of a random sample estimates that 36% of a population supports a proposal, with a margin of error of 4 percentage points. Based on this survey, is it plausible that exactly 33% of the population supports the proposal?$q$, $q$["No, because the margin of error only applies to the mean","Cannot be determined without more information","Yes, because it falls within the margin of error","Yes, because the sample size is large enough"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The survey's plausible range is 36% ± 4, which is from 32% to 40%. Since 33% falls within this range, the claim is plausible based on this survey.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-405$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 1, null, $q$A news headline states, 'Ice cream sales and drowning incidents both rise in the summer, so ice cream sales cause drowning incidents.' What is the flaw in this reasoning?$q$, $q$["Both variables are likely driven by a confounding variable (warm weather), not a direct cause-and-effect relationship.","Ice cream sales can only be measured in winter.","Drowning incidents are not related to any other variable.","The claim is correct because both quantities increase together."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Warm weather (a confounding variable) likely increases both ice cream sales and swimming (and thus drowning risk) independently; the correlation does not mean one causes the other.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-406$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A city wants to find out whether a new bike-share program reduces residents' commute times. It compares commute times between residents who voluntarily sign up for the program and residents who do not sign up, without random assignment. What is the main weakness of this study design?$q$, $q$["Because participants self-selected into the program, the two groups could differ in other ways (such as living closer to transit) that also affect commute time.","The study cannot be conducted without a government budget.","Commute times cannot be compared between different groups of residents.","The program must be tested on every resident in the city to be valid."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Residents who choose to join a bike-share program might already live in more bike-friendly areas or have shorter commutes for other reasons, so without random assignment this difference (not the program) could explain any gap in commute times — a self-selection/confounding issue.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-407$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A company claims that a new energy drink improves athletic performance, citing a study in which participants' sprint times improved after drinking it. The study did not include a control group that did not drink the beverage. Why does this weaken the claim?$q$, $q$["Without a control group, it's unclear whether the improvement was due to the drink or another factor, such as practice effects from running the sprint multiple times.","Sprint times cannot be measured accurately without an energy drink.","A study without a control group always proves causation more strongly.","The sample size is automatically too small without a control group."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Without a comparison group that didn't receive the treatment, improvements could be due to other factors (such as practice or motivation), not the drink itself — a lack of a control group undermines causal claims.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-408$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A small study reports that a new treatment reduced symptoms in 80% of patients, based on a sample of 10 patients, none of whom were randomly selected. A competing study reports a 65% improvement rate from a randomized sample of 400 patients. Which study's result is likely a more reliable estimate of the treatment's true effect in the broader population, and why?$q$, $q$["The second study, because its much larger, randomly selected sample is less subject to both sampling variability and selection bias.","The first study, because an 80% improvement rate is a better outcome regardless of sample size.","Both studies are equally reliable, since percentages don't depend on sample size.","The first study, because smaller samples always generalize better to the population."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A larger, randomly selected sample reduces both random sampling error and selection bias, making its estimate more trustworthy than a tiny, non-random sample, even though its reported rate is lower.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-409$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey about internet usage is conducted only among people who respond to a questionnaire posted on a social media site. What type of bias does this sampling method most likely introduce?$q$, $q$["Selection bias, because people who use the internet and social media less are unlikely to see or respond to the survey.","Measurement bias, because online responses are always recorded incorrectly.","No bias, because online surveys reach the entire population equally.","Response bias, because social media users always lie on surveys."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Posting a survey about internet usage only on social media systematically excludes people who use the internet less, so the sample overrepresents heavy users — a form of selection/sampling bias.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-410$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A polling organization surveys a random sample of registered voters and reports that 54% support a measure, with a margin of error of 3 percentage points. Which of the following statements is best supported by this poll?$q$, $q$["More than half of all registered voters definitely support the measure","It is plausible that the true percentage of all registered voters who support the measure is between 51% and 57%","Exactly 54% of all registered voters support the measure","The poll proves that the measure will pass"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A margin of error defines a plausible range for the true population value: 54% ± 3 percentage points, or 51% to 57%. It does not establish an exact value or a certainty.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-411$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A study observes that students who take an SAT prep course tend to have higher test scores than students who do not, but students chose for themselves whether to take the course. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["A causal relationship can be concluded because the sample size was large","The results cannot be generalized or associated in any way","Taking the course causes higher test scores","There is an association between taking the course and higher scores, but a causal relationship cannot be concluded because students were not randomly assigned to take the course"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Without random assignment to the course, other factors (such as motivation or prior ability) could explain the association, so only a correlation — not causation — can be concluded.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-412$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$Two random samples were taken from the same large population to estimate a proportion, one of 900 people and one of 3600 people. Assuming the same confidence level, which statement about the margins of error is most likely true?$q$, $q$["Neither sample has a margin of error.","The sample of 900 has the smaller margin of error.","The sample of 3600 has the smaller margin of error.","The two samples have the same margin of error."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A larger random sample gives a smaller margin of error, because sampling variability shrinks as the sample grows.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-413$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 1200 residents of a city was surveyed. 52% of those surveyed said that they support extending library hours. The margin of error for this estimate is 2 percentage points at a 95% confidence level. Which statement is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["More than 54% of all residents give this response.","Exactly 52% of all residents of the city support extending library hours.","Between 50% and 54% of the 1200 people surveyed gave this response.","It is plausible that between 50% and 54% of all residents in the city support extending library hours."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The estimate 52% ± 2 points gives the interval 50% to 54% as the plausible range for the whole population.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-414$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A cyclist rides at a constant speed of 90 kilometers per hour. What is the speed, in meters per second?$q$, $q$["1500","25","15","324"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$90 km/h = 90·1000 m / 3600 s = 25 m/s.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-415$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A recipe uses 4 cups of flour for every 9 cups of milk. A baker uses 60 cups of flour. How many cups of milk are needed?$q$, $q$["135","60","540","65"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ratio flour : milk stays 4 : 9. 60/4 = 15 batches, so milk = 15·9 = 135.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-416$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey about a proposed bike lane included 45 members of Club A and 68 members of Club B. Of the members of Club A, 13 favored; of the members of Club B, 54 favored. The rest opposed. If a person who favored is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is among the members of Club A?$q$, $q$["13/113","13/45","13/67","54/67"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$13 of the 67 people who favored were members of Club A, so the probability is 13/67.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-417$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 2, null, $q$On a map, 2 centimeters represent 15 kilometers. Two towns are 20 centimeters apart on the map. How many kilometers apart are the actual towns?$q$, $q$["8/3","150","300","35"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Set up the proportion 2/15 = 20/x, so x = 20·15/2 = 150.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-418$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A line of best fit is y = 6x + 62, where x is the number of workers and y is the boxes packed per hour. According to the model, what does the number 6 represent?$q$, $q$["The maximum possible boxes packed per hour.","The predicted increase in the boxes packed per hour for each additional unit of number of workers.","The predicted boxes packed per hour when number of workers is 0.","The predicted number of workers when the boxes packed per hour is 0."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$6 is the slope, the predicted change in y for every 1-unit increase in x.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-419$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$Of 100 students, 39 take French, 49 take Spanish, and 23 take both. If a student is chosen at random, what is the probability that the student takes French or Spanish?$q$, $q$["13/20","7/20","14/20","22/25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Use inclusion–exclusion: 39 + 49 − 23 = 65 students take at least one, so the probability is 65/100 = 13/20.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-420$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$The ratio of cats to dogs animals in a collection is 5 to 2. If there are 49 animals in all, how many are cats?$q$, $q$["44","35","10","14"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The 7 ratio parts represent 49 items, so each part is 7, and there are 5·7 = 35 cats animals.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-421$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$120 is 60% of what number?$q$, $q$["200","300","180","720"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$60% of w is 120: w = 120/0.6 = 200.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-422$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$Data set A: 14, 18, 22, 26, 30.
