  ($q$pd-426$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$Of 120 students, 36 take French, 32 take Spanish, and 19 take both. If a student is chosen at random, what is the probability that the student takes French or Spanish?$q$, $q$["17/30","50/120","71/120","49/120"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Use inclusion–exclusion: 36 + 32 − 19 = 49 students take at least one, so the probability is 49/120 = 49/120.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-427$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A box contains 4 red pens and 6 black pens. Two pens are chosen at random without replacement. What is the probability that both pens are red?$q$, $q$["4/10","8/90","4/25","2/15"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first is red with probability 4/10; given that, the second is red with probability 3/9. Their product is 12/90 = 2/15.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-428$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey about a survey question about a new park included 63 students in Section 1 and 92 students in Section 2. Of the students in Section 1, 47 said yes; of the students in Section 2, 63 said yes. The rest said no. If a person who said yes is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is among the students in Section 1?$q$, $q$["47/63","47/155","47/110","63/110"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$47 of the 110 people who said yes were students in Section 1, so the probability is 47/110.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-429$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A bag contains 8 red, 8 blue, and 8 green marbles. If one marble is chosen at random, what is the probability that it is not red?$q$, $q$["16/24","16/8","17/24","8/24"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 24 marbles, and 8+8 = 16 are not red.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-430$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$In an observational study, researchers found that employees who telework had higher job satisfaction, on average, than those who did not. The participants chose their own habits. Which statement is best supported?$q$, $q$["The habit definitely causes higher job satisfaction.","There is no association between the two variables.","The study proves that changing the habit would change the outcomes for everyone.","There is an association between the habit and higher job satisfaction, but the study cannot show that the habit causes it."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Without random assignment, confounding factors may explain the association, so only an association (not causation) can be concluded.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-431$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$The price of an item changed from $50 to $60. What was the percent increase in price?$q$, $q$["17%","25%","10%","20%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The change is $10, and 10/50 = 0.2 = 20%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-432$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey about a certification exam included 49 residents of Oak Street and 117 residents of Pine Street. Of the residents of Oak Street, 33 passed; of the residents of Pine Street, 25 passed. The rest did not pass. If a person who did not pass is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is among the residents of Pine Street?$q$, $q$["16/108","92/166","92/108","92/117"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$16 of the residents of Oak Street and 92 of the residents of Pine Street did not pass, for 108 total. The probability is 92/108.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-433$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 1200 residents of a city was surveyed. 62% of those surveyed said that they support extending library hours. The margin of error for this estimate is 4 percentage points at a 95% confidence level. Which statement is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["More than 66% of all residents give this response.","Exactly 62% of all residents of the city support extending library hours.","Between 58% and 66% of the 1200 people surveyed gave this response.","It is plausible that between 58% and 66% of all residents in the city support extending library hours."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The estimate 62% ± 4 points gives the interval 58% to 66% as the plausible range for the whole population.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-434$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$In a survey of 400 adults, 40% own a smartphone. Of the adults who own a smartphone, 25% also own a tablet. How many of the adults surveyed own both a smartphone and a tablet?$q$, $q$["40","100","260","160"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$40% of 400 is 160 smartphone owners. 25% of them is 40.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-435$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$Two random samples were taken from the same large population to estimate a proportion, one of 400 people and one of 1600 people. Assuming the same confidence level, which statement about the margins of error is most likely true?$q$, $q$["Neither sample has a margin of error.","The sample of 1600 has the smaller margin of error.","The two samples have the same margin of error.","The sample of 400 has the smaller margin of error."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A larger random sample gives a smaller margin of error, because sampling variability shrinks as the sample grows.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-436$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$A line of best fit for a data set relating x (temperature in degrees) and y (ice-cream sales) is y = x + 34. What y-value does the line predict when x = 8?$q$, $q$["43","−26","42","−42"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$y = (8) + 34 = 42.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-437$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A bag contains 3 red, 9 blue, and 3 green marbles. If one marble is chosen at random, what is the probability that it is not red?$q$, $q$["12/3","9/15","3/15","12/15"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are 15 marbles, and 9+3 = 12 are not red.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-438$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A survey about a gym membership included 83 residents of Oak Street and 55 residents of Pine Street. Of the residents of Oak Street, 72 renewed; of the residents of Pine Street, 15 renewed. The rest did not renew. If a person who renewed is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is among the residents of Oak Street?$q$, $q$["15/87","72/138","72/87","72/83"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$72 of the 87 people who renewed were residents of Oak Street, so the probability is 72/87.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-439$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$80 volunteers were randomly assigned to two equal groups. One group used a plant-based fertilizer; the other did not. The group using a plant-based fertilizer had significantly better tomato yield. Which conclusion is best supported?$q$, $q$["A plant-based fertilizer has no relationship with tomato yield.","A plant-based fertilizer would improve tomato yield for every person in the world.","Using a plant-based fertilizer likely caused the improvement in tomato yield among volunteers like these.","The volunteers' tomato yield were better only because they volunteered."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Random assignment to treatment and control groups supports a causal conclusion for the kind of volunteers in the study. Since the volunteers were not randomly selected from a larger population, the result cannot be generalized to everyone.$q$, null),
