  ($q$pd-530$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$The mean of five numbers is 20. Four of the numbers are 19, 21, 25, 23. What is the fifth number?$q$, $q$["32","11","12","20"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The five numbers sum to 20 × 5 = 100. The four known numbers sum to 88, so the fifth is 100 − 88 = 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-531$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$In a poll of a random sample of voters, 39% said they support a proposed park, with a margin of error of 2 percentage points. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["Exactly 39% of all voters support the park.","Between 37% and 41% of the voters in the sample support the park.","The poll shows that a majority of all voters support the park.","The percent of all voters who support the park is plausibly between 37% and 41%."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The margin of error describes a plausible range for the population value: 39% ± 2%, i.e., 37% to 41%. It does not give an exact value, and the sample percent itself is known to be 39%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-532$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the median of the data set 8, 30, 9, 26, 10?$q$, $q$["22","10","17","9"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Order the values: 8, 9, 10, 26, 30. The middle value is 10.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-533$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$The price of a stock increased by 20% on Monday and then decreased by 20% on Tuesday. The price at the end of Tuesday was what percent of the price at the start of Monday?$q$, $q$["120%","80%","96%","100%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Multiply the factors: 1.2 × 0.8 = 0.96, so the final price is 96% of the starting price.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-534$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, $q$A survey of 151 people recorded the following. Among the adult members, 51 renewed this year and 51 did not renew. Among the student members, 26 renewed this year and 23 did not renew.$q$, $q$If one of the adult members is selected at random, what is the probability that the person selected is among those who renewed this year?$q$, $q$["26/77","51/77","1/2","51/151"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the 102 adult members are considered. Of these, 51 renewed this year, so the probability is 51/102 = 1/2.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-535$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$A car uses 1 gallon of gas for every 50 miles driven. Gas costs $5 per gallon. What is the cost of the gas, in dollars, for a 300-mile trip?$q$, $q$["30","15","150","250"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Gallons needed = 300/50 = 6. Cost = 6 × $5 = $30.$q$, null),
