  ($q$pd-645$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A researcher surveyed a random sample of 500 customers selected from the chain's loyalty-card database. Which of the following is the largest population to which the results can reasonably be generalized?$q$, $q$["All customers of a regional grocery chain","Only the people who were surveyed","All people who use social media","All people in the same state"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Random sampling from a list of all customers of a regional grocery chain supports generalizing to that group, but not beyond it.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-646$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$What is 75% of 60?$q$, $q$["45","120","15","90"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$75% of 60 = 0.75 × 60 = 45.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-647$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$In a study, students were randomly assigned to either receive a new tutoring program or not receive it. The group that received a new tutoring program showed a statistically significant improvement in test scores. Which conclusion is best supported by the study?$q$, $q$["Test scores and a new tutoring program are correlated, but no causal conclusion is possible.","A new tutoring program likely caused the improvement in test scores for students like those in the study.","Neither group's test scores changed.","A new tutoring program improves test scores for everyone, regardless of group."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Random assignment supports a causal conclusion, but only for populations similar to the participants.$q$, null),
