  ($q$pd-655$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A bag contains 3 red, 5 blue, and 9 green marbles. If one marble is chosen at random, what is the probability that it is blue?$q$, $q$["12/17","5/12","1/3","5/17"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are 5 blue marbles out of 17 total, so the probability is 5/17.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-656$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$A store discounts an item by 25%, and then takes an additional 40% off the discounted price. The two discounts together are equivalent to a single discount of what percent?$q$, $q$["32.5%","65%","15%","55%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The price becomes 0.75 × 0.6 = 0.45 of the original, which is a discount of 55%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-657$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$The mean of 9 numbers is 31. When one of the numbers, 55, is removed, what is the mean of the remaining 8 numbers?$q$, $q$["28","31","24","7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The total is 9 × 31 = 279. Without 55, the total is 224, so the new mean is 224/8 = 28.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-658$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A cyclist travels at a constant speed of 15 miles per hour. At this rate, how many feet does the cyclist travel in 1 second? (1 mile = 5,280 feet)$q$, $q$["22","79200","44","1320"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$15 miles per hour = 15 × 5,280 feet per 3,600 seconds = 22 feet per second.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-659$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$After a 25% increase, the price of a ticket is $120. What was the price before the increase?$q$, $q$["$90","$106","$96","$95"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Let x be the original price: 1.25x = 120 → x = 96.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-660$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$Of 300 members surveyed, 40 are members who run. Of those 40, 32 also swim. If one of the members who run is chosen at random, what is the probability that the person also swim?$q$, $q$["1/5","4/5","8/75","2/15"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Restricted to the 40 members who run, 32 also swim. The probability is 32/40 = 4/5.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-661$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the mean of the data set 5, 11, 27, 21, 16?$q$, $q$["16","27","15","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sum is 80, and 80/5 = 16.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-662$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$How many inches are in 8 feet?$q$, $q$["108","96","84","2/3"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$There are 12 inches in 1 fee, so 8 feet = 8 × 12 = 96 inches.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-663$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$In one class, 15 students had a mean test score of 62. In another class, 10 students had a mean score of 92 on the same test. What is the mean score of all 25 students?$q$, $q$["72","77","74","75"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Total points: 15(62) + 10(92) = 930 + 920 = 1850. Divide by 25: 1850/25 = 74.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-664$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The mean score of 12 students on a quiz is 68. A new student takes the quiz and scores 94. What is the mean score of all 13 students?$q$, $q$["68","71","70","81"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The original total is 12 × 68 = 816. Adding 94 gives 910. The new mean is 910/13 = 70.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-665$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><tr><th></th><th>Yes</th><th>No</th><th>Total</th></tr><tr><td>Morning shift</td><td>32</td><td>40</td><td>72</td></tr><tr><td>Evening shift</td><td>14</td><td>60</td><td>74</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>46</td><td>100</td><td>146</td></tr></table>The table summarizes the responses of 146 people in a survey. If one of the people in the evening shift row is selected at random, what is the probability that the answer recorded is “Yes”?$q$, $q$["7/73","30/37","7/23","7/37"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the 74 people in the Evening shift row are considered. Of them, 14 answered “Yes,” so the probability is 14/74.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-666$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A summer camp has boys and girls in the ratio 4 to 9. If 56 boys attend, how many girls attend?$q$, $q$["135","126","61","504"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Since 56 = 4 × 14, the scale factor is 14. So there are 9 × 14 = 126 girls.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-667$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot shows the relationship between the age of a tree, x, in years, and its height, y, in feet. The line of best fit is y = 2.2x + 12. Based on the line of best fit, what is the predicted value of y when x = 6?$q$, $q$["25.2","13.2","39.6","20.2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 6: y = 2.2(6) + 12 = 13.2 + 12 = 25.2.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-668$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$Researchers surveyed a random sample of 500 high school students in one state and found that students who reported more hours of sleep also tended to report higher grades. Which conclusion is best supported by this information?$q$, $q$["Getting more sleep causes students to earn higher grades.","Higher grades cause students to sleep more.","There is an association between sleep and grades among high school students in that state.","Students in every state who sleep more earn higher grades."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A random sample supports generalizing an association to the state's high school students, but the study was observational, so it cannot establish cause and effect in either direction.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-669$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$If 4 pounds of apples cost $48, how much do 9 pounds of apples cost at the same rate?$q$, $q$["$57","$108","$21.33","$120"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The cost of one is $48/4 = $12. So 9 cost 9 × $12 = $108.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-670$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A mosaic uses red tiles and blue tiles in the ratio 7 to 9. If 91 red tiles are used, how many blue tiles are used?$q$, $q$["126","819","117","93"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Since 91 = 7 × 13, the scale factor is 13. So there are 9 × 13 = 117 blue tiles.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-671$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$In a study, 120 volunteers were randomly assigned to drink either green tea or water daily for 8 weeks. The green tea group showed a greater average decrease in blood pressure. Which conclusion is best supported by this information?$q$, $q$["Drinking green tea likely caused a greater decrease in blood pressure for people similar to the volunteers.","Green tea will lower the blood pressure of every person who drinks it.","No conclusion can be drawn, because the volunteers chose their drink.","Green tea lowers blood pressure for all adults worldwide."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Random assignment supports a cause-and-effect conclusion, but because the participants were volunteers, it applies to people like them, not everyone. The volunteers did not choose their drinks, so the claim that no conclusion can be drawn because they chose their drink misreads the design.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-672$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$How many grams are in 8 kilograms?$q$, $q$["8000","1/125","7000","9000"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 1000 grams in 1 kilogram, so 8 kilograms = 8 × 1000 = 8000 grams.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-673$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$What is 80% of 60?$q$, $q$["12","48","480","58"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$80% of 60 = 0.8 × 60 = 48.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-674$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$The data set 95, 40, 21, 19, 44, 10, 72, 21, 34 lists the scores of a team in its recent games. What is the range of the scores?$q$, $q$["85","40","−61","95"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The range is the maximum minus the minimum: 95 − 10 = 85.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-675$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A box holds 12 cards, and 8 of them are marked with a star. Two cards are selected at random without replacement. What is the probability that both are marked with a star?$q$, $q$["7/18","4/9","7/11","14/33"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first selection has probability 8/12. After one is removed, 7 of the remaining 11 are marked with a star. Multiply: (8/12)(7/11) = 14/33.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-676$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$In one class, 18 students had a mean test score of 94. In another class, 27 students had a mean score of 79 on the same test. What is the mean score of all 45 students?$q$, $q$["85","86.5","87","86"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total points: 18(94) + 27(79) = 1692 + 2133 = 3825. Divide by 45: 3825/45 = 85.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-677$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A county has an area of 40 square miles and a population density of 340 people per square mile. What is the population of the county?$q$, $q$["380","13,600","8","136,000"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Population = density × area = 340 × 40 = 13,600.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-678$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The line of best fit for a data set is y = 0.6x + 45, where x is the number of minutes a car has been parked and y is the parking fee, in dollars. Which is the best interpretation of 0.6 in this context?$q$, $q$["The predicted value of y when x = 0.6","A predicted total fee of $0.6","The predicted fee for 0 minutes","A predicted fee increase of $0.6 for each additional minute"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The slope 0.6 is the predicted change in y for each increase of 1 in x, so it represents a predicted fee increase of $0.6 for each additional minute.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-679$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The line of best fit for a data set is y = 12x + 3.2, where x is a city's population in thousands and y is the number of restaurants. Which is the best interpretation of 12 in this context?$q$, $q$["About 12 more restaurants predicted for each additional person","The number of restaurants predicted for a population of 0","The predicted value of y when x = 12","About 12 more restaurants predicted for each additional 1,000 people"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The slope 12 is the predicted change in y for each increase of 1 in x, so it represents about 12 more restaurants predicted for each additional 1,000 people.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-680$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A box holds 19 seed packets, and 9 of them are for tomatoes. Two seed packets are selected at random without replacement. What is the probability that both are for tomatoes?$q$, $q$["72/361","4/19","4/9","81/361"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The first selection has probability 9/19. After one is removed, 8 of the remaining 18 are for tomatoes. Multiply: (9/19)(8/18) = 4/19.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-681$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$How many feet are in 6 yards?$q$, $q$["2","15","18","21"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$There are 3 feet in 1 yard, so 6 yards = 6 × 3 = 18 feet.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-682$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$How many minutes are in 6 hours?$q$, $q$["1/10","360","300","420"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$There are 60 minutes in 1 hour, so 6 hours = 6 × 60 = 360 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-683$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$After a 20% increase, the price of a lamp is $384. What was the price before the increase?$q$, $q$["$307.20","$320","$364","$344"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The new price is 120% of the original: 1.2 × P = 384, so P = 384/1.2 = 320.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-684$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$What is 25% of 800?$q$, $q$["200","2000","32","600"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$25% of 800 = 0.25 × 800 = 200.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-685$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The mean score of 11 students on a quiz is 90. A new student takes the quiz and scores 54. What is the mean score of all 12 students?$q$, $q$["87","72","90","88"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The original total is 11 × 90 = 990. Adding 54 gives 1044. The new mean is 1044/12 = 87.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-686$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 50 residents of a town was surveyed, and 11 of them said they use the public library at least once a month. The town has 2,400 residents in all. Based on the sample, which is the best estimate of the total number of residents who would say this?$q$, $q$["110","528","264","1,872"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sample proportion is 11/50. Applying it to all 2,400 residents: 2,400 × 11/50 = 528.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-687$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$In one class, 17 students had a mean test score of 61. In another class, 12 students had a mean score of 90 on the same test. What is the mean score of all 29 students?$q$, $q$["73","76","75.5","74"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Total points: 17(61) + 12(90) = 1037 + 1080 = 2117. Divide by 29: 2117/29 = 73.$q$, null),
