  ($q$pd-738$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 80 season-ticket holders of a baseball team was surveyed, and 16 of them said they would buy a parking pass. The baseball team has 1,200 season-ticket holders in all. Based on the sample, which is the best estimate of the total number of season-ticket holders who would say this?$q$, $q$["240","160","960","192"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sample proportion is 16/80. Applying it to all 1,200 season-ticket holders: 1,200 × 16/80 = 240.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-739$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$A punch recipe mixes liters of water and liters of juice concentrate in the ratio 2 to 3. If 28 liters of water are used, how many liters of juice concentrate are used?$q$, $q$["45","42","84","29"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Since 28 = 2 × 14, the scale factor is 14. So there are 3 × 14 = 42 liters of juice concentrate.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-740$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot shows the relationship between the age of a tree, x, in years, and its height, y, in feet. The line of best fit is y = 8x + 10. Based on the line of best fit, what is the predicted value of y when x = 8?$q$, $q$["74","26","144","64"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 8: y = 8(8) + 10 = 64 + 10 = 74.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-741$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$The price of a jacket was decreased by 20% and then the new price was increased by 20%. Which describes the overall change from the original price?$q$, $q$["40% decrease","4% increase","No change","4% decrease"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Multiply the multipliers: (0.8)(1.2) = 0.96. So the final price is 96% of the original, a 4% decrease.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-742$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$Based on a random sample, a survey estimated that 57% of residents of a county approve of a proposal, with a margin of error of 5 percentage points. Which is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["Between 52% and 62% of the people in the sample approve of the proposal.","It is plausible that between 52% and 62% of all residents of a county approve of the proposal.","It is plausible that between 47% and 67% of all residents of a county approve of the proposal.","Exactly 57% of all residents of a county approve of the proposal."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The margin of error gives a range of plausible values for the population: 57 ± 5, or 52% to 62%. The sample's own percentage is known exactly, so the margin applies to the whole population, not the sample.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-743$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the mean of the data set 16, 17, 29, 12, 33, 43?$q$, $q$["31","23","30","25"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sum of the 6 values is 150, so the mean is 150/6 = 25.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-744$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$In one class, 30 students had a mean test score of 74. In another class, 9 students had a mean score of 87 on the same test. What is the mean score of all 39 students?$q$, $q$["79","80.5","78","77"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Total points: 30(74) + 9(87) = 2220 + 783 = 3003. Divide by 39: 3003/39 = 77.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-745$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot shows the relationship between the age of a tree, x, in years, and its height, y, in feet. The line of best fit is y = 12x + 25. Based on the line of best fit, what is the predicted value of y when x = 6?$q$, $q$["97","72","43","372"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 6: y = 12(6) + 25 = 72 + 25 = 97.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-746$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the median of the data set 48, 32, 22, 31, 13, 15, 33, 24, 24, 30?$q$, $q$["29","14","30","27"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Order the values: 13, 15, 22, 24, 24, 30, 31, 32, 33, 48. With 10 values, the median is the mean of the two middle values, (24 + 30)/2 = 27.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-747$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$The data set 52, 56, 15, 53, 72, 44, 81 lists the scores of a team in its recent games. What is the range of the scores?$q$, $q$["66","81","53","29"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The range is the maximum minus the minimum: 81 − 15 = 66.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-748$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><tr><th></th><th>Passed</th><th>Did not pass</th><th>Total</th></tr><tr><td>Group A</td><td>12</td><td>46</td><td>58</td></tr><tr><td>Group B</td><td>56</td><td>45</td><td>101</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>68</td><td>91</td><td>159</td></tr></table>The table summarizes the responses of 159 people in a survey. If one of the people in the group b row is selected at random, what is the probability that the answer recorded is “Passed”?$q$, $q$["56/159","45/101","14/17","56/101"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the 101 people in the Group B row are considered. Of them, 56 answered “Passed,” so the probability is 56/101.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-749$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the median of the data set 14, 27, 37, 10, 19, 8, 47, 9, 31?$q$, $q$["21","19","39","27"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Order the values: 8, 9, 10, 14, 19, 27, 31, 37, 47. With 9 values, the median is the middle value, 19.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-750$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A bag contains 9 red marbles, 7 blue marbles, and 4 green marbles. If one marble is chosen at random, what is the probability that it is green?$q$, $q$["1/5","4/5","1/4","1/3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$There are 9 + 7 + 4 = 20 marbles, and 4 are green. The probability is 4/20 = 1/5.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-751$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the mean of the data set 20, 30, 20, 38, 25, 17?$q$, $q$["30","45/2","21","25"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sum of the 6 values is 150, so the mean is 150/6 = 25.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-752$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$After a 25% increase, the price of a lamp is $100. What was the price before the increase?$q$, $q$["$80","$50","$133.33","$75"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The new price is 125% of the original: 1.25 × P = 100, so P = 100/1.25 = 80.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-753$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><tr><th></th><th>Prefer tea</th><th>Prefer coffee</th><th>Total</th></tr><tr><td>Morning shift</td><td>60</td><td>44</td><td>104</td></tr><tr><td>Evening shift</td><td>10</td><td>21</td><td>31</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>70</td><td>65</td><td>135</td></tr></table>The table summarizes the responses of 135 people in a survey. If one of the people who answered “Prefer tea” is selected at random, what is the probability that the person is in the Morning shift row?$q$, $q$["15/26","6/7","1/7","4/9"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the 70 people who answered “Prefer tea” are considered. Of them, 60 are in the Morning shift row, so the probability is 60/70.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-754$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The line of best fit for a data set is y = 0.35x + 3.2, where x is the number of years since 2010 and y is the average price, in dollars, of a movie ticket. Which is the best interpretation of 0.35 in this context?$q$, $q$["The predicted ticket price in 2010","A predicted increase of $0.35 in the ticket price every 0.35 years","A predicted increase of $0.35 in the average ticket price each year","The predicted value of y when x = 0.35"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The slope 0.35 is the predicted change in y for each increase of 1 in x, so it represents a predicted increase of $0.35 in the average ticket price each year.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-755$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$Last year, the price of a bicycle was $50. This year it is $65. By what percent did it increase?$q$, $q$["23.1%","130%","15%","30%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The increase is 65 − 50 = 15. As a percent of the original: 15/50 = 0.3, or 30%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-756$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$After a 10% discount, the price of a lamp is $108. What was the price before the discount?$q$, $q$["$118.80","$98","$128","$120"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The new price is 90% of the original: 0.9 × P = 108, so P = 108/0.9 = 120.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-757$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The mean score of 6 students on a quiz is 69. A new student takes the quiz and scores 97. What is the mean score of all 7 students?$q$, $q$["83","73","74","69"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The original total is 6 × 69 = 414. Adding 97 gives 511. The new mean is 511/7 = 73.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-758$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the median of the data set 16, 19, 23, 2, 1, 47, 19, 6, 19, 1?$q$, $q$["17.5","24","19","19.5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Order the values: 1, 1, 2, 6, 16, 19, 19, 19, 23, 47. With 10 values, the median is the mean of the two middle values, (16 + 19)/2 = 35/2.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-759$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$Last year, the price of a bicycle was $400. This year it is $440. By what percent did it increase?$q$, $q$["9.1%","110%","40%","10%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The increase is 440 − 400 = 40. As a percent of the original: 40/400 = 0.1, or 10%.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-760$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A train travels at a constant speed of 60 miles per hour. How many minutes does it take the train to travel 330 miles?$q$, $q$["550","360","350","330"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Time = distance ÷ speed = 330/60 = 5.5 hours. Multiply by 60 minutes per hour: 5.5 × 60 = 330 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-761$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$How many inches are in 3/2 feet?$q$, $q$["12","18","1/8","30"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$There are 12 inches in 1 fee, so 3/2 feet = 3/2 × 12 = 18 inches.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-762$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$If 6 packs of batteries cost $15, how much do 10 packs of batteries cost at the same rate?$q$, $q$["$9","$25","$27.50","$150"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The cost of one is $15/6 = $2.50. So 10 cost 10 × $2.50 = $25.$q$, null),
