  ($q$pd-764$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The line of best fit for a data set is y = 4.2x + 120, where x is a city's population in thousands and y is the number of restaurants. Which is the best interpretation of 4.2 in this context?$q$, $q$["The number of restaurants predicted for a population of 0","About 4.2 more restaurants predicted for each additional 1,000 people","About 4.2 more restaurants predicted for each additional person","The predicted value of y when x = 4.2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The slope 4.2 is the predicted change in y for each increase of 1 in x, so it represents about 4.2 more restaurants predicted for each additional 1,000 people.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-765$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, null, $q$If 3 bottles of water cost $9, how much do 9 bottles of water cost at the same rate?$q$, $q$["$3","$27","$18","$30"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The cost of one is $9/3 = $3. So 9 cost 9 × $3 = $27.$q$, null),
