  ($q$pd-767$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$The price of a jacket was decreased by 30% and then the new price was increased by 25%. Which describes the overall change from the original price?$q$, $q$["25/2% increase","25/2% decrease","5% decrease","55% decrease"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiply the multipliers: (0.7)(1.25) = 0.875. So the final price is 87.5% of the original, a 25/2% decrease.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-768$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A train travels at a constant speed of 40 miles per hour. How many minutes does it take the train to travel 330 miles?$q$, $q$["825","505","495","525"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Time = distance ÷ speed = 330/40 = 8.25 hours. Multiply by 60 minutes per hour: 8.25 × 60 = 495 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-769$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A train travels at a constant speed of 60 miles per hour. How many minutes does it take the train to travel 210 miles?$q$, $q$["210","230","240","350"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Time = distance ÷ speed = 210/60 = 3.5 hours. Multiply by 60 minutes per hour: 3.5 × 60 = 210 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-770$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A website posted a poll asking visitors whether they prefer e-books or print books. Of the 2,000 visitors who responded, 70% preferred e-books. Which conclusion is best supported by this information?$q$, $q$["The results may not represent all readers, because the respondents chose to participate.","The sample is large enough that the result must apply to all readers.","E-books cause people to read more than print books.","About 70% of all readers prefer e-books."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A voluntary-response sample on a website is not random, so its results can't be generalized to all readers no matter how large it is. Nothing in the poll addresses cause and effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-771$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$Last year, the number of students in a club was 500. This year it is 575. By what percent did it increase?$q$, $q$["13%","15%","115%","75%"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The increase is 575 − 500 = 75. As a percent of the original: 75/500 = 0.15, or 15%.$q$, null),
