  ($q$pd-775$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$Based on a random sample, a survey estimated that 43% of voters in a state approve of a proposal, with a margin of error of 4 percentage points. Which is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["It is plausible that between 35% and 51% of all voters in a state approve of the proposal.","Between 39% and 47% of the people in the sample approve of the proposal.","It is plausible that between 39% and 47% of all voters in a state approve of the proposal.","Exactly 43% of all voters in a state approve of the proposal."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The margin of error gives a range of plausible values for the population: 43 ± 4, or 39% to 47%. The sample's own percentage is known exactly, so the margin applies to the whole population, not the sample.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-776$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, null, $q$A box holds 16 light bulbs, and 8 of them are defective. Two light bulbs are selected at random without replacement. What is the probability that both are defective?$q$, $q$["1/4","7/15","7/30","7/32"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The first selection has probability 8/16. After one is removed, 7 of the remaining 15 are defective. Multiply: (8/16)(7/15) = 7/30.$q$, null),
