  ($q$pd-785$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$After a 15% discount, the price of a lamp is $102. What was the price before the discount?$q$, $q$["$117.30","$87","$120","$132"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The new price is 85% of the original: 0.85 × P = 102, so P = 102/0.85 = 120.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-786$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$Last year, the number of students in a club was 60. This year it is 75. By what percent did it increase?$q$, $q$["125%","15%","25%","20%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The increase is 75 − 60 = 15. As a percent of the original: 15/60 = 0.25, or 25%.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h1$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The number p is 60% of the number r, and r is 10% greater than the number s. If s is positive, p is what percent of s?$q$, $q$["70%","6%","54%","66%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$r = 1.1s and p = 0.6r, so p = 0.6(1.1s) = 0.66s. So p is 66% of s. Adding or subtracting the percents directly is a common mistake, because the two percents are of different bases.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h2$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$A store discounts the original price of a jacket by 15%. A sales tax of 8% is then applied to the discounted price, and the final amount paid is $716.04. What was the original price of the jacket?$q$, $q$["$769.94","$757.57","$780.00","$842.40"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, the discounted price is 0.85P, and after tax the amount is 1.08(0.85P) = 0.918P. Setting this equal to 716.04 gives P = 716.04/0.918 = 780.00. Undoing the percents by reversing them as additions (e.g., adding back 15%) gives wrong answers because each percent applies to a different base.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h3$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$The mean of a data set of 17 numbers is 64. When one number is removed from the data set, the mean of the remaining numbers is 60. What number was removed?$q$, $q$["4","128","960","145"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The original sum is 17(64) = 1088. The remaining 16 numbers sum to 16(60) = 960. The removed number is 1088 − 960 = 128.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h4$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$In a class, the 16 students in section A had a mean test score of 75, and the students in section B had a mean test score of 90. The mean score of all the students in both sections combined was 80. How many students are in section B?$q$, $q$["8","16","9","24"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let b be the number of students in section B. The total of all scores is 16(75) + 90b, and it also equals 80(16 + b). So 1200 + 90b = 1280 + 80b, which gives 10b = 80 and b = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h5$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>4</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>The frequency table summarizes a data set of 23 values. What is the median of the data set?$q$, $q$["6","5.43","7","5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are 23 values, so the median is the 12th value when ordered. Counting up the cumulative frequencies (6, 12, 20, 21, 23), that position falls at the value 5. Note the median is not the middle row of the table (6) unless the frequencies happen to put it there.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h6$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Morning</th><th>Afternoon</th><th>Evening</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rated positive</td><td>8</td><td>30</td><td>43</td><td>81</td></tr><tr><td>Rated negative</td><td>60</td><td>9</td><td>12</td><td>81</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>68</td><td>39</td><td>55</td><td>162</td></tr></tbody></table>The table summarizes 162 customer visits recorded at a clinic. A visit will be selected at random from those in the "Rated positive" category. What is the probability that the visit is in the "Evening" category?$q$, $q$["43/55","43/162","43/81","1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the 81 visits in the "Rated positive" row are eligible. Of these, 43 are in the "Evening" column, so the probability is 43/81.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h7$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$A contractor charges $2.00 per square foot to refinish a floor. A rectangular floor has an area of 260 square yards. What is the total charge, in dollars, to refinish the floor? (1 yard = 3 feet)$q$, $q$["$1,560.00","$4,680.00","$520.00","$57.778"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$One square yard is 3 ft × 3 ft = 9 square feet, so 260 square yards = 2340 square feet. The charge is 2340 × $2.00 = $4,680.00. Multiplying by 3 instead of 9 is a common error.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h8$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 4, null, $q$Working together at constant rates, pipes A and B can fill the tank in 5/3 hours. Working alone, pipe A can fill the tank in 10 hours. How many hours would it take pipe B, working alone, to fill the tank?$q$, $q$["2","25/3","10/3","12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The combined rate is 1/(5/3) = 3/5 of the job per hour. Subtracting A's rate: 3/5 − 1/10 = 1/2. So B alone takes 2 hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h9$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 400 employees of a large company and found that 74% of those in the sample support a proposed park. The associated margin of error is 3 percentage points. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["Exactly 74% of all employees of the company support a proposed park.","Between 71% and 77% of the people in the sample support a proposed park.","It is plausible that between 71% and 77% of all residents of the state support a proposed park.","It is plausible that between 71% and 77% of all employees of the company support a proposed park."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A margin of error describes uncertainty when estimating a population value from a random sample. The plausible range for the population is 74% ± 3%, or 71% to 77%, and the conclusion applies only to the population sampled (employees of the company). The sample percent itself is known exactly (74%), so a range for the sample is wrong, and "exactly 74%" ignores sampling variability.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h10$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 200 adults in a large city. The participants were randomly assigned to either use a daily stretching routine or not. After eight weeks, those who used a daily stretching routine had significantly better reported back pain than those who did not. Which of the following conclusions is best supported?$q$, $q$["Using a daily stretching routine causes improved reported back pain for participants like those in the study, but the result may not generalize to all adults in the city.","Among adults in the city, use of a daily stretching routine is associated with improved reported back pain, but a cause-and-effect relationship cannot be concluded.","Using a daily stretching routine causes improved reported back pain for adults in the city.","Among the participants in the study, use of a daily stretching routine is associated with improved reported back pain, but neither causation nor a generalization to all adults in the city is justified."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two features matter. Random sampling from a population lets results generalize to that population; this study used a random sample, so generalizing to all adults in the city is justified. Random assignment to treatments lets you conclude cause and effect; participants were randomly assigned, so a causal conclusion is justified.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h11$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A scatterplot relates x, the hours studied, and y, the test score, for a set of observations. A line of best fit for the data is y = 4x − 4. One observation has an x-value of 23 and a y-value of 84. What is the residual for this observation (actual value minus predicted value)?$q$, $q$["4","−4","84","88"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The predicted value is 4(23) − 4 = 88. The residual is actual − predicted = 84 − 88 = −4. A negative residual means the point lies below the line.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h12$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 3, null, $q$There are 190 trees in a park, each of which is oak, maple, or pine. The ratio of oak to maple is 3 : 7, and the ratio of maple to pine is 1 : 4. How many are pine?$q$, $q$["140","54","15","35"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Combine the ratios by matching the maple parts: oak : maple : pine = 3 : 7 : 28. These parts add to 38, so each part is 190/38 = 5. The number of pine is 28 × 5 = 140.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h13$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 4, null, $q$A box contains 7 defective batteries and 4 working batteries. Two batteries are selected at random without replacement. What is the probability that at least one of them is defective?$q$, $q$["6/55","21/55","7/11","49/55"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Use the complement. The probability that neither is defective is (4/11)(3/10) = 6/55. So the probability of at least one defective is 1 − 6/55 = 49/55.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h14$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The price of an item increased by 300%. By what percent must the new price decrease to return the item to its original price?$q$, $q$["300%","150%","75%","80%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, the new price is 4P. To return to P, the new price must be multiplied by 1/4 = 0.25, a decrease of 75%. The decrease is smaller than 300% because it is taken from a larger base.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h15$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$There are 17,000 fish in a lake. In a random sample of 200 of them, 96 are trout, and 75% of those 96 also are longer than 30 centimeters. Based on the sample, which of the following is the best estimate of the number of fish in the lake that are trout longer than 30 centimeters?$q$, $q$["8160","6120","12750","72"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In the sample, 96 of 200 are trout, and 75% of those meet the second condition, so 96 × 0.75 = 72 of the 200 sampled meet both conditions. That is a proportion of 0.36. Applying it to the population: 17,000 × 72/200 = 6,120.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h16$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A data set consists of 12 values between 20 and 40. A new value of 107 is added to the data set. Which of the following best describes how the mean and the median change?$q$, $q$["The mean increases substantially more than the median does.","The median increases substantially more than the mean does.","The mean and the median increase by the same amount.","Neither the mean nor the median changes."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The value 107 is far above all the other values. The mean uses every value's size, so it is pulled strongly upward. The median depends only on the middle position, which moves by at most half a position, and the middle values are all between 20 and 40. So the median changes very little.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h17$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The number x is 125% of the number y, and y is 40% less than the number z. If z is positive, x is what percent of z?$q$, $q$["85%","50%","175%","75%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$y = 0.6z and x = 1.25y, so x = 1.25(0.6z) = 0.75z. So x is 75% of z. Adding or subtracting the percents directly is a common mistake, because the two percents are of different bases.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h18$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$A store discounts the original price of a jacket by 10%. A sales tax of 6% is then applied to the discounted price, and the final amount paid is $634.41. What was the original price of the jacket?$q$, $q$["$660.84","$655.98","$665.00","$704.90"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, the discounted price is 0.9P, and after tax the amount is 1.06(0.9P) = 0.954P. Setting this equal to 634.41 gives P = 634.41/0.954 = 665.00. Undoing the percents by reversing them as additions (e.g., adding back 10%) gives wrong answers because each percent applies to a different base.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h19$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$The mean of a data set of 19 numbers is 52. When one number is removed from the data set, the mean of the remaining numbers is 53.5. What number was removed?$q$, $q$["3","25","963","44"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The original sum is 19(52) = 988. The remaining 18 numbers sum to 18(53.5) = 963. The removed number is 988 − 963 = 25.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h20$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$In a class, the 8 students in section A had a mean test score of 80, and the students in section B had a mean test score of 62. The mean score of all the students in both sections combined was 70. How many students are in section B?$q$, $q$["10","8","12","18"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let b be the number of students in section B. The total of all scores is 8(80) + 62b, and it also equals 70(8 + b). So 640 + 62b = 560 + 70b, which gives −8b = −80 and b = 10.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h21$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>6</td></tr></tbody></table>The frequency table summarizes a data set of 25 values. What is the median of the data set?$q$, $q$["11.48","8","12","11"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are 25 values, so the median is the 13th value when ordered. Counting up the cumulative frequencies (3, 9, 15, 19, 25), that position falls at the value 11. Note the median is not the middle row of the table (11) unless the frequencies happen to put it there.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h22$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Under 30</th><th>30 to 49</th><th>50 and older</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Prefers streaming</td><td>43</td><td>14</td><td>43</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>Prefers cable</td><td>26</td><td>15</td><td>11</td><td>52</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>69</td><td>29</td><td>54</td><td>152</td></tr></tbody></table>The table summarizes 152 respondents to a media survey. If one respondent in the "50 and older" category is selected at random, what is the probability that the respondent is in the "Prefers cable" category?$q$, $q$["11/52","11/152","11/54","27/76"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The selection is restricted to the 54 respondents in the "50 and older" column. Of these, 11 are in the "Prefers cable" row, so the probability is 11/54.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h23$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$An object travels at a constant speed of 35 meters per second. What is this speed in kilometers per hour?$q$, $q$["4728779608739021/2251799813685248","126","126000","175/18"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In one hour there are 3,600 seconds, so the object travels 35 × 3,600 = 126,000 meters per hour. Since 1 kilometer = 1,000 meters, that is 126,000/1,000 = 126 kilometers per hour.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h24$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 4, null, $q$Working together at constant rates, pipes A and B can fill the tank in 40/9 hours. Working alone, pipe A can fill the tank in 10 hours. How many hours would it take pipe B, working alone, to fill the tank?$q$, $q$["8","50/9","80/9","18"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The combined rate is 1/(40/9) = 9/40 of the job per hour. Subtracting A's rate: 9/40 − 1/10 = 1/8. So B alone takes 8 hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h25$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 1200 households in a certain county and found that 71% of those in the sample own a pet. The associated margin of error is 3 percentage points. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["Exactly 71% of all households in the county own a pet.","Between 68% and 74% of the people in the sample own a pet.","It is plausible that between 68% and 74% of all residents of the state own a pet.","It is plausible that between 68% and 74% of all households in the county own a pet."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A margin of error describes uncertainty when estimating a population value from a random sample. The plausible range for the population is 71% ± 3%, or 68% to 74%, and the conclusion applies only to the population sampled (households in the county). The sample percent itself is known exactly (71%), so a range for the sample is wrong, and "exactly 71%" ignores sampling variability.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h26$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 200 adults in a large city. The participants were randomly assigned to either use a reduced-sugar diet or not. After eight weeks, those who used a reduced-sugar diet had significantly better afternoon energy levels than those who did not. Which of the following conclusions is best supported?$q$, $q$["Using a reduced-sugar diet causes improved afternoon energy levels for participants like those in the study, but the result may not generalize to all adults in the city.","Among adults in the city, use of a reduced-sugar diet is associated with improved afternoon energy levels, but a cause-and-effect relationship cannot be concluded.","Using a reduced-sugar diet causes improved afternoon energy levels for adults in the city.","Among the participants in the study, use of a reduced-sugar diet is associated with improved afternoon energy levels, but neither causation nor a generalization to all adults in the city is justified."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two features matter. Random sampling from a population lets results generalize to that population; this study used a random sample, so generalizing to all adults in the city is justified. Random assignment to treatments lets you conclude cause and effect; participants were randomly assigned, so a causal conclusion is justified.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h27$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A scatterplot relates x, the hours studied, and y, the test score, for a set of observations. A line of best fit for the data is y = 1.5x + 30. One observation has an x-value of 7 and a y-value of 42.5. What is the residual for this observation (actual value minus predicted value)?$q$, $q$["−2","2","42.5","40.5"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The predicted value is 1.5(7) + 30 = 40.5. The residual is actual − predicted = 42.5 − 40.5 = 2. A positive residual means the point lies above the line.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h28$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 3, null, $q$The ratio of fiction to biography books on a shelf is 1 : 5, and the ratio of biography to poetry books is 9 : 7. What is the ratio of fiction to poetry books?$q$, $q$["9 : 35","1 : 7","7 : 45","10 : 12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Make the biography parts match. The least common multiple of 5 and 9 is 45. Scaling gives fiction : biography = 9 : 45 and biography : poetry = 45 : 35. So fiction : poetry = 9 : 35, which simplifies to 9 : 35.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h29$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 4, null, $q$A tray contains 4 vanilla cupcakes and 4 chocolate cupcakes. Two cupcakes are selected at random, one after the other, without replacement. What is the probability that both are vanilla?$q$, $q$["1/4","3/16","7/16","3/14"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first is vanilla with probability 4/8. Given that, 3 of the remaining 7 are vanilla, so the second is vanilla with probability 3/7. Multiplying: (4/8)(3/7) = 3/14.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h30$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$A company's monthly revenue increased by 10% in January and then increased by 30% in February. Which of the following describes the overall change over the two periods?$q$, $q$["An increase of 40%","A decrease of 43%","An increase of 43%","An increase of 49%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Multiply the growth factors: (1.1)(1.3) = 1.43. This is more than 1, so the overall change is an increase of 43%. Simply adding the percents (+10% and +30%) is incorrect because the second change applies to the new amount.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h31$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$There are 16,000 residents of a town. In a random sample of 250 of them, 87 cycle to work, and 25% of those 87 also do so every day. Based on the sample, which of the following is the best estimate of the number of residents of the town that cycle to work every day?$q$, $q$["5568","1392","4000","22"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In the sample, 87 of 250 cycle to work, and 25% of those meet the second condition, so 87 × 0.25 = 21.75 of the 250 sampled meet both conditions. That is a proportion of 0.087. Applying it to the population: 16,000 × 21.75/250 = 1,392.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h32$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A data set consists of 6 values between 20 and 40. A new value of 101 is added to the data set. Which of the following best describes how the mean and the median change?$q$, $q$["The mean increases substantially more than the median does.","The median increases substantially more than the mean does.","The mean and the median increase by the same amount.","Neither the mean nor the median changes."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The value 101 is far above all the other values. The mean uses every value's size, so it is pulled strongly upward. The median depends only on the middle position, which moves by at most half a position, and the middle values are all between 20 and 40. So the median changes very little.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h33$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The number x is 60% of the number y, and y is 20% less than the number z. If z is positive, x is what percent of z?$q$, $q$["40%","12%","72%","48%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$y = 0.8z and x = 0.6y, so x = 0.6(0.8z) = 0.48z. So x is 48% of z. Adding or subtracting the percents directly is a common mistake, because the two percents are of different bases.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h34$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$A store discounts the original price of a jacket by 25%. A sales tax of 8% is then applied to the discounted price, and the final amount paid is $384.75. What was the original price of the jacket?$q$, $q$["$463.55","$442.46","$475.00","$513.00"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, the discounted price is 0.75P, and after tax the amount is 1.08(0.75P) = 0.81P. Setting this equal to 384.75 gives P = 384.75/0.81 = 475.00. Undoing the percents by reversing them as additions (e.g., adding back 25%) gives wrong answers because each percent applies to a different base.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h35$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$The mean of a data set of 9 numbers is 36. When one number is removed from the data set, the mean of the remaining numbers is 34. What number was removed?$q$, $q$["2","52","272","61"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The original sum is 9(36) = 324. The remaining 8 numbers sum to 8(34) = 272. The removed number is 324 − 272 = 52.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h36$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$In a class, the 15 students in section A had a mean test score of 76, and the students in section B had a mean test score of 64. The mean score of all the students in both sections combined was 70. How many students are in section B?$q$, $q$["15","17","30","12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let b be the number of students in section B. The total of all scores is 15(76) + 64b, and it also equals 70(15 + b). So 1140 + 64b = 1050 + 70b, which gives −6b = −90 and b = 15.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h37$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>9</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>8</td></tr></tbody></table>The frequency table summarizes a data set of 28 values. What is the mean of the data set minus the median of the data set?$q$, $q$["1","−2","0","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sum of the values is 9(4) + 11(3) + 13(4) + 15(9) + 17(8) = 392, so the mean is 392/28 = 14. The median is the average of the 14th and 15th value, which is 15. The difference is 14 − 15 = −1.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h38$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Under 30</th><th>30 to 49</th><th>50 and older</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Prefers streaming</td><td>59</td><td>5</td><td>45</td><td>109</td></tr><tr><td>Prefers cable</td><td>54</td><td>45</td><td>8</td><td>107</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>113</td><td>50</td><td>53</td><td>216</td></tr></tbody></table>The table summarizes 216 respondents to a media survey. If one respondent in the "30 to 49" category is selected at random, what is the probability that the respondent is in the "Prefers cable" category?$q$, $q$["45/107","5/24","9/10","25/108"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The selection is restricted to the 50 respondents in the "30 to 49" column. Of these, 45 are in the "Prefers cable" row, so the probability is 45/50 = 9/10.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h39$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$A material has a density of 1.2 grams per cubic centimeter. What is its density in kilograms per cubic meter? (1 kilogram = 1,000 grams and 1 meter = 100 centimeters)$q$, $q$["120","1,200","12","1,200,000"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$1 cubic meter = (100 cm)³ = 1,000,000 cubic centimeters. So 1.2 g/cm³ = 1.2 × 1,000,000 g/m³ = 1,200,000 g/m³. Dividing by 1,000 to convert grams to kilograms gives 1,200 kg/m³.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h40$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$Working alone at a constant rate, pipe A can fill the tank in 6 minutes. Working alone at a constant rate, pipe B can fill the tank in 9 minutes. How many minutes would it take pipes A and B, working together at these rates, to fill the tank?$q$, $q$["18/5","15/2","15","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rates add, not times. Pipe A does 1/6 of the job per minute and pipe B does 1/9. Together they do 1/6 + 1/9 = 5/18 of the job per minute, so the time is 18/5 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h41$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 1200 registered voters in a certain city and found that 55% of those in the sample use public transit at least once a week. The associated margin of error is 4 percentage points. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["Exactly 55% of all registered voters in the city use public transit at least once a week.","Between 51% and 59% of the people in the sample use public transit at least once a week.","It is plausible that between 51% and 59% of all residents of the state use public transit at least once a week.","It is plausible that between 51% and 59% of all registered voters in the city use public transit at least once a week."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A margin of error describes uncertainty when estimating a population value from a random sample. The plausible range for the population is 55% ± 4%, or 51% to 59%, and the conclusion applies only to the population sampled (registered voters in the city). The sample percent itself is known exactly (55%), so a range for the sample is wrong, and "exactly 55%" ignores sampling variability.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h42$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 200 high school students in a certain state. Participants chose for themselves whether to use a daily stretching routine. After eight weeks, those who used a daily stretching routine had significantly better reported back pain than those who did not. Which of the following conclusions is best supported?$q$, $q$["Using a daily stretching routine causes improved reported back pain for high school students in the state.","Using a daily stretching routine causes improved reported back pain for participants like those in the study, but the result may not generalize to all high school students in the state.","Among high school students in the state, use of a daily stretching routine is associated with improved reported back pain, but a cause-and-effect relationship cannot be concluded.","Among the participants in the study, use of a daily stretching routine is associated with improved reported back pain, but neither causation nor a generalization to all high school students in the state is justified."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two features matter. Random sampling from a population lets results generalize to that population; this study used a random sample, so generalizing to all high school students in the state is justified. Random assignment to treatments lets you conclude cause and effect; participants chose their own treatment, so only an association can be concluded.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h43$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A scatterplot relates x, the hours studied, and y, the test score, for a set of observations. A line of best fit for the data is y = 0.8x + 17. One observation has an x-value of 5 and a y-value of 23. What is the residual for this observation (actual value minus predicted value)?$q$, $q$["−2","2","23","21"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The predicted value is 0.8(5) + 17 = 21. The residual is actual − predicted = 23 − 21 = 2. A positive residual means the point lies above the line.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h44$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 3, null, $q$The ratio of red to blue marbles in a bag is 6 : 4, and the ratio of blue to green marbles is 5 : 4. What is the ratio of red to green marbles?$q$, $q$["15 : 8","6 : 4","24 : 20","11 : 8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Make the blue parts match. The least common multiple of 4 and 5 is 20. Scaling gives red : blue = 30 : 20 and blue : green = 20 : 16. So red : green = 30 : 16, which simplifies to 15 : 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h45$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 4, null, $q$A tray contains 4 vanilla cupcakes and 7 chocolate cupcakes. Two cupcakes are selected at random without replacement. What is the probability that at least one of them is vanilla?$q$, $q$["21/55","6/55","8/11","34/55"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Use the complement. The probability that neither is vanilla is (7/11)(6/10) = 21/55. So the probability of at least one vanilla is 1 − 21/55 = 34/55.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h46$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$A company's monthly revenue increased by 20% in the first quarter and then increased by 10% in the second quarter. Which of the following describes the overall change over the two periods?$q$, $q$["An increase of 30%","A decrease of 32%","An increase of 32%","An increase of 38%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Multiply the growth factors: (1.2)(1.1) = 1.32. This is more than 1, so the overall change is an increase of 32%. Simply adding the percents (+20% and +10%) is incorrect because the second change applies to the new amount.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h47$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$There are 30,000 fish in a lake. In a random sample of 400 of them, 64 are trout, and 25% of those 64 also are longer than 30 centimeters. Based on the sample, which of the following is the best estimate of the number of fish in the lake that are trout longer than 30 centimeters?$q$, $q$["4800","1200","7500","16"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In the sample, 64 of 400 are trout, and 25% of those meet the second condition, so 64 × 0.25 = 16 of the 400 sampled meet both conditions. That is a proportion of 0.04. Applying it to the population: 30,000 × 16/400 = 1,200.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h48$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A data set consists of 12 values between 20 and 40. A new value of 95 is added to the data set. Which of the following best describes how the mean and the median change?$q$, $q$["The mean increases substantially more than the median does.","The median increases substantially more than the mean does.","The mean and the median increase by the same amount.","Neither the mean nor the median changes."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The value 95 is far above all the other values. The mean uses every value's size, so it is pulled strongly upward. The median depends only on the middle position, which moves by at most half a position, and the middle values are all between 20 and 40. So the median changes very little.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h49$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The number x is 40% of the number y, and y is 60% greater than the number z. If z is positive, x is what percent of z?$q$, $q$["100%","24%","16%","64%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$y = 1.6z and x = 0.4y, so x = 0.4(1.6z) = 0.64z. So x is 64% of z. Adding or subtracting the percents directly is a common mistake, because the two percents are of different bases.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h50$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$A store discounts the original price of a jacket by 20%. A sales tax of 9% is then applied to the discounted price, and the final amount paid is $492.68. What was the original price of the jacket?$q$, $q$["$553.57","$538.01","$565.00","$615.85"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, the discounted price is 0.8P, and after tax the amount is 1.09(0.8P) = 0.872P. Setting this equal to 492.68 gives P = 492.68/0.872 = 565.00. Undoing the percents by reversing them as additions (e.g., adding back 20%) gives wrong answers because each percent applies to a different base.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h51$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$The mean of a data set of 12 numbers is 86. When one number is removed from the data set, the mean of the remaining numbers is 85. What number was removed?$q$, $q$["1","97","935","109"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The original sum is 12(86) = 1032. The remaining 11 numbers sum to 11(85) = 935. The removed number is 1032 − 935 = 97.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h52$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$In a class, the 31 students in section A had a mean test score of 67, and the students in section B had a mean test score of 95. The mean score of all the students in both sections combined was 81. How many students are in section B?$q$, $q$["31","33","62","28"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let b be the number of students in section B. The total of all scores is 31(67) + 95b, and it also equals 81(31 + b). So 2077 + 95b = 2511 + 81b, which gives 14b = 434 and b = 31.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h53$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>7</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>5</td></tr></tbody></table>The frequency table summarizes a data set of 28 values. What is the median of the data set?$q$, $q$["12.89","16","10","13"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are 28 values, so the median is the mean of the 14th and 15th values when ordered. Counting up the cumulative frequencies (6, 12, 16, 23, 28), that position falls at the value 13. Note the median is not the middle row of the table (13) unless the frequencies happen to put it there.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h54$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Under 30</th><th>30 to 49</th><th>50 and older</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Prefers streaming</td><td>12</td><td>29</td><td>38</td><td>79</td></tr><tr><td>Prefers cable</td><td>43</td><td>56</td><td>7</td><td>106</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>55</td><td>85</td><td>45</td><td>185</td></tr></tbody></table>The table summarizes 185 respondents to a media survey. If a respondent that is NOT in the "50 and older" category is selected at random, what is the probability that it is in the "Prefers cable" category?$q$, $q$["99/185","99/106","99/140","7/45"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Respondents not in "50 and older" number 185 − 45 = 140. Of these, the ones in "Prefers cable" number 106 − 7 = 99. The probability is 99/140.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h55$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$A material has a density of 11.3 grams per cubic centimeter. What is its density in kilograms per cubic meter? (1 kilogram = 1,000 grams and 1 meter = 100 centimeters)$q$, $q$["1,130","11,300","113","11,300,000"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$1 cubic meter = (100 cm)³ = 1,000,000 cubic centimeters. So 11.3 g/cm³ = 11.3 × 1,000,000 g/m³ = 11,300,000 g/m³. Dividing by 1,000 to convert grams to kilograms gives 11,300 kg/m³.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h56$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$Working alone at a constant rate, pipe A can fill the tank in 8 hours. Working alone at a constant rate, pipe B can fill the tank in 7 hours. How many hours would it take pipes A and B, working together at these rates, to fill the tank?$q$, $q$["56/15","15/2","15","1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rates add, not times. Pipe A does 1/8 of the job per hour and pipe B does 1/7. Together they do 1/8 + 1/7 = 15/56 of the job per hour, so the time is 56/15 hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h57$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 1200 registered voters in a certain city and found that 52% of those in the sample use public transit at least once a week. The associated margin of error is 3 percentage points. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["Exactly 52% of all registered voters in the city use public transit at least once a week.","Between 49% and 55% of the people in the sample use public transit at least once a week.","It is plausible that between 49% and 55% of all residents of the state use public transit at least once a week.","It is plausible that between 49% and 55% of all registered voters in the city use public transit at least once a week."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A margin of error describes uncertainty when estimating a population value from a random sample. The plausible range for the population is 52% ± 3%, or 49% to 55%, and the conclusion applies only to the population sampled (registered voters in the city). The sample percent itself is known exactly (52%), so a range for the sample is wrong, and "exactly 52%" ignores sampling variability.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h58$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher recruited 200 volunteers from among adults in a large city. Participants chose for themselves whether to use a daily stretching routine. After eight weeks, those who used a daily stretching routine had significantly better reported back pain than those who did not. Which of the following conclusions is best supported?$q$, $q$["Using a daily stretching routine causes improved reported back pain for adults in the city.","Using a daily stretching routine causes improved reported back pain for participants like those in the study, but the result may not generalize to all adults in the city.","Among the participants in the study, use of a daily stretching routine is associated with improved reported back pain, but neither causation nor a generalization to all adults in the city is justified.","Among adults in the city, use of a daily stretching routine is associated with improved reported back pain, but a cause-and-effect relationship cannot be concluded."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two features matter. Random sampling from a population lets results generalize to that population; this study used volunteers, so the results may not generalize. Random assignment to treatments lets you conclude cause and effect; participants chose their own treatment, so only an association can be concluded.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h59$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A scatterplot relates x, the hours studied, and y, the test score, for a set of observations. A line of best fit for the data is y = 2.4x + 3. One observation has an x-value of 14 and a y-value of 31.1. What is the residual for this observation (actual value minus predicted value)?$q$, $q$["5.5","−11/2","31.1","36.6"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The predicted value is 2.4(14) + 3 = 36.6. The residual is actual − predicted = 31.1 − 36.6 = −5.5. A negative residual means the point lies below the line.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h60$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 3, null, $q$The ratio of red to blue marbles in a bag is 1 : 5, and the ratio of blue to green marbles is 7 : 4. What is the ratio of red to green marbles?$q$, $q$["7 : 20","1 : 4","4 : 35","8 : 9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Make the blue parts match. The least common multiple of 5 and 7 is 35. Scaling gives red : blue = 7 : 35 and blue : green = 35 : 20. So red : green = 7 : 20, which simplifies to 7 : 20.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h61$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 4, null, $q$A bag contains 4 red marbles and 6 blue marbles. Two marbles are selected at random without replacement. What is the probability that one is red and the other is blue?$q$, $q$["4/15","12/25","6/25","8/15"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are two orders. Red then blue: (4/10)(6/9). Blue then red: (6/10)(4/9). The total is 2(4)(6)/(10·9) = 8/15.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h62$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The price of a stock decreased by 40% in the first year and then decreased by 10% in the second year. Which of the following describes the overall change over the two periods?$q$, $q$["A decrease of 50%","An increase of 46%","A decrease of 46%","A decrease of 52%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Multiply the growth factors: (0.6)(0.9) = 0.54. This is less than 1, so the overall change is a decrease of 46%. Simply adding the percents (−40% and −10%) is incorrect because the second change applies to the new amount.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h63$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$There are 3,000 fish in a lake. In a random sample of 250 of them, 112 are trout, and 75% of those 112 also are longer than 30 centimeters. Based on the sample, which of the following is the best estimate of the number of fish in the lake that are trout longer than 30 centimeters?$q$, $q$["1344","1008","2250","84"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In the sample, 112 of 250 are trout, and 75% of those meet the second condition, so 112 × 0.75 = 84 of the 250 sampled meet both conditions. That is a proportion of 0.336. Applying it to the population: 3,000 × 84/250 = 1,008.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h64$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$Each of the 9 values in a data set with a positive standard deviation is multiplied by 3. Which of the following statements about the new data set compared with the original must be true?$q$, $q$["The mean and the standard deviation are both multiplied by 3.","The mean is multiplied by 3, and the standard deviation is unchanged.","The mean is multiplied by 3, and the standard deviation is multiplied by 9.","The mean is unchanged, and the standard deviation is multiplied by 3."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying every value by 3 stretches the data set: every value, and every distance between values, is multiplied by 3. So the mean, median, range, and standard deviation are all multiplied by 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h65$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The number p is 125% of the number r, and r is 40% greater than the number s. If s is positive, p is what percent of s?$q$, $q$["165%","50%","75%","175%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$r = 1.4s and p = 1.25r, so p = 1.25(1.4s) = 1.75s. So p is 175% of s. Adding or subtracting the percents directly is a common mistake, because the two percents are of different bases.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h66$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$A store discounts the original price of a jacket by 20%. A sales tax of 6% is then applied to the discounted price, and the final amount paid is $555.44. What was the original price of the jacket?$q$, $q$["$645.86","$626.54","$655.00","$694.30"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, the discounted price is 0.8P, and after tax the amount is 1.06(0.8P) = 0.848P. Setting this equal to 555.44 gives P = 555.44/0.848 = 655.00. Undoing the percents by reversing them as additions (e.g., adding back 20%) gives wrong answers because each percent applies to a different base.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h67$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$The mean of a data set of 7 numbers is 69. When one number is removed from the data set, the mean of the remaining numbers is 69. What number was removed?$q$, $q$["0","69","414","76"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The original sum is 7(69) = 483. The remaining 6 numbers sum to 6(69) = 414. The removed number is 483 − 414 = 69.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h68$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$In a class, the 40 students in section A had a mean test score of 84, and the students in section B had a mean test score of 68. The mean score of all the students in both sections combined was 78. How many students are in section B?$q$, $q$["24","40","26","64"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let b be the number of students in section B. The total of all scores is 40(84) + 68b, and it also equals 78(40 + b). So 3360 + 68b = 3120 + 78b, which gives −10b = −240 and b = 24.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h69$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>7</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>The frequency table summarizes a data set of 27 values. What is the median of the data set?$q$, $q$["12.89","10","14","13"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are 27 values, so the median is the 14th value when ordered. Counting up the cumulative frequencies (2, 10, 19, 24, 27), that position falls at the value 13. Note the median is not the middle row of the table (13) unless the frequencies happen to put it there.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h70$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Under 30</th><th>30 to 49</th><th>50 and older</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Prefers streaming</td><td>10</td><td>25</td><td>38</td><td>73</td></tr><tr><td>Prefers cable</td><td>52</td><td>28</td><td>28</td><td>108</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>62</td><td>53</td><td>66</td><td>181</td></tr></tbody></table>The table summarizes 181 respondents to a media survey. If a respondent that is NOT in the "Under 30" category is selected at random, what is the probability that it is in the "Prefers streaming" category?$q$, $q$["63/181","63/73","9/17","5/31"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Respondents not in "Under 30" number 181 − 62 = 119. Of these, the ones in "Prefers streaming" number 73 − 10 = 63. The probability is 63/119 = 9/17.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h71$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$Water flows into an empty tank at a constant rate of 11 gallons per minute. The tank holds 4,000 gallons. To the nearest tenth of an hour, how long does it take to fill the tank?$q$, $q$["363.6 hours","6.1 hours","3.6 hours","21.8 hours"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In one hour, 11 × 60 = 660 gallons flow in. The time to fill 4,000 gallons is 4000/660 ≈ 6.1 hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h72$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 4, null, $q$Working together at constant rates, printers A and B can print the order in 42/13 minutes. Working alone, printer A can print the order in 6 minutes. How many minutes would it take printer B, working alone, to print the order?$q$, $q$["7","36/13","84/13","13"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The combined rate is 1/(42/13) = 13/42 of the job per minute. Subtracting A's rate: 13/42 − 1/6 = 1/7. So B alone takes 7 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h73$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 1200 students at a large university and found that 71% of those in the sample use public transit at least once a week. The associated margin of error is 5 percentage points. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["Exactly 71% of all students at the university use public transit at least once a week.","Between 66% and 76% of the people in the sample use public transit at least once a week.","It is plausible that between 66% and 76% of all residents of the state use public transit at least once a week.","It is plausible that between 66% and 76% of all students at the university use public transit at least once a week."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A margin of error describes uncertainty when estimating a population value from a random sample. The plausible range for the population is 71% ± 5%, or 66% to 76%, and the conclusion applies only to the population sampled (students at the university). The sample percent itself is known exactly (71%), so a range for the sample is wrong, and "exactly 71%" ignores sampling variability.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h74$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher recruited 200 volunteers from among adults in a large city. The participants were randomly assigned to either use a new study app or not. After eight weeks, those who used a new study app had significantly better test scores than those who did not. Which of the following conclusions is best supported?$q$, $q$["Using a new study app causes improved test scores for adults in the city.","Among adults in the city, use of a new study app is associated with improved test scores, but a cause-and-effect relationship cannot be concluded.","Using a new study app causes improved test scores for participants like those in the study, but the result may not generalize to all adults in the city.","Among the participants in the study, use of a new study app is associated with improved test scores, but neither causation nor a generalization to all adults in the city is justified."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two features matter. Random sampling from a population lets results generalize to that population; this study used volunteers, so the results may not generalize. Random assignment to treatments lets you conclude cause and effect; participants were randomly assigned, so a causal conclusion is justified.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h75$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A scatterplot relates x, the hours studied, and y, the test score, for a set of observations. A line of best fit for the data is y = −1.5x − 13. One observation has an x-value of 7 and a y-value of −29. What is the residual for this observation (actual value minus predicted value)?$q$, $q$["5.5","−11/2","−29","−23.5"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The predicted value is −1.5(7) − 13 = −23.5. The residual is actual − predicted = −29 + 23.5 = −5.5. A negative residual means the point lies below the line.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h76$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 3, null, $q$The ratio of fiction to biography books on a shelf is 9 : 2, and the ratio of biography to poetry books is 4 : 3. What is the ratio of fiction to poetry books?$q$, $q$["6 : 1","9 : 3","27 : 8","13 : 5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Make the biography parts match. The least common multiple of 2 and 4 is 4. Scaling gives fiction : biography = 18 : 4 and biography : poetry = 4 : 3. So fiction : poetry = 18 : 3, which simplifies to 6 : 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h77$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 4, null, $q$A tray contains 3 vanilla cupcakes and 4 chocolate cupcakes. Two cupcakes are selected at random without replacement. What is the probability that at least one of them is vanilla?$q$, $q$["2/7","1/7","6/7","5/7"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Use the complement. The probability that neither is vanilla is (4/7)(3/6) = 2/7. So the probability of at least one vanilla is 1 − 2/7 = 5/7.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h78$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$A company's monthly revenue increased by 30% in the first quarter and then increased by 25% in the second quarter. Which of the following describes the overall change over the two periods?$q$, $q$["An increase of 55%","A decrease of 62.5%","An increase of 62.5%","An increase of 68.5%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Multiply the growth factors: (1.3)(1.25) = 1.625. This is more than 1, so the overall change is an increase of 62.5%. Simply adding the percents (+30% and +25%) is incorrect because the second change applies to the new amount.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h79$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$There are 18,000 trees in a forest preserve. In a random sample of 150 of them, 33 are oaks, and 25% of those 33 also show signs of leaf blight. Based on the sample, which of the following is the best estimate of the number of trees in the preserve that are oaks showing signs of leaf blight?$q$, $q$["3960","990","4500","8"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In the sample, 33 of 150 are oaks, and 25% of those meet the second condition, so 33 × 0.25 = 8.25 of the 150 sampled meet both conditions. That is a proportion of 0.055. Applying it to the population: 18,000 × 8.25/150 = 990.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h80$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$Each of the 6 values in a data set is increased by 8. Which of the following statements about the new data set compared with the original data set must be true?$q$, $q$["The mean increases by 8, and the standard deviation is unchanged.","The mean and the standard deviation both increase by 8.","The mean is unchanged, and the standard deviation increases by 8.","The mean increases by 8, and the standard deviation increases by 48."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the same number to every value shifts the whole data set, so the mean (and median) increase by 8. The spread does not change: every distance between values, and every distance from the mean, stays the same, so the standard deviation and range are unchanged.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h81$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The number a is 45% of the number b, and b is 80% greater than the number c. If c is positive, a is what percent of c?$q$, $q$["125%","36%","9%","81%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$b = 1.8c and a = 0.45b, so a = 0.45(1.8c) = 0.81c. So a is 81% of c. Adding or subtracting the percents directly is a common mistake, because the two percents are of different bases.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h82$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$A store discounts the original price of a jacket by 20%. A sales tax of 6% is then applied to the discounted price, and the final amount paid is $203.52. What was the original price of the jacket?$q$, $q$["$236.65","$229.57","$240.00","$254.40"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, the discounted price is 0.8P, and after tax the amount is 1.06(0.8P) = 0.848P. Setting this equal to 203.52 gives P = 203.52/0.848 = 240.00. Undoing the percents by reversing them as additions (e.g., adding back 20%) gives wrong answers because each percent applies to a different base.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h83$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$The mean of a data set of 6 numbers is 29. When one number is removed from the data set, the mean of the remaining numbers is 22. What number was removed?$q$, $q$["7","64","110","70"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The original sum is 6(29) = 174. The remaining 5 numbers sum to 5(22) = 110. The removed number is 174 − 110 = 64.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h84$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$In a class, the 32 students in section A had a mean test score of 73, and the students in section B had a mean test score of 95. The mean score of all the students in both sections combined was 79. How many students are in section B?$q$, $q$["12","32","13","44"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let b be the number of students in section B. The total of all scores is 32(73) + 95b, and it also equals 79(32 + b). So 2336 + 95b = 2528 + 79b, which gives 16b = 192 and b = 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h85$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>6</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>The frequency table summarizes a data set of 20 values. What is the median of the data set?$q$, $q$["11.4","9","13","12"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are 20 values, so the median is the mean of the 10th and 11th values when ordered. Counting up the cumulative frequencies (5, 7, 15, 17, 20), that position falls at the value 12. Note the median is not the middle row of the table (12) unless the frequencies happen to put it there.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h86$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Morning</th><th>Afternoon</th><th>Evening</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rated positive</td><td>58</td><td>38</td><td>7</td><td>103</td></tr><tr><td>Rated negative</td><td>10</td><td>17</td><td>10</td><td>37</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>68</td><td>55</td><td>17</td><td>140</td></tr></tbody></table>The table summarizes 140 customer visits recorded at a clinic. If a visit that is NOT in the "Morning" category is selected at random, what is the probability that it is in the "Rated negative" category?$q$, $q$["27/140","27/37","3/8","5/34"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Visits not in "Morning" number 140 − 68 = 72. Of these, the ones in "Rated negative" number 37 − 10 = 27. The probability is 27/72 = 3/8.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h87$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$An object travels at a constant speed of 25 meters per second. What is this speed in kilometers per hour?$q$, $q$["3/2","90","90000","125/18"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In one hour there are 3,600 seconds, so the object travels 25 × 3,600 = 90,000 meters per hour. Since 1 kilometer = 1,000 meters, that is 90,000/1,000 = 90 kilometers per hour.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h88$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 4, null, $q$Working together at constant rates, pipes A and B can fill the tank in 28/11 hours. Working alone, pipe A can fill the tank in 4 hours. How many hours would it take pipe B, working alone, to fill the tank?$q$, $q$["7","16/11","56/11","11"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The combined rate is 1/(28/11) = 11/28 of the job per hour. Subtracting A's rate: 11/28 − 1/4 = 1/7. So B alone takes 7 hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h89$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 1200 students at a large university and found that 65% of those in the sample would attend a weekend event. The associated margin of error is 3 percentage points. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["Exactly 65% of all students at the university would attend a weekend event.","Between 62% and 68% of the people in the sample would attend a weekend event.","It is plausible that between 62% and 68% of all residents of the state would attend a weekend event.","It is plausible that between 62% and 68% of all students at the university would attend a weekend event."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A margin of error describes uncertainty when estimating a population value from a random sample. The plausible range for the population is 65% ± 3%, or 62% to 68%, and the conclusion applies only to the population sampled (students at the university). The sample percent itself is known exactly (65%), so a range for the sample is wrong, and "exactly 65%" ignores sampling variability.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h90$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 200 members of a national hiking club. The participants were randomly assigned to either use a new study app or not. After eight weeks, those who used a new study app had significantly better test scores than those who did not. Which of the following conclusions is best supported?$q$, $q$["Using a new study app causes improved test scores for participants like those in the study, but the result may not generalize to all members of the club.","Among members of the club, use of a new study app is associated with improved test scores, but a cause-and-effect relationship cannot be concluded.","Using a new study app causes improved test scores for members of the club.","Among the participants in the study, use of a new study app is associated with improved test scores, but neither causation nor a generalization to all members of the club is justified."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two features matter. Random sampling from a population lets results generalize to that population; this study used a random sample, so generalizing to all members of the club is justified. Random assignment to treatments lets you conclude cause and effect; participants were randomly assigned, so a causal conclusion is justified.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h91$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A scatterplot relates x, the age of a tree (years), and y, the height (feet), for a set of observations. A line of best fit for the data is y = −0.6x + 25. One observation has an x-value of 9 and a y-value of 21.1. What is the residual for this observation (actual value minus predicted value)?$q$, $q$["−1.5","3/2","21.1","19.6"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The predicted value is −0.6(9) + 25 = 19.6. The residual is actual − predicted = 21.1 − 19.6 = 1.5. A positive residual means the point lies above the line.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h92$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 3, null, $q$The ratio of fiction to biography books on a shelf is 2 : 6, and the ratio of biography to poetry books is 4 : 8. What is the ratio of fiction to poetry books?$q$, $q$["1 : 6","2 : 8","16 : 24","6 : 14"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Make the biography parts match. The least common multiple of 6 and 4 is 12. Scaling gives fiction : biography = 4 : 12 and biography : poetry = 12 : 24. So fiction : poetry = 4 : 24, which simplifies to 1 : 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h93$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 4, null, $q$A box contains 4 defective batteries and 3 working batteries. Two batteries are selected at random without replacement. What is the probability that at least one of them is defective?$q$, $q$["1/7","2/7","4/7","6/7"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Use the complement. The probability that neither is defective is (3/7)(2/6) = 1/7. So the probability of at least one defective is 1 − 1/7 = 6/7.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h94$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The price of an item increased by 20%. By what percent must the new price decrease to return the item to its original price?$q$, $q$["20%","10%","16.67%","80%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, the new price is 1.2P. To return to P, the new price must be multiplied by 1/1.2 = 0.83, a decrease of 16.67%. The decrease is smaller than 20% because it is taken from a larger base.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h95$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$There are 18,500 trees in a forest preserve. In a random sample of 250 of them, 87 are oaks, and 50% of those 87 also show signs of leaf blight. Based on the sample, which of the following is the best estimate of the number of trees in the preserve that are oaks showing signs of leaf blight?$q$, $q$["6438","3219","9250","44"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In the sample, 87 of 250 are oaks, and 50% of those meet the second condition, so 87 × 0.5 = 43.5 of the 250 sampled meet both conditions. That is a proportion of 0.174. Applying it to the population: 18,500 × 43.5/250 = 3,219.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h96$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A data set consists of 7 values between 20 and 40. A new value of 135 is added to the data set. Which of the following best describes how the mean and the median change?$q$, $q$["The mean increases substantially more than the median does.","The median increases substantially more than the mean does.","The mean and the median increase by the same amount.","Neither the mean nor the median changes."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The value 135 is far above all the other values. The mean uses every value's size, so it is pulled strongly upward. The median depends only on the middle position, which moves by at most half a position, and the middle values are all between 20 and 40. So the median changes very little.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h97$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The number p is 80% of the number r, and r is 60% greater than the number s. If s is positive, p is what percent of s?$q$, $q$["140%","48%","32%","128%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$r = 1.6s and p = 0.8r, so p = 0.8(1.6s) = 1.28s. So p is 128% of s. Adding or subtracting the percents directly is a common mistake, because the two percents are of different bases.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h98$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$A store discounts the original price of a jacket by 15%. A sales tax of 8% is then applied to the discounted price, and the final amount paid is $573.75. What was the original price of the jacket?$q$, $q$["$616.94","$607.03","$625.00","$675.00"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, the discounted price is 0.85P, and after tax the amount is 1.08(0.85P) = 0.918P. Setting this equal to 573.75 gives P = 573.75/0.918 = 625.00. Undoing the percents by reversing them as additions (e.g., adding back 15%) gives wrong answers because each percent applies to a different base.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h99$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$The mean of a data set of 19 numbers is 72. When one number is removed from the data set, the mean of the remaining numbers is 73.5. What number was removed?$q$, $q$["3","45","1323","64"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The original sum is 19(72) = 1368. The remaining 18 numbers sum to 18(73.5) = 1323. The removed number is 1368 − 1323 = 45.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h100$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$In a class, the 24 students in section A had a mean test score of 60, and the students in section B had a mean test score of 74. The mean score of all the students in both sections combined was 68. How many students are in section B?$q$, $q$["32","24","38","56"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let b be the number of students in section B. The total of all scores is 24(60) + 74b, and it also equals 68(24 + b). So 1440 + 74b = 1632 + 68b, which gives 6b = 192 and b = 32.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h101$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>9</td></tr></tbody></table>The frequency table summarizes a data set of 37 values. What is the median of the data set?$q$, $q$["14.22","10","12","14"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are 37 values, so the median is the 19th value when ordered. Counting up the cumulative frequencies (9, 12, 21, 28, 37), that position falls at the value 14. Note the median is not the middle row of the table (14) unless the frequencies happen to put it there.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h102$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Site A</th><th>Site B</th><th>Site C</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Flowering</td><td>27</td><td>38</td><td>27</td><td>92</td></tr><tr><td>Not flowering</td><td>41</td><td>38</td><td>5</td><td>84</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>68</td><td>76</td><td>32</td><td>176</td></tr></tbody></table>The table summarizes 176 plants in a botanical study. A plant will be selected at random from those in the "Flowering" category. What is the probability that the plant is in the "Site B" category?$q$, $q$["1/2","19/88","19/46","23/44"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the 92 plants in the "Flowering" row are eligible. Of these, 38 are in the "Site B" column, so the probability is 38/92 = 19/46.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h103$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$An object travels at a constant speed of 20 meters per second. What is this speed in kilometers per hour?$q$, $q$["5404319552844595/4503599627370496","72","72000","50/9"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In one hour there are 3,600 seconds, so the object travels 20 × 3,600 = 72,000 meters per hour. Since 1 kilometer = 1,000 meters, that is 72,000/1,000 = 72 kilometers per hour.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h104$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$Working alone at a constant rate, printer A can print the order in 12 minutes. Working alone at a constant rate, printer B can print the order in 10 minutes. How many minutes would it take printers A and B, working together at these rates, to print the order?$q$, $q$["60/11","11","22","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rates add, not times. Printer A does 1/12 of the job per minute and printer B does 1/10. Together they do 1/12 + 1/10 = 11/60 of the job per minute, so the time is 60/11 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h105$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 600 registered voters in a certain city and found that 47% of those in the sample support a proposed park. The associated margin of error is 3 percentage points. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["Exactly 47% of all registered voters in the city support a proposed park.","Between 44% and 50% of the people in the sample support a proposed park.","It is plausible that between 44% and 50% of all residents of the state support a proposed park.","It is plausible that between 44% and 50% of all registered voters in the city support a proposed park."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A margin of error describes uncertainty when estimating a population value from a random sample. The plausible range for the population is 47% ± 3%, or 44% to 50%, and the conclusion applies only to the population sampled (registered voters in the city). The sample percent itself is known exactly (47%), so a range for the sample is wrong, and "exactly 47%" ignores sampling variability.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h106$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher recruited 200 volunteers from among high school students in a certain state. The participants were randomly assigned to either use a daily stretching routine or not. After eight weeks, those who used a daily stretching routine had significantly better reported back pain than those who did not. Which of the following conclusions is best supported?$q$, $q$["Using a daily stretching routine causes improved reported back pain for high school students in the state.","Among high school students in the state, use of a daily stretching routine is associated with improved reported back pain, but a cause-and-effect relationship cannot be concluded.","Using a daily stretching routine causes improved reported back pain for participants like those in the study, but the result may not generalize to all high school students in the state.","Among the participants in the study, use of a daily stretching routine is associated with improved reported back pain, but neither causation nor a generalization to all high school students in the state is justified."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two features matter. Random sampling from a population lets results generalize to that population; this study used volunteers, so the results may not generalize. Random assignment to treatments lets you conclude cause and effect; participants were randomly assigned, so a causal conclusion is justified.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h107$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A scatterplot relates x, the age of a tree (years), and y, the height (feet), for a set of observations. A line of best fit for the data is y = −0.6x + 34. One observation has an x-value of 6 and a y-value of 27.9. What is the residual for this observation (actual value minus predicted value)?$q$, $q$["2.5","−5/2","27.9","30.4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The predicted value is −0.6(6) + 34 = 30.4. The residual is actual − predicted = 27.9 − 30.4 = −2.5. A negative residual means the point lies below the line.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h108$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 3, null, $q$The ratio of oak to maple trees in a park is 8 : 7, and the ratio of maple to pine trees is 3 : 9. What is the ratio of oak to pine trees?$q$, $q$["8 : 21","8 : 9","72 : 21","11 : 16"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Make the maple parts match. The least common multiple of 7 and 3 is 21. Scaling gives oak : maple = 24 : 21 and maple : pine = 21 : 63. So oak : pine = 24 : 63, which simplifies to 8 : 21.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h109$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 4, null, $q$A box contains 8 defective batteries and 8 working batteries. Two batteries are selected at random without replacement. What is the probability that one is defective and the other is working?$q$, $q$["4/15","1/2","1/4","8/15"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are two orders. Defective then working: (8/16)(8/15). Working then defective: (8/16)(8/15). The total is 2(8)(8)/(16·15) = 8/15.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h110$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The number of members of a club increased by 30% in January and then decreased by 20% in February. Which of the following describes the overall change over the two periods?$q$, $q$["An increase of 10%","A decrease of 4%","An increase of 4%","An increase of 6%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Multiply the growth factors: (1.3)(0.8) = 1.04. This is more than 1, so the overall change is an increase of 4%. Simply adding the percents (+30% and −20%) is incorrect because the second change applies to the new amount.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h111$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$There are 17,500 residents of a town. In a random sample of 250 of them, 120 cycle to work, and 40% of those 120 also do so every day. Based on the sample, which of the following is the best estimate of the number of residents of the town that cycle to work every day?$q$, $q$["8400","3360","7000","48"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In the sample, 120 of 250 cycle to work, and 40% of those meet the second condition, so 120 × 0.4 = 48 of the 250 sampled meet both conditions. That is a proportion of 0.192. Applying it to the population: 17,500 × 48/250 = 3,360.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h112$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$Each of the 12 values in a data set is increased by 8. Which of the following statements about the new data set compared with the original data set must be true?$q$, $q$["The mean increases by 8, and the standard deviation is unchanged.","The mean and the standard deviation both increase by 8.","The mean is unchanged, and the standard deviation increases by 8.","The mean increases by 8, and the standard deviation increases by 96."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the same number to every value shifts the whole data set, so the mean (and median) increase by 8. The spread does not change: every distance between values, and every distance from the mean, stays the same, so the standard deviation and range are unchanged.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h113$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The number a is 30% of the number b, and b is 25% less than the number c. If c is positive, a is what percent of c?$q$, $q$["5%","7.5%","37.5%","22.5%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$b = 0.75c and a = 0.3b, so a = 0.3(0.75c) = 0.23c. So a is 22.5% of c. Adding or subtracting the percents directly is a common mistake, because the two percents are of different bases.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h114$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$A store discounts the original price of a jacket by 40%. A sales tax of 8% is then applied to the discounted price, and the final amount paid is $119.88. What was the original price of the jacket?$q$, $q$["$176.29","$154.41","$185.00","$199.80"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, the discounted price is 0.6P, and after tax the amount is 1.08(0.6P) = 0.648P. Setting this equal to 119.88 gives P = 119.88/0.648 = 185.00. Undoing the percents by reversing them as additions (e.g., adding back 40%) gives wrong answers because each percent applies to a different base.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h115$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$The mean of a data set of 13 numbers is 84. When one number is removed from the data set, the mean of the remaining numbers is 88. What number was removed?$q$, $q$["4","36","1056","49"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The original sum is 13(84) = 1092. The remaining 12 numbers sum to 12(88) = 1056. The removed number is 1092 − 1056 = 36.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h116$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$In a class, the 8 students in section A had a mean test score of 62, and the students in section B had a mean test score of 77. The mean score of all the students in both sections combined was 74. How many students are in section B?$q$, $q$["32","8","48","40"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let b be the number of students in section B. The total of all scores is 8(62) + 77b, and it also equals 74(8 + b). So 496 + 77b = 592 + 74b, which gives 3b = 96 and b = 32.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h117$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>8</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>6</td></tr></tbody></table>The frequency table summarizes a data set of 27 values. What is the median of the data set?$q$, $q$["14.11","11","15","14"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are 27 values, so the median is the 14th value when ordered. Counting up the cumulative frequencies (5, 12, 15, 21, 27), that position falls at the value 14. Note the median is not the middle row of the table (14) unless the frequencies happen to put it there.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h118$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Grade 10</th><th>Grade 11</th><th>Grade 12</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Plays a sport</td><td>26</td><td>19</td><td>15</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>Does not play a sport</td><td>52</td><td>55</td><td>6</td><td>113</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>78</td><td>74</td><td>21</td><td>173</td></tr></tbody></table>The table summarizes 173 students surveyed at a high school. If a student that is NOT in the "Grade 11" category is selected at random, what is the probability that it is in the "Plays a sport" category?$q$, $q$["41/173","41/60","41/99","19/74"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Students not in "Grade 11" number 173 − 74 = 99. Of these, the ones in "Plays a sport" number 60 − 19 = 41. The probability is 41/99.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h119$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$A contractor charges $2.25 per square foot to refinish a floor. A rectangular floor has an area of 20 square yards. What is the total charge, in dollars, to refinish the floor? (1 yard = 3 feet)$q$, $q$["$135.00","$405.00","$45.00","$5.00"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$One square yard is 3 ft × 3 ft = 9 square feet, so 20 square yards = 180 square feet. The charge is 180 × $2.25 = $405.00. Multiplying by 3 instead of 9 is a common error.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h120$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 4, null, $q$Working together at constant rates, printers A and B can print the order in 18/11 minutes. Working alone, printer A can print the order in 2 minutes. How many minutes would it take printer B, working alone, to print the order?$q$, $q$["9","4/11","36/11","11"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The combined rate is 1/(18/11) = 11/18 of the job per minute. Subtracting A's rate: 11/18 − 1/2 = 1/9. So B alone takes 9 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h121$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 400 employees of a large company and found that 42% of those in the sample would attend a weekend event. The associated margin of error is 2 percentage points. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["Exactly 42% of all employees of the company would attend a weekend event.","Between 40% and 44% of the people in the sample would attend a weekend event.","It is plausible that between 40% and 44% of all residents of the state would attend a weekend event.","It is plausible that between 40% and 44% of all employees of the company would attend a weekend event."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A margin of error describes uncertainty when estimating a population value from a random sample. The plausible range for the population is 42% ± 2%, or 40% to 44%, and the conclusion applies only to the population sampled (employees of the company). The sample percent itself is known exactly (42%), so a range for the sample is wrong, and "exactly 42%" ignores sampling variability.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h122$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 200 high school students in a certain state. Participants chose for themselves whether to use a new study app. After eight weeks, those who used a new study app had significantly better test scores than those who did not. Which of the following conclusions is best supported?$q$, $q$["Using a new study app causes improved test scores for high school students in the state.","Using a new study app causes improved test scores for participants like those in the study, but the result may not generalize to all high school students in the state.","Among high school students in the state, use of a new study app is associated with improved test scores, but a cause-and-effect relationship cannot be concluded.","Among the participants in the study, use of a new study app is associated with improved test scores, but neither causation nor a generalization to all high school students in the state is justified."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two features matter. Random sampling from a population lets results generalize to that population; this study used a random sample, so generalizing to all high school students in the state is justified. Random assignment to treatments lets you conclude cause and effect; participants chose their own treatment, so only an association can be concluded.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h123$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A scatterplot relates x, the age of a tree (years), and y, the height (feet), for a set of observations. A line of best fit for the data is y = 4x − 5. One observation has an x-value of 20 and a y-value of 69. What is the residual for this observation (actual value minus predicted value)?$q$, $q$["6","−6","69","75"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The predicted value is 4(20) − 5 = 75. The residual is actual − predicted = 69 − 75 = −6. A negative residual means the point lies below the line.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h124$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 3, null, $q$There are 80 books on a shelf, each of which is fiction, biography, or poetry. The ratio of fiction to biography is 7 : 5, and the ratio of biography to poetry is 5 : 4. How many are poetry?$q$, $q$["20","25","35","21"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Combine the ratios by matching the biography parts: fiction : biography : poetry = 7 : 5 : 4. These parts add to 16, so each part is 80/16 = 5. The number of poetry is 4 × 5 = 20.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h125$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 4, null, $q$A tray contains 2 vanilla cupcakes and 7 chocolate cupcakes. Two cupcakes are selected at random without replacement. What is the probability that one is vanilla and the other is chocolate?$q$, $q$["7/36","28/81","14/81","7/18"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are two orders. Vanilla then chocolate: (2/9)(7/8). Chocolate then vanilla: (7/9)(2/8). The total is 2(2)(7)/(9·8) = 7/18.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h126$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The population of a town increased by 25% in January and then increased by 40% in February. Which of the following describes the overall change over the two periods?$q$, $q$["An increase of 65%","A decrease of 75%","An increase of 75%","An increase of 81%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Multiply the growth factors: (1.25)(1.4) = 1.75. This is more than 1, so the overall change is an increase of 75%. Simply adding the percents (+25% and +40%) is incorrect because the second change applies to the new amount.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h127$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$There are 7,000 trees in a forest preserve. In a random sample of 300 of them, 115 are oaks, and 60% of those 115 also show signs of leaf blight. Based on the sample, which of the following is the best estimate of the number of trees in the preserve that are oaks showing signs of leaf blight?$q$, $q$["5900712402397867/2199023255552","1610","4200","69"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In the sample, 115 of 300 are oaks, and 60% of those meet the second condition, so 115 × 0.6 = 69 of the 300 sampled meet both conditions. That is a proportion of 0.23. Applying it to the population: 7,000 × 69/300 = 1,610.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h128$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A data set consists of 8 values between 20 and 40. A new value of 143 is added to the data set. Which of the following best describes how the mean and the median change?$q$, $q$["The mean increases substantially more than the median does.","The median increases substantially more than the mean does.","The mean and the median increase by the same amount.","Neither the mean nor the median changes."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The value 143 is far above all the other values. The mean uses every value's size, so it is pulled strongly upward. The median depends only on the middle position, which moves by at most half a position, and the middle values are all between 20 and 40. So the median changes very little.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h129$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The number p is 150% of the number r, and r is 10% less than the number s. If s is positive, p is what percent of s?$q$, $q$["140%","15%","165%","135%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$r = 0.9s and p = 1.5r, so p = 1.5(0.9s) = 1.35s. So p is 135% of s. Adding or subtracting the percents directly is a common mistake, because the two percents are of different bases.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h130$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$A store discounts the original price of a jacket by 35%. A sales tax of 5% is then applied to the discounted price, and the final amount paid is $477.75. What was the original price of the jacket?$q$, $q$["$682.50","$612.71","$700.00","$735.00"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, the discounted price is 0.65P, and after tax the amount is 1.05(0.65P) = 0.6825P. Setting this equal to 477.75 gives P = 477.75/0.6825 = 700.00. Undoing the percents by reversing them as additions (e.g., adding back 35%) gives wrong answers because each percent applies to a different base.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h131$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$The mean of a data set of 7 numbers is 76. When one number is removed from the data set, the mean of the remaining numbers is 67. What number was removed?$q$, $q$["9","130","402","137"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The original sum is 7(76) = 532. The remaining 6 numbers sum to 6(67) = 402. The removed number is 532 − 402 = 130.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h132$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$In a class, the 19 students in section A had a mean test score of 87, and the students in section B had a mean test score of 78. The mean score of all the students in both sections combined was 81. How many students are in section B?$q$, $q$["38","19","40","57"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let b be the number of students in section B. The total of all scores is 19(87) + 78b, and it also equals 81(19 + b). So 1653 + 78b = 1539 + 81b, which gives −3b = −114 and b = 38.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h133$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table>The frequency table summarizes a data set of 19 values. What is the median of the data set?$q$, $q$["14","14.95","12","16"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are 19 values, so the median is the 10th value when ordered. Counting up the cumulative frequencies (2, 4, 8, 15, 19), that position falls at the value 16. Note the median is not the middle row of the table (14) unless the frequencies happen to put it there.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h134$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Grade 10</th><th>Grade 11</th><th>Grade 12</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Plays a sport</td><td>21</td><td>55</td><td>10</td><td>86</td></tr><tr><td>Does not play a sport</td><td>57</td><td>56</td><td>36</td><td>149</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>78</td><td>111</td><td>46</td><td>235</td></tr></tbody></table>The table summarizes 235 students surveyed at a high school. If one student in the "Grade 10" category is selected at random, what is the probability that the student is in the "Plays a sport" category?$q$, $q$["21/86","21/235","7/26","78/235"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The selection is restricted to the 78 students in the "Grade 10" column. Of these, 21 are in the "Plays a sport" row, so the probability is 21/78 = 7/26.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h135$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$A material has a density of 2.7 grams per cubic centimeter. What is its density in kilograms per cubic meter? (1 kilogram = 1,000 grams and 1 meter = 100 centimeters)$q$, $q$["270","2,700","27","2,700,000"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$1 cubic meter = (100 cm)³ = 1,000,000 cubic centimeters. So 2.7 g/cm³ = 2.7 × 1,000,000 g/m³ = 2,700,000 g/m³. Dividing by 1,000 to convert grams to kilograms gives 2,700 kg/m³.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h136$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$Working alone at a constant rate, pipe A can fill the tank in 4 minutes. Working alone at a constant rate, pipe B can fill the tank in 5 minutes. How many minutes would it take pipes A and B, working together at these rates, to fill the tank?$q$, $q$["20/9","9/2","9","1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rates add, not times. Pipe A does 1/4 of the job per minute and pipe B does 1/5. Together they do 1/4 + 1/5 = 9/20 of the job per minute, so the time is 20/9 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h137$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 1200 employees of a large company and found that 41% of those in the sample use public transit at least once a week. The associated margin of error is 6 percentage points. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["Exactly 41% of all employees of the company use public transit at least once a week.","Between 35% and 47% of the people in the sample use public transit at least once a week.","It is plausible that between 35% and 47% of all residents of the state use public transit at least once a week.","It is plausible that between 35% and 47% of all employees of the company use public transit at least once a week."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A margin of error describes uncertainty when estimating a population value from a random sample. The plausible range for the population is 41% ± 6%, or 35% to 47%, and the conclusion applies only to the population sampled (employees of the company). The sample percent itself is known exactly (41%), so a range for the sample is wrong, and "exactly 41%" ignores sampling variability.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h138$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher recruited 200 volunteers from among high school students in a certain state. Participants chose for themselves whether to use a new study app. After eight weeks, those who used a new study app had significantly better test scores than those who did not. Which of the following conclusions is best supported?$q$, $q$["Using a new study app causes improved test scores for high school students in the state.","Using a new study app causes improved test scores for participants like those in the study, but the result may not generalize to all high school students in the state.","Among the participants in the study, use of a new study app is associated with improved test scores, but neither causation nor a generalization to all high school students in the state is justified.","Among high school students in the state, use of a new study app is associated with improved test scores, but a cause-and-effect relationship cannot be concluded."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two features matter. Random sampling from a population lets results generalize to that population; this study used volunteers, so the results may not generalize. Random assignment to treatments lets you conclude cause and effect; participants chose their own treatment, so only an association can be concluded.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h139$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A scatterplot relates x, the hours studied, and y, the test score, for a set of observations. A line of best fit for the data is y = −1.5x − 18. One observation has an x-value of 17 and a y-value of −44. What is the residual for this observation (actual value minus predicted value)?$q$, $q$["0.5","−1/2","−44","−43.5"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The predicted value is −1.5(17) − 18 = −43.5. The residual is actual − predicted = −44 + 43.5 = −0.5. A negative residual means the point lies below the line.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h140$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 3, null, $q$The ratio of red to blue marbles in a bag is 5 : 9, and the ratio of blue to green marbles is 9 : 5. What is the ratio of red to green marbles?$q$, $q$["1 : 1","5 : 5","25 : 81","14 : 14"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Make the blue parts match. The least common multiple of 9 and 9 is 9. Scaling gives red : blue = 5 : 9 and blue : green = 9 : 5. So red : green = 5 : 5, which simplifies to 1 : 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h141$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 4, null, $q$A tray contains 3 vanilla cupcakes and 6 chocolate cupcakes. Two cupcakes are selected at random, one after the other, without replacement. What is the probability that both are vanilla?$q$, $q$["1/9","2/27","5/18","1/12"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first is vanilla with probability 3/9. Given that, 2 of the remaining 8 are vanilla, so the second is vanilla with probability 2/8. Multiplying: (3/9)(2/8) = 1/12.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h142$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The price of an item increased by 150%. By what percent must the new price decrease to return the item to its original price?$q$, $q$["150%","75%","60%","37.5%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, the new price is 2.5P. To return to P, the new price must be multiplied by 1/2.5 = 0.4, a decrease of 60%. The decrease is smaller than 150% because it is taken from a larger base.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h143$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$There are 25,500 trees in a forest preserve. In a random sample of 150 of them, 34 are oaks, and 60% of those 34 also show signs of leaf blight. Based on the sample, which of the following is the best estimate of the number of trees in the preserve that are oaks showing signs of leaf blight?$q$, $q$["5780","3468","15300","20"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In the sample, 34 of 150 are oaks, and 60% of those meet the second condition, so 34 × 0.6 = 20.4 of the 150 sampled meet both conditions. That is a proportion of 0.136. Applying it to the population: 25,500 × 20.4/150 = 3,468.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h144$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$Each of the 8 values in a data set with a positive standard deviation is multiplied by 2. Which of the following statements about the new data set compared with the original must be true?$q$, $q$["The mean and the standard deviation are both multiplied by 2.","The mean is multiplied by 2, and the standard deviation is unchanged.","The mean is multiplied by 2, and the standard deviation is multiplied by 4.","The mean is unchanged, and the standard deviation is multiplied by 2."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying every value by 2 stretches the data set: every value, and every distance between values, is multiplied by 2. So the mean, median, range, and standard deviation are all multiplied by 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h145$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The number a is 30% of the number b, and b is 50% greater than the number c. If c is positive, a is what percent of c?$q$, $q$["80%","15%","−20%","45%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$b = 1.5c and a = 0.3b, so a = 0.3(1.5c) = 0.45c. So a is 45% of c. Adding or subtracting the percents directly is a common mistake, because the two percents are of different bases.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h146$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$A store discounts the original price of a jacket by 20%. A sales tax of 7.5% is then applied to the discounted price, and the final amount paid is $520.30. What was the original price of the jacket?$q$, $q$["$594.63","$577.53","$605.00","$650.37"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, the discounted price is 0.8P, and after tax the amount is 1.075(0.8P) = 0.86P. Setting this equal to 520.30 gives P = 520.30/0.86 = 605.00. Undoing the percents by reversing them as additions (e.g., adding back 20%) gives wrong answers because each percent applies to a different base.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h147$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A student's mean score on 14 quizzes is 72. The student will take 2 more quizzes and wants the mean of all 16 quiz scores to be exactly 73. If the student earns the same score on each of the 2 new quizzes, what must that score be?$q$, $q$["74","80","94","75"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The current total is 14(72) = 1008. For a mean of 73 over 16 quizzes, the total must be 16(73) = 1168. The 2 new quizzes must add 160 points, so each must be 160/2 = 80.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h148$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$In a class, the 12 students in section A had a mean test score of 68, and the students in section B had a mean test score of 76. The mean score of all the students in both sections combined was 73. How many students are in section B?$q$, $q$["20","12","30","32"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let b be the number of students in section B. The total of all scores is 12(68) + 76b, and it also equals 73(12 + b). So 816 + 76b = 876 + 73b, which gives 3b = 60 and b = 20.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h149$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>7</td></tr></tbody></table>The frequency table summarizes a data set of 23 values. What is the median of the data set?$q$, $q$["11","11.78","5","14"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are 23 values, so the median is the 12th value when ordered. Counting up the cumulative frequencies (7, 8, 9, 16, 23), that position falls at the value 14. Note the median is not the middle row of the table (11) unless the frequencies happen to put it there.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h150$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Morning</th><th>Afternoon</th><th>Evening</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rated positive</td><td>41</td><td>8</td><td>26</td><td>75</td></tr><tr><td>Rated negative</td><td>25</td><td>14</td><td>39</td><td>78</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>66</td><td>22</td><td>65</td><td>153</td></tr></tbody></table>The table summarizes 153 customer visits recorded at a clinic. If one visit in the "Afternoon" category is selected at random, what is the probability that the visit is in the "Rated negative" category?$q$, $q$["7/39","14/153","7/11","22/153"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The selection is restricted to the 22 visits in the "Afternoon" column. Of these, 14 are in the "Rated negative" row, so the probability is 14/22 = 7/11.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h151$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$Water flows into an empty tank at a constant rate of 14 gallons per minute. The tank holds 6,000 gallons. To the nearest tenth of an hour, how long does it take to fill the tank?$q$, $q$["428.6 hours","7.1 hours","4.3 hours","25.7 hours"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In one hour, 14 × 60 = 840 gallons flow in. The time to fill 6,000 gallons is 6000/840 ≈ 7.1 hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h152$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$Working alone at a constant rate, printer A can print the order in 2 hours. Working alone at a constant rate, printer B can print the order in 12 hours. How many hours would it take printers A and B, working together at these rates, to print the order?$q$, $q$["12/7","7","14","10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rates add, not times. Printer A does 1/2 of the job per hour and printer B does 1/12. Together they do 1/2 + 1/12 = 7/12 of the job per hour, so the time is 12/7 hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h153$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 300 students at a large university and found that 69% of those in the sample own a pet. The associated margin of error is 5 percentage points. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["Exactly 69% of all students at the university own a pet.","Between 64% and 74% of the people in the sample own a pet.","It is plausible that between 64% and 74% of all residents of the state own a pet.","It is plausible that between 64% and 74% of all students at the university own a pet."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A margin of error describes uncertainty when estimating a population value from a random sample. The plausible range for the population is 69% ± 5%, or 64% to 74%, and the conclusion applies only to the population sampled (students at the university). The sample percent itself is known exactly (69%), so a range for the sample is wrong, and "exactly 69%" ignores sampling variability.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h154$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher recruited 200 volunteers from among adults in a large city. The participants were randomly assigned to either use a daily stretching routine or not. After eight weeks, those who used a daily stretching routine had significantly better reported back pain than those who did not. Which of the following conclusions is best supported?$q$, $q$["Using a daily stretching routine causes improved reported back pain for adults in the city.","Among adults in the city, use of a daily stretching routine is associated with improved reported back pain, but a cause-and-effect relationship cannot be concluded.","Using a daily stretching routine causes improved reported back pain for participants like those in the study, but the result may not generalize to all adults in the city.","Among the participants in the study, use of a daily stretching routine is associated with improved reported back pain, but neither causation nor a generalization to all adults in the city is justified."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two features matter. Random sampling from a population lets results generalize to that population; this study used volunteers, so the results may not generalize. Random assignment to treatments lets you conclude cause and effect; participants were randomly assigned, so a causal conclusion is justified.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h155$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A scatterplot relates x, the hours studied, and y, the test score, for a set of observations. A line of best fit for the data is y = 0.8x + 1. One observation has an x-value of 10 and a y-value of 11. What is the residual for this observation (actual value minus predicted value)?$q$, $q$["−2","2","11","9"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The predicted value is 0.8(10) + 1 = 9. The residual is actual − predicted = 11 − 9 = 2. A positive residual means the point lies above the line.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h156$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 3, null, $q$The ratio of fiction to biography books on a shelf is 3 : 9, and the ratio of biography to poetry books is 5 : 3. What is the ratio of fiction to poetry books?$q$, $q$["5 : 9","3 : 3","9 : 45","8 : 12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Make the biography parts match. The least common multiple of 9 and 5 is 45. Scaling gives fiction : biography = 15 : 45 and biography : poetry = 45 : 27. So fiction : poetry = 15 : 27, which simplifies to 5 : 9.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h157$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 4, null, $q$A box contains 5 defective batteries and 4 working batteries. Two batteries are selected at random without replacement. What is the probability that one is defective and the other is working?$q$, $q$["5/18","40/81","20/81","5/9"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are two orders. Defective then working: (5/9)(4/8). Working then defective: (4/9)(5/8). The total is 2(5)(4)/(9·8) = 5/9.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h158$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The number of members of a club decreased by 80% in January and then decreased by 20% in February. Which of the following describes the overall change over the two periods?$q$, $q$["A decrease of 100%","An increase of 84%","A decrease of 84%","A decrease of 90%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Multiply the growth factors: (0.19999999999999996)(0.8) = 0.16. This is less than 1, so the overall change is a decrease of 84%. Simply adding the percents (−80% and −20%) is incorrect because the second change applies to the new amount.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h159$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$There are 28,500 residents of a town. In a random sample of 150 of them, 26 cycle to work, and 60% of those 26 also do so every day. Based on the sample, which of the following is the best estimate of the number of residents of the town that cycle to work every day?$q$, $q$["4940","2964","17100","16"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In the sample, 26 of 150 cycle to work, and 60% of those meet the second condition, so 26 × 0.6 = 15.6 of the 150 sampled meet both conditions. That is a proportion of 0.104. Applying it to the population: 28,500 × 15.6/150 = 2,964.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h160$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$Each of the 7 values in a data set with a positive standard deviation is multiplied by 3. Which of the following statements about the new data set compared with the original must be true?$q$, $q$["The mean and the standard deviation are both multiplied by 3.","The mean is multiplied by 3, and the standard deviation is unchanged.","The mean is multiplied by 3, and the standard deviation is multiplied by 9.","The mean is unchanged, and the standard deviation is multiplied by 3."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying every value by 3 stretches the data set: every value, and every distance between values, is multiplied by 3. So the mean, median, range, and standard deviation are all multiplied by 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h161$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The number x is 30% of the number y, and y is 10% less than the number z. If z is positive, x is what percent of z?$q$, $q$["20%","3%","33%","27%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$y = 0.9z and x = 0.3y, so x = 0.3(0.9z) = 0.27z. So x is 27% of z. Adding or subtracting the percents directly is a common mistake, because the two percents are of different bases.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h162$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$A store discounts the original price of a jacket by 25%. A sales tax of 8% is then applied to the discounted price, and the final amount paid is $623.70. What was the original price of the jacket?$q$, $q$["$751.45","$717.26","$770.00","$831.60"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, the discounted price is 0.75P, and after tax the amount is 1.08(0.75P) = 0.81P. Setting this equal to 623.70 gives P = 623.70/0.81 = 770.00. Undoing the percents by reversing them as additions (e.g., adding back 25%) gives wrong answers because each percent applies to a different base.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h163$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$The mean of a data set of 10 numbers is 35. When one number is removed from the data set, the mean of the remaining numbers is 24. What number was removed?$q$, $q$["11","134","216","144"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The original sum is 10(35) = 350. The remaining 9 numbers sum to 9(24) = 216. The removed number is 350 − 216 = 134.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h164$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$In a class, the 39 students in section A had a mean test score of 90, and the students in section B had a mean test score of 68. The mean score of all the students in both sections combined was 79. How many students are in section B?$q$, $q$["39","41","78","22"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let b be the number of students in section B. The total of all scores is 39(90) + 68b, and it also equals 79(39 + b). So 3510 + 68b = 3081 + 79b, which gives −11b = −429 and b = 39.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h165$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>5</td></tr></tbody></table>The frequency table summarizes a data set of 29 values. What is the median of the data set?$q$, $q$["9.14","7","10","9"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are 29 values, so the median is the 15th value when ordered. Counting up the cumulative frequencies (4, 10, 18, 24, 29), that position falls at the value 9. Note the median is not the middle row of the table (9) unless the frequencies happen to put it there.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h166$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Under 30</th><th>30 to 49</th><th>50 and older</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Prefers streaming</td><td>46</td><td>53</td><td>50</td><td>149</td></tr><tr><td>Prefers cable</td><td>52</td><td>28</td><td>21</td><td>101</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>98</td><td>81</td><td>71</td><td>250</td></tr></tbody></table>The table summarizes 250 respondents to a media survey. If one respondent in the "50 and older" category is selected at random, what is the probability that the respondent is in the "Prefers cable" category?$q$, $q$["21/101","21/250","21/71","71/250"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The selection is restricted to the 71 respondents in the "50 and older" column. Of these, 21 are in the "Prefers cable" row, so the probability is 21/71.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h167$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$Water flows into an empty tank at a constant rate of 13 gallons per minute. The tank holds 9,000 gallons. To the nearest tenth of an hour, how long does it take to fill the tank?$q$, $q$["692.3 hours","11.5 hours","6.9 hours","41.5 hours"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In one hour, 13 × 60 = 780 gallons flow in. The time to fill 9,000 gallons is 9000/780 ≈ 11.5 hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h168$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 4, null, $q$Working together at constant rates, printers A and B can print the order in 10/3 minutes. Working alone, printer A can print the order in 10 minutes. How many minutes would it take printer B, working alone, to print the order?$q$, $q$["5","20/3","15","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The combined rate is 1/(10/3) = 3/10 of the job per minute. Subtracting A's rate: 3/10 − 1/10 = 1/5. So B alone takes 5 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h169$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 300 registered voters in a certain city and found that 71% of those in the sample own a pet. The associated margin of error is 2 percentage points. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["Exactly 71% of all registered voters in the city own a pet.","Between 69% and 73% of the people in the sample own a pet.","It is plausible that between 69% and 73% of all residents of the state own a pet.","It is plausible that between 69% and 73% of all registered voters in the city own a pet."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A margin of error describes uncertainty when estimating a population value from a random sample. The plausible range for the population is 71% ± 2%, or 69% to 73%, and the conclusion applies only to the population sampled (registered voters in the city). The sample percent itself is known exactly (71%), so a range for the sample is wrong, and "exactly 71%" ignores sampling variability.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h170$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher recruited 200 volunteers from among high school students in a certain state. Participants chose for themselves whether to use a reduced-sugar diet. After eight weeks, those who used a reduced-sugar diet had significantly better afternoon energy levels than those who did not. Which of the following conclusions is best supported?$q$, $q$["Using a reduced-sugar diet causes improved afternoon energy levels for high school students in the state.","Using a reduced-sugar diet causes improved afternoon energy levels for participants like those in the study, but the result may not generalize to all high school students in the state.","Among the participants in the study, use of a reduced-sugar diet is associated with improved afternoon energy levels, but neither causation nor a generalization to all high school students in the state is justified.","Among high school students in the state, use of a reduced-sugar diet is associated with improved afternoon energy levels, but a cause-and-effect relationship cannot be concluded."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two features matter. Random sampling from a population lets results generalize to that population; this study used volunteers, so the results may not generalize. Random assignment to treatments lets you conclude cause and effect; participants chose their own treatment, so only an association can be concluded.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h171$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A scatterplot relates x, the hours studied, and y, the test score, for a set of observations. A line of best fit for the data is y = −1.5x − 12. One observation has an x-value of 15 and a y-value of −38.5. What is the residual for this observation (actual value minus predicted value)?$q$, $q$["4","−4","−38.5","−34.5"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The predicted value is −1.5(15) − 12 = −34.5. The residual is actual − predicted = −38.5 + 34.5 = −4. A negative residual means the point lies below the line.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h172$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 3, null, $q$There are 38 trees in a park, each of which is oak, maple, or pine. The ratio of oak to maple is 4 : 5, and the ratio of maple to pine is 2 : 4. How many are pine?$q$, $q$["20","12","8","10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Combine the ratios by matching the maple parts: oak : maple : pine = 4 : 5 : 10. These parts add to 19, so each part is 38/19 = 2. The number of pine is 10 × 2 = 20.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h173$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 4, null, $q$A bag contains 8 red marbles and 3 blue marbles. Two marbles are selected at random, one after the other, without replacement. What is the probability that both are red?$q$, $q$["64/121","56/121","15/22","28/55"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first is red with probability 8/11. Given that, 7 of the remaining 10 are red, so the second is red with probability 7/10. Multiplying: (8/11)(7/10) = 28/55.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h174$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The number of members of a club decreased by 10% in the first year and then decreased by 40% in the second year. Which of the following describes the overall change over the two periods?$q$, $q$["A decrease of 50%","An increase of 46%","A decrease of 46%","A decrease of 52%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Multiply the growth factors: (0.9)(0.6) = 0.54. This is less than 1, so the overall change is a decrease of 46%. Simply adding the percents (−10% and −40%) is incorrect because the second change applies to the new amount.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h175$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$There are 8,500 residents of a town. In a random sample of 500 of them, 165 cycle to work, and 40% of those 165 also do so every day. Based on the sample, which of the following is the best estimate of the number of residents of the town that cycle to work every day?$q$, $q$["2805","1122","3400","66"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In the sample, 165 of 500 cycle to work, and 40% of those meet the second condition, so 165 × 0.4 = 66 of the 500 sampled meet both conditions. That is a proportion of 0.132. Applying it to the population: 8,500 × 66/500 = 1,122.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h176$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$Each of the 12 values in a data set with a positive standard deviation is multiplied by 2. Which of the following statements about the new data set compared with the original must be true?$q$, $q$["The mean and the standard deviation are both multiplied by 2.","The mean is multiplied by 2, and the standard deviation is unchanged.","The mean is multiplied by 2, and the standard deviation is multiplied by 4.","The mean is unchanged, and the standard deviation is multiplied by 2."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiplying every value by 2 stretches the data set: every value, and every distance between values, is multiplied by 2. So the mean, median, range, and standard deviation are all multiplied by 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h177$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$The number x is 75% of the number y, and y is 20% greater than the number z. If z is positive, x is what percent of z?$q$, $q$["95%","15%","60%","90%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$y = 1.2z and x = 0.75y, so x = 0.75(1.2z) = 0.9z. So x is 90% of z. Adding or subtracting the percents directly is a common mistake, because the two percents are of different bases.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h178$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 3, null, $q$A store discounts the original price of a jacket by 40%. A sales tax of 6% is then applied to the discounted price, and the final amount paid is $429.30. What was the original price of the jacket?$q$, $q$["$650.45","$564.96","$675.00","$715.50"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the original price is P, the discounted price is 0.6P, and after tax the amount is 1.06(0.6P) = 0.636P. Setting this equal to 429.30 gives P = 429.30/0.636 = 675.00. Undoing the percents by reversing them as additions (e.g., adding back 40%) gives wrong answers because each percent applies to a different base.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h179$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$The mean of a data set of 19 numbers is 66. When one number is removed from the data set, the mean of the remaining numbers is 68.5. What number was removed?$q$, $q$["4","21","1233","40"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The original sum is 19(66) = 1254. The remaining 18 numbers sum to 18(68.5) = 1233. The removed number is 1254 − 1233 = 21.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h180$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$In a class, the 22 students in section A had a mean test score of 64, and the students in section B had a mean test score of 94. The mean score of all the students in both sections combined was 83. How many students are in section B?$q$, $q$["38","22","42","60"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let b be the number of students in section B. The total of all scores is 22(64) + 94b, and it also equals 83(22 + b). So 1408 + 94b = 1826 + 83b, which gives 11b = 418 and b = 38.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h181$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table>The frequency table summarizes a data set of 25 values. What is the median of the data set?$q$, $q$["7.2","6","8","7"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are 25 values, so the median is the 13th value when ordered. Counting up the cumulative frequencies (2, 6, 15, 22, 25), that position falls at the value 7. Note the median is not the middle row of the table (7) unless the frequencies happen to put it there.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h182$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 3, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Morning</th><th>Afternoon</th><th>Evening</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rated positive</td><td>5</td><td>7</td><td>47</td><td>59</td></tr><tr><td>Rated negative</td><td>29</td><td>8</td><td>21</td><td>58</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>34</td><td>15</td><td>68</td><td>117</td></tr></tbody></table>The table summarizes 117 customer visits recorded at a clinic. If a visit that is NOT in the "Morning" category is selected at random, what is the probability that it is in the "Rated negative" category?$q$, $q$["29/117","1/2","29/83","29/34"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Visits not in "Morning" number 117 − 34 = 83. Of these, the ones in "Rated negative" number 58 − 29 = 29. The probability is 29/83.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h183$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$A contractor charges $2.25 per square foot to refinish a floor. A rectangular floor has an area of 120 square yards. What is the total charge, in dollars, to refinish the floor? (1 yard = 3 feet)$q$, $q$["$810.00","$2,430.00","$270.00","$30.00"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$One square yard is 3 ft × 3 ft = 9 square feet, so 120 square yards = 1080 square feet. The charge is 1080 × $2.25 = $2,430.00. Multiplying by 3 instead of 9 is a common error.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h184$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 3, null, $q$Working alone at a constant rate, printer A can print the order in 4 minutes. Working alone at a constant rate, printer B can print the order in 6 minutes. How many minutes would it take printers A and B, working together at these rates, to print the order?$q$, $q$["12/5","5","10","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rates add, not times. Printer A does 1/4 of the job per minute and printer B does 1/6. Together they do 1/4 + 1/6 = 5/12 of the job per minute, so the time is 12/5 minutes.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h185$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 500 registered voters in a certain city and found that 59% of those in the sample use public transit at least once a week. The associated margin of error is 4 percentage points. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["Exactly 59% of all registered voters in the city use public transit at least once a week.","Between 55% and 63% of the people in the sample use public transit at least once a week.","It is plausible that between 55% and 63% of all residents of the state use public transit at least once a week.","It is plausible that between 55% and 63% of all registered voters in the city use public transit at least once a week."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A margin of error describes uncertainty when estimating a population value from a random sample. The plausible range for the population is 59% ± 4%, or 55% to 63%, and the conclusion applies only to the population sampled (registered voters in the city). The sample percent itself is known exactly (59%), so a range for the sample is wrong, and "exactly 59%" ignores sampling variability.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h186$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 3, null, $q$A researcher selected a random sample of 200 members of a national hiking club. Participants chose for themselves whether to use a daily stretching routine. After eight weeks, those who used a daily stretching routine had significantly better reported back pain than those who did not. Which of the following conclusions is best supported?$q$, $q$["Using a daily stretching routine causes improved reported back pain for members of the club.","Using a daily stretching routine causes improved reported back pain for participants like those in the study, but the result may not generalize to all members of the club.","Among members of the club, use of a daily stretching routine is associated with improved reported back pain, but a cause-and-effect relationship cannot be concluded.","Among the participants in the study, use of a daily stretching routine is associated with improved reported back pain, but neither causation nor a generalization to all members of the club is justified."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two features matter. Random sampling from a population lets results generalize to that population; this study used a random sample, so generalizing to all members of the club is justified. Random assignment to treatments lets you conclude cause and effect; participants chose their own treatment, so only an association can be concluded.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h187$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 3, null, $q$A scatterplot relates x, the temperature (°C), and y, the daily sales (dozens), for a set of observations. A line of best fit for the data is y = 3.5x + 22. One observation has an x-value of 8 and a y-value of 47. What is the residual for this observation (actual value minus predicted value)?$q$, $q$["3","−3","47","50"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The predicted value is 3.5(8) + 22 = 50. The residual is actual − predicted = 47 − 50 = −3. A negative residual means the point lies below the line.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h188$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 3, null, $q$The ratio of red to blue marbles in a bag is 6 : 7, and the ratio of blue to green marbles is 8 : 7. What is the ratio of red to green marbles?$q$, $q$["48 : 49","6 : 7","42 : 56","14 : 14"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Make the blue parts match. The least common multiple of 7 and 8 is 56. Scaling gives red : blue = 48 : 56 and blue : green = 56 : 49. So red : green = 48 : 49, which simplifies to 48 : 49.$q$, null),
  ($q$psd-h189$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 4, null, $q$A bag contains 2 red marbles and 8 blue marbles. Two marbles are selected at random without replacement. What is the probability that one is red and the other is blue?$q$, $q$["8/45","8/25","4/25","16/45"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$There are two orders. Red then blue: (2/10)(8/9). Blue then red: (8/10)(2/9). The total is 2(2)(8)/(10·9) = 16/45.$q$, null)
